  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Actinomyces and Nocardia',
    'A 35-year-old man presents with a slowly enlarging, hard swelling at the angle of the jaw with several small draining sinus tracts that express yellow granules. He had a dental extraction 6 weeks ago. The organism is a gram-positive branching filamentous rod that is not acid fast and grows only under anaerobic conditions. He has no pulmonary symptoms.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Penicillin"},{"id":"B","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol"},{"id":"D","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"E","text":"Vancomycin"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is actinomycosis, caused by Actinomyces, which forms long branching filaments, is an anaerobe, is catalase negative, is not acid fast, and is normal oral, reproductive, and GI microbiota. It produces oral and facial abscesses that drain through sinus tracts, often after dental caries or extraction, and forms yellow sulfur granules. The mnemonic SNAP applies: sulfonamides for Nocardia and penicillin for Actinomyces.',
    '{"B":"TMP-SMX is the treatment for Nocardia, which is aerobic, catalase positive, and weakly acid fast, unlike this anaerobic organism.","C":"This multidrug regimen is for tuberculosis, and the source notes that Nocardia can mimic TB but with a negative PPD; neither organism is a mycobacterium.","D":"Doxycycline is used for atypical organisms such as Mycoplasma and Chlamydia and for Lyme disease, not for actinomycosis.","E":"Vancomycin targets gram-positive cocci such as MRSA and enterococci, not the treatment of choice for Actinomyces."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Actinomyces and Nocardia',
    'A 62-year-old man receiving chronic high-dose corticosteroids for autoimmune disease presents with 3 weeks of productive cough, fever, and night sweats. Chest imaging shows a cavitary upper lobe lesion resembling tuberculosis, but a PPD is negative. Sputum culture grows a weakly acid-fast, branching, filamentous gram-positive rod that is catalase positive and grows aerobically. Brain MRI shows a ring-enhancing lesion.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the organism and its treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Nocardia, treated with penicillin"},{"id":"B","text":"Actinomyces, treated with sulfonamides"},{"id":"C","text":"Nocardia, treated with sulfonamides such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Actinomyces, treated with penicillin"},{"id":"E","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis, treated with rifampin and isoniazid"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Nocardia is an aerobic, catalase-positive, weakly acid-fast branching filamentous gram-positive rod found in soil. It causes pulmonary infection in immunocompromised patients that can mimic TB but with a negative PPD, and it can spread to the CNS as a cerebral abscess. Treatment is with sulfonamides such as TMP-SMX, summarized by the mnemonic that treatment is a SNAP: Sulfonamides for Nocardia, Penicillin for Actinomyces.',
    '{"A":"Although the organism is correctly identified as Nocardia, penicillin is the treatment for Actinomyces, not for Nocardia.","B":"Both the organism identity and the drug pairing are reversed here; Actinomyces is anaerobic, not acid fast, and treated with penicillin.","D":"Actinomyces is an anaerobe, catalase negative, and not acid fast, and it causes oral and facial disease with sulfur granules rather than this cavitary pulmonary and CNS picture.","E":"TB can produce cavitary upper lobe disease with CNS involvement, but it is acid-fast with a different morphology on culture and would show a strongly positive acid-fast stain rather than weakly acid-fast branching filaments."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Actinomyces and Nocardia',
    'Two organisms isolated in a hospital microbiology laboratory both appear as long, branching, filamentous gram-positive rods that resemble fungi under the microscope. One grew aerobically and was catalase positive; the other grew only under anaerobic conditions and was catalase negative.',
    'Which of the following best distinguishes these two organisms on staining?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The aerobic isolate is weakly acid fast, whereas the anaerobic isolate is not acid fast"},{"id":"B","text":"The anaerobic isolate is weakly acid fast, whereas the aerobic isolate is not acid fast"},{"id":"C","text":"Both isolates are strongly acid fast and are therefore classified as mycobacteria"},{"id":"D","text":"The aerobic isolate is gram negative, whereas the anaerobic isolate is gram positive"},{"id":"E","text":"Both isolates are candida species that form pseudohyphae"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Both Nocardia and Actinomyces are gram-positive and form long branching filaments resembling fungi, which is why they are often contrasted in a table. Nocardia is the aerobe, catalase positive, and weakly acid fast, found in soil. Actinomyces is the anaerobe, catalase negative, and not acid fast, and is normal oral, reproductive, and GI microbiota.',
    '{"B":"This reverses the pair; acid fastness is a property of Nocardia, the aerobic organism.","C":"Neither is strongly acid fast; mycobacteria such as M tuberculosis are the acid-fast rods that grow slowly in culture.","D":"Both organisms are gram positive, which is part of why they are mistaken for fungi in tissue.","E":"Candida are true fungi with pseudohyphae and do not grow as the aerobic and anaerobic filamentous bacterial pair described."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Antifungal drugs and mechanisms',
    'A 66-year-old man with febrile neutropenia is started on an antifungal that his pharmacist explains targets a structure unique to fungi and forms pores in the membrane, allowing electrolytes to leak out. During infusion he develops fever, chills, hypotension, and later is noted to have rising creatinine and low potassium and magnesium.',
    'Which of the following drugs is described, and what is its mechanism of action?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amphotericin B, which binds ergosterol and forms membrane pores"},{"id":"B","text":"Flucytosine, which inhibits DNA and RNA synthesis"},{"id":"C","text":"Terbinafine, which inhibits squalene epoxidase"},{"id":"D","text":"Fluconazole, which inhibits ergosterol synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"Caspofungin, which inhibits beta-glucan synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Amphotericin B binds ergosterol, which is unique to fungi, and forms membrane pores that let electrolytes leak out; the mnemonic is that amphotericin ''tears'' holes in the fungal membrane. Its classic adverse effects are fever and chills (''shake and bake''), hypotension, nephrotoxicity, arrhythmias, anemia, and IV phlebitis, which together earn it the name ''amphoterrible.'' Because renal tubular permeability is altered, potassium and magnesium must be supplemented. Hydration reduces nephrotoxicity, and liposomal formulations are less toxic.',
    '{"B":"Flucytosine is converted to 5-fluorouracil by fungal cytosine deaminase and inhibits DNA and RNA biosynthesis, and its hallmark toxicity is myelosuppression.","C":"Terbinafine inhibits the fungal enzyme squalene epoxidase, a different step in the sterol pathway, and it is used for dermatophytoses rather than serious systemic mycoses.","D":"Fluconazole inhibits the cytochrome P-450 enzyme that converts lanosterol to ergosterol, which is a synthesis step rather than pore formation in the completed membrane.","E":"Caspofungin is an echinocandin that inhibits cell wall synthesis by blocking beta-glucan production, not by interacting with membrane sterols."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Antifungal drugs and mechanisms',
    'A 59-year-old man with acute leukemia has prolonged neutropenia and develops invasive aspergillosis with a pulmonary nodule and halo sign. His team wants to choose a drug class that targets the fungal cell wall, keeping amphotericin B in reserve because of a rising creatinine.',
    'Which of the following correctly pairs an agent with its mechanism of action?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amphotericin B interferes with microtubule function and mitosis"},{"id":"B","text":"Fluconazole inhibits beta-glucan synthesis in the cell wall"},{"id":"C","text":"Caspofungin binds ergosterol and forms membrane pores"},{"id":"D","text":"Caspofungin inhibits synthesis of beta-glucan in the cell wall"},{"id":"E","text":"Flucytosine inhibits squalene epoxidase and blocks sterol synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Echinocandins, including anidulafungin, caspofungin, and micafungin, inhibit cell wall synthesis by blocking beta-glucan production, and they are used for invasive aspergillosis and Candida infections as second-line or alternative therapy when amphotericin is undesirable. Their characteristic adverse effects are GI upset and flushing from histamine release. Because they target the cell wall, a structure with no human counterpart, they are attractive when amphotericin''s nephrotoxicity must be avoided.',
    '{"A":"Microtubule disruption and mitotic interference are the mechanisms of griseofulvin, whereas amphotericin B acts at the membrane sterol level.","B":"Fluconazole is an azole that inhibits the cytochrome P-450 enzyme converting lanosterol to ergosterol, and beta-glucan synthesis inhibition belongs to the echinocandins.","C":"Ergosterol binding with pore formation is the mechanism of amphotericin B and nystatin, not of the echinocandins, which act on the cell wall.","E":"Flucytosine works as an antimetabolite converted to 5-fluorouracil to inhibit DNA and RNA synthesis, and squalene epoxidase inhibition is the mechanism of terbinafine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Antifungal drugs and mechanisms',
    'A 38-year-old man with AIDS and cryptococcal meningitis is started on amphotericin B and a second agent that is converted to 5-fluorouracil within the fungus by cytosine deaminase, inhibiting DNA and RNA biosynthesis. Two weeks later he develops leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. A woman using an azole for a resistant fungal infection is noted to have gynecomastia and a prolonged QT interval.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely cause of the myelosuppression in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Fluconazole"},{"id":"B","text":"Nystatin"},{"id":"C","text":"Flucytosine"},{"id":"D","text":"Voriconazole"},{"id":"E","text":"Amphotericin B"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Flucytosine is converted to 5-fluorouracil by fungal cytosine deaminase, inhibiting DNA and RNA biosynthesis, and is used for systemic fungal infections, especially Cryptococcus meningitis, in combination with amphotericin B. Its characteristic adverse effect is myelosuppression, which explains the leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. Combining it with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis is the classic regimen, with fluconazole then used for chronic suppression. Remember that its toxicity profile differs from that of amphotericin, which causes fever, chills, and nephrotoxicity.',
    '{"A":"Fluconazole inhibits ergosterol synthesis and causes liver dysfunction and QT prolongation, and the gynecomastia mentioned in the stem is typical of azoles, especially ketoconazole, not of the drug causing cytopenias.","B":"Nystatin is a topical polyene used for oral candidiasis and diaper rash, and its minimal systemic absorption means it does not cause marrow suppression.","D":"Voriconazole is used for invasive aspergillosis and causes visual disturbances and QT prolongation, but the marrow suppression here belongs to the antimetabolite partner drug.","E":"Amphotericin B causes fever and chills, hypotension, nephrotoxicity, arrhythmias, and anemia, but not the classic myelosuppression described with this pyrimidine analog."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antifungal drugs',
    'A 41-year-old man with AIDS presents with 2 weeks of headache, fever, and neck stiffness. CSF India ink preparation shows encapsulated yeast, and cryptococcal antigen is positive. He is started on liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine, with aggressive intravenous hydration and electrolyte monitoring.',
    'Which of the following best explains amphotericin B''s mechanism and its signature toxicities?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It inhibits beta-glucan synthesis in the fungal cell wall and causes flushing from histamine release"},{"id":"B","text":"It inhibits lanosterol conversion to ergosterol and causes gynecomastia with QT prolongation"},{"id":"C","text":"It is converted to 5-fluorouracil and inhibits DNA and RNA synthesis, causing myelosuppression"},{"id":"D","text":"Ergosterol pore formation with fever, nephrotoxicity, and anemia"},{"id":"E","text":"It inhibits squalene epoxidase and causes hepatotoxicity with taste disturbance"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Amphotericin B binds ergosterol, a sterol unique to fungal membranes, and forms pores that permit leakage of electrolytes, which is why potassium and magnesium must be supplemented. Its toxicities are fever and chills, hypotension, nephrotoxicity, arrhythmias, anemia, and IV phlebitis, which is why it is nicknamed amphoterrible; hydration and liposomal formulations reduce nephrotoxicity. It is used for serious systemic mycoses, and for cryptococcal meningitis it is combined with flucytosine.',
    '{"A":"Beta-glucan synthesis inhibition describes the echinocandins such as caspofungin, and their flushing is from histamine release.","B":"Blocking lanosterol-to-ergosterol conversion describes the azoles, with ketoconazole causing gynecomastia by inhibiting testosterone synthesis and azoles prolonging QT.","C":"Conversion to 5-fluorouracil by cytosine deaminase describes flucytosine, which causes myelosuppression.","E":"Squalene epoxidase inhibition describes terbinafine, used for dermatophytoses such as onychomycosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antifungal drugs',
    'A 58-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia and prolonged neutropenia develops fever and pleuritic chest pain. Chest CT shows a nodule with a halo of ground-glass opacity, and biopsy shows septate hyphae with acute-angle branching consistent with invasive aspergillosis. She is started on voriconazole, and the team monitors her liver enzymes and QT interval.',
    'Which of the following best describes voriconazole''s mechanism and class effects?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It inhibits squalene epoxidase and deposits in keratin, causing hepatotoxicity and taste disturbance"},{"id":"B","text":"Ergosterol synthesis blockade with hepatotoxicity and QT prolongation"},{"id":"C","text":"It binds ergosterol directly and forms pores in the fungal membrane"},{"id":"D","text":"It inhibits beta-glucan synthesis and causes flushing from histamine release"},{"id":"E","text":"It disrupts microtubule function and is teratogenic and carcinogenic"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Azoles inhibit fungal sterol synthesis by inhibiting the cytochrome P-450 enzyme that converts lanosterol to ergosterol; voriconazole is used for Aspergillus and some Candida, while isavuconazole covers serious Aspergillus and Mucor. Azole class effects include liver dysfunction from cytochrome P-450 inhibition, QT prolongation, and inhibition of testosterone synthesis with gynecomastia, especially with ketoconazole. Fluconazole is used for chronic suppression of cryptococcal meningitis in HIV and for candidal infections of all types.',
    '{"A":"Squalene epoxidase inhibition with keratin deposition is terbinafine and griseofulvin territory, not the azole mechanism.","C":"Direct ergosterol binding with pore formation is amphotericin B and nystatin.","D":"Beta-glucan synthesis inhibition with histamine-mediated flushing describes echinocandins such as caspofungin.","E":"Microtubule disruption with teratogenicity and carcinogenicity describes griseofulvin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antifungal drugs',
    'A 66-year-old man with a prolonged intensive care stay and candidemia is started on caspofungin. During the infusion he develops flushing of the face and trunk. He has no rash or airway compromise, and tryptase is not elevated. His blood cultures clear over the next week.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism and this adverse effect?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Echinocandins bind ergosterol and cause flushing from cytokine release with nephrotoxicity"},{"id":"B","text":"Beta-glucan synthesis inhibition with flushing"},{"id":"C","text":"Echinocandins are nucleoside analogs that inhibit DNA synthesis and cause myelosuppression"},{"id":"D","text":"Echinocandins disrupt microtubules and cause flushing from autonomic instability"},{"id":"E","text":"Echinocandins inhibit squalene epoxidase and cause flushing from a disulfiram-like reaction"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Echinocandins such as anidulafungin, caspofungin, and micafungin inhibit cell wall synthesis by inhibiting beta-glucan synthesis, which makes them valuable for invasive aspergillosis and Candida infections. Their adverse effects are GI upset and flushing caused by histamine release. This is a nonallergic infusion-related effect rather than an immunoglobulin-mediated reaction.',
    '{"A":"Ergosterol binding describes amphotericin B, whose membrane pore formation causes nephrotoxicity, not the echinocandin cell wall mechanism.","C":"Nucleoside analog DNA synthesis inhibition describes antiviral drugs such as acyclovir, not echinocandins.","D":"Microtubule disruption describes griseofulvin, which is used orally for dermatophytes.","E":"Squalene epoxidase inhibition is terbinafine''s mechanism, and disulfiram-like reactions belong to metronidazole and griseofulvin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antimycobacterial drugs',
    'A 39-year-old man with a positive interferon-gamma release assay and a normal chest radiograph is diagnosed with latent tuberculosis infection. He is started on a rifamycin-based regimen. Three weeks later he notices that his urine and tears have turned orange-red, and he is alarmed. He also takes an oral contraceptive and has a new CD4 count of 250 cells/mm3 relevant to future therapy decisions.',
    'Which of the following best explains the orange-red discoloration, and what drug interaction should be anticipated?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Orange body fluids with CYP450 induction"},{"id":"B","text":"Rifampin causes orange discoloration but has no effect on hepatic metabolism"},{"id":"C","text":"Ethambutol causes red-orange discoloration along with optic neuropathy and is a strong CYP450 inducer"},{"id":"D","text":"Isoniazid causes red-orange body fluid discoloration by inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"Pyrazinamide causes orange discoloration through hyperuricemia and hepatotoxicity"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Rifampin inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase to reduce mRNA synthesis, and it produces a nonhazardous red-orange discoloration of body fluids along with minor hepatotoxicity and significant drug interactions through CYP450 induction. That induction reduces the effectiveness of oral contraceptives and other hepatically metabolized drugs, so counseling must include a backup contraceptive method. In patients with HIV, rifabutin is favored over rifampin because it induces CYP450 less.',
    '{"B":"Rifampin is a potent CYP450 inducer, and this induction is precisely why interactions with contraceptives and antiretrovirals occur.","C":"Ethambutol causes optic neuropathy, not orange body fluids, and it is not a CYP450 inducer.","D":"Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis and causes peripheral neuropathy and hepatotoxicity; it does not discolor body fluids orange-red.","E":"Pyrazinamide causes hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia but is not responsible for red-orange discoloration."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antimycobacterial drugs',
    'A 47-year-old man from Guatemala with a history of alcohol use disorder is started on isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for active pulmonary tuberculosis. Eight weeks later he presents with painful tingling and numbness of the feet in a stocking distribution. His aspartate aminotransferase has risen to three times normal. He also had a seizure of unclear cause at home.',
    'Which of the following best explains the neuropathy and hepatotoxicity?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethambutol causes vitamin B6 deficiency, peripheral neuropathy, and hepatotoxicity"},{"id":"B","text":"Mycolic acid inhibition causing B6 deficiency and hepatotoxicity"},{"id":"C","text":"Pyrazinamide causes vitamin B6 deficiency and peripheral neuropathy through inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis"},{"id":"D","text":"Isoniazid inhibits arabinosyltransferase and causes optic neuropathy with red-green color blindness"},{"id":"E","text":"Isoniazid inhibits RNA polymerase and causes red-orange body fluids with CYP450 induction"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis and requires bacterial catalase-peroxidase encoded by katG to become active, which is also where INH resistance arises. Its adverse effects are vitamin B6 deficiency with peripheral neuropathy and sideroblastic anemia, hepatotoxicity that is worse with alcohol and increasing age, CYP450 inhibition, and drug-induced lupus, and it is administered with pyridoxine. INH overwdoes can cause seizures that are often refractory to benzodiazepines, and the mnemonic is that INH Injures Neurons and Hepatocytes.',
    '{"A":"Ethambutol does not deplete vitamin B6; its characteristic toxicity is optic neuropathy.","C":"Pyrazinamide''s mechanism is uncertain and its toxicities are hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia, not B6 deficiency or mycolic acid synthesis inhibition.","D":"Arabinosyltransferase inhibition with optic neuropathy is ethambutol''s mechanism and toxicity, which produces red-green color blindness rather than a stocking-distribution neuropathy.","E":"RNA polymerase inhibition with orange body fluids and CYP450 induction is rifampin''s profile, the reverse of isoniazid''s CYP450 inhibition."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antimycobacterial drugs',
    'A 62-year-old man receiving rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for tuberculosis reports that colors look washed out and he has trouble distinguishing red from green while reading. His visual acuity has also decreased in both eyes. He otherwise feels well, and his liver enzymes are near baseline.',
    'Which of the following best explains these visual symptoms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rifampin causes red-green color blindness by inhibiting RNA polymerase in retinal cells"},{"id":"B","text":"Pyrazinamide causes optic neuropathy by raising uric acid and precipitating crystals in the eye"},{"id":"C","text":"Chloramphenicol causes optic neuritis by blocking the 50S ribosomal subunit"},{"id":"D","text":"Arabinosyltransferase inhibition with optic neuropathy"},{"id":"E","text":"Isoniazid causes optic neuritis by inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis in the optic nerve"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Ethambutol inhibits arabinosyltransferase, reducing arabinogalactan synthesis and therefore cell wall formation, and its characteristic toxicity is dose-related optic neuropathy with red-green color blindness or decreased visual acuity that is typically reversible. The First Aid mnemonic to remember the drug is to pronounce it "eyethambutol." Pyrazinamide and the other first-line agents contribute other toxicities instead.',
    '{"A":"Rifampin inhibits RNA polymerase and produces orange body fluids and CYP450 induction, not color vision loss.","B":"Pyrazinamide causes hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia; it is not associated with crystal-induced optic damage.","C":"Chloramphenicol does block the 50S peptidyltransferase center and causes aplastic anemia, but it is not part of this regimen and optic neuritis is not its classic toxicity.","E":"Isoniazid does inhibit mycolic acid synthesis, but its toxicity is peripheral neuropathy and hepatotoxicity, not optic neuritis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antimycobacterial drugs',
    'A 34-year-old man with HIV and a CD4+ count of 40 cells/mm3 presents with fever, night sweats, weight loss, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Blood and bone marrow cultures grow Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare, and the infectious disease consultant recommends a macrolide-based regimen rather than standard tuberculosis therapy.',
    'Which of the following is the appropriate treatment regimen for this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethambutol and streptomycin only"},{"id":"B","text":"Rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 6 months"},{"id":"C","text":"Macrolide plus ethambutol"},{"id":"D","text":"Dapsone and rifampin for 12 months, adding clofazimine"},{"id":"E","text":"Isoniazid and pyridoxine alone as monotherapy"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'For Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare, treatment is azithromycin or clarithromycin plus ethambutol, and rifabutin or ciprofloxacin may be added; prophylaxis in at-risk patients is azithromycin or rifabutin. This contrasts with tuberculosis, which is treated with the rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol combination. The patient''s low CD4 count with disseminated disease is the classic presentation of MAC in advanced HIV.',
    '{"A":"Ethambutol with streptomycin omits the required macrolide backbone for MAC and pairs an aminoglycoside that is not the primary MAC agent.","B":"RIPE therapy is the regimen for Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which is not the organism cultured here and is typically susceptible to isoniazid and pyrazinamide that MAC does not respond to.","D":"Dapsone plus rifampin with clofazimine for the lepromatous form is the regimen for Mycobacterium leprae, not MAC.","E":"Isoniazid monotherapy is never adequate because rifamycin monotherapy or inadequate regimens rapidly select resistance; MAC requires a macrolide plus ethambutol."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antimycobacterial drugs',
    'A 30-year-old man with smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis is started on rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol. After 6 weeks his sputum remains positive, and susceptibility testing shows resistance to isoniazid. The laboratory reports this resistance arises from a mutation in a specific gene. A colleague asks about pyrazinamide''s role, since its mechanism is uncertain.',
    'Which of the following statements about first-line antimycobacterial mechanisms and resistance is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Isoniazid resistance arises from a ribosomal RNA point mutation, and pyrazinamide is a prodrug activated by cytosine deaminase"},{"id":"B","text":"Isoniazid resistance arises from a mutation in the gene encoding arabinosyltransferase, and pyrazinamide inhibits mycolic acid synthesis"},{"id":"C","text":"Isoniazid resistance arises from mutations in RNA polymerase, and pyrazinamide works best at alkaline pH"},{"id":"D","text":"Isoniazid resistance arises from katG mutations, and pyrazinamide works best at the acidic pH found in host phagolysosomes"},{"id":"E","text":"Rifamycin resistance arises from katG mutations, and pyrazinamide inhibits arabinogalactan synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Isoniazid is converted to its active form by bacterial catalase-peroxidase encoded by katG, so INH resistance arises from mutations in katG. Pyrazinamide''s mechanism is uncertain, but it works best at the acidic pH found in host phagolysosomes, which is where intracellular mycobacteria reside. Rifamycin resistance instead arises from mutations in the gene encoding RNA polymerase.',
    '{"A":"Ribosomal RNA point mutation is the linezolid resistance mechanism, and activation by cytosine deaminase describes flucytosine, an antifungal.","B":"Arabinosyltransferase is the target of ethambutol, and mycolic acid synthesis is inhibited by isoniazid, so this pairing of resistance mechanism and target is incorrect.","C":"RNA polymerase mutations cause rifamycin resistance, not isoniazid resistance, and pyrazinamide''s activity depends on acidic rather than alkaline pH.","E":"katG mutations confer isoniazid resistance, and arabinogalactan synthesis is inhibited by ethambutol, not pyrazinamide."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiprotozoal and anthelmintic drugs',
    'A 29-year-old woman returns from a 3-week trip to sub-Saharan Africa with cyclical fevers, chills, and sweats. Blood smear shows ring forms and banana-shaped gametocytes diagnostic of Plasmodium falciparum. She is treated with artemisinin-based combination therapy. She asks why chloroquine is not used for her infection.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of chloroquine and the reason for its failure here?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Chloroquine forms free radical metabolites that damage parasite DNA, and resistance arises from efflux pumps"},{"id":"B","text":"Blocks hemozoin formation; a membrane pump lowers drug levels"},{"id":"C","text":"Chloroquine blocks heme polymerization, and P falciparum is intrinsically unresponsive because it lacks a food vacuole"},{"id":"D","text":"Chloroquine inhibits the mitochondrial electron transport chain, and resistance is due to a mutated cytochrome"},{"id":"E","text":"Chloroquine inhibits dihydrofolate reductase in plasmodia, and resistance arises from a mutated enzyme"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Chloroquine blocks detoxification of heme into hemozoin, so toxic heme accumulates within the parasite; it is used for plasmodial species other than P falciparum. P falciparum resistance results from a membrane pump that reduces the intracellular concentration of the drug, which is why combination therapy is needed for falciparum malaria. Chloroquine also causes retinopathy that depends on cumulative dose and pruritus, especially in dark-skinned individuals.',
    '{"A":"Free radical metabolite formation that damages DNA is metronidazole''s mechanism, not chloroquine''s.","C":"P falciparum does have a food vacuole; its resistance is acquired through a membrane pump rather than intrinsic absence of the target organelle.","D":"Chloroquine does not act on the electron transport chain, and its target is heme detoxification within the food vacuole.","E":"Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition is the mechanism of trimethoprim and pyrimethamine, not chloroquine, which acts on heme detoxification."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiprotozoal and anthelmintic drugs',
    'A 34-year-old woman with HIV and a CD4+ count of 90 cells/mm3 presents with headache, fever, and a ring-enhancing brain lesion with surrounding edema. Serology is positive for Toxoplasma IgG. She is started on pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine with leucovorin, and the team plans secondary prophylaxis with TMP-SMX.',
    'Which of the following best explains the choice of pyrimethamine with sulfadiazine?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Sequential folate blockade with leucovorin rescue"},{"id":"B","text":"Pyrimethamine inhibits squalene epoxidase and sulfadiazine binds ergosterol, giving synergy"},{"id":"C","text":"Pyrimethamine blocks heme detoxification and sulfadiazine inhibits folate synthesis, with leucovorin to protect the parasite"},{"id":"D","text":"Both agents inhibit the 50S ribosomal subunit of Toxoplasma at sequential sites"},{"id":"E","text":"Pyrimethamine forms free radicals and sulfadiazine inhibits DNA gyrase, giving synergy"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Pyrimethamine inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and sulfadiazine inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, producing a sequential block of the folate pathway analogous to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole; leucovorin supplies host folate to limit marrow toxicity while the parasite, which cannot use preformed folate, remains blocked. Reactivation of latent Toxoplasma is a classic cause of ring-enhancing brain lesions in AIDS. TMP-SMX at CD4+ counts below 100 provides dual prophylaxis against Pneumocystis and toxoplasmosis.',
    '{"B":"Squalene epoxidase inhibition and ergosterol binding are antifungal mechanisms (terbinafine and amphotericin B), not antiprotozoal ones.","C":"Blocking heme detoxification is chloroquine''s antimalarial mechanism, and leucovorin protects the host rather than the parasite.","D":"Neither drug targets the ribosome; they act in the folate synthesis and reduction pathway.","E":"Free radical formation is metronidazole''s mechanism, and DNA gyrase inhibition belongs to fluoroquinolones."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiprotozoal and anthelmintic drugs',
    'A 41-year-old man presents with intense nocturnal perianal itching. Cellophane tape preparation from the perianal skin shows oval eggs. He also reports that his toddler and spouse are similarly affected. The physician prescribes a single-dose agent that paralyzes the worm, and instructs the whole household to take it.',
    'Which of the following drugs is most appropriate for this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"B","text":"Metronidazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Chloroquine"},{"id":"D","text":"Permethrin"},{"id":"E","text":"Pyrantel pamoate"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is enterobiasis, the classic cause of nocturnal perianal pruritus with eggs recovered by cellophane tape, and pyrantel pamoate is one of the First Aid antihelminthic agents used for such intestinal nematode infections, given to the whole household because of easy person-to-person spread. Antihelminthic therapy in First Aid also includes albendazole/mebendazole, which treat "bendy worms" by disrupting microtubules and cellular motility, ivermectin, praziquantel, and diethylcarbamazine.',
    '{"A":"Praziquantel increases calcium permeability and vacuolization and is used for flukes and cestodes such as schistosomes and Taenia, not for pinworm.","B":"Metronidazole treats Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas, bacterial vaginosis, and anaerobes; it has no anthelmintic activity.","C":"Chloroquine is an antimalarial that blocks heme detoxification and is not used for intestinal nematodes.","D":"Permethrin is an anti-mite and anti-louse topical therapy for scabies and lice, not an intestinal anthelmintic."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiprotozoal and anthelmintic drugs',
    'A 33-year-old man with HIV has a pruritic rash with burrows in the web spaces of his fingers and itchy papules on the penis, and his roommate has the same rash. His clinician prescribes a topical agent, noting that oral ivermectin is an alternative. The clinician also explains that the same topical class is used for lice.',
    'Which of the following best describes the anti-mite and anti-louse agents in First Aid?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Metronidazole and tinidazole are the primary topical agents for scabies and lice"},{"id":"B","text":"Permethrin and malathion are antifolate agents used for scabies, with ivermectin acting on the 30S ribosome"},{"id":"C","text":"Permethrin, malathion, and ivermectin"},{"id":"D","text":"Griseofulvin and terbinafine treat scabies by inhibiting squalene epoxidase"},{"id":"E","text":"Ivermectin increases calcium permeability and causes vacuolization of the parasite"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'First Aid lists permethrin, malathion, and topical or oral ivermectin as the anti-mite and anti-louse therapies used for scabies from Sarcoptes scabiei and for lice from Pediculus and Pthirus species. Malathion acts as an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. Oral ivermectin is a practical alternative for scabies, particularly when topical application is difficult or when many contacts require treatment.',
    '{"A":"Nitroimidazoles such as metronidazole are for anaerobic bacteria and protozoa such as Trichomonas and Giardia, not scabies or lice.","B":"Permethrin and malathion are not antifolate agents, and ivermectin does not act on the bacterial 30S ribosome.","D":"Griseofulvin and terbinafine are antifungals used for dermatophytoses such as tinea and onychomycosis, not for mites.","E":"Increased calcium permeability with vacuolization describes praziquantel, which is used for flukes and cestodes."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 32-year-old man is newly diagnosed with HIV. His CD4+ count is 220 cells/mm3 and his viral load is 95,000 copies per milliliter. Before starting a three-drug regimen of two NRTIs plus an integrase inhibitor, genotyping identifies the HLA-B*5701 allele, and the pharmacist adjusts the regimen accordingly.',
    'Which drug is contraindicated in this patient, and why?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Abacavir, because HLA-B*5701 predicts hypersensitivity"},{"id":"B","text":"Efavirenz, because HLA-B*5701 predicts a high risk of vivid dreams and CNS symptoms"},{"id":"C","text":"Dolutegravir, because HLA-B*5701 predicts creatine kinase elevation"},{"id":"D","text":"Zidovudine, because HLA-B*5701 predicts dose-dependent hepatotoxicity"},{"id":"E","text":"Tenofovir, because it causes nephrotoxicity in patients with the HLA-B*5701 allele"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Abacavir is contraindicated in patients carrying the HLA-B*5701 allele because of an increased risk of hypersensitivity reaction; screening for this allele before initiating therapy is why genotype information is used here. NRTIs as a class competitively inhibit nucleotide binding to reverse transcriptase and terminate the DNA chain because they lack a 3''-OH group, and they must be phosphorylated to be active. All NRTIs can cause myelosuppression, which is reversible with G-CSF and erythropoietin, and nephrotoxicity.',
    '{"B":"Efavirenz causes vivid dreams and CNS symptoms as an NNRTI class-typical effect, but this is unrelated to HLA-B*5701.","C":"Integrase inhibitors such as dolutegravir increase creatine kinase and weight gain, and HLA-B*5701 does not predict this effect.","D":"Zidovudine is used for general prophylaxis and during pregnancy to reduce fetal transmission, and HLA-B*5701 is not associated with its dose-dependent hematologic toxicity.","E":"Tenofovir does cause nephrotoxicity as a class effect of NRTIs, but HLA-B*5701 is not its predictive marker; the allele specifically predicts abacavir hypersensitivity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 39-year-old woman with HIV is started on efavirenz plus two NRTIs. Within 2 weeks she reports intense nightmares, insomnia, and a new diffuse rash, and her liver enzymes are mildly elevated. Her regimen also includes a protease inhibitor booster. The team counseled her in advance about potential CNS effects.',
    'Which of the following best describes the NNRTI class and efavirenz''s specific adverse effects?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"NNRTIs bind gp41 and inhibit viral fusion at the cell membrane"},{"id":"B","text":"NNRTIs inhibit HIV integrase and raise creatine kinase with weight gain"},{"id":"C","text":"NNRTIs are chain terminators that incorporate into viral DNA and require phosphorylation to be active"},{"id":"D","text":"NNRTIs inhibit HIV protease and cause hyperglycemia and GI intolerance"},{"id":"E","text":"Direct reverse transcriptase binding with vivid dreams"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'NNRTIs such as doravirine, efavirenz, and rilpivirine bind reverse transcriptase at a site different from the NRTI site; they do not require phosphorylation to be active and do not compete with nucleotides. Rash and hepatotoxicity are common to all NNRTIs, and vivid dreams and CNS symptoms are characteristic of efavirenz. NNRTIs are also among the antiretrovirals that are not effective against HIV-2.',
    '{"A":"Binding gp41 to block fusion describes the entry inhibitor enfuvirtide, which causes injection-site skin reactions.","B":"Integrase inhibition with increased creatine kinase and weight gain defines the integrase strand transfer inhibitors such as dolutegravir and bictegravir.","C":"Chain termination with required phosphorylation describes NRTIs, which are incorporated into DNA and lack a 3''-OH group; NNRTIs are non-nucleoside binders instead.","D":"Inhibiting HIV protease with hyperglycemia and GI intolerance describes protease inhibitors such as atazanavir and ritonavir."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 45-year-old man with HIV on darunavir plus ritonavir and two NRTIs develops fatigue, polydipsia, and polyuria. His fasting glucose is 210 mg/dL. He also reports nausea and diarrhea. The pharmacist reviews the regimen, noting that all protease inhibitors require boosting and that ritonavir''s main role is inhibition of cytochrome P-450.',
    'Which of the following best describes the protease inhibitor class?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Protease blockade prevents virion maturation"},{"id":"B","text":"They are nucleoside analogs that cause myelosuppression and nephrotoxicity"},{"id":"C","text":"They terminate DNA chain elongation and require phosphorylation for activity"},{"id":"D","text":"They bind CCR-5 on T cells and monocytes to prevent gp120 interaction"},{"id":"E","text":"They inhibit integrase and increase creatine kinase levels"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Protease inhibitors prevent maturation of new virions because maturation depends on HIV-1 protease, the pol gene product that cleaves polypeptide products of HIV mRNA into functional parts; the mnemonic is that navir (never) tease a protease. All protease inhibitors require boosting with either ritonavir or cobicistat, and ritonavir itself is used only as a boosting agent because it inhibits cytochrome P-450. Their adverse effects are hyperglycemia and GI intolerance with nausea and diarrhea.',
    '{"B":"Nucleoside analogs with myelosuppression and nephrotoxicity are the NRTIs, which interfere with reverse transcription rather than virion maturation.","C":"Chain termination after phosphorylation is the NRTI mechanism, not the protease inhibitor mechanism.","D":"CCR-5 binding to block gp120 interaction is maraviroc''s entry inhibition mechanism.","E":"Integrase inhibition with creatine kinase elevation is the integrase strand transfer inhibitor profile."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 28-year-old man with newly diagnosed HIV is about to start antiretroviral therapy. His regimen will include zidovudine, and his baseline labs show mild anemia. Six weeks later his hemoglobin has dropped and he has neutropenia. The team also identifies a patient starting tenofovir with a rising creatinine, and a pregnant patient receiving zidovudine for prevention of transmission.',
    'Which of the following best describes the NRTI class and its adverse effects?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They are protease inhibitors that cause hyperglycemia and GI intolerance"},{"id":"B","text":"They are integrase inhibitors that increase creatine kinase and weight"},{"id":"C","text":"They are entry inhibitors that cause injection-site reactions and bind CCR-5"},{"id":"D","text":"Chain termination with myelosuppression and nephrotoxicity"},{"id":"E","text":"They bind reverse transcriptase directly without phosphorylation and cause rash with vivid dreams"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'NRTIs competitively inhibit nucleotide binding to reverse transcriptase and terminate the DNA chain because they lack a 3''-OH group; tenofovir is a nucleoTide while the others are nucleosides, and all must be phosphorylated to be active. They cause myelosuppression, reversible with G-CSF and erythropoietin, and nephrotoxicity. Zidovudine can be used for general prophylaxis and during pregnancy to reduce the risk of fetal transmission, and tenofovir with emtricitabine can be used as pre-exposure prophylaxis.',
    '{"A":"Protease inhibition with hyperglycemia and GI intolerance is the protease inhibitor profile.","B":"Integrase inhibition with creatine kinase elevation and weight gain describes integrase strand transfer inhibitors.","C":"Entry inhibitors such as enfuvirtide and maraviroc act at fusion and docking, not at reverse transcription.","E":"Direct binding without phosphorylation and vivid dreams describe NNRTIs such as efavirenz, which also cause rash and hepatotoxicity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 36-year-old woman with HIV and a history of suboptimal adherence is failing therapy and now has a high viral load. Her new regimen includes an integrase inhibitor, and later a CCR-5 antagonist is considered because her virus is CCR-5 tropic. A colleague asks whether all antiretrovirals are effective against both HIV-1 and HIV-2, and how entry inhibitors differ.',
    'Which of the following statements about antiretroviral coverage and entry inhibitors is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"All antiretrovirals are equally effective against HIV-1 and HIV-2, and enfuvirtide binds CCR-5"},{"id":"B","text":"NNRTIs and enfuvirtide miss HIV-2; enfuvirtide binds gp41"},{"id":"C","text":"Integrase inhibitors are the only class ineffective against HIV-2, and maraviroc binds gp41"},{"id":"D","text":"Protease inhibitors do not require boosting, and enfuvirtide inhibits HIV protease"},{"id":"E","text":"NRTIs are ineffective against HIV-2, and maraviroc inhibits viral integrase"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Most antiretrovirals are active against both HIV-1 and HIV-2, with the exceptions being NNRTIs and enfuvirtide, which are not effective against HIV-2. Enfuvirtide binds gp41 to inhibit viral fusion, producing injection-site skin reactions, and maraviroc binds CCR-5 on the surface of T cells and monocytes to inhibit interaction with gp120, acting as a docking inhibitor. A standard regimen uses three drugs, typically two NRTIs plus preferably an integrase inhibitor, to prevent resistance.',
    '{"A":"The classes are not all equally effective: NNRTIs and enfuvirtide fail against HIV-2, and enfuvirtide binds gp41 rather than CCR-5.","C":"Integrase inhibitors are effective against HIV-2; the ineffective agents are NNRTIs and enfuvirtide, and maraviroc binds CCR-5 rather than gp41.","D":"All protease inhibitors require boosting with ritonavir or cobicistat, and enfuvirtide is a fusion inhibitor rather than a protease inhibitor.","E":"NRTIs remain active against HIV-2, and maraviroc targets the CCR-5 co-receptor rather than integrase."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiretroviral drugs and adverse effects',
    'A 41-year-old man with HIV and pulmonary tuberculosis needs simultaneous treatment of both diseases. His HIV regimen includes a protease inhibitor, and the tuberculosis regimen includes rifampin. The pharmacist warns that rifampin is a potent inducer of CYP and UGT enzymes and would lower the protease inhibitor concentration, and recommends substituting a different rifamycin.',
    'Which of the following adjustments is most appropriate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Add cobicistat to overcome rifampin induction of the protease inhibitor"},{"id":"B","text":"Stop the protease inhibitor permanently and use integrase inhibitor monotherapy"},{"id":"C","text":"Increase the protease inhibitor dose and continue rifampin, since rifampin has no interaction with antiretrovirals"},{"id":"D","text":"Use rifabutin instead of rifampin because it induces CYP450 less than rifampin"},{"id":"E","text":"Switch the rifampin to isoniazid monotherapy to avoid drug interactions"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Rifampin is a potent inducer of CYP and UGT enzymes and reduces protease inhibitor concentrations, so rifabutin, which induces CYP450 less, is favored over rifampin in patients with HIV infection. Rifamycins all inhibit DNA-dependent RNA polymerase to reduce mRNA synthesis, so rifabutin retains antimycobacterial activity. Rifamycin monotherapy rapidly leads to resistance, so a full combination regimen must be maintained.',
    '{"A":"Adding cobicistat does not overcome potent CYP450 induction by rifampin, which is why substituting rifabutin is preferred.","B":"Integrase inhibitor monotherapy is inadequate because antiretroviral therapy requires at least three drugs to prevent resistance, and stopping effective therapy risks HIV progression.","C":"Rifampin does have a major interaction with protease inhibitors through CYP450 induction, and dose escalation does not reliably overcome it.","E":"Isoniazid monotherapy for tuberculosis is unacceptable because monotherapy rapidly selects resistance; combination therapy is required."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiviral drugs: acyclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet',
    'A 44-year-old man with a kidney transplant develops fever, worsening vision, and floaters 4 months after transplant. Fundoscopy shows a necrotizing retinitis with a classic brushfire border, and vitreous PCR is positive for cytomegalovirus. The team chooses oral valganciclovir, noting the need to monitor blood counts.',
    'Which of the following best explains ganciclovir''s activation and toxicity relative to acyclovir?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It inhibits influenza neuraminidase and prevents release of progeny virus"},{"id":"B","text":"It is a pyrophosphate analog that binds the pyrophosphate-binding site of viral polymerase"},{"id":"C","text":"It is monophosphorylated by cellular kinases only, which makes it less toxic than acyclovir and free of marrow effects"},{"id":"D","text":"It requires no kinase activation, which is why it is effective against acyclovir-resistant HSV"},{"id":"E","text":"CMV kinase activation with myelosuppression"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Ganciclovir is a guanosine analog whose 5''-monophosphate is formed by a CMV viral kinase, with the triphosphate then formed by cellular kinases to preferentially inhibit viral DNA polymerase; valganciclovir is a prodrug with better oral bioavailability. Its adverse effects are myelosuppression with leukopenia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia, plus renal toxicity, making it more toxic to host enzymes than acyclovir. Mutated viral kinase underlies ganciclovir resistance.',
    '{"A":"Neuraminidase inhibition describes oseltamivir and zanamivir, which act against influenza rather than CMV.","B":"The pyrophosphate analog that binds the pyrophosphate site is foscarnet, not ganciclovir.","C":"Activation requires the CMV viral kinase for the first phosphorylation step, and ganciclovir is more toxic, with significant myelosuppression.","D":"Requiring no kinase activation describes foscarnet and cidofovir, which are used for acyclovir-resistant HSV and CMV retinitis when ganciclovir fails."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiviral drugs: acyclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet',
    'A 47-year-old woman with AIDS has CMV retinitis that progressed despite ganciclovir, and her virus is also resistant to acyclovir with a documented mutation in viral thymidine kinase. She has normal renal function at baseline. She receives foscarnet and is monitored for kidney injury and electrolyte disturbance, and on day 6 she has a seizure with hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia.',
    'Which of the following best describes foscarnet''s mechanism and toxicity?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It inhibits neuraminidase and causes neuropsychiatric events"},{"id":"B","text":"Pyrophosphate analog needing no kinase, causing nephrotoxicity"},{"id":"C","text":"It inhibits IMP dehydrogenase and causes hemolytic anemia"},{"id":"D","text":"It is a guanosine analog requiring viral thymidine kinase and causes crystalline nephropathy if not hydrated"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor requiring phosphorylation, and its main toxicity is myelosuppression"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analog that binds the pyrophosphate-binding site of viral DNA and RNA polymerases and HIV reverse transcriptase, and it does not require any kinase activation, which is why it remains active against thymidine kinase-deficient acyclovir-resistant HSV. It is used for CMV retinitis when ganciclovir fails and for acyclovir-resistant HSV. Its adverse effects are nephrotoxicity and multiple electrolyte abnormalities that can lead to seizures. Resistance maps to mutated DNA polymerase.',
    '{"A":"Neuraminidase inhibition describes oseltamivir and zanamivir for influenza.","C":"IMP dehydrogenase inhibition with hemolytic anemia describes ribavirin.","D":"Requiring viral thymidine kinase describes acyclovir, whose resistance arises from mutated thymidine kinase; foscarnet bypasses that step entirely.","E":"Foscarnet is not a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor and is not dependent on phosphorylation; myelosuppression is ganciclovir toxicity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiviral drugs: acyclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet',
    'A 33-year-old woman presents with a painful cluster of vesicles on the labia and bilateral tender inguinal lymphadenopathy. She is prescribed valacyclovir for 7 days, and she asks whether the medicine will prevent future outbreaks and whether she can take it with her usual fluid intake. Her creatinine is normal.',
    'Which of the following best explains the pharmacology of valacyclovir?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is a prodrug of ganciclovir requiring a CMV viral kinase for activation"},{"id":"B","text":"It is a pyrophosphate analog that does not require kinase activation"},{"id":"C","text":"It inhibits viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase and is used for influenza"},{"id":"D","text":"It inhibits the influenza cap-snatching endonuclease"},{"id":"E","text":"Acyclovir prodrug requiring viral thymidine kinase"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Valacyclovir is a prodrug of acyclovir with better oral bioavailability; acyclovir is a guanosine analog monophosphorylated by HSV/VZV thymidine kinase and therefore only activated in infected cells, after which the triphosphate preferentially inhibits viral DNA polymerase by chain termination. It is used for mucocutaneous and genital HSV lesions, HSV encephalitis, and prophylaxis in immunocompromised patients, but it has no effect on latent virus. Its adverse effect is obstructive crystalline nephropathy and acute kidney injury if patients are not adequately hydrated, which answers her question about fluid intake.',
    '{"A":"A prodrug of ganciclovir with CMV kinase dependence is valganciclovir, used for CMV.","B":"The pyrophosphate analog without kinase activation is foscarnet.","C":"Inhibition of viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase describes remdesivir, which is used for COVID-19 requiring hospitalization.","D":"Inhibiting cap snatching describes baloxavir, used for influenza within 48 hours of symptom onset."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Antiviral drugs: acyclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet',
    'A 29-year-old man with a history of recurrent genital herpes develops a severe, nonhealing perianal ulceration despite high-dose acyclovir. He is HIV positive with a CD4+ count of 120 cells/mm3. Viral sequencing shows a stop codon in the thymidine kinase gene. The team selects an agent that does not require thymidine kinase activation.',
    'Which of the following is the mechanism by which acyclovir fails in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Methylation of the viral 23S rRNA binding site prevents drug attachment"},{"id":"B","text":"Production of a plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase inactivates the drug"},{"id":"C","text":"Modification of D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac reduces drug binding"},{"id":"D","text":"Mutation of viral DNA polymerase prevents chain termination by the drug"},{"id":"E","text":"Thymidine kinase mutation blocks activation"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Acyclovir is monophosphorylated by HSV/VZV thymidine kinase, so a mutation in that viral enzyme prevents activation of guanosine analogs and produces acyclovir resistance. The alternative in this situation is foscarnet, which binds the pyrophosphate site of the viral polymerase without requiring kinase activation. Foscarnet resistance instead arises from mutated DNA polymerase.',
    '{"A":"23S rRNA methylation is a bacterial (macrolide) resistance mechanism and is irrelevant to a viral guanosine analog.","B":"Plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase inactivation applies to chloramphenicol, a bacterial drug.","C":"D-Ala-D-Lac modification of cell wall precursors is the enterococcal vancomycin resistance mechanism.","D":"Mutated DNA polymerase is the resistance mechanism for foscarnet and cidofovir, not the classic mechanism by which acyclovir fails; the sequencing here shows a thymidine kinase defect."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 27-year-old woman presents with fever, headache, neck stiffness, and photophobia. CSF Gram stain shows gram-positive diplococci, and CSF culture grows Streptococcus pneumoniae. She has no drug allergies, and the team plans empiric therapy with ceftriaxone plus vancomycin while awaiting susceptibilities.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of the beta-lactam class to which both drugs belong?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Binding D-Ala-D-Ala to prevent incorporation of cell wall precursors into the nascent peptidoglycan"},{"id":"B","text":"Transpeptidase blockade with activation of autolysins"},{"id":"C","text":"Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocking translocation during elongation"},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV to cause double-strand breaks"},{"id":"E","text":"Inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase to block bacterial folate synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Beta-lactams are D-Ala-D-Ala structural analogs that bind penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan, and they also activate autolytic enzymes, so the cell wall cannot be maintained and the bacterium lyses. Ceftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin that crosses the blood-brain barrier and is used for meningitis, and vancomycin is added for resistant pneumococci. Neither cephalosporins nor vancomycin is degraded by beta-lactamases.',
    '{"A":"Binding D-Ala-D-Ala describes vancomycin, a glycopeptide whose bulky structure prevents cross-linking, whereas beta-lactams are the structural analog that occupies the transpeptidase active site.","C":"Blocking translocation at the 50S subunit describes macrolides and clindamycin, which are protein synthesis inhibitors.","D":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV describes fluoroquinolones, which target DNA integrity.","E":"Dihydropteroate synthase inhibition describes sulfonamides, which interrupt folate synthesis rather than cell wall cross-linking."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 62-year-old man with a history of penicillin anaphylaxis requires elective dental extraction and has a prosthetic valve, so he needs infective endocarditis prophylaxis. His dentist wonders whether a first-generation cephalosporin would be safe. He later develops an infected prosthetic joint with a penicillinase-producing Staphylococcus aureus, and the team selects an antibiotic that is penicillinase resistant because of its bulky R group.',
    'Which of the following drugs is a penicillinase-resistant penicillin appropriate for this S aureus infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Penicillin G"},{"id":"B","text":"Nafcillin"},{"id":"C","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"id":"D","text":"Piperacillin"},{"id":"E","text":"Ampicillin"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Nafcillin, oxacillin, and dicloxacillin are penicillinase-resistant penicillins: their bulky R group blocks access of beta-lactamase to the beta-lactam ring, which is why they work against S aureus except MRSA. The First Aid mnemonic is "use naf (nafcillin) for staph." Their spectrum is narrow, and adverse effects include hypersensitivity and interstitial nephritis.',
    '{"A":"Penicillin G is the prototype beta-lactam and is beta-lactamase sensitive, so it fails against penicillinase-producing S aureus.","C":"Amoxicillin, like ampicillin, is penicillinase sensitive despite better oral bioavailability than ampicillin.","D":"Piperacillin is an antipseudomonal penicillin with an extended spectrum but is still penicillinase sensitive and requires a beta-lactamase inhibitor.","E":"Ampicillin is an aminopenicillin that is deliberately penicillinase sensitive and is used with clavulanate; it would be destroyed by the S aureus penicillinase."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 33-year-old man presents with a 1-week history of cough, fever, and right lower lobe consolidation. He is treated with ampicillin empirically. Two days later he develops watery diarrhea and cramping, and stool testing is positive for Clostridioides difficile toxin. His physician notes that the antibiotic was effective against his pneumonia but that the aminopenicillin spectrum explains both the benefit and the adverse effect.',
    'Which of the following sets of organisms is appropriate extended-spectrum coverage for ampicillin and amoxicillin?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Anaerobes below the diaphragm only, with no gram-negative activity"},{"id":"B","text":"H influenzae, H pylori, E coli, enterococci, Listeria, Proteus"},{"id":"C","text":"Gram-positive cocci only, with no gram-negative rods"},{"id":"D","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter, and multidrug-resistant S aureus"},{"id":"E","text":"MRSA, VRE, and atypical organisms such as Mycoplasma and Chlamydia"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Aminopenicillins have wider spectrum than penicillin and cover Haemophilus influenzae, H pylori, E coli, enterococci, Listeria monocytogenes, Proteus mirabilis, Salmonella, and Shigella; the First Aid mnemonic is HHEELPSS, with ampicillin and amoxicillin noted to kill enterococci. They remain penicillinase sensitive, which is why they are combined with clavulanic acid when beta-lactamase producers are possible. Adverse effects include hypersensitivity, rash, and pseudomembranous colitis, exactly what happened in this patient.',
    '{"A":"Restricting activity to anaerobic organisms below the diaphragm describes metronidazole''s clinical niche, not the broad aerobic gram-negative coverage of aminopenicillins.","C":"Plain penicillin G has a gram-positive predominance, but aminopenicillins specifically extend to gram-negative organisms including Salmonella, Shigella, and E coli.","D":"Coverage of Pseudomonas and multidrug-resistant S aureus requires antipseudomonal penicillins such as piperacillin with a beta-lactamase inhibitor, not plain ampicillin.","E":"MRSA and VRE require vancomycin, linezolid, or daptomycin; atypicals require macrolides or tetracyclines, and 1st-4th generation cephalosporins and aminopenicillins are all part of the LAME group that misses them."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 45-year-old woman has a severe hospital-acquired pneumonia with Pseudomonas aeruginosa susceptible only to a beta-lactam plus an aminoglycoside. The team selects piperacillin-tazobactam and gentamicin. During rounds the pharmacist asks which penicillins reliably cover Pseudomonas and why such a penicillin must always be paired with a beta-lactamase inhibitor.',
    'Which penicillin provides antipseudomonal coverage and therefore requires a beta-lactamase inhibitor for reliable use?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Piperacillin"},{"id":"B","text":"Cefazolin"},{"id":"C","text":"Dicloxacillin"},{"id":"D","text":"Penicillin V"},{"id":"E","text":"Aztreonam"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Piperacillin is the antipseudomonal penicillin: it is an extended-spectrum penicillin with activity against Pseudomonas, gram-negative rods, and anaerobes, and because it is penicillinase sensitive it is used with a beta-lactamase inhibitor such as tazobactam. Its main adverse effect is hypersensitivity. The pairing of piperacillin and tazobactam is one of the CAST inhibitor combinations.',
    '{"B":"Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin used for gram-positive cocci, PEcK organisms, and surgical prophylaxis of S aureus wound infection; it has no antipseudomonal activity.","C":"Dicloxacillin is a penicillinase-resistant penicillin for S aureus with a narrow spectrum and no gram-negative or antipseudomonal coverage.","D":"Penicillin V is a penicillinase-sensitive oral penicillin with mostly gram-positive coverage.","E":"Aztreonam is a monobactam, not a penicillin; it covers gram-negative rods including Pseudomonas but is classified separately and is not combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 29-year-old man has a purulent urethral discharge and is diagnosed with gonorrhea. He also has an uncomplicated outpatient infection for which his physician chooses a first-generation cephalosporin, cefazolin, before a knee arthroscopy to prevent an S aureus wound infection. A medical student asks how the generations differ in spectrum and what the LAME mnemonic means.',
    'Which of the following statements about cephalosporin coverage is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Fifth-generation ceftaroline covers MRSA and Enterococcus faecalis and also covers Pseudomonas"},{"id":"B","text":"Third-generation cephalosporins cannot cross the blood-brain barrier and are never used for meningitis"},{"id":"C","text":"All cephalosporin generations reliably cover enterococci and Listeria monocytogenes"},{"id":"D","text":"1st-4th generation cephalosporins miss LAME organisms"},{"id":"E","text":"First-generation cephalosporins cover Pseudomonas aeruginosa and anaerobes well"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The LAME mnemonic identifies the four categories missed by 1st through 4th generation cephalosporins: Listeria, atypicals such as Chlamydia and Mycoplasma, MRSA, and enterococci. Cephalosporins are beta-lactams that are less susceptible to penicillinases and are bactericidal. Their adverse effects include hypersensitivity, autoimmune hemolytic anemia, a disulfiram-like reaction, and vitamin K deficiency, with a low rate of cross-reactivity even in penicillin-allergic patients.',
    '{"A":"Ceftaroline does cover MRSA and E faecalis unlike earlier generations, but it specifically does not cover Pseudomonas.","B":"Third-generation cephalosporins can cross the blood-brain barrier, which is why ceftriaxone is used for meningitis.","C":"Enterococci and Listeria are precisely the organisms that 1st-4th generation cephalosporins fail to cover, which is why ampicillin is added when Listeria is suspected in meningitis.","E":"First-generation agents such as cefazolin cover gram-positive cocci and PEcK organisms, and Pseudomonas coverage requires cefepime, ceftazidime, or piperacillin; anaerobes are not covered."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics: penicillin and cephalosporins',
    'A 34-year-old woman with a penicillin allergy documented as anaphylaxis is diagnosed with gram-negative sepsis from a multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa urinary source. She also has chronic kidney disease, and the team wants to avoid aminoglycosides. The pharmacist recommends a monobactam, noting that it has no cross-allergenicity with penicillins and is less susceptible to beta-lactamases.',
    'Which of the following best describes the spectrum and mechanism of this recommended drug?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Binds PBP3 and covers gram-negative rods only"},{"id":"B","text":"Aztreonam is a fluoroquinolone that inhibits DNA gyrase in gram-negative organisms"},{"id":"C","text":"Aztreonam is a beta-lactamase inhibitor that has no intrinsic antibacterial activity"},{"id":"D","text":"Aztreonam is a glycopeptide that binds D-Ala-D-Ala and covers gram-positive organisms only"},{"id":"E","text":"Aztreonam is a carbapenem that covers gram-positive cocci, gram-negative rods, and anaerobes"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Aztreonam is a monobactam that prevents peptidoglycan cross-linking by binding penicillin-binding protein 3; it covers gram-negative rods only, with no activity against gram-positive rods or anaerobes, and it is synergistic with aminoglycosides. It has no cross-allergenicity with penicillins, making it valuable in penicillin-allergic patients and in those with renal insufficiency who cannot tolerate aminoglycosides. It is usually nontoxic, with occasional GI upset.',
    '{"B":"Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV; aztreonam is a cell wall synthesis inhibitor.","C":"Beta-lactamase inhibitors such as clavulanic acid and tazobactam are CAST agents that protect partner drugs; aztreonam has intrinsic gram-negative activity and binds PBP3.","D":"Binding D-Ala-D-Ala is the mechanism of vancomycin, a glycopeptide with gram-positive activity, not of the monobactam aztreonam.","E":"Carbapenems such as imipenem and meropenem are broad-spectrum beta-lactams covering gram-positive cocci, gram-negative rods, and anaerobes; aztreonam is a monobactam with much narrower gram-negative coverage."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactamase inhibitors and carbapenems',
    'A 70-year-old man in the intensive care unit has septic shock from a carbapenemase-producing Klebsiella pneumoniae. The microbiology laboratory reports that the isolate is resistant to imipenem and meropenem. The infectious disease consultant explains that carbapenemases hydrolyze the beta-lactam ring and that combination agents with novel inhibitors may be required.',
    'Which of the following mechanisms explains the resistance observed?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Methylation of 23S rRNA-binding site preventing drug attachment"},{"id":"B","text":"Modification of D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac in the cell wall precursor"},{"id":"C","text":"Point mutation in ribosomal RNA that prevents drug binding"},{"id":"D","text":"Carbapenemase hydrolysis of the beta-lactam ring"},{"id":"E","text":"Reduced uptake of drug due to loss of the outer membrane porin alone"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Carbapenem resistance in this setting is due to carbapenemases such as KPC produced by K pneumoniae, E coli, and Klebsiella aerogenes, which hydrolyze the beta-lactam ring. First Aid lists this as the resistance mechanism for the carbapenem class. Beta-lactamase inhibitors were developed specifically to protect beta-lactams from this kind of enzymatic destruction.',
    '{"A":"23S rRNA methylation confers macrolide resistance, not carbapenem resistance.","B":"Modification of D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac is the vancomycin resistance mechanism in enterococci, not a carbapenem resistance mechanism.","C":"Ribosomal RNA point mutation confers linezolid resistance, an entirely different drug class.","E":"Porin loss reduces drug entry but is not the mechanism emphasized for carbapenems; enzymatic hydrolysis by carbapenemases is the defining resistance mechanism for this class."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactamase inhibitors and carbapenems',
    'A 58-year-old woman requires treatment for a life-threatening mixed anaerobic and gram-negative intra-abdominal infection. She is given imipenem, and the pharmacist explains that it is always coadministered with cilastatin. Two days later she develops confusion and myoclonic jerks, and the team attributes this to drug accumulation.',
    'Why is imipenem always administered with cilastatin, and what is the reason for the neurologic symptoms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cilastatin blocks hepatic metabolism, and the seizures reflect hypoglycemia from hepatic failure"},{"id":"B","text":"Cilastatin prevents nephrotoxicity by chelating the drug in the bladder, and the seizures are from hypocalcemia"},{"id":"C","text":"Cilastatin blocks renal dehydropeptidase I; seizures occur at high levels"},{"id":"D","text":"Cilastatin is an aminoglycoside given for synergy, and the seizures are due to ototoxicity"},{"id":"E","text":"Cilastatin is a beta-lactamase inhibitor that extends imipenem''s spectrum to MRSA and VRE"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Imipenem is a broad-spectrum beta-lactamase-resistant carbapenem that binds penicillin-binding proteins, and it is always given with cilastatin, an inhibitor of renal dehydropeptidase I, to reduce inactivation of the drug in renal tubules: "the kill is lastin'' with cilastatin." Carbapenem adverse effects are GI distress, rash, and CNS toxicity including seizures at high plasma levels, which is why meropenem, with a lower seizure risk and stability to dehydropeptidase I, may be preferred.',
    '{"A":"Cilastatin acts in the kidney on dehydropeptidase I rather than blocking hepatic metabolism, and the neurologic findings reflect carbapenem CNS toxicity rather than hypoglycemia.","B":"Cilastatin is not a chelator, and carbapenem seizures are a direct CNS effect of high drug levels rather than a consequence of hypocalcemia.","D":"Cilastatin is a dehydropeptidase inhibitor, not an aminoglycoside, and aminoglycoside toxicity affects hearing and kidneys rather than causing seizures.","E":"Cilastatin is not a beta-lactamase inhibitor; AVIBACTAM, clavulanic acid, sulbactam, and tazobactam are the CAST inhibitors, and carbapenems do not cover MRSA or VRE."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Beta-lactamase inhibitors and carbapenems',
    'A 66-year-old man with a complicated diverticular abscess needs broad anaerobic and gram-negative coverage. The stewardship team recommends ertapenem instead of imipenem because of once-daily dosing and a specific spectrum limitation that is acceptable here. A medical student asks which beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations exist and what bacteria they target.',
    'Which of the following statements about beta-lactamase inhibitors and carbapenem coverage is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Tazobactam is a cephalosporin that replaces piperacillin in the combination"},{"id":"B","text":"CAST inhibitors protect partner drugs; ertapenem misses Pseudomonas"},{"id":"C","text":"Clavulanate is a carbapenem used alone for Pseudomonas and MRSA"},{"id":"D","text":"Ertapenem is the only carbapenem with reliable Pseudomonas coverage"},{"id":"E","text":"Beta-lactamase inhibitors have intrinsic bactericidal activity and are used as monotherapy"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Beta-lactamase inhibitors include clavulanic acid, avibactam, sulbactam, and tazobactam, remembered as CAST and exemplified by amoxicillin-clavulanate, ceftazidime-avibactam, ampicillin-sulbactam, and piperacillin-tazobactam; they have little intrinsic activity and exist to protect the partner penicillin from destruction. Carbapenems cover gram-positive cocci, gram-negative rods, and anaerobes, but unlike the others, ertapenem lacks Pseudomonas activity, which is why it was acceptable for this abscess.',
    '{"A":"Tazobactam is a beta-lactamase inhibitor, not a cephalosporin, and it is added to protect piperacillin rather than to replace it.","C":"Clavulanic acid is a beta-lactamase inhibitor, not a carbapenem, and it has no meaningful activity against Pseudomonas or MRSA on its own.","D":"It is the opposite: ertapenem is the carbapenem that does not cover Pseudomonas, whereas imipenem and meropenem do.","E":"Beta-lactamase inhibitors have negligible antibacterial activity by themselves and must be paired with a partner beta-lactam."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Fluoroquinolones',
    'A 68-year-old man with a Pseudomonas aeruginosa urinary tract infection is prescribed ciprofloxacin. He also takes prednisone for polymyalgia rheumatica and reports that he has been taking his calcium carbonate supplement and drinking milk with each dose. Three weeks later he presents with sudden posterior ankle pain and inability to bear weight, and ultrasound shows an Achilles tendon rupture.',
    'Which of the following best explains this adverse effect and the drugs involved?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"DNA gyrase inhibition with tendon rupture in older patients on steroids"},{"id":"B","text":"Fluoroquinolones form a disulfiram-like reaction that damages tendon collagen"},{"id":"C","text":"Fluoroquinolones are beta-lactams that activate autolytic enzymes in tenocytes"},{"id":"D","text":"Fluoroquinolones inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, depleting folate needed for tendon repair"},{"id":"E","text":"Fluoroquinolones inhibit the 50S ribosome and cause tendon rupture through impaired collagen synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Fluoroquinolones are bactericidal inhibitors of prokaryotic topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase) and topoisomerase IV, covering gram-negative rods of the urinary and GI tracts including Pseudomonas. They can cause tendinitis or tendon rupture, particularly in people older than 60 and in those taking prednisone, which is the mnemonic that fluoroquinolones hurt attachments to your bones. Concurrent divalent cations such as dairy and antacids also markedly decrease oral absorption, which may have reduced this patient''s drug levels.',
    '{"B":"A disulfiram-like reaction belongs to metronidazole and some cephalosporins, and it produces flushing and hypotension rather than tendon injury.","C":"Beta-lactams activate autolytic enzymes to lyse bacteria; fluoroquinolones do not act on the cell wall, and their toxicity is not mediated by autolysins.","D":"Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition is trimethoprim''s mechanism, which produces megaloblastic anemia rather than tendinopathy.","E":"Fluoroquinolones do not inhibit the 50S ribosome; they target bacterial topoisomerases involved in DNA integrity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Fluoroquinolones',
    'A 15-year-old competitive gymnast develops acute sinusitis and is prescribed a fluoroquinolone by an urgent care clinician. Her parents question the choice because she is a growing adolescent with a prior cartilage issue. The pharmacist recommends an alternative agent and explains the contraindications of the class.',
    'Which of the following best explains why fluoroquinolones should be avoided in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cartilage damage limits use under age 18 and in pregnancy"},{"id":"B","text":"They are contraindicated because they are teratogenic by inhibiting microtubule function"},{"id":"C","text":"They are contraindicated because they cause disulfiram-like reactions in adolescents"},{"id":"D","text":"They are contraindicated because they cause gray baby syndrome by failing to conjugate in the liver"},{"id":"E","text":"They are contraindicated because they cause kernicterus in adolescents with G6PD deficiency"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Fluoroquinolones are contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding and in children younger than 18 because of possible damage to cartilage, which is the reason this adolescent should receive a different agent. They may also prolong the QT interval and inhibit cytochrome P-450 (ciprofloxacin), and they can cause GI upset, superinfections, rashes, headache, and dizziness. Their bactericidal mechanism is inhibition of DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV.',
    '{"B":"Teratogenicity by disrupting microtubules describes griseofulvin, an antifungal, not fluoroquinolones.","C":"Disulfiram-like reactions are caused by metronidazole and some cephalosporins; the cartilage contraindication is the relevant restriction here.","D":"Gray baby syndrome comes from chloramphenicol in premature infants who lack UDP-glucuronosyltransferase.","E":"Kernicterus in infants is associated with sulfonamides, which displace bilirubin from albumin, not with fluoroquinolones."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Fluoroquinolones',
    'A 55-year-old man is treated with levofloxacin for community-acquired pneumonia. After 2 days he reports palpitations, and telemetry shows a prolonged QT interval. The pharmacist also notes that he has been taking aluminum hydroxide antacids frequently for dyspepsia, and his clinical response has been poor. The microbiology report shows a Pseudomonas isolate susceptible to ciprofloxacin only at high MIC.',
    'Which of the following statements about fluoroquinolone pharmacology and resistance is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Concurrent divalent cations enhance absorption; resistance results solely from ribosomal methylation"},{"id":"B","text":"Fluoroquinolones are inactivated by beta-lactamases, and resistance arises from carbapenemase production"},{"id":"C","text":"Cations reduce absorption; gyrase mutation and efflux cause resistance"},{"id":"D","text":"Fluoroquinolones require activation by viral thymidine kinase, and resistance arises from mutated thymidine kinase"},{"id":"E","text":"Fluoroquinolones are bacteriostatic, and resistance arises from plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Concurrent ingestion of divalent cations such as dairy and antacids markedly decreases oral fluoroquinolone absorption, which explains this patient''s poor response. Resistance can be chromosome-encoded mutation in DNA gyrase, plasmid-mediated, or through efflux pumps. The class is bactericidal and some agents prolong the QT interval; ciprofloxacin also inhibits cytochrome P-450.',
    '{"A":"Divalent cations chelate fluoroquinolones and reduce absorption, and ribosomal methylation is the macrolide resistance mechanism.","B":"Fluoroquinolones are not beta-lactams and are not hydrolyzed by beta-lactamases or carbapenemases.","D":"Activation by viral thymidine kinase pertains to acyclovir, an antiviral guanosine analog.","E":"They are bactericidal, and acetyltransferase-mediated inactivation is the chloramphenicol resistance mechanism."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Linezolid and quinupristin-dalfopristin',
    'A 59-year-old woman with a vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium bacteremia is started on linezolid. Two weeks later her platelet count falls from 240,000 to 62,000 per microliter of blood, and she reports numbness and tingling in both feet. She is also taking a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, and she develops agitation, diaphoresis, and hyperreflexia after a dose increase.',
    'Which of the following best explains these findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Linezolid inhibits cell wall cross-linking and causes red man syndrome with flushing"},{"id":"B","text":"50S 23S rRNA binding causes thrombocytopenia, neuropathy, serotonin excess"},{"id":"C","text":"Linezolid inhibits DNA gyrase and causes QT prolongation with tendon rupture"},{"id":"D","text":"Linezolid blocks peptidyltransferase at the 50S subunit and causes aplastic anemia with a disulfiram-like reaction"},{"id":"E","text":"Linezolid binds the 30S subunit and causes photosensitivity with teeth discoloration"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Linezolid inhibits protein synthesis by binding the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit and preventing formation of the initiation complex, and it covers gram-positive organisms including MRSA and VRE. Its characteristic adverse effects are myelosuppression, especially thrombocytopenia, peripheral neuropathy, and serotonin syndrome from partial monoamine oxidase inhibition, which explains the interaction with the SSRI. Its own resistance mechanism is a point mutation in ribosomal RNA.',
    '{"A":"Red man syndrome with flushing is the vancomycin infusion reaction, and linezolid does not act on the cell wall.","C":"QT prolongation with tendinopathy is the fluoroquinolone toxicity profile.","D":"Peptidyltransferase blockade at the 50S with aplastic anemia and disulfiram-like effect belongs to chloramphenicol and metronidazole, not linezolid.","E":"30S binding with photosensitivity and tooth discoloration describes tetracyclines."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Linezolid and quinupristin-dalfopristin',
    'A 47-year-old man with a methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium infection is treated with linezolid after failing vancomycin. Four weeks into therapy the culture still grows the organism, and sequencing shows a single nucleotide change in the ribosomal RNA gene. The team considers adding quinupristin-dalfopristin, a streptogramin that inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit.',
    'Which of the following statements about this drug class is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ribosomal RNA point mutation; streptogramins block the 50S subunit"},{"id":"B","text":"Linezolid resistance arises from plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase, and streptogramins are beta-lactams"},{"id":"C","text":"Linezolid is a bactericidal cell wall inhibitor, and streptogramins are guanosine analogs"},{"id":"D","text":"Linezolid resistance arises from methylation of the 23S rRNA-binding site, and streptogramins are aminoglycosides"},{"id":"E","text":"Linezolid and streptogramins both act on the 30S subunit and are ineffective against gram-positive organisms"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Linezolid binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and its resistance arises from a point mutation of ribosomal RNA, which differs from the methylation-based macrolide resistance mechanism. Quinupristin-dalfopristin is a streptogramin combination that inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit and is grouped in First Aid''s protein synthesis inhibitor diagram alongside linezolid. Both agents are used for resistant gram-positive infections.',
    '{"B":"Plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase is chloramphenicol resistance, and streptogramins are not beta-lactams but 50S protein synthesis inhibitors.","C":"Linezolid is bacteriostatic with variable activity and inhibits protein synthesis, not the cell wall; streptogramins are not nucleoside analogs.","D":"Methylation of 23S rRNA is macrolide resistance, and streptogramins are not aminoglycosides, which are 30S binders.","E":"Both drugs act at the 50S subunit and are specifically useful against resistant gram-positive organisms such as MRSA and VRE."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Linezolid and quinupristin-dalfopristin',
    'A 63-year-old woman with a resistant gram-positive infection has been on linezolid for 6 weeks. Her complete blood count shows a platelet count of 55,000 per microliter and a hemoglobin of 8.9 g/dL, and she reports fatigue and a symmetric burning sensation in her distal extremities. She takes no other myelosuppressive drugs and denies alcohol use.',
    'Which of the following sets of adverse effects is most consistent with prolonged linezolid therapy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Thrombocytopenia and peripheral neuropathy"},{"id":"B","text":"Tendon rupture and QT prolongation"},{"id":"C","text":"Aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome"},{"id":"D","text":"Optic neuritis and red-green color blindness"},{"id":"E","text":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Linezolid''s signature toxicities are myelosuppression, especially thrombocytopenia, peripheral neuropathy, and serotonin syndrome from partial MAO inhibition. Here the low platelet count and symmetric distal burning sensation fit the first two. These effects are why blood counts need monitoring during prolonged therapy for resistant gram-positive infection.',
    '{"B":"Tendinopathy with tendon rupture and QT prolongation are fluoroquinolone adverse effects.","C":"Aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome are chloramphenicol''s distinct toxicities, the latter due to immature hepatic glucuronidation.","D":"Optic neuropathy with red-green color blindness is ethambutol''s dose-related toxicity.","E":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity are the aminoglycoside toxicity pair, worsened by loop diuretics."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Macrolides and ketolides',
    'A 22-year-old college student has 2 weeks of persistent dry cough, sore throat, headache, and malaise. Chest radiograph shows bilateral patchy infiltrates that look worse than the patient feels. He is diagnosed with Mycoplasma pneumoniae pneumonia and prescribed azithromycin. He has a history of penicillin anaphylaxis.',
    'Which of the following best explains why a macrolide is appropriate here?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Macrolides cover Legionella only when combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor"},{"id":"B","text":"Macrolides cover MRSA and VRE, which is why they are chosen over penicillins"},{"id":"C","text":"Macrolides are bactericidal 30S inhibitors that cover Mycoplasma and Legionella but not Chlamydia"},{"id":"D","text":"Covers atypicals and streptococci in penicillin allergy"},{"id":"E","text":"Macrolides are folate antagonists that cover atypical organisms including Mycoplasma"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Macrolides inhibit protein synthesis by blocking translocation at the 50S subunit and are bacteriostatic. Their clinical uses include atypical pneumonias caused by Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, and Legionella, STIs caused by Chlamydia, gram-positive coccal infections such as streptococcal pharyngitis in patients allergic to penicillin, and Bordetella pertussis. This patient''s indolent atypical pneumonia with a normal-sounding examination is the classic macrolide indication.',
    '{"A":"Beta-lactamase inhibitors protect beta-lactams and are irrelevant to macrolide activity, which is not susceptible to beta-lactamases.","B":"MRSA and VRE are covered by linezolid, vancomycin, daptomycin, or ceftaroline; macrolides are part of the LAME organisms'' alternatives, not a resistant gram-positive solution.","C":"Macrolides do cover Chlamydia, and they are bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal; the 30S subunit belongs to aminoglycosides and tetracyclines.","E":"Folate antagonism describes sulfonamides and trimethoprim, which act on the folate pathway rather than the ribosome."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Macrolides and ketolides',
    'A 71-year-old man is treated with erythromycin for a respiratory infection. He takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation and theophylline for COPD. Three days later he develops palpitations and lightheadedness, and the ECG shows a markedly prolonged QT interval. His INR has also risen. He reports nausea, abdominal cramping, and a new rash with eosinophilia.',
    'Which of the following adverse effects of macrolides best explains this constellation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity from accumulation in cochlear and tubular cells"},{"id":"B","text":"Aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome from impaired glucuronidation"},{"id":"C","text":"Optic neuritis with red-green color blindness from arabinosyltransferase inhibition"},{"id":"D","text":"QT prolongation, GI motility, rash, and CYP450 inhibition"},{"id":"E","text":"Tendon rupture from inhibition of DNA gyrase in tenocytes"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Macrolide toxicity is remembered as MACRO: GI Motility issues, Arrhythmia from prolonged QT interval, acute Cholestatic hepatitis, Rash, and eOsinophilia. Clarithromycin and erythromycin inhibit cytochrome P-450, which increases serum concentrations of theophylline and oral anticoagulants, explaining the elevated INR and theophylline toxicity. This patient demonstrates several MACRO features at once.',
    '{"A":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity are aminoglycoside effects worsened by loop diuretics.","B":"Aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome are chloramphenicol toxicities related to hepatic glucuronidation, not to macrolides.","C":"Optic neuropathy with red-green color vision loss is ethambutol''s toxicity.","E":"Tendinopathy and tendon rupture belong to fluoroquinolones, which inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Macrolides and ketolides',
    'An 8-year-old child with a penicillin allergy develops a streptococcal pharyngitis and is prescribed azithromycin. After 5 days of therapy, the culture still grows Streptococcus pyogenes, and the laboratory reports that the isolate methylates the 23S rRNA binding site. The infectious disease physician switches therapy based on the resistance mechanism.',
    'Which of the following statements about macrolide resistance is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Modification of D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac reduces drug affinity"},{"id":"B","text":"Methylation of the 23S rRNA binding site"},{"id":"C","text":"Decreased drug uptake and increased efflux through plasmid-encoded transport pumps"},{"id":"D","text":"Plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase inactivates the drug by acetylation"},{"id":"E","text":"Mutation in the gene encoding RNA polymerase prevents drug binding"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Macrolide resistance most commonly arises from methylation of the 23S rRNA-binding site, which prevents the drug from binding the 50S subunit and blocking translocation. Because this mechanism is ribosomal and affects the entire class, switching to another macrolide would not help. First Aid lists this ribosomal methylation as the mechanism of resistance for macrolides.',
    '{"A":"D-Ala-D-Ala modification is the vancomycin resistance mechanism in enterococci.","C":"Altered uptake and increased efflux through plasmid-encoded pumps is the tetracycline resistance mechanism.","D":"Plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase is the resistance mechanism for chloramphenicol, which acts at the 50S peptidyltransferase center.","E":"Mutation in the RNA polymerase gene confers rifamycin resistance, an antimycobacterial class."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: aminoglycosides',
    'A 68-year-old woman with pyelonephritis and sepsis from a resistant gram-negative rod is given gentamicin in addition to a beta-lactam. On day 4 she reports ringing in her ears and reduced hearing, and her creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 1.7 mg/dL. She was also recently started on furosemide for volume overload.',
    'Which of the following best explains these adverse effects?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cochlear and tubular accumulation; worse with loop diuretics"},{"id":"B","text":"Aminoglycosides cause aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome from hepatic glucuronidation failure"},{"id":"C","text":"Aminoglycosides cause optic neuritis from arabinosyltransferase inhibition in the optic nerve"},{"id":"D","text":"Aminoglycosides cause a disulfiram-like reaction that presents as tinnitus after ethanol exposure"},{"id":"E","text":"Aminoglycosides cause peripheral neuropathy from pyridoxine deficiency induced by vitamin B6 antagonism"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Aminoglycosides are the classic dose-related ototoxic and nephrotoxic drugs: they damage cochlear hair cells and proximal tubular cells, and ototoxicity is especially likely when loop diuretics such as furosemide are coadministered. Their other signature toxicity is neuromuscular blockade, which is an absolute contraindication in myasthenia gravis, and they are also teratogenic. They are bactericidal and are used with beta-lactams for severe gram-negative rod infections because they require oxygen-dependent uptake.',
    '{"B":"Aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome belong to chloramphenicol, which fails to conjugate the drug in premature infants; gentamicin does not cause these.","C":"Optic neuritis is ethambutol''s dose-related toxicity; aminoglycosides affect the eighth cranial nerve and kidneys.","D":"A disulfiram-like reaction is caused by metronidazole and some cephalosporins, not by aminoglycosides, and does not manifest as dose-related hearing loss.","E":"Peripheral neuropathy from pyridoxine deficiency is the classic isoniazid toxicity; aminoglycosides can cause neuromuscular blockade but not via vitamin B6 antagonism."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: aminoglycosides',
    'A 42-year-old man with an anaerobic lung abscess from aspiration is being treated with metronidazole plus a beta-lactam. The consultant explains that gentamicin could not be part of the regimen even if gram-negative coverage were desired, because of a fundamental requirement of aminoglycoside uptake. The pharmacist also notes synergy between aminoglycosides and beta-lactams.',
    'Why are aminoglycosides ineffective against anaerobic organisms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They are destroyed by anaerobe-specific beta-lactamases"},{"id":"B","text":"Uptake needs oxygen, so anaerobes are resistant"},{"id":"C","text":"They cannot cross the anaerobic cell wall because it lacks peptidoglycan"},{"id":"D","text":"They require anaerobic conditions to bind the 30S subunit"},{"id":"E","text":"They are inactivated by the low intracellular pH found only in anaerobes"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Aminoglycosides require O2 for uptake into bacteria, which is why they are ineffective against anaerobes; the First Aid mnemonic notes that the "Mean" aminoglycoside GNATS cannot kill anaerobes. Their mechanism is irreversible inhibition of the initiation complex through 30S subunit binding, with mRNA misreading and blocked translocation. Aminoglycosides act synergistically with beta-lactams for severe gram-negative rod infections.',
    '{"A":"Aminoglycosides are not beta-lactam drugs and are not destroyed by beta-lactamases; their resistance comes from plasmid-encoded transferase enzymes that acetylase, phosphorylate, or adenylate the drug.","C":"Anaerobes have normal peptidoglycan walls; the block is at drug uptake, not at the cell wall.","D":"They bind the 30S subunit regardless of oxygen tension; oxygen is required for entry into the cell rather than for binding.","E":"Reduced drug uptake in anaerobic conditions is the correct central mechanism; intracellular pH is not the described barrier for this class."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: aminoglycosides',
    'A 5-day-old neonate with a complicated gram-negative urinary tract infection is receiving gentamicin. The mother asks why the baby needs the medicine, and the resident explains that aminoglycoside action is not merely static: it binds irreversibly to the 30S subunit, causing misreading of mRNA and blocking translocation, which is why the drug is bactericidal. The resident also reviews how bacteria become resistant.',
    'Which of the following is the principal mechanism of acquired aminoglycoside resistance?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Loss of a porin that limits drug entry"},{"id":"B","text":"Methylation of the 23S rRNA binding site"},{"id":"C","text":"Alteration of the D-Ala-D-Ala cell wall precursor target"},{"id":"D","text":"Modification of D-Ala-D-Ala precursors"},{"id":"E","text":"A point mutation of rRNA blocking binding"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Aminoglycoside resistance is mainly enzymatic: bacterial transferases inactivate the drug by acetylation, phosphorylation, or adenylation. The drugs are bactericidal because they irreversibly inhibit the initiation complex at the 30S subunit, cause misreading of mRNA, and block translocation, and they are used for severe gram-negative rod infections with beta-lactam synergy. Neomycin is used for bowel surgery preparation.',
    '{"A":"Porin loss reduces uptake but is not the principal acquired mechanism, and aminoglycosides are used against gram-negative organisms, not gram-positive ones.","B":"23S rRNA methylation confers macrolide resistance; aminoglycosides target the 30S subunit.","C":"D-Ala-D-Ala modification is how enterococci resist vancomycin, a cell wall active agent, not how bacteria resist aminoglycosides.","E":"Ribosomal RNA point mutation is the resistance mechanism for linezolid, not the transferase-mediated enzymatic inactivation characteristic of aminoglycosides."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: aminoglycosides',
    'A 74-year-old man with myasthenia gravis is admitted with aspiration pneumonia. The night team orders gentamicin for presumed gram-negative coverage before cultures return. The following morning the patient is much weaker, with worsening ptosis and respiratory difficulty requiring increased ventilatory support. The pharmacist flags the antibiotic as contraindicated in this patient.',
    'Which of the following best explains the antibiotic''s contribution to this patient''s deterioration?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Aminoglycosides cause hypokalemia from renal tubular wasting and worsen muscle strength"},{"id":"B","text":"Aminoglycosides inhibit pyridoxine and cause acute peripheral neuropathy with weakness"},{"id":"C","text":"Aminoglycosides induce a myasthenic crisis by increasing acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction"},{"id":"D","text":"Aminoglycosides cause neuromuscular blockade, an absolute contraindication in myasthenia gravis"},{"id":"E","text":"Aminoglycosides inhibit presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels in the same way as botulinum toxin"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Aminoglycosides cause neuromuscular blockade, which is an absolute contraindication in patients with myasthenia gravis; the impairment of neuromuscular transmission explains the rapidly worsening weakness and respiratory failure here. Their other adverse effects are nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, and teratogenicity. For severe gram-negative rod infections, aminoglycosides are used synergistically with beta-lactams, but not in this patient.',
    '{"A":"Hypokalemia from renal tubular wasting is characteristic of amphotericin B, and the acute weakness here tracks the aminoglycoside exposure directly.","B":"Pyridoxine antagonism with peripheral neuropathy is isoniazid''s toxicity, and it develops over weeks of therapy rather than as an acute worsening with an antibiotic dose.","C":"Aminoglycosides impair rather than enhance neuromuscular transmission; they do not increase acetylcholine release.","E":"Presynaptic calcium channel blockade describes botulinum toxin''s mechanism; aminoglycosides act postsynaptically to impair neuromuscular transmission."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, tetracyclines',
    'A 26-year-old woman with Rocky Mountain spotted fever is treated with chloramphenicol because of a severe allergy to doxycycline. Two weeks into therapy she develops a rising reticulocyte count followed by pancytopenia with aplastic anemia. She later asks why this drug is avoided in neonates, and the pharmacist explains it involves the same enzyme system.',
    'Which of the following best explains chloramphenicol''s mechanism of action and hematologic toxicity?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It forms toxic free radicals that damage DNA in marrow stem cells, causing predictable pancytopenia"},{"id":"B","text":"It binds the 30S subunit to prevent aminoacyl-tRNA attachment; anemia results from mitochondrial damage in marrow"},{"id":"C","text":"It blocks translocation at the 50S subunit; aplastic anemia occurs because it is a folate antagonist"},{"id":"D","text":"It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, causing megaloblastic anemia that responds to leucovorin"},{"id":"E","text":"Dose-dependent anemia, but aplastic anemia is dose independent"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Chloramphenicol blocks peptidyltransferase at the 50S ribosomal subunit and is bacteriostatic. Its hematologic adverse effects are split into a dose-dependent anemia and a dose-independent aplastic anemia, which is why its use is limited despite low cost and good CSF penetration. It also causes gray baby syndrome in premature infants because they lack hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase needed for conjugation.',
    '{"A":"Free radical DNA damage in bacterial cells describes metronidazole''s mechanism, not the mechanism of chloramphenicol-associated aplastic anemia.","B":"Preventing aminoacyl-tRNA attachment at the 30S subunit is tetracycline''s mechanism, not chloramphenicol''s 50S peptidyltransferase action.","C":"Blocking translocation at the 50S subunit describes macrolides and clindamycin; chloramphenicol is not a folate antagonist.","D":"Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition with megaloblastic anemia responding to leucovorin is trimethoprim''s profile."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, tetracyclines',
    'A 3-week-old premature infant born at 32 weeks develops sepsis and receives chloramphenicol because of limited alternatives. Within 3 days he becomes hypotensive, with abdominal distention, vomiting, and a striking grayish cyanotic discoloration of the skin. Laboratory studies show metabolic acidosis and no evidence of adrenal hemorrhage.',
    'Which of the following best explains this infant''s clinical presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Immune-mediated hemolysis from maternal IgG produces the gray discoloration and shock"},{"id":"B","text":"Immaturity of hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase causes drug accumulation, producing gray baby syndrome"},{"id":"C","text":"Immature renal tubular secretion of the drug causes crystalline nephropathy and uremic frost"},{"id":"D","text":"Deficiency of hepatic catalase-peroxidase prevents activation of the drug, causing a toxic metabolite buildup"},{"id":"E","text":"Immature bone marrow causes dose-independent aplastic anemia with secondary skin discoloration"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Gray baby syndrome occurs in premature infants because they lack adequate liver UDP-glucuronosyltransferase to conjugate and clear chloramphenicol, so the drug accumulates and produces cardiovascular collapse, abdominal distention, and a gray cyanotic appearance. This is a distinct toxic effect from the drug''s dose-independent aplastic anemia, and it is part of why chloramphenicol use is limited and why it appears on the list of drugs to avoid in neonates.',
    '{"A":"Hemolysis with gray discoloration would suggest an antibody-mediated process, but the presentation described follows directly from impaired hepatic conjugation of the drug.","C":"Crystalline nephropathy and acute kidney injury from inadequate hydration is acyclovir''s toxicity, not a cause of gray discoloration in neonates.","D":"Catalase-peroxidase is required to activate isoniazid, an antimycobacterial drug, and does not apply to chloramphenicol.","E":"Aplastic anemia from chloramphenicol is a dose-independent idiosyncratic reaction that develops later and does not produce this acute shock with gray skin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, tetracyclines',
    'A 19-year-old woman with a severe necrotizing infection of the tonsil and deep neck space grows Streptococcus pyogenes. She is treated with clindamycin plus penicillin because clindamycin is effective against invasive group A streptococcal infection. One week later she develops profuse watery diarrhea, cramping, and leukocytosis, and stool studies confirm Clostridioides difficile toxin.',
    'Which of the following best describes clindamycin''s mechanism and its classic adverse effect?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"50S translocation blockade with C difficile colitis"},{"id":"B","text":"It binds the 30S subunit and prevents aminoacyl-tRNA attachment; the classic adverse effect is photosensitivity"},{"id":"C","text":"It inhibits DNA gyrase; the classic adverse effect is tendinopathy and tendon rupture"},{"id":"D","text":"It inhibits the initiation complex at the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit; the classic adverse effect is thrombocytopenia"},{"id":"E","text":"It blocks peptidyltransferase at the 50S subunit; the classic adverse effect is aplastic anemia"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Clindamycin blocks peptide transfer and translocation at the 50S ribosomal subunit and is bacteriostatic. Its classic adverse effect is pseudomembranous colitis from C difficile overgrowth, presenting with fever and diarrhea, which is what occurred here. Clindamycin covers anaerobic infections such as Bacteroides and C perfringens in aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, and oral infections, and is effective in invasive group A streptococcal disease; the metronidazole comparison is that clindamycin treats anaerobes above the diaphragm and metronidazole below.',
    '{"B":"30S binding that prevents aminoacyl-tRNA attachment is tetracycline''s mechanism, and its signature toxicity is teeth and bone effects.","C":"DNA gyrase inhibition with tendinopathy is the fluoroquinolone profile.","D":"Binding 23S rRNA to prevent initiation complex formation with thrombocytopenia is linezolid''s mechanism and toxicity.","E":"Peptidyltransferase blockade with aplastic anemia belongs to chloramphenicol, a different 50S drug."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Protein synthesis inhibitors: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, tetracyclines',
    'A 7-year-old boy with a tick bite develops fever, headache, myalgias, and a rash spreading from the wrists toward the trunk. Doxycycline is prescribed. His mother is concerned because she has heard that tetracyclines stain children''s teeth, and she asks about the drug''s target and how it should be taken.',
    'Which of the following statements about tetracyclines is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"30S binding blocked by divalent cations"},{"id":"B","text":"They bind the 30S subunit irreversibly and are bactericidal for anaerobes because uptake requires oxygen"},{"id":"C","text":"They are activated by viral thymidine kinase and cause crystalline nephropathy"},{"id":"D","text":"They inhibit arabinosyltransferase and require acidic gastric pH for absorption"},{"id":"E","text":"They bind the 50S subunit and block peptidyltransferase; they are bactericidal against anaerobes"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic 30S binders that prevent attachment of aminoacyl-tRNA to the ribosomal acceptor site, and divalent cations such as Ca2+ and Mg2+ in milk, antacids, and iron preparations chelate them and inhibit gut absorption. They accumulate intracellularly, which makes them effective against Rickettsia and Chlamydia and Borrelia burgdorferi, and doxycycline is effective against community-acquired MRSA and is the exception that may be used in children. Doxycycline is fecally eliminated, so it can be used in renal failure.',
    '{"B":"Oxygen-dependent uptake with bactericidal action and activity against anaerobes is contradicted by the facts: tetracyclines are bacteriostatic and divalent cations limit their absorption, and the oxygen requirement belongs to aminoglycosides.","C":"Activation by viral thymidine kinase is acyclovir''s mechanism, and crystalline nephropathy is acyclovir''s adverse effect.","D":"Arabinosyltransferase inhibition is ethambutol''s mechanism; tetracycline absorption is limited by divalent cations, not enhanced by acidic pH.","E":"50S peptidyltransferase blockade describes chloramphenicol, and tetracyclines are bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Sulfonamides and folate antagonist pathways',
    'A 24-year-old woman has uncomplicated cystitis with dysuria and frequency. She is prescribed trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. A medical student asks why the combination is preferred, given that either drug alone is bacteriostatic.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism and rationale of the combination?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and trimethoprim inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, giving a sequential block"},{"id":"B","text":"Both drugs inhibit the 50S ribosomal subunit at sequential steps of protein synthesis"},{"id":"C","text":"Sequential folate blockade gives bactericidal synergy"},{"id":"D","text":"Both drugs inhibit dihydrofolate reductase and therefore produce dose-dependent synergy"},{"id":"E","text":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits DNA gyrase and trimethoprim inhibits folate synthesis, giving synergy"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Sulfonamides inhibit dihydropteroate synthase and thus folate synthesis, and trimethoprim inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase; the two act sequentially in the folate pathway, converting a bacteriostatic effect into a bactericidal one. TMP-SMX is used for simple UTI, for Shigella and Salmonella infection, and for Pneumocystis jirovecii treatment and prophylaxis. Either agent alone is only bacteriostatic.',
    '{"A":"The order is reversed: sulfonamides act earlier at dihydropteroate synthase, and trimethoprim acts downstream at dihydrofolate reductase.","B":"Neither drug acts on the ribosome; the folate pathway supplies thymidine and purines needed for DNA and RNA synthesis, not protein synthesis directly.","D":"Only trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase; sulfonamides act one step earlier, which is what makes the sequential block synergistic.","E":"DNA gyrase inhibition is the fluoroquinolone mechanism, and the folate pathway antagonism is not mediated through gyrase."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Sulfonamides and folate antagonist pathways',
    'A 31-year-old man with HIV and a CD4+ count of 80 cells/mm3 is prescribed trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for prophylaxis. On routine labs he has a potassium of 5.9 mEq/L, and a peripheral smear shows macroovalocytes with hypersegmented neutrophils consistent with megaloblastic anemia. His physician reduces the dose and considers adding leucovorin.',
    'Which of the following best explains these laboratory findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Folate deficiency with megaloblastic anemia and hyperkalemia"},{"id":"B","text":"Trimethoprim causes aplastic anemia through dose-independent idiosyncratic mechanisms"},{"id":"C","text":"Trimethoprim inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, causing hemolysis in G6PD deficiency and hyperkalemia"},{"id":"D","text":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, causing megaloblastic anemia and hyperkalemia"},{"id":"E","text":"Trimethoprim causes myelosuppression through mitochondrial toxicity that reverses only with G-CSF"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Trimethoprim inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase and causes megaloblastic anemia, leukopenia, and granulocytopenia, which can be avoided by coadministering leucovorin (folinic acid); the mnemonic is that TMP Treats Marrow Poorly. At high doses it also causes hyperkalemia by a mechanism similar to potassium-sparing diuretics. Sulfonamides contribute the earlier blockade at dihydropteroate synthase, and this patient''s CD4 count of 80 is the indication for TMP-SMX prophylaxis.',
    '{"B":"Dose-independent aplastic anemia is chloramphenicol''s idiosyncratic toxicity, and trimethoprim causes dose-related folate deficiency instead.","C":"Dihydropteroate synthase is the sulfonamide target, and G6PD-related hemolysis is a sulfonamide and dapsone toxicity rather than a trimethoprim effect.","D":"The enzymes are reversed: trimethoprim, not sulfamethoxazole, inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is responsible for the hematologic and potassium effects.","E":"Linezolid causes myelosuppression through ribosomal and mitochondrial effects, whereas trimethoprim''s marrow effect is folate-mediated and reverses with leucovorin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Sulfonamides and folate antagonist pathways',
    'A 28-year-old woman at 38 weeks gestation presents in labor with a fever of 38.6 C and ruptured membranes for 18 hours. She is treated with antibiotics and delivers a jaundiced neonate 2 days later. The infant has a high indirect bilirubin and is at risk for kernicterus. The neonatology team reviews the list of drugs to avoid in pregnancy and in neonates.',
    'Which of the following drug classes is contraindicated in this setting because of kernicterus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Fluoroquinolones"},{"id":"B","text":"Tetracyclines"},{"id":"C","text":"Chloramphenicol"},{"id":"D","text":"Sulfonamides"},{"id":"E","text":"Aminoglycosides"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Sulfonamides are among the antimicrobials to avoid in pregnancy because they cause kernicterus; they displace bilirubin from albumin and, in the neonate, permit bilirubin to cross into the basal ganglia. First Aid''s pregnancy mnemonic groups sulfonamides for kernicterus, aminoglycosides for ototoxicity, fluoroquinolones for cartilage damage, clarithromycin as embryotoxic, tetracyclines for discolored teeth and inhibited bone growth, ribavirin and griseofulvin as teratogens, and chloramphenicol for gray baby syndrome.',
    '{"A":"Fluoroquinolones are avoided in pregnancy and in children under 18 because of possible cartilage damage.","B":"Tetracyclines are avoided because they cause discolored teeth and inhibition of bone growth by chelating calcium.","C":"Chloramphenicol is avoided because it causes gray baby syndrome in premature infants lacking hepatic glucuronidation, not kernicterus.","E":"Aminoglycosides are avoided in pregnancy because of ototoxicity to the fetal eighth nerve, not because they cause kernicterus."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Sulfonamides and folate antagonist pathways',
    'A 22-year-old man from Southeast Asia with a new diagnosis of lepromatous leprosy is treated with multidrug therapy. He develops darkening of the skin lesions, mild hemolytic anemia, and a methemoglobin level of 8%. He is G6PD deficient. The team reviews alternatives among folate-pathway agents, including dapsone, which is structurally related to sulfonamides.',
    'Which of the following best describes dapsone and its role?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is a sulfonamide that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is used only for tuberculoid leprosy"},{"id":"B","text":"Sulfonamide-like drug for leprosy that causes hemolysis and methemoglobinemia"},{"id":"C","text":"It is a rifamycin that inhibits RNA polymerase and causes orange-red body fluids"},{"id":"D","text":"It is a tetracycline derivative that causes photosensitivity and tooth discoloration"},{"id":"E","text":"It is an antipseudomonal penicillin used with a beta-lactamase inhibitor for leprosy"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Dapsone is similar to sulfonamides in mechanism but structurally distinct; it is used for both lepromatous and tuberculoid leprosy, for Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis, and in combination with trimethoprim for treatment. First Aid lists hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, methemoglobinemia, and agranulocytosis as its adverse effects. Long-term therapy for leprosy also includes rifampin, with clofazimine added for the lepromatous form.',
    '{"A":"Dapsone is used for both lepromatous and tuberculoid leprosy, and its mechanism mirrors sulfonamides in the folate pathway rather than dihydrofolate reductase inhibition.","C":"Rifampin is the rifamycin used with dapsone in leprosy, and the orange secretions and RNA polymerase inhibition describe rifampin itself.","D":"Tetracyclines cause photosensitivity and tooth discoloration; dapsone''s toxicity is hemolysis and methemoglobinemia.","E":"Antipseudomonal penicillins are irrelevant to leprosy, which is treated with dapsone, rifampin, and clofazimine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Vancomycin and cell wall inhibitors',
    'A 64-year-old man with MRSA bacteremia is receiving intravenous vancomycin. During the first infusion he develops flushing of the face, neck, and upper chest with pruritus and mild hypotension, but no urticaria and no wheezing. The infusion has been running at the standard rate for only 15 minutes.',
    'Which of the following best explains this reaction and its management?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Infusion-related histamine release; slow the rate and give antihistamines"},{"id":"B","text":"A DRESS syndrome presenting within minutes because of prior sensitization"},{"id":"C","text":"An aminoglycoside ototoxic effect, managed by audiometry before the next dose"},{"id":"D","text":"Nephrotoxicity from accumulation of the drug in renal tubules, requiring immediate dialysis"},{"id":"E","text":"IgE-mediated anaphylaxis requiring permanent discontinuation of all glycopeptides"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is the vancomycin infusion reaction, formerly called red man syndrome: diffuse flushing and pruritus from idiopathic histamine release driven by rapid infusion. It is not a true allergy, and it is largely preventable by pretreating with antihistamines and slowing the infusion rate. Vancomycin''s other adverse effects are nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, thrombophlebitis, and DRESS syndrome.',
    '{"B":"DRESS syndrome is a delayed hypersensitivity reaction with rash, eosinophilia, and organ involvement that appears days to weeks after exposure, not within minutes.","C":"Ototoxicity is a complication of aminoglycosides or prolonged vancomycin use rather than an immediate flushing reaction during infusion.","D":"Nephrotoxicity from vancomycin is a cumulative renal effect that does not present as acute flushing and pruritus during the first minutes of an infusion.","E":"Because the mechanism is non-IgE histamine release, it does not mandate permanent discontinuation, and many patients tolerate the drug when it is infused more slowly."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Vancomycin and cell wall inhibitors',
    'A 55-year-old woman has an Enterococcus faecium bloodstream infection. The laboratory reports that the isolate is resistant to vancomycin and that the resistance was acquired through a modified cell wall precursor. The team switches to linezolid. A medical student asks how vancomycin works and how resistance develops in enterococci.',
    'Which of the following best describes vancomycin''s mechanism and the mechanism of resistance in enterococci?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Binds D-Ala-D-Ala; resistance modifies the target to D-Ala-D-Lac"},{"id":"B","text":"It inhibits arabinosyltransferase; resistance arises from mutations in katG"},{"id":"C","text":"It inhibits peptidyltransferase at the 50S subunit; resistance arises from a plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase"},{"id":"D","text":"It binds penicillin-binding proteins and activates autolysins; resistance arises from an altered PBP target"},{"id":"E","text":"It disrupts the cell membrane by forming pores; resistance arises from reduced ergosterol content"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Vancomycin inhibits cell wall peptidoglycan formation by binding the D-Ala-D-Ala portion of cell wall precursors, and it is bactericidal for most bacteria though bacteriostatic against C difficile. Vancomycin resistance occurs through amino acid modification of the target from D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac; the mnemonic is that if you lack a D-Ala (dollar), you can''t ride the van (vancomycin). It is not susceptible to beta-lactamases.',
    '{"B":"Arabinosyltransferase inhibition is ethambutol''s mechanism, and katG mutations underlie isoniazid resistance.","C":"Peptidyltransferase inhibition at the 50S subunit is chloramphenicol''s mechanism, and plasmid-encoded acetyltransferase is chloramphenicol''s resistance mechanism.","D":"Binding penicillin-binding proteins and activating autolysins is the beta-lactam mechanism, and an altered PBP is the basis of MRSA resistance, not vancomycin resistance in enterococci.","E":"Pore formation in the cell membrane is how amphotericin B and nystatin bind ergosterol, a completely different target."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Vancomycin and cell wall inhibitors',
    'A 71-year-old hospitalized man develops watery diarrhea 6 days after receiving clindamycin for a dental infection. Stool studies confirm Clostridioides difficile toxin. He is treated with oral vancomycin, and the pharmacist explains why the oral route is chosen, noting that the drug is not absorbed and is only bacteriostatic against this organism.',
    'Which of the following best explains the use of oral vancomycin in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Oral vancomycin is used because it is bactericidal against anaerobes below the diaphragm"},{"id":"B","text":"Oral vancomycin is preferred because it has no effect on normal colonic flora"},{"id":"C","text":"Unabsorbed drug reaches high luminal colonic levels"},{"id":"D","text":"Oral vancomycin is given to cover gram-negative rods in the gut that drive colitis"},{"id":"E","text":"Oral vancomycin achieves high serum levels that sterilize the bloodstream in pseudomembranous colitis"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Vancomycin is used against gram-positive organisms only, and for C difficile the oral route is specified because the drug is not absorbed from the gut, so it reaches high concentrations in the colonic lumen where the organism resides. First Aid notes vancomycin is bactericidal against most bacteria but bacteriostatic against C difficile. Intravenous vancomycin is used for serious multidrug-resistant systemic infections such as MRSA and sensitive enterococci.',
    '{"A":"The bactericidal anaerobic coverage below the diaphragm is metronidazole''s niche, and vancomycin is actually bacteriostatic against C difficile.","B":"Vancomycin does alter colonic flora, which is part of why it is effective, and the claim of no effect on flora is the opposite of its action.","D":"Vancomycin has no gram-negative activity at all; it covers gram-positive bacteria including gram-positive anaerobes such as C difficile.","E":"Oral vancomycin is poorly absorbed, so it does not achieve the serum levels needed for bloodstream infection; systemic gram-positive infections require the intravenous route."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Antimicrobials', 'Vancomycin and cell wall inhibitors',
    'A 48-year-old woman with vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium bacteremia from an infected central line has renal insufficiency, so the team wants to avoid nephrotoxic agents. They choose a lipopeptide that disrupts bacterial membranes and note that it would be inappropriate if she had pneumonia. The pharmacist asks how daptomycin differs from vancomycin and polymyxins in mechanism.',
    'Which of the following best describes daptomycin''s mechanism of action and limitation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It binds D-Ala-D-Ala precursors of the cell wall and is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant"},{"id":"B","text":"It binds ergosterol in the membrane and causes leakage of electrolytes, which is why it fails in pneumonia"},{"id":"C","text":"It disrupts membranes of gram-negative organisms by binding phospholipids, acting as a polymyxin analog"},{"id":"D","text":"It inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit by blocking the initiation complex"},{"id":"E","text":"Transmembrane channels; inactivated by surfactant"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Daptomycin is a lipopeptide that disrupts gram-positive coccal cell membranes by creating transmembrane channels, and it is used for S aureus skin infections especially MRSA, bacteremia, infective endocarditis, and VRE. It is not used for pneumonia because it avidly binds to and is inactivated by surfactant. It can cause myopathy and rhabdomyolysis, which is the basis of the mnemonic "dapto-myo-skin."',
    '{"A":"Binding D-Ala-D-Ala is vancomycin''s cell wall mechanism; daptomycin acts on the membrane, and its surfactant inactivation is unrelated to cell wall binding.","B":"Ergosterol binding with electrolyte leakage is amphotericin B''s antifungal mechanism; daptomycin targets bacterial membrane phospholipids rather than a sterol.","C":"Disrupting gram-negative membranes by binding phospholipids describes the polymyxins such as colistin and polymyxin B, which cover gram-negative organisms; daptomycin is gram-positive only.","D":"Blocking formation of the initiation complex at the 50S subunit is linezolid''s mechanism, not daptomycin''s."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Antiparasitic drugs',
    'A 37-year-old man presents with 5 days of bloody, mucoid diarrhea with tenesmus and lower abdominal pain after drinking untreated well water at a rural property. He is afebrile. Examination shows mild left lower quadrant tenderness. Stool testing is positive for Entamoeba histolytica antigen, and abdominal ultrasound is normal.',
    'Which of the following drugs is most appropriate for treating the tissue-invading form of this patient''s organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Sulfadiazine"},{"id":"B","text":"Sodium stibogluconate"},{"id":"C","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"D","text":"Atovaquone"},{"id":"E","text":"Metronidazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Metronidazole is the drug First Aid lists for invasive Entamoeba histolytica disease, and it is also the treatment for Giardia lamblia and Trichomonas vaginalis. After metronidazole clears tissue trophozoites, a luminal agent such as paromomycin is used to eradicate asymptomatic cyst carriage. Knowing that metronidazole covers these three protozoa but not the coccidia is the high-yield drug-organism match.',
    '{"A":"Sulfadiazine is paired with pyrimethamine for Toxoplasma gondii and is also the sulfa option for coccidia, but it has no role in amebic colitis.","B":"Sodium stibogluconate is an antimonial for leishmaniasis, including cutaneous ulcers and kala-azar, and it is not active against an intestinal ameba.","C":"Praziquantel is the first-line drug for Schistosoma, Clonorchis, Taenia, and Diphyllobothrium, placing it with trematodes and cestodes rather than with protozoa.","D":"Atovaquone is used for Babesia with azithromycin and for malaria with proguanil, reflecting its effect on mitochondrial electron transport, not on Entamoeba."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Antiparasitic drugs',
    'A 40-year-old woman who volunteered in Malawi presents with an itchy rash after bathing in a freshwater lake, followed 3 weeks later by fever, bloody diarrhea, and eosinophilia. Stool examination shows eggs with a lateral spine, and serology for Schistosoma is positive. She is started on an oral agent that is the mainstay of therapy for her infection.',
    'For which of the following conditions is the drug used to treat this patient also specifically recommended in First Aid?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cyclospora infection with prolonged watery diarrhea"},{"id":"B","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum infection with megaloblastic anemia"},{"id":"C","text":"Strongyloides stercoralis hyperinfection syndrome"},{"id":"D","text":"Babesia microti infection in an asplenic patient"},{"id":"E","text":"Asymptomatic Entamoeba histolytica cyst passage"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'The drug for schistosomiasis is praziquantel, which First Aid lists for both Schistosoma species as well as for Clonorchis sinensis and for the cestodes Taenia solium and Diphyllobothrium latum. That is why Diphyllobothrium latum infection, which presents with megaloblastic anemia from vitamin B12 competition, is the correct matching association here. Praziquantel paralyzes the flukes and makes them susceptible to host defenses.',
    '{"A":"Cyclospora responds to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and praziquantel has no antiprotozoal activity.","C":"Strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome requires ivermectin, an anthelmintic for nematodes, and praziquantel has no activity against it.","D":"Babesia microti is an intraerythrocytic protozoan treated with atovaquone plus azithromycin, so a flukicide is inappropriate.","E":"Asymptomatic Entamoeba histolytica cyst passers receive paromomycin, a luminal amebicide rather than a drug for helminths."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Antiparasitic drugs',
    'A 63-year-old man from northern Wisconsin with a history of splenectomy for trauma presents with 5 days of fever, chills, and dark urine after multiple tick bites while hiking. Blood smear shows intraerythrocytic ring forms with a tetrad in some RBCs, and PCR confirms Babesia microti with parasitemia of 8%. He reports an anaphylactic allergy to macrolide antibiotics, so azithromycin cannot be used.',
    'Which of the following antiparasitic combinations is the most appropriate alternative regimen?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Atovaquone plus pyrimethamine"},{"id":"B","text":"Chloroquine plus primaquine"},{"id":"C","text":"Sulfadiazine plus clindamycin"},{"id":"D","text":"Metronidazole plus ivermectin"},{"id":"E","text":"Erythromycin plus doxycycline"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'First Aid pairs atovaquone with pyrimethamine for Babesia, and the other commonly taught regimen is atovaquone plus azithromycin; with a macrolide allergy, the atovaquone-based combination with pyrimethamine is the appropriate alternative. Combination therapy is used because a single agent does not reliably clear the parasite, and asplenic patients cannot clear infected RBCs efficiently. Distinguishing the Babesia pair from the malaria regimens is high yield.',
    '{"E":"Erythromycin plus doxycycline is not a First Aid antiparasitic regimen, and doxycycline''s role is malaria prophylaxis rather than babesiosis treatment.","B":"Chloroquine plus primaquine treats Plasmodium vivax or ovale, where primaquine eradicates hypnozoites, and it targets a different organism and life cycle stage.","C":"Sulfadiazine with clindamycin is not the Babesia combination; sulfadiazine belongs with pyrimethamine for Toxoplasma gondii.","D":"Metronidazole plus ivermectin combines drugs for amebae and soil-transmitted helminths, neither of which is a tick-borne erythrocyte parasite."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Antiparasitic drugs',
    'A 55-year-old man from a rural area of Peru has a 14-cm cystic lesion in the right lung and a second lesion in the liver, both with eggshell calcification, and ELISA for Echinococcus is positive. He also reports a recent brief diarrheal illness that resolved without treatment. The surgical team is planning resection of the larger cyst and has asked about preoperative antiparasitic therapy.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate antiparasitic agent and the reason for using it?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Niclosamide for treating the complicated hydatid cyst"},{"id":"B","text":"Praziquantel for treating the complicated hydatid cyst"},{"id":"C","text":"Metronidazole for eradicating the cyst before surgery"},{"id":"D","text":"Albendazole for treating the complicated hydatid cyst"},{"id":"E","text":"Ivermectin for eradicating the cyst before surgery"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Echinococcus granulosus forms hydatid cysts, most often in liver and lungs, and First Aid lists albendazole as the treatment with surgery reserved for complicated cysts. The eggshell calcification is the classic imaging description, and because cyst rupture can cause anaphylaxis, anthelmintic therapy around surgery reduces that risk. Distinguishing albendazole from the other anthelmintics is the high-yield point for this cestode.',
    '{"A":"Niclosamide appears with praziquantel for Diphyllobothrium latum rather than as the agent for hydatid cyst disease.","B":"Praziquantel is appropriate for Taenia, Diphyllobothrium, Schistosoma, and Clonorchis, but First Aid lists albendazole as the medical agent for Echinococcus.","C":"Metronidazole is a protozoal drug for Giardia, Entamoeba, and Trichomonas, and it has no activity against a cestode cyst.","E":"Ivermectin is used for Strongyloides, Onchocerca volvulus, and scabies, and it does not treat Echinococcus hydatid disease."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Arboviruses and other zoonotic viruses',
    'A 31-year-old woman returns from travel to Southeast Asia with 4 days of high fever, severe retro-orbital headache, myalgias, arthralgias, and a maculopapular rash. Laboratory studies show leukopenia, neutropenia, and a platelet count of 88,000/mm3. She has no bleeding.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the most likely virus and its vector?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Chikungunya virus, an alphavirus with joint swelling as a highly specific finding"},{"id":"B","text":"West Nile virus, a flavivirus causing acute asymmetric flaccid paralysis"},{"id":"C","text":"Zika virus, a flavivirus causing microcephaly in the newborn"},{"id":"D","text":"Dengue virus, a flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes"},{"id":"E","text":"Yellow fever virus, a flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes with jaundice and black vomitus"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Dengue fever is caused by dengue virus, a flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes, and presents with fever, rash, headache, myalgias, arthralgias, retro-orbital pain, and neutropenia with thrombocytopenia and leukopenia. Diagnosis uses RT-PCR or serology, and treatment is supportive with intravascular volume repletion. Dengue hemorrhagic fever, most common with a second infection by a different serotype through antibody-dependent enhancement, adds bleeding and plasma leakage.',
    '{"A":"Chikungunya is an alphavirus/togavirus whose arthralgias and joint swelling are its distinguishing features, but the retro-orbital pain with neutropenia points to dengue.","B":"West Nile virus causes meningoencephalitis and acute asymmetric flaccid paralysis, which are not described in this patient.","C":"Zika virus causes conjunctivitis with low-grade fever and itchy rash in about 20% of cases, and its major concern is congenital infection rather than this acute febrile syndrome.","E":"Yellow fever features fever with jaundice, black vomitus, hemorrhage, and backache, and biopsy shows Councilman bodies; jaundice is absent here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Arboviruses and other zoonotic viruses',
    'A 28-year-old woman who is 9 weeks pregnant develops a pruritic rash, conjunctivitis, and low-grade fever after travel to a tropical region with an ongoing outbreak. Fetal ultrasound at 22 weeks shows ventriculomegaly and subcortical calcifications, and the newborn has microcephaly.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative virus and its transmission?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lassa fever virus, an arenavirus spread by rodents"},{"id":"B","text":"Ebola virus, a filovirus requiring direct contact with bodily fluids"},{"id":"C","text":"Hantavirus, a bunyavirus causing hemorrhagic fever and pneumonia"},{"id":"D","text":"Zika virus, a flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes with sexual and vertical transmission"},{"id":"E","text":"Dengue virus, an Aedes-transmitted flavivirus causing hemorrhagic fever in pregnancy"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Zika virus is a flavivirus most commonly transmitted by Aedes mosquito bites and can also spread sexually and vertically. Infection causes conjunctivitis, low-grade fever, and an itchy rash in about 20% of cases and may be complicated by Guillain-Barre syndrome. In pregnancy it can cause miscarriage or congenital Zika syndrome, with ventriculomegaly and subcortical calcifications on imaging and microcephaly, ocular anomalies, and motor abnormalities in the newborn.',
    '{"A":"Lassa fever is an arenavirus spread by rodents that causes hemorrhagic fever and encephalitis, not congenital microcephaly in an outbreak setting.","B":"Ebola is a filovirus transmitted by direct contact with bodily fluids and fomites such as dead bodies; it causes hemorrhagic fever rather than congenital brain malformations.","C":"Hantavirus, a segmented bunyavirus, causes hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome and pneumonia, and it is acquired from rodent excreta rather than mosquitoes.","E":"Dengue causes dengue fever with retro-orbital pain and neutropenia and can progress to hemorrhagic fever, but it is not the characteristic cause of congenital microcephaly with subcortical calcifications."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Aspergillus fumigatus',
    'A 24-year-old man with chronic granulomatous disease is admitted with three days of fever, pleuritic chest pain, and hemoptysis despite broad-spectrum antibacterial therapy. Chest CT shows a nodular lesion with surrounding ground-glass opacity. A lung biopsy demonstrates thin filaments with regular cross-walls and branches splitting at 45° angles.',
    'Which of the following best explains this patient''s specific susceptibility to this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Defective T-cell function with CD4+ lymphopenia"},{"id":"B","text":"Absence of B cells and immunoglobulin production"},{"id":"C","text":"Impaired macrophage phagosome acidification only"},{"id":"D","text":"Diabetic ketoacidosis with acidic tissue pH"},{"id":"E","text":"Defective neutrophil oxidative burst"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Aspergillus fumigatus is catalase-positive, so it is killed by the oxidative burst of neutrophils; patients with neutrophil dysfunction such as chronic granulomatous disease are therefore at high risk for invasive aspergillosis. The organism shows acute-angle, 45-degree branching of septate hyphae, which distinguishes it from the broad non-septate right-angle hyphae of Mucor. Treatment is voriconazole, with echinocandins as second-line therapy. Aspergillomas instead form in pre-existing lung cavities, especially after tuberculosis.',
    '{"A":"CD4+ lymphopenia is the defect that predisposes to Pneumocystis pneumonia and cryptococcal disease, not specifically to invasive aspergillosis.","B":"Absent B cells cause agammaglobulinemia and recurrent infection with encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, and this is not the classic Aspergillus risk.","C":"Isolated phagosome acidification failure does not describe chronic granulomatous disease, whose defect is in the NADPH oxidase respiratory burst.","D":"Diabetic ketoacidosis is the classic setting for Mucor and Rhizopus infections, not the specific susceptibility for Aspergillus."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Aspergillus fumigatus',
    'A 63-year-old man with a history of treated cavitary tuberculosis has a 4-cm cavitary lesion in the upper lobe. He has had recurrent hemoptysis and a chronic cough. Imaging shows a solid rounded fungal mass within the pre-existing cavity. He also has asthma with poorly controlled symptoms and newly documented bronchiectasis.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line antifungal for invasive aspergillosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Fluconazole"},{"id":"B","text":"Nystatin"},{"id":"C","text":"Griseofulvin"},{"id":"D","text":"Terbinafine"},{"id":"E","text":"Voriconazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Voriconazole is the treatment of choice for Aspergillus infections, with echinocandins reserved as second-line therapy. Voriconazole is an azole that inhibits the cytochrome P-450 enzyme converting lanosterol to ergosterol. In this patient the solid fungal mass occupying a pre-existing tuberculous cavity represents an aspergilloma, a classic complication emphasized after TB infection. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, associated with asthma and cystic fibrosis, is a separate hypersensitivity phenomenon that can cause bronchiectasis and eosinophilia.',
    '{"A":"Fluconazole has reliable activity against Candida but not against Aspergillus; it is used for candidal infections and chronic suppression of cryptococcal meningitis.","B":"Nystatin acts like amphotericin B but is used topically only, such as ''swish and swallow'' therapy for oral candidiasis.","C":"Griseofulvin disrupts microtubules and mitosis and is limited to superficial dermatophyte infections such as tinea.","D":"Terbinafine inhibits squalene epoxidase and is used for dermatophytoses, especially onychomycosis, not for invasive aspergillosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Babesia',
    'A 62-year-old man from Cape Cod, Massachusetts, presents in July with 6 days of high fever, drenching sweats, and fatigue after hiking in wooded areas. He recalls a tick bite 3 weeks earlier without a rash. Examination shows a temperature of 39.4 C and scleral icterus. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.6 g/dL (baseline 14.2 g/dL), elevated indirect bilirubin and lactate dehydrogenase, and a low haptoglobin. Peripheral blood smear shows ring forms within RBCs and a tetrad configuration in one erythrocyte.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Babesia microti"},{"id":"B","text":"Plasmodium falciparum"},{"id":"C","text":"Trypanosoma cruzi"},{"id":"D","text":"Leishmania donovani"},{"id":"E","text":"Toxoplasma gondii"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Babesiosis is caused by Babesia microti, which is endemic to the northeastern and north central United States, making a summer hike in New England the classic setting. The vector is the Ixodes tick, the same tick that transmits Borrelia burgdorferi and Anaplasma species. Diagnosis rests on ring forms with a Maltese cross tetrad on smear plus PCR, and treatment is atovaquone plus azithromycin, with asplenia increasing the risk of severe disease.',
    '{"B":"Plasmodium falciparum produces intraerythrocytic rings but never forms Maltese crosses, requires travel to an endemic region, and is treated with chloroquine, mefloquine, or artesunate.","C":"Trypanosoma cruzi appears as extracellular trypomastigotes on a blood smear and causes Chagas cardiomyopathy, not an acute hemolytic anemia following a tick bite.","D":"Leishmania donovani is an intracellular parasite of macrophages causing hepatosplenomegaly and pancytopenia, and it is transmitted by the sandfly rather than a tick.","E":"Toxoplasma gondii causes ring-enhancing brain lesions or congenital disease and is diagnosed serologically or by biopsy of tachyzoites, not by an intraerythrocytic tetrad."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Babesia',
    'A 58-year-old man with a history of splenectomy after trauma presents with 2 weeks of fever to 39.5 C, worsening fatigue, and dark urine. He lives in Wisconsin and spent the summer at a lake cabin. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.4 g/dL, indirect hyperbilirubinemia, a low haptoglobin, and parasites visible in more than 5% of RBCs. PCR confirms Babesia microti.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient''s infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amphotericin B plus sodium stibogluconate"},{"id":"B","text":"Metronidazole plus paromomycin"},{"id":"C","text":"Chloroquine plus primaquine"},{"id":"D","text":"Artesunate plus doxycycline"},{"id":"E","text":"Atovaquone plus azithromycin"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Without a spleen, infected RBCs cannot be cleared efficiently, so babesiosis carries a high risk of severe disease. First Aid pairs atovaquone with azithromycin for Babesia, and that combination is the standard approach. Because Babesia can mimic Plasmodium on smear, matching the drug pair to the tick-borne parasite prevents the common error of starting antimalarial therapy.',
    '{"A":"Amphotericin B plus sodium stibogluconate treats visceral leishmaniasis, an intracellular macrophage infection, rather than a parasite living inside RBCs.","B":"Metronidazole plus paromomycin combines tissue and luminal activity against Entamoeba histolytica, which causes colitis and liver abscess rather than hemolytic anemia.","C":"Chloroquine plus primaquine is a malaria regimen whose primaquine component targets Plasmodium hypnozoites, and neither drug is active against Babesia.","D":"Artesunate plus doxycycline is used for severe Plasmodium falciparum malaria, and this is not the First Aid regimen for a tick-borne erythrocyte parasite."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacillus anthracis',
    'A 41-year-old man who works at a wool and hide processing plant develops 3 days of fever, myalgias, nonproductive cough, and chest discomfort. Chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum with pleural effusions. Blood cultures grow large gram-positive rods that form spores, and the colonies have a halo of projections resembling a medusa head.',
    'Which of the following components of the responsible toxin acts as an adenylyl cyclase?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Poly d-glutamate capsule"},{"id":"B","text":"Lecithinase"},{"id":"C","text":"Edema factor"},{"id":"D","text":"Protective antigen"},{"id":"E","text":"Lethal factor"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Anthrax toxin has three exotoxin components: protective antigen, lethal factor, and edema factor. Edema factor acts as an adenylyl cyclase, raising intracellular cAMP and upsetting homeostasis to produce edema and necrosis. Protective antigen is the binding component, while lethal factor inhibits MAP kinase and promotes macrophage apoptosis.',
    '{"A":"The poly d-glutamate polypeptide capsule is a separate antiphagocytic virulence factor of Bacillus anthracis and is not part of the toxin complex.","B":"Lecithinase is a phospholipase that causes myonecrosis and hemolysis in Clostridium perfringens infection, not a component of anthrax toxin.","D":"Protective antigen is the cell-binding component that delivers the other two factors into the cytosol; it has no adenylyl cyclase activity, though antibodies against it are protective, hence the name.","E":"Lethal factor is a protease that inhibits MAP kinase and drives macrophage apoptosis, producing necrosis and shock rather than a cAMP increase."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacillus anthracis',
    'A 37-year-old goat farmer in a rural area notices an enlarging painless lesion on his forearm. Over several days it becomes a painless papule surrounded by vesicles, then ulcerates with a black necrotic eschar and surrounding nonpitting edema. He is afebrile and feels otherwise well. Gram stain of the exudate shows large gram-positive rods with central spores.',
    'Which of the following best describes this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cutaneous anthrax, which uncommonly progresses to bacteremia and death"},{"id":"B","text":"Necrotizing fasciitis from Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"id":"C","text":"Gas gangrene from Clostridium perfringens"},{"id":"D","text":"Cutaneous diphtheria with a gray pseudomembrane"},{"id":"E","text":"Ecthyma gangrenosum from Pseudomonas bacteremia"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Cutaneous anthrax begins as a painless papule surrounded by vesicles, which becomes an ulcer with a black painless necrotic eschar and local edema, typically after contact with contaminated animals or animal products. It uncommonly progresses to bacteremia and death, in contrast to pulmonary anthrax, which is often fatal. Both forms may be complicated by hemorrhagic meningitis.',
    '{"B":"Streptococcal necrotizing fasciitis produces severe pain out of proportion to findings with dusky bullous skin and systemic toxicity, quite unlike this painless localized lesion in a well patient.","C":"Gas gangrene from C perfringens presents with soft tissue crepitus, myonecrosis, and rapid systemic deterioration after contaminated wound injury.","D":"Cutaneous or wound diphtheria produces a grayish membrane over a mucosal or skin defect and is caused by club-shaped rods with metachromatic granules, not by spore-forming rods.","E":"Ecthyma gangrenosum is a rapidly progressive necrotic cutaneous lesion caused by Pseudomonas bacteremia in immunocompromised patients, and its lesion is painful with surrounding erythema rather than a painless eschar."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacillus anthracis',
    'A 30-year-old laboratory technician is exposed to a suspicious white powder during an incident involving a suspected bioterrorist release at a postal facility. She has no symptoms yet and is wearing standard protective equipment when she presents to the occupational health clinic within 6 hours of exposure.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate management for this asymptomatic exposed individual?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Oral rehydration solution and supportive care"},{"id":"B","text":"Diphtheria antitoxin and erythromycin"},{"id":"C","text":"Intrapartum penicillin prophylaxis"},{"id":"D","text":"Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline prophylaxis"},{"id":"E","text":"Rifampin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone prophylaxis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Prophylaxis against anthrax after exposure to aerosolized spores is ciprofloxacin or doxycycline. Pulmonary anthrax follows inhalation of spores, most commonly from contaminated animals or animal products but also a potential bioweapon, and presents with flulike symptoms that rapidly progress to fever, pulmonary hemorrhage, mediastinitis with a widened mediastinum, and shock.',
    '{"A":"Oral rehydration is the cornerstone of cholera management and would do nothing to prevent inhalational anthrax.","B":"Diphtheria antitoxin with erythromycin treats Corynebacterium diphtheriae exotoxin disease and has no activity against anthrax.","C":"Intrapartum penicillin prophylaxis is the group B streptococcal prevention strategy, which is unrelated to spore exposure.","E":"Rifampin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone prophylaxis is the postexposure approach for close contacts of meningococcal or Haemophilus influenzae type b disease, not anthrax."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacillus cereus',
    'Four college students develop nausea and projectile vomiting 3 hours after eating fried rice that had been cooked the previous evening, left on the counter overnight, and reheated in a microwave the next morning. None has fever or bloody diarrhea, and all recover within 12 hours. The leftover rice grows large gram-positive rods that form spores.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ingestion of live vegetative bacteria that produce enterotoxin in the colon after a 12-hour incubation"},{"id":"B","text":"Production of exfoliative toxin in the small intestine"},{"id":"C","text":"Permanent activation of Gs with profuse secretory diarrhea"},{"id":"D","text":"Ingestion of preformed heat-stable enterotoxin from spores that survived cooking"},{"id":"E","text":"Invasion of colonic mucosa with bloody dysentery"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is the emetic type of Bacillus cereus food poisoning, produced by cereulide, a preformed toxin that causes nausea and vomiting within 1 to 5 hours. Bacillus cereus spores survive cooking of rice, and keeping the rice warm allows spores to germinate and form enterotoxin, the basis of the reheated rice syndrome.',
    '{"A":"Delayed-onset disease from enterotoxin produced in the gut after ingestion of live bacteria is the diarrheal type of B cereus poisoning, with watery nonbloody diarrhea after 8 to 18 hours, and it is also the mechanism of Clostridium perfringens poisoning at 10 to 12 hours.","B":"Exfoliative toxin is a Staphylococcus aureus product that causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, a skin peeling disease rather than a food poisoning.","C":"Permanent Gs activation with profuse secretory diarrhea is the mechanism of cholera toxin, which produces rice-water stools rather than a brief vomiting illness.","E":"Invasion of colonic mucosa with bloody dysentery describes enteroinvasive E coli or Shigella, which produce fever and bloody stools rather than vomiting within hours."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacillus cereus',
    'A 25-year-old man develops watery, nonbloody diarrhea and crampy abdominal pain about 14 hours after eating a large portion of leftover pasta that had been stored at room temperature overnight and then lightly warmed. He has no fever and no vomiting. Symptoms resolve on their own within 24 hours. Stool studies are negative for blood and leukocytes.',
    'Which of the following best describes the appropriate management for this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Oral vancomycin for toxin-mediated colitis"},{"id":"B","text":"Intravenous ceftriaxone for suspected invasive disease"},{"id":"C","text":"Antitoxin administration with wound debridement"},{"id":"D","text":"Supportive care only, since antibiotics are ineffective against toxins"},{"id":"E","text":"Oral ciprofloxacin based on stool culture results"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The diarrheal type of Bacillus cereus food poisoning produces watery, nonbloody diarrhea and abdominal pain within 8 to 18 hours, and management is supportive care because antibiotics are ineffective against preformed toxins. The illness is self-limited, resolving within about a day, and the spores that survived cooking are the source.',
    '{"A":"Oral vancomycin is the treatment for Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis, which follows antibiotic use and causes colitis with systemic findings rather than brief self-limited diarrhea.","B":"Ceftriaxone would be appropriate for invasive enteric fever or severe shigellosis, and this patient has no fever, no blood in stool, and no evidence of invasive disease.","C":"Antitoxin and debridement are used for tetanus or necrotizing clostridial wound infection, not for a self-limited food-borne intoxication.","E":"Ciprofloxacin would be used for a bacterial pathogen such as Campylobacter or Salmonella; here there is no invasive organism to treat and the illness is toxin mediated."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial cell wall synthesis inhibitors and PBP targets',
    'A 55-year-old man with a prosthetic valve has rapidly progressive heart failure and a new regurgitant murmur. Blood cultures grow an organism that forms clusters, and the team begins a cell wall-active beta-lactam. The pharmacist notes that the drug must acylate enzymes embedded within the cytoplasmic membrane.',
    'Which enzymes are the direct targets of beta-lactam antibiotics in this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The 30S ribosomal subunit, where the drug arrests protein synthesis in the bacterium"},{"id":"B","text":"DNA gyrase, the enzyme the drug inhibits in order to block DNA replication in the cell"},{"id":"C","text":"Transpeptidases known as penicillin-binding proteins, embedded in the membrane"},{"id":"D","text":"Dihydrofolate reductase, which the drug blocks in order to halt bacterial folate synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"RNA polymerase, which the drug inhibits in order to prevent transcription of bacterial genes"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'The peptidoglycan wall is a sugar backbone with peptide side chains cross-linked by transpeptidase, and penicillin-binding proteins - the membrane-embedded transpeptidases and related enzymes - are the targets of beta-lactams. Acylation of these PBPs prevents cross-linking, weakening the wall until osmotic lysis occurs. The cytoplasmic membrane is a phospholipid bilayer sac with embedded proteins including PBPs and is the site of both oxidative and transport enzymes, which is why these drugs act at the membrane-wall interface. This explains why cell-wall inhibitors work best on actively dividing bacteria rather than stationary-phase cells.',
    '{"A":"The 30S ribosomal subunit is the target of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines and is not involved in cell wall assembly.","B":"DNA gyrase is inhibited by fluoroquinolones, which block DNA replication rather than wall cross-linking.","D":"Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by trimethoprim, and sulfonamides act on dihydropteroate synthase, both of which block folate synthesis.","E":"RNA polymerase is not the beta-lactam target; rifampin inhibits bacterial RNA polymerase as a separate mechanism."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial cell wall synthesis inhibitors and PBP targets',
    'A 59-year-old woman with a vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium urinary infection is treated with intravenous vancomycin without response. Susceptibility testing shows an elevated minimal inhibitory concentration, and the microbiology report notes that the drug cannot reach its usual substrate.',
    'What is the normal target of vancomycin, and why is it ineffective here?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It binds the 50S rRNA; resistance adds a methylase at that ribosomal binding site in the subunit"},{"id":"B","text":"It inhibits RNA polymerase; resistance mutates the binding pocket for the drug in that enzyme"},{"id":"C","text":"It blocks DNA gyrase; resistance expresses an efflux pump that expels the drug from the cell"},{"id":"D","text":"It binds the peptidoglycan D-Ala-D-Ala terminus; resistance replaces this precursor with D-Ala-D-lactate"},{"id":"E","text":"It inhibits dihydropteroate synthase; resistance bypasses the pathway with a new enzyme of low affinity"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Vancomycin acts on the cell wall by binding the peptidoglycan precursor terminus, and resistance arises when the target is reprogrammed to a low-affinity precursor. In the First Aid resistance diagram, vancomycin resistance is categorized as drug target modification together with penicillinase-resistant penicillins, cephalosporins acting on PBPs, rRNA methylases for macrolides and linezolid, and altered sulfonamide and fluoroquinolone targets. Transposon-mediated transfer of vanA, the classic element being Tn1546 moving from Enterococcus to S aureus, exemplifies how target-modification genes spread. The clinical takeaway is that target change, not drug destruction, defeats vancomycin.',
    '{"A":"50S rRNA methylation is a macrolide and linezolid resistance mechanism (erm genes), not the basis of vancomycin resistance in enterococci.","B":"RNA polymerase mutations are associated with resistance to agents acting on transcription such as rifampin, not vancomycin.","C":"Fluoroquinolone resistance arises through altered DNA gyrase and reduced uptake/efflux; vancomycin does not inhibit gyrase.","E":"Sulfonamide resistance commonly results from a bypass enzyme with low drug affinity; vancomycin does not target folate synthesis."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial genetics: transformation, transduction, conjugation',
    'In a teaching laboratory, a competent strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae is incubated with purified DNA extracted from a penicillin-resistant strain. After incubation, 2% of the recipient cells become resistant. When deoxyribonuclease (DNase) is added to the same mixture, no resistant colonies appear.',
    'Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer explains these results?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Specialized transduction"},{"id":"B","text":"Transformation"},{"id":"C","text":"Transposition"},{"id":"D","text":"Generalized transduction"},{"id":"E","text":"Conjugation"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Transformation is the uptake and expression of short pieces of naked environmental bacterial DNA, usually from lysed donor cells, by a competent bacterium. Because the DNA is naked, DNase degrades it and prevents transformation, which is exactly what the experiment shows. SHiN organisms - S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria - are the classic examples of naturally competent bacteria. This mechanism is one reason penicillin resistance can spread among pneumococci without any phage or plasmid transfer.',
    '{"A":"Specialized transduction requires a lysogenic phage that excises flanking bacterial genes; it is unaffected by DNase and needs a prophage.","C":"Transposition is a \"jumping\" process in which a transposon copies or excises itself and inserts elsewhere in the same or an unrelated DNA molecule; it is not uptake of environmental DNA.","D":"Generalized transduction requires a lytic phage to package bacterial DNA in capsids; adding DNase would not abolish it, and no phage is present here.","E":"Conjugation requires cell-to-cell contact and a sex pilus, and transfers plasmid DNA across a mating bridge; free DNA in the medium is not used."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial genetics: transformation, transduction, conjugation',
    'Two strains of E coli are mixed in culture: one carries an F+ plasmid with genes for the sex pilus and conjugation, the other is F-. After mating, the recipient becomes F+ and acquires the plasmid, but no chromosomal markers are transferred.',
    'Which of the following best characterizes this F+ x F- event?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A defective phage capsid packages donor chromosomal DNA"},{"id":"B","text":"Naked DNA is bound and imported by a competent recipient"},{"id":"C","text":"A transposon copies itself and inserts into a new site in the chromosome"},{"id":"D","text":"A lysogenic phage excises with flanking bacterial genes"},{"id":"E","text":"A single strand of plasmid DNA is transferred across a conjugal bridge"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'In F+ x F- conjugation, the F+ cell''s sex pilus contacts the F- cell and a single strand of the F plasmid crosses the conjugal bridge; the recipient becomes F+ but gains no chromosomal DNA. This is the simplest form of conjugation, distinct from Hfr x F-, in which the integrated F plasmid drags along flanking chromosomal genes so the recipient stays F- but may become recombinant for bacterial genes. Because plasmid transfer is efficient and plasmids often carry resistance cassettes, conjugation is a major route for spreading antibiotic resistance among gram-negative organisms.',
    '{"A":"Phage capsid packaging of host DNA describes generalized transduction, which transfers chromosomal rather than plasmid markers through a lytic phage.","B":"Uptake of naked DNA describes transformation, which is also chromosomal and does not require a mating bridge.","C":"Transposition moves a transposon within or between DNA molecules without a donor-recipient cell apparatus and does not create an F+ strain.","D":"Excision of a prophage with flanking genes is specialized transduction, which is viral and transfers chromosomal genes."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial genetics: transformation, transduction, conjugation',
    'In a hospital outbreak, a strain of E coli carrying a plasmid with ampicillin resistance is mixed in a culture with a chromosomally marked recipient. After the culture grows, the recipient acquires both the plasmid resistance gene and, rarely, chromosomal markers adjacent to an integrated F plasmid sequence. The recipient remains unable to transfer the plasmid on its own.',
    'Which process produced the rare recombinant that carries adjacent chromosomal markers?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A transposon must copy or excise itself and insert into an unrelated DNA molecule"},{"id":"B","text":"The process requires a lytic phage and a capsid packaging error involving host DNA"},{"id":"C","text":"The recipient must be competent and the donor DNA must be free in the surrounding medium"},{"id":"D","text":"A lysogenic phage must integrate as a prophage and later excise with flanking genes"},{"id":"E","text":"Naked DNA is bound and imported by a naturally competent recipient bacterium"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'When the F plasmid becomes incorporated into the bacterial chromosome the cell is called a high-frequency recombination (Hfr) cell. During Hfr x F- mating the leading portion of the plasmid is transferred along with a few flanking chromosomal genes, so the recipient may recombine those bacterial genes while remaining F-. This is a legitimate conduit for chromosomal gene spread and reflects why the F plasmid is described as a fertility factor. The clue is the linkage of the integrated plasmid to the transferred chromosomal markers.',
    '{"A":"Lysogenic conversion is the expression of phage-encoded genes (e.g., toxin genes) by a lysogenized bacterium; no chromosomal marker transfer occurs.","B":"Generalized transduction uses a lytic phage with a packaging error and produces random chromosomal transfer, not transfer of genes flanking an integrated plasmid.","C":"Transformation takes up naked DNA from the environment and does not depend on an integrated F plasmid or a mating bridge.","D":"Specialized transduction requires a lysogenic phage and transfers genes at the prophage integration site, typically toxin genes."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial genetics: transformation, transduction, conjugation',
    'A genetics instructor is contrasting bacterial gene transfer methods. She asks students to match each mechanism with its key molecular requirement, emphasizing that one method transfers bacterial DNA through a phage capsid after a packaging error.',
    'Which mode of gene transfer is described as a "packaging error" of a lytic phage?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Generalized transduction"},{"id":"B","text":"Conjugation"},{"id":"C","text":"Transposition"},{"id":"D","text":"Specialized transduction"},{"id":"E","text":"Transformation"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'In generalized transduction a lytic phage infects a bacterium, cleaves the bacterial DNA, and by packaging error some pieces of host chromosomal DNA are enclosed in phage capsids. Those phages then infect another bacterium and deliver the bacterial genes. Any bacterial gene can in principle be transferred this way, in contrast to specialized transduction where only genes adjacent to the prophage integration site are carried. Distinguishing "packaging error" (generalized) from "excision event" (specialized) is the classic exam hook.',
    '{"B":"Conjugation requires contact through a sex pilus and transfers plasmid DNA; no capsid is involved.","C":"Transposition is a jumping process that moves a transposon between DNA molecules without a phage.","D":"Specialized transduction is an excision event in which a lysogenic phage carries flanking bacterial genes; it is not a packaging error but a site-specific error of excision.","E":"Transformation is uptake of naked DNA by competent cells and involves no phage."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial growth phases and growth requirements',
    'A microbiology laboratory incubates a broth culture of E coli at 37 degrees C with continuous agitation and measures colony-forming units hourly. During the first few hours the counts rise, then the rate of rise slows as nutrients become limiting, then counts plateau while total cell mass remains constant.',
    'In the plateau phase described, which combination best explains the stable viable count?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cells have lost their cell wall and converted into stable cell-wall-deficient L-forms in the broth"},{"id":"B","text":"All the cells have entered lysogeny and stopped translating protein from messenger RNA"},{"id":"C","text":"Cells have lost their cytoplasmic membrane and become obligate intracellular organisms"},{"id":"D","text":"Spore formation has converted nearly every cell into a dormant, heat-resistant form in the culture"},{"id":"E","text":"Cell division and cell death are balanced, with growth limited by nutrient depletion and waste"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Bacterial batch growth proceeds through lag, exponential (log), stationary, and death phases. In stationary phase, the rate of cell division equals the rate of cell death, so viable counts are stable because nutrients are depleted and inhibitory waste products accumulate. This matters clinically: cell wall-active antibiotics such as beta-lactams work on dividing cells, which is why organisms in stationary phase or in biofilms respond poorly. Some gram-positive genera respond to nutrient limitation by forming spores, a survival strategy rather than continued replication.',
    '{"A":"L-form conversion to cell-wall-deficient states is not the normal response to nutrient limitation; wall synthesis simply slows.","B":"Lysogeny means a temperate phage is integrated, not that translation stops; it is unrelated to the plateau in plate counts.","C":"Loss of a cytoplasmic membrane is not a viable state, and obligate intracellular organisms such as Rickettsia, Chlamydia, and Coxiella rely on host ATP.","D":"Spores form in some gram-positive genera such as Bacillus and Clostridium when nutrients are limited, but not all E coli cells sporulate, and spores retain considerable viability rather than disappearing."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial growth phases and growth requirements',
    'A 30-year-old man with a wound from a deep farm injury is suspected to have Clostridium perfringens infection. The laboratory attempts culture and notes that the organisms require anaerobic conditions; in the presence of oxygen the culture fails to grow.',
    'Which growth feature best explains why anaerobes such as this organism are killed in oxygen-rich environments?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They require oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor in order to generate ATP aerobically"},{"id":"B","text":"They lack catalase and/or superoxide dismutase, so they are susceptible to oxidative damage"},{"id":"C","text":"They lack ribosomes and depend entirely on host cell ATP for protein synthesis"},{"id":"D","text":"They grow only at temperatures above 121 degrees C under high-pressure saturated steam"},{"id":"E","text":"They produce catalase but no superoxide dismutase, so peroxide accumulates within them"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Anaerobes such as Clostridioides, Clostridium, Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, and Actinomyces israelii lack catalase and/or superoxide dismutase, so they cannot detoxify the reactive oxygen intermediates produced in aerobic conditions, and they are thus susceptible to oxidative damage (mnemonic: Anaerobes Can''t Breathe Fresh Air). They are normal microbiota in the GI tract and are typically pathogenic elsewhere, for example in aspiration pneumonia. Two practical consequences follow: culture requires anaerobic techniques, and aminoglycosides are ineffective against them because these drugs require oxygen-dependent uptake to enter the bacterial cell. Their cultures are often foul smelling from short-chain fatty acids and produce gas in tissue.',
    '{"A":"Using oxygen as a terminal electron acceptor describes facultative (and obligate aerobic) organisms such as streptococci, staphylococci, and enteric gram-negatives, and it would not explain anaerobe death in oxygen.","C":"Lacking ribosomes is not the reason: obligate intracellular organisms such as Rickettsia, Chlamydia, and Coxiella rely on host ATP but still possess ribosomes.","D":"Growth above 121 degrees C is a property of extreme thermophiles, not clinically relevant anaerobes, which grow at body temperature.","E":"The statement is inverted: anaerobes lack catalase and/or superoxide dismutase, and catalase-positive organisms are the ones that survive oxidative stress."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial structures and function',
    'A 62-year-old man with a chronic indwelling urinary catheter develops fever and cloudy urine. Urine culture grows Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The catheter surface is covered with an adherent multilayered community of organisms embedded in a loose network of polysaccharides.',
    'Which bacterial structure most directly accounts for the adherent, device-associated community in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Spore coat"},{"id":"B","text":"Sex pilus"},{"id":"C","text":"Slime (S) layer"},{"id":"D","text":"Lipoteichoic acid"},{"id":"E","text":"Periplasmic space"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'The slime (S) layer is a loose network of polysaccharides that mediates adherence to surfaces and plays a key role in biofilm formation on indwelling catheters and prosthetic devices. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the classic respiratory-tract biofilm former in cystic fibrosis and ventilator-associated pneumonia, but the principle of a polysaccharide matrix on foreign material is general. Capsule and slime are both polysaccharide envelope layers, yet only the slime layer is described as loose and adherence/biomfilm oriented, while the capsule is a discrete antiphagocytic layer. The looser matrix reflects the glycan-rich extracellular material that makes device infections so hard to eradicate.',
    '{"A":"A spore coat is the keratinlike, dipicolinic-acid-containing covering that makes Bacillus and Clostridium spores resistant to heat and chemicals; it is a dormant survival form, not biofilm matrix.","B":"A sex pilus is the specialized appendage that forms during conjugation, drawing F+ and F- cells together so a single strand of plasmid DNA crosses the mating bridge; it is a mating structure, not a component of a surface biofilm.","D":"Lipoteichoic acid is a gram-positive surface molecule that induces TNF-alpha and IL-1 and has no role in forming a polysaccharide matrix on plastic; Pseudomonas is gram-negative and lacks it entirely.","E":"The periplasm is the space between the cytoplasmic and outer membranes in gram-negative organisms where peptidoglycan sits and beta-lactamases accumulate; it is a compartment, not an adherent extracellular material."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial structures and function',
    'A medical student is reviewing the bacterial structures table. She is asked about the role of the sex pilus, a structure distinct from the ordinary fimbriae used for surface adherence.',
    'What is the principal function of the sex pilus in bacteria?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"To anchor lipopolysaccharide in the outer membrane"},{"id":"B","text":"To provide motility and allow directional movement in viscous environments"},{"id":"C","text":"To resist osmotic pressure and dehydration"},{"id":"D","text":"To recognize and import naked DNA from the environment"},{"id":"E","text":"To form the conjugal bridge through which plasmid DNA is transferred"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Pili or fimbriae are glycoprotein appendages that mediate adherence of bacteria to host cell surfaces, and a specialized sex pilus forms during conjugation. In F+ x F- conjugation, the F+ bacterium''s sex pilus contacts the F- recipient and a single strand of plasmid DNA passes across the conjugal (mating) bridge; no chromosomal DNA is transferred in this simple F+ x F- event. The same pilus therefore serves as the physical conduit for horizontal gene transfer of plasmids, which is the classic trick that spreads resistance genes. The distinction from ordinary fimbriae is functional: adherence pili bind host glycoproteins, whereas the sex pilus mediates cell-to-cell contact for mating.',
    '{"A":"Lipid A anchors LPS in the outer leaflet of the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, and this membrane assembly does not use pili.","B":"The flagellum, not the sex pilus, is the organelle of motility; it is a protein appendage whose rotation propels the cell.","C":"Rigid support against osmotic pressure is provided by the peptidoglycan cell wall, whose sugar backbone is cross-linked by transpeptidase; bacterial spores and thick coats handle dehydration.","D":"Recognition and import of naked environmental DNA is the defining step of transformation, a feature of competent organisms such as S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial structures and function',
    'A 4-year-old boy has a sore throat, fever, and a swollen tonsil with a whitish exudate. A rapid antigen test identifies group A streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes). The physician explains that many of this organism''s surface components are antigenic and immunologically active.',
    'Which envelope component of this organism is a surface molecule that induces TNF-alpha and IL-1 directly, and is found only in gram-positive organisms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Porin trimers"},{"id":"B","text":"Lipoteichoic acid"},{"id":"C","text":"The O polysaccharide of LPS"},{"id":"D","text":"Peptidoglycan cross-linked by transpeptidase"},{"id":"E","text":"Lipid A of the outer membrane"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Lipoteichoic acids are found only in gram-positive organisms and extend from the cytoplasmic membrane all the way to the exterior of the cell. They induce TNF-alpha and IL-1, contributing to the inflammatory response and to the toxic-shock-like syndromes seen with gram-positive infections (for example the S aureus and S pyogenes superantigen illnesses). The underlying architecture is that gram-positive cells have a single, thick peptidoglycan wall with teichoic and lipoteichoic acids threaded through it, with no outer membrane. Because these acids are proinflammatory rather than protective, they are distinct from the antiphagocytic capsule that also decorates some of these organisms.',
    '{"A":"Porins are outer membrane proteins of gram-negative bacteria that transport small molecules across the outer membrane and are antigenic; they are absent in gram-positive organisms.","C":"The O polysaccharide is the antigenic component of gram-negative LPS and determines serotype; gram-positive organisms have no outer membrane.","D":"Peptidoglycan is present in both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms and is the rigid support layer; its recognition by host pattern receptors is not the specific gram-positive-only molecule described.","E":"Lipid A is the toxic component of LPS and is found in gram-negative organisms only; it induces TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 but is not a gram-positive surface molecule."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial structures and function',
    'A 58-year-old patient with cirrhosis and recurrent gram-negative bacteremia receives intravenous antibiotics. One morning he is hypotensive, feverish, with acrocyanosis. The team notes that several hours of intravenous antibiotic therapy have already been given, and the shock is attributed to components released from the dying organisms.',
    'Which structural component of these bacteria is most directly responsible for the fever, hypotension, and DIC?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide"},{"id":"B","text":"Peptidoglycan peptide side chains"},{"id":"C","text":"The slime layer"},{"id":"D","text":"Lipoteichoic acid"},{"id":"E","text":"The spore coat"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Lipopolysaccharide consists of an O antigen plus a core polysaccharide plus lipid A, and lipid A is the toxic fragment that signals through TLR4/CD14. The consequence is macrophage activation with release of TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 (fever, hypotension), activation of complement (C3a and C5a with histamine release, edema), and activation of tissue factor with DIC. Endotoxin is extremely heat stable, is not secreted but released when cells are lysed or from blebs on living cells, and it is poorly antigenic so no toxoid or vaccine exists. Neisseria species make lipooligosaccharide (LOS) instead of classical LPS, but the lipid A still drives the same response.',
    '{"B":"Peptidoglycan provides rigid support against osmotic pressure; although fragments are immunostimulatory, the fever-hypotension-DIC triad is the classic endotoxin (lipid A) effect.","C":"The slime layer is a loose polysaccharide network for adherence and biofilm formation, with no role in the cytokinone-driven shock physiology described.","D":"Lipoteichoic acid does induce TNF-alpha and IL-1 but is a gram-positive molecule; the organisms here are gram-negative, whose shock is LPS-mediated.","E":"A spore coat is the keratinlike, heat-resistant outer layer of bacterial spores formed by Bacillus and Clostridium; it neither causes nor protects against endotoxin shock."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial virulence factors: capsules, pili, biofilms',
    'A 5-year-old boy with sickle cell disease and functional asplenia presents with fever and a stiff neck. Cerebrospinal fluid Gram stain shows gram-positive diplococci, and the organism is capsulated. The team emphasizes the need for vaccines against encapsulated organisms.',
    'Why is the capsule a major determinant of virulence for these organisms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Capsule-mediated antiphagocytosis, which prevents clearance by neutrophils and macrophages"},{"id":"B","text":"Superantigen production that cross-links the TCR and MHC class II molecules of host cells"},{"id":"C","text":"A type III secretion system that injects toxins directly into the cytoplasm of host cells"},{"id":"D","text":"Biofilm formation by adherent organisms on the surface of the prosthetic device"},{"id":"E","text":"IgA protease secretion that cleaves secreted antibody at mucosal surfaces of the host"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The capsule is a discrete layer of polysaccharides (rarely proteins) and is a major determinant of virulence: being highly charged and hydrophilic, it acts as a barrier to phagocytosis and complement-mediated lysis. Encapsulated organisms include Pseudomonas aeruginosa, S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, N meningitidis, E coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and group B streptococci (Please SHiNE my SKiS). They are opsonized and then cleared by the spleen, so asplenic patients (No Spleen Here) have reduced opsonizing ability and increased risk of severe infection, requiring vaccines against N meningitidis, S pneumoniae, and H influenzae.',
    '{"B":"Sequence homology with human cardiac myosin is a feature of M protein of group A streptococci, underlying molecular mimicry in rheumatic fever.","C":"Fibronectin cleavage is not a property described for these capsules; the capsule acts as a physical and electrostatic shield.","D":"Binding the Fc region of IgG is Protein A, expressed by S aureus, not a capsule.","E":"Cleaving secretory IgA is the IgA protease activity of S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria (mnemonic SHiN) - notable here as a separate mechanism."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial virulence factors: capsules, pili, biofilms',
    'A 21-year-old woman with a central venous catheter for home parenteral nutrition has recurrent bloodstream infections with Staphylococcus epidermidis. Cultures are repeatedly positive, and the catheter must eventually be removed. Workup shows the organism grows as a thick adherent layer on the catheter surface.',
    'Which virulence process is most important in this catheter-associated infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"IgA protease secretion"},{"id":"B","text":"Biofilm formation on the prosthetic surface"},{"id":"C","text":"Superantigen production"},{"id":"D","text":"Type III secretion system injection of toxins"},{"id":"E","text":"Capsule-mediated antiphagocytosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'S epidermidis is the classic in vivo biofilm producer for catheter and prosthetic device infections, while viridans streptococci form dental plaques and cause infective endocarditis, Pseudomonas colonizes the respiratory tree in cystic fibrosis, nontypeable H influenzae causes otitis media, and Pseudomonas causes contact lens-associated keratitis. A biofilm is a community embedded in a polysaccharide matrix (contributed by the slime layer) that resists antibiotics and host clearance, which is why infected prosthetics usually require removal. Recognizing the device-organism pairing is as important as knowing the mechanism.',
    '{"A":"IgA protease is produced by S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria (SHiN) to colonize mucosa; it does not explain persistence on a plastic catheter.","C":"Superantigens such as TSST-1 and erythrogenic exotoxin A cause fever, rash, and shock by cross-linking TCR and MHC class II; this patient has no inflammatory shock syndrome.","D":"Toxin injection systems are not the described mechanism for coagulase-negative staphylococcal device infection; adhesion and matrix production are.","E":"Some organisms do use thick capsules to resist phagocytosis, but a smooth adherent layer on a device reflects the biofilm phenotype rather than capsule-driven immune evasion."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial virulence factors: capsules, pili, biofilms',
    'A 67-year-old man with a prosthetic heart valve and poor dentition develops fever and a new murmur. Blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic streptococci, later identified as S mutans, and echocardiography shows a vegetation.',
    'Which virulence mechanism most directly initiates colonization of the dental surface and damaged endocardium in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"M protein that extends from the cell wall and resists phagocytosis by host neutrophils"},{"id":"B","text":"Pili (fimbriae) that extend from the bacterial surface and mediate adherence to host cells"},{"id":"C","text":"Capsular polysaccharide that coats the outer surface of the organism and blocks phagocytosis"},{"id":"D","text":"Protein A that binds the Fc region of IgG on the surface of the bacterial cell"},{"id":"E","text":"Lipid A that anchors lipopolysaccharide in the outer leaflet of the outer membrane"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Viridans streptococci (S mutans, S sanguinis) are classic in vivo biofilm producers, causing dental plaques and infective endocarditis. The biofilm on the tooth seeds bacteremia during dental manipulation, and the organisms then adhere to damaged or prosthetic endocardium. First Aid''s biofilm table pairs S epidermidis with catheters and prosthetic devices, viridans streptococci with dental plaque and endocarditis, Pseudomonas with cystic fibrosis and ventilator-associated pneumonia, nontypeable H influenzae with otitis media, and Pseudomonas again with contact lens keratitis. These pairings are the high-yield test targets.',
    '{"A":"M protein is a group A streptococcal virulence factor with molecular mimicry to cardiac myosin; these are viridans streptococci, not group A streptococci.","B":"Protein A is expressed by S aureus and binds the Fc region of IgG to prevent opsonization and phagocytosis, which is not the mechanism here.","D":"A polypeptide capsule is characteristic of Bacillus anthracis; the viridans streptococci are not described this way.","E":"Lipid A drives endotoxin responses in gram-negative sepsis and plays no part in gram-positive dental plaque or vegetation formation."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Bacterial virulence factors: capsules, pili, biofilms',
    'A 4-year-old child has recurrent ear infections. The organism isolated is nontypeable, unencapsulated Haemophilus influenzae. The pediatrician explains that the adherence of this organism to respiratory mucosa depends on a surface structure that determines its tissue tropism.',
    'Which virulence factor is most directly responsible for adherence of bacteria to the respiratory mucosa?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lipid A"},{"id":"B","text":"Capsular polysaccharide"},{"id":"C","text":"M protein"},{"id":"D","text":"Pili (fimbriae)"},{"id":"E","text":"Protein A"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Pili (fimbriae) are glycoprotein appendages that mediate adherence of bacteria to cell surfaces, which is the first mechanistic step in colonization and tissue tropism. Nontypeable, unencapsulated H influenzae is the classic cause of otitis media in young children, illustrating that adherence can be more important than encapsulation for mucosal disease. The sex pilus is a specialized version of this structure dedicated to conjugation rather than host adherence. IgA protease, which cleaves secretory IgA, is a complementary mucosal virulence factor secreted by S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria.',
    '{"A":"Lipid A activates TLR4 and drives fever, shock, and DIC in gram-negative sepsis; it does not mediate attachment.","B":"The capsule protects against phagocytosis and complement lysis; notably, this organism is unencapsulated, and the encapsulated type b capsule is antigenic rather than the adherence factor.","C":"M protein helps group A streptococci resist phagocytosis and mimics cardiac myosin; it is not the general mucosal adhesin here.","E":"Protein A binds the Fc region of IgG and is expressed by S aureus; it is an immune-evasion factor, not an adhesin for respiratory epithelium."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacteroides fragilis',
    'A 61-year-old man undergoes emergency surgery for a perforated sigmoid colon. Four days postoperatively he develops fever, tachycardia, and hypotension. Abdominal CT shows a fluid collection with a thick rim in the pelvis. Anaerobic culture of the aspirated fluid grows a gram-negative rod that does not grow in room air.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible, and what is the correct characterization of this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa, an aerobic oxidase-positive gram-negative rod"},{"id":"B","text":"Actinomyces israelii, an anaerobic gram-positive branching filamentous rod"},{"id":"C","text":"Bacteroides fragilis, an anaerobic gram-negative rod, a common cause of intra-abdominal abscesses"},{"id":"D","text":"Escherichia coli, a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod that is not anaerobic"},{"id":"E","text":"Clostridium difficile, a gram-positive anaerobic spore-forming rod"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Bacteroides fragilis is an anaerobic gram-negative rod and a normal component of colonic microbiota, so it seeds infection when the bowel is breached by perforation, producing intra-abdominal abscesses. It is the classic anaerobe implicated in abscesses arising from colonic sources. Perforation of the colon releases a polymicrobial flora in which anaerobes such as Bacteroides are prominent.',
    '{"A":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a strict aerobe, oxidase positive, often nosocomial and linked to burns, catheters, and cystic fibrosis.","B":"Actinomyces is gram positive with branching filaments and typically causes oral and facial abscesses with sulfur granules, not this colonic-source anaerobic abscess.","D":"Escherichia coli is a lactose-fermenting facultative anaerobe usually associated with UTI, neonatal meningitis, and diarrhea syndromes rather than a strictly anaerobic isolate.","E":"Clostridium difficile is gram positive and spore-forming, and causes antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis rather than a postoperative peritoneal abscess."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bacteroides fragilis',
    'A 55-year-old man with diverticulitis develops a pelvic abscess requiring drainage. Gram stain of the pus shows a mixed population of organisms, and the anaerobic culture grows Bacteroides fragilis in addition to coliforms. The surgical team is choosing empiric antibiotic coverage while awaiting final susceptibilities.',
    'Which of the following best explains why anaerobic coverage is essential in this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Bacteroides fragilis is a spore-forming organism that survives routine disinfection of tissue"},{"id":"B","text":"Bacteroides fragilis grows only in the presence of 6.5% sodium chloride"},{"id":"C","text":"Bacteroides fragilis requires a urea-containing medium to grow"},{"id":"D","text":"Bacteroides fragilis is a strict anaerobe from colonic microbiota, so aerobic-only regimens may miss it in abscesses"},{"id":"E","text":"Bacteroides fragilis is a facultative anaerobe that rapidly develops beta-lactamase resistance to all drugs"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Bacteroides fragilis is an obligate anaerobe that is part of normal colonic microbiota, and it flourishes in the low-oxygen environment of an abscess. Because it will not be recovered on routine aerobic culture, empiric coverage must include anaerobic activity when a colonic-source abscess is suspected. The mixed growth described is typical of the polymicrobial flora released by bowel perforation.',
    '{"A":"The key property here is anaerobic growth, and Bacteroides is a non-spore-forming rod.","B":"Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride is a test that distinguishes enterococci, not Bacteroides.","C":"Urease production is characteristic of Proteus mirabilis, which promotes struvite stone formation, and is not a growth requirement of Bacteroides.","E":"Bacteroides fragilis is a strict anaerobe rather than a facultative one, and the issue is lack of anaerobic coverage rather than universal resistance."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Blastomyces dermatitidis',
    'A 52-year-old landscaper from Wisconsin presents with chronic cough, low-grade fever, and a slowly enlarging verrucous plaque on his forearm. He was initially told the skin lesion might be a squamous cell carcinoma. Biopsy of the lesion shows yeast forms surrounded by granulomatous inflammation.',
    'Which of the following microscopic findings would most likely be seen in the biopsy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Narrow-based budding yeasts with a thick capsule"},{"id":"B","text":"Small yeasts within macrophages, smaller than a red blood cell"},{"id":"C","text":"Spherules containing endospores much larger than a red blood cell"},{"id":"D","text":"Broad-based budding yeasts roughly the size of a red blood cell"},{"id":"E","text":"Irregular nonseptate hyphae branching at wide angles"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Blastomyces dermatitidis is a dimorphic fungus endemic to the eastern and central United States and the Great Lakes region. It shows broad-based budding yeasts approximately the size of a red blood cell: blasto buds broadly. Infection begins as inflammatory lung disease and classically disseminates to bone and skin, producing verrucous lesions that may be mistaken for squamous cell carcinoma. Itraconazole can be used for local disease, and amphotericin B is used for systemic infection.',
    '{"A":"Narrow-based budding with a prominent capsule is Cryptococcus neoformans, acquired from pigeon droppings and typically causing meningoencephalitis in immunocompromised patients.","B":"Intracellular yeasts smaller than a red blood cell are Histoplasma capsulatum, which hides inside macrophages and is linked to bird and bat droppings.","C":"Spherules filled with endospores are Coccidioides immitis, which is endemic to the southwestern United States and associated with dust exposure.","E":"Broad nonseptate hyphae branching at wide angles describe Mucor and Rhizopus, which produce rhinocerebral disease with black necrotic eschars rather than verrucous skin plaques."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Blastomyces dermatitidis',
    'A 61-year-old man from the Great Lakes region is diagnosed with pulmonary blastomycosis. He has mild disease confined to the lungs and no evidence of dissemination. He asks about treatment and specifically wants to avoid intravenous therapy.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for his localized disease?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Flucytosine monotherapy"},{"id":"B","text":"Amphotericin B"},{"id":"C","text":"Itraconazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Caspofungin"},{"id":"E","text":"Griseofulvin"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'For systemic mycoses such as blastomycosis, fluconazole or itraconazole is used for local infection, while amphotericin B is reserved for systemic infection. Itraconazole is specifically listed for Blastomyces, Coccidioides, Histoplasma, and Sporothrix. Because this patient has disease confined to the lungs, an oral azole is appropriate and avoids the nephrotoxicity of amphotericin. Azoles work by inhibiting the cytochrome P-450 enzyme that converts lanosterol to ergosterol.',
    '{"A":"Flucytosine is used in combination with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis and is not used as monotherapy for blastomycosis.","B":"Amphotericin B is the right choice for systemic or severe disease, but it requires intravenous administration and carries fever, chills, and nephrotoxicity that are unnecessary here.","D":"Caspofungin is an echinocandin whose main indication is invasive aspergillosis and Candida infections; it is not standard therapy for blastomycosis.","E":"Griseofulvin only treats superficial dermatophyte infections such as tinea and has no role in a systemic dimorphic mycosis."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bordetella pertussis',
    'A 6-week-old infant is brought in by his mother with 1 week of increasingly severe coughing spells that occur in paroxysms and end with a high-pitched inspiratory whoop and vomiting. He has had no fever. Physical examination shows subconjunctival hemorrhage, and the complete blood count shows a marked lymphocytosis with a normal total white count differential otherwise.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors of this organism disables the inhibitory Gi protein?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pertussis toxin"},{"id":"B","text":"Tracheal cytotoxin"},{"id":"C","text":"Adenylate cyclase toxin"},{"id":"D","text":"Endotoxin"},{"id":"E","text":"Pertactin"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This infant has whooping cough in the paroxysmal stage, with paroxysms of intense cough followed by an inspiratory whoop and posttussive vomiting. Bordetella pertussis virulence factors include pertussis toxin, which disables Gi, adenylate cyclase toxin, which raises cAMP, and tracheal cytotoxin, which destroys respiratory ciliated epithelium. The organism also produces marked lymphocytosis, unlike most acute bacterial infections.',
    '{"B":"Tracheal cytotoxin destroys the ciliated respiratory epithelium, explaining the cough, but it does not act on Gi proteins.","C":"Adenylate cyclase toxin raises cAMP by acting as an adenylate cyclase; it is a different toxin from pertussis toxin.","D":"Endotoxin is the lipopolysaccharide component of gram-negative outer membranes, causing fever and shock, and is not the toxin that disables Gi.","E":"Pertactin is a surface adhesin used in acellular vaccines that helps the organism bind respiratory epithelium, not a Gi-inactivating enzyme."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bordetella pertussis',
    'A 14-year-old girl presents with 2 weeks of intermittent coughing fits that occur several times an hour, especially at night, and sometimes end in vomiting. She was vaccinated as a young child. She has had no fever and her lungs are clear on examination. A nasopharyngeal swab is positive for Bordetella pertussis by PCR.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"id":"B","text":"A macrolide such as azithromycin"},{"id":"C","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"id":"D","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"E","text":"Doxycycline"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Bordetella pertussis is treated with macrolides, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is used if the patient is allergic to macrolides. Treatment eradicates the organism and reduces transmission, although the paroxysmal cough may persist for weeks. Disease is prevented by the Tdap vaccine for pre-teens and adults and DTaP for children.',
    '{"A":"Ceftriaxone is used for meningitis, gonorrhea, and severe pneumonia, not as therapy for pertussis.","C":"Amoxicillin is a beta-lactam used for mucosal Haemophilus infections and is not the recommended therapy for pertussis.","D":"TMP-SMX is the alternative reserved for patients with macrolide allergy, not the first choice.","E":"Doxycycline is used for atypical organisms and tick-borne disease, and is not the standard treatment for pertussis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Bordetella pertussis',
    'A 3-month-old infant has not yet received her routine immunizations. She presents with 10 days of worsening cough that now occurs in paroxysms, followed by a whoop and post-tussive emesis. Two weeks earlier she had only nasal congestion and low-grade fever. The pediatrician discusses the three clinical stages of the illness and how to prevent disease in her older siblings.',
    'Which of the following best describes the sequence of clinical stages of whooping cough?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A single continuous stage of cough without distinct phases"},{"id":"B","text":"Immediate paroxysmal cough after a 2-day incubation without a catarrhal prodrome"},{"id":"C","text":"Catarrhal stage with low-grade fever and coryza, then paroxysmal stage with whooping cough, then convalescent stage"},{"id":"D","text":"Paroxysmal stage with coryza, then catarrhal stage with whoops, then a permanent carrier state"},{"id":"E","text":"Convalescent stage with coryza, then catarrhal stage with whoops, then a paroxysmal stage"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Bordetella pertussis causes three clinical stages: catarrhal with low-grade fevers and coryza, paroxysmal with paroxysms of intense cough followed by an inspiratory whoop and posttussive vomiting, and convalescent with gradual recovery of a chronic cough. Prevention is with Tdap for pre-teens and adults and DTaP for children. Close contacts are managed with prophylaxis.',
    '{"A":"The illness classically has three distinct stages, which is why the stage at presentation guides diagnosis and infection control.","B":"There is a catarrhal prodrome that typically lasts 1 to 2 weeks before the paroxysmal stage begins.","D":"The order is reversed; coryza belongs to the catarrhal stage, and whooping cough defines the paroxysmal stage that follows it.","E":"This inverts the entire sequence; convalescence is the final phase of gradual recovery."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Borrelia burgdorferi',
    'A 30-year-old man from Connecticut presents in July with 5 days of fever, myalgias, and fatigue. He noticed a slowly expanding red annular rash on his thigh that began 2 weeks after hiking in the woods. He recalls removing a small tick. Examination shows a 12-cm erythematous lesion with a central clearing at the site.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"id":"B","text":"Azithromycin"},{"id":"C","text":"Penicillin G intramuscularly"},{"id":"D","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"E","text":"Ceftriaxone intravenously"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is stage 1 early localized Lyme disease with erythema migrans, the pathognomonic bull''s-eye lesion, transmitted by the Ixodes deer tick. Doxycycline is first-line therapy. Amoxicillin is the alternative for pregnant patients and children younger than 8 years, and ceftriaxone is reserved for cases requiring intravenous therapy.',
    '{"A":"Amoxicillin is the appropriate alternative for pregnant patients and children under 8, not the first-line choice for this adult.","B":"Azithromycin is not the recommended agent for Lyme disease; doxycycline is preferred.","C":"Intramuscular penicillin G is the treatment for syphilis, not Lyme disease.","E":"Intravenous ceftriaxone is used only when intravenous therapy is required, such as severe cardiac, neurologic, or refractory disease, not for early localized erythema migrans."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Borrelia burgdorferi',
    'A 45-year-old woman from Massachusetts was treated for erythema migrans 4 weeks ago. She now returns with new multiple smaller annular skin lesions on her trunk, intermittent palpitations, and drooping of the left side of her face. An ECG shows a first-degree atrioventricular block.',
    'Which of the following best describes this stage of disease and its manifestations?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Erythema migrans recurrence due to reinfection from a new tick bite"},{"id":"B","text":"Post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome with persistent subjective symptoms only"},{"id":"C","text":"Early disseminated Lyme disease with secondary lesions, carditis with AV block, and facial nerve palsy"},{"id":"D","text":"Late disseminated Lyme disease with encephalopathy and chronic arthritis"},{"id":"E","text":"A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction from treatment of spirochetal infection"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Stage 2 early disseminated Lyme disease occurs days to weeks after the initial infection and is characterized by secondary skin lesions, carditis with AV block, facial nerve (Bell) palsy, and migratory myalgias or transient arthritis. The mnemonic for the classic findings is facial nerve palsy, arthritis, cardiac block, and erythema migrans. Patients at this stage may require intravenous therapy such as ceftriaxone.',
    '{"A":"Reinfection would typically present with a new erythema migrans at a tick bite site rather than multiple secondary lesions plus carditis and facial palsy.","B":"Post-treatment syndrome is a diagnosis of persistent subjective symptoms without objective findings such as AV block or cranial nerve palsy.","D":"Late disseminated disease presents months to years later with encephalopathy, chronic arthritis, and peripheral neuropathy; this patient''s findings are acute and disseminated early.","E":"A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is a transient flulike response within hours of starting antibiotics, not a new rash with AV block weeks later."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Borrelia burgdorferi',
    'A 33-year-old landscaper from a northeastern state develops fever, chills, headache, and myalgias within 12 hours of his first dose of doxycycline for newly diagnosed Lyme disease. He has no new rash and no focal neurologic findings. His symptoms resolve within a day without further intervention.',
    'Which of the following best explains this reaction?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Development of drug-induced lupus from the tetracycline"},{"id":"B","text":"Progression to early disseminated Lyme disease with new spirochetemia"},{"id":"C","text":"Host inflammatory response to the sudden release of bacterial antigens after antibiotics are started"},{"id":"D","text":"An IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction to doxycycline"},{"id":"E","text":"A direct endotoxin-mediated cytokine storm from gram-negative bacteremia"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is a flulike illness with fever, chills, headache, and myalgia that occurs shortly after antibiotics are started, caused by the host response to the sudden release of bacterial antigens. It classically occurs during treatment of spirochetal infections such as Lyme disease and syphilis. It is self-limited and managed supportively.',
    '{"A":"Drug-induced lupus develops over weeks to months with autoantibodies, not within hours of the first dose.","B":"Disseminated disease develops days to weeks after infection, whereas this reaction begins within hours of starting antibiotics and resolves spontaneously.","D":"Anaphylaxis would present with urticaria, angioedema, wheezing, and hypotension rather than a self-limited flulike illness.","E":"Borrelia burgdorferi is a spirochete, not a gram-negative bacillus with classic endotoxin-mediated shock, and this presentation is the well-described treatment-associated reaction."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Campylobacter jejuni',
    'A 5-year-old boy develops 3 days of fever, crampy abdominal pain, and bloody diarrhea after a family picnic where undercooked chicken was served. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative organism with polar flagella that is oxidase positive and grows at 42°C. Three weeks after recovery he develops progressive symmetric ascending weakness with areflexia.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely explanation for his new neurologic findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Antibodies blocking postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors"},{"id":"B","text":"Direct invasion of anterior horn cells by the organism"},{"id":"C","text":"Toxin-mediated inhibition of acetylcholine release at motor terminals"},{"id":"D","text":"Immune complex deposition in peripheral nerve endothelium"},{"id":"E","text":"Molecular mimicry after infection, causing Guillain-Barré syndrome"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Campylobacter jejuni is a common antecedent to Guillain-Barré syndrome, an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy with ascending symmetric weakness and areflexia that follows infection by molecular mimicry. It is also a common antecedent to reactive arthritis. The organism is comma or S shaped with polar flagella, oxidase positive, and grows at 42°C, as reflected in the saying that Campylobacter likes the hot campfire.',
    '{"A":"Antibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis, with fatigable weakness that typically starts in the ocular muscles and no sensory involvement.","B":"Anterior horn cell invasion causes poliomyelitis, with asymmetric flaccid paralysis during or soon after febrile illness, not a delayed symmetric ascending neuropathy.","C":"Inhibition of acetylcholine release at motor terminals is the mechanism of botulinum toxin, which produces descending flaccid paralysis with prominent bulbar and ocular findings.","D":"Immune complex deposition in small vessels drives post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, not the demyelinating polyneuropathy described here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Campylobacter jejuni',
    'A 27-year-old man who works at a poultry processing plant presents with 4 days of fever, crampy periumbilical pain, and watery diarrhea that has become bloody. He drinks unpasteurized milk at work and handles raw chicken daily. The laboratory reports an organism that requires an elevated incubation temperature and a microaerophilic atmosphere to grow.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Salmonella typhi"},{"id":"B","text":"Vibrio cholerae"},{"id":"C","text":"Shigella dysenteriae"},{"id":"D","text":"Helicobacter pylori"},{"id":"E","text":"Campylobacter jejuni"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Campylobacter jejuni is a major cause of bloody diarrhea, especially in children, and is transmitted fecal-orally through undercooked poultry or meat, unpasteurized milk, and contact with infected animals. It is oxidase positive, comma or S shaped with polar flagella, and is uniquely identified by its ability to grow at 42°C, which is why the laboratory used an elevated temperature.',
    '{"A":"Salmonella typhi causes the systemic illness typhoid fever with a truncal macular rash and pulse-temperature dissociation, and it does not require 42°C for growth.","B":"Vibrio cholerae is also comma shaped and oxidase positive but grows in alkaline media and causes profuse rice-water diarrhea rather than bloody dysentery.","C":"Shigella dysenteriae produces Shiga toxin and causes bacillary dysentery; it is nonmotile, does not grow selectively at 42°C, and its reservoir is humans only.","D":"Helicobacter pylori is urease positive and colonizes the gastric antrum, causing gastritis and peptic ulcers rather than acute bloody diarrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Candida albicans',
    'A 58-year-old woman with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with intense vulvar itching and a thick white vaginal discharge. She has taken several courses of broad-spectrum antibiotics for a urinary tract infection over the past two months. A wet mount shows budding yeasts with elongated chains attached to the parent cell.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Aspergillus fumigatus"},{"id":"B","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"},{"id":"C","text":"Trichomonas vaginalis"},{"id":"D","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},{"id":"E","text":"Candida albicans"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Candida albicans is a dimorphic fungus that forms pseudohyphae and budding yeasts at 20°C and germ tubes at 37°C. Vulvovaginal candidiasis classically occurs in patients with diabetes mellitus or recent antibiotic use, and oral or esophageal thrush is typical in immunocompromised patients, including neonates, steroid users, diabetics, and those with AIDS. Oral fluconazole or topical azoles treat vaginal disease. The white, thick discharge with itching and the wet-mount pseudohyphae are the classic clues.',
    '{"A":"Aspergillus fumigatus forms septate hyphae with acute-angle branching and causes invasive pulmonary disease in neutropenic hosts, not a vaginal infection.","B":"Histoplasma capsulatum causes granulomatous pulmonary and disseminated disease associated with bird and bat droppings, not vulvovaginal symptoms.","C":"Trichomonas vaginalis produces a frothy, malodorous, green-yellow discharge with motile flagellated trophozoites on wet mount, and it is a sexually transmitted protozoan rather than a budding yeast.","D":"Cryptococcus neoformans is an encapsulated yeast that causes meningoencephalitis and pneumonia in immunocompromised patients and is not a cause of vulvovaginitis."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Candida albicans',
    'A 33-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia is admitted for induction chemotherapy. On hospital day 18 she remains profoundly neutropenic and develops persistent fevers, diffuse abdominal pain, and multiple small erythematous papules on her trunk. Blood cultures grow a yeast that produces smooth white colonies.',
    'Which of the following is the best explanation for her increased susceptibility to this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Absence of gastric acid allowing overgrowth"},{"id":"B","text":"Deficiency of phagocytes, the primary host defense"},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of T-cell-mediated immunity specifically"},{"id":"D","text":"Loss of complement-mediated opsonization only"},{"id":"E","text":"Impaired IgA production in mucosal surfaces"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Candida albicans is a dimorphic fungus that causes disseminated candidiasis especially in neutropenic patients, because host defense against Candida relies on phagocytes. This is why profound neutropenia after chemotherapy is the classic setup, and why blood cultures turning positive for a yeast in a febrile neutropenic patient raise concern for hematogenous spread. Treatment of systemic disease uses fluconazole, echinocandins, or amphotericin B. Note the contrast with organisms controlled by T cells, such as Pneumocystis and Cryptococcus.',
    '{"A":"Gastric acid loss predisposes to gut bacterial overgrowth and diarrhea, and while neither acid suppression nor achlorhydria is the classic Candida risk factor, phagocyte deficiency is.","C":"Defective T-cell immunity is the primary predisposing defect for Pneumocystis pneumonia and cryptococcosis, not the main defect for disseminated candidiasis.","D":"Complement deficiency is typically associated with recurrent neisserial infections and encapsulated bacteria rather than with disseminated candidiasis.","E":"Reduced mucosal IgA is not the principal risk factor described for disseminated Candida disease in neutropenic patients."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cestodes: Taenia, Diphyllobothrium, Echinococcus',
    'A 52-year-old woman presents with 6 months of dull right upper quadrant discomfort and progressive abdominal fullness. She raises sheep on a family farm in rural Peru and lives with three herding dogs. Examination shows a palpable liver edge. Abdominal CT reveals a large, well-defined cystic lesion in the right hepatic lobe with internal septations and thin peripheral calcification. Enzyme immunoassay for the suspected organism is positive.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate medical therapy for this patient''s condition?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Niclosamide"},{"id":"B","text":"Surgical resection of the right hepatic lobe performed immediately"},{"id":"C","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"D","text":"Metronidazole plus paromomycin"},{"id":"E","text":"Albendazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The lesion is a hydatid cyst of Echinococcus granulosus, acquired by ingesting eggs from food contaminated with dog feces, with sheep serving as the intermediate host - precisely the farm exposure described. The eggshell calcification pattern and the liver or lung location are typical, and because cyst rupture can cause anaphylaxis, needle aspiration is avoided. First Aid lists albendazole as the medical agent, reserving surgery for complicated cysts.',
    '{"A":"Niclosamide is paired with praziquantel for Diphyllobothrium latum, the tapeworm that competes for vitamin B12, so it targets a different cestode.","B":"Immediate resection risks puncturing the hydatid cyst and precipitating anaphylaxis, so the First Aid approach is albendazole with surgery limited to complicated cysts.","C":"Praziquantel is the treatment for the other cestodes Taenia and Diphyllobothrium and for Schistosoma and Clonorchis, but First Aid lists albendazole for Echinococcus.","D":"Metronidazole plus paromomycin are amebiasis drugs for Entamoeba histolytica, a different liver process showing anchovy paste exudate rather than a calcified hydatid cyst."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cestodes: Taenia, Diphyllobothrium, Echinococcus',
    'A 29-year-old man presents to the emergency department after two generalized tonic-clonic seizures. He is afebrile and has no focal neurologic deficit. MRI of the brain shows multiple small cystic lesions with a central dot and surrounding edema in the cerebral cortex. He grew up in rural Oaxaca, Mexico, where he ate pork frequently and lived with dogs, and where sanitation was poor.',
    'Which of the following organisms most likely caused these lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},{"id":"B","text":"Taenia solium"},{"id":"C","text":"Echinococcus granulosus"},{"id":"D","text":"Trichuris trichiura"},{"id":"E","text":"Schistosoma mansoni"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Neurocysticercosis arises when Taenia solium eggs are ingested from food contaminated with human feces, and First Aid lists brain cysts with seizures as the hint association for this organism. Eggs ingested by humans produce cysticercosis, whereas eating the larval cysts in undercooked pork produces only the intestinal tapeworm. Treatment is praziquantel, with albendazole used for neurocysticercosis, and seizures require anticonvulsant therapy.',
    '{"A":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes for vitamin B12 in the intestine and causes megaloblastic anemia, not cortical cysts with a central dot and seizures.","C":"Echinococcus granulosus produces hydatid cysts in liver and lungs after ingestion of eggs from dog feces, and it does not form the classic small cerebral cysticercal lesions.","D":"Trichuris trichiura is a whipworm acquired by ingesting eggs and it colonizes the cecum, causing loose stools, anemia, and rectal prolapse rather than brain cysts.","E":"Schistosoma mansoni causes portal hypertension and intestinal disease after cercariae penetrate skin, and it does not form cystic CNS lesions."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cestodes: Taenia, Diphyllobothrium, Echinococcus',
    'A 61-year-old man from Scandinavia presents with 4 months of fatigue, symmetric tingling in both hands and feet, and unsteady gait. He eats raw salmon as gravlax several times per week. Examination shows decreased vibratory sense and impaired proprioception in the lower extremities. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL, mean corpuscular volume 114 fL, hypersegmented neutrophils, and a low serum vitamin B12 level. Stool examination shows a long tapeworm.',
    'Which of the following best explains this patient''s presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It forms hydatid cysts in the liver and lungs with a risk of anaphylaxis"},{"id":"B","text":"It converts to a cystic larval stage in the brain and causes seizures"},{"id":"C","text":"It is acquired from undercooked pork and causes intestinal obstruction"},{"id":"D","text":"It lives as a large intestinal tapeworm that competes for vitamin B12 and causes megaloblastic anemia"},{"id":"E","text":"It competes for folate and causes a megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is Diphyllobothrium latum, the broad tapeworm acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish such as salmon. In the intestine the worm competes with the host for vitamin B12, which is why First Aid nicknames it DiphylloB12 latum and lists megaloblastic anemia as the clue. The cobalamin deficit affects the dorsal columns, producing the paresthesias and impaired proprioception. Treatment is praziquantel or niclosamide.',
    '{"A":"Hydatid cysts in liver and lungs describe Echinococcus granulosus from ingested dog-fecal eggs, which produces mass lesions instead of a slow-onset neurologic syndrome.","B":"Conversion to a cystic larval stage in the brain describes Taenia solium cysticercosis, which causes seizures rather than the paresthesias and ataxia of cobalamin deficiency.","C":"Intestinal obstruction from a tapeworm is not the First Aid association for Taenia solium, which is linked to cysticercosis and neurocysticercosis rather than to obstruction.","E":"The tapeworm in question depletes vitamin B12, not folate, and the hypersegmented neutrophils here reflect the cobalamin deficit that also explains the neurologic findings."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cestodes: Taenia, Diphyllobothrium, Echinococcus',
    'A 44-year-old man in a rural Alaskan village who eats raw freshwater fish regularly presents with fatigue and paresthesias. Laboratory studies confirm macrocytic anemia with a low vitamin B12 level, and stool examination shows a large tapeworm.',
    'Which of the following parasite-disease associations best explains this patient''s findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Taenia solium, for causing seizures through neurocysticercosis"},{"id":"B","text":"Echinococcus granulosus, for producing hydatid cysts with eggshell calcification in the liver"},{"id":"C","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum, for vitamin B12 deficiency with megaloblastic anemia"},{"id":"D","text":"Taenia solium, for vitamin B12 deficiency from intestinal competition"},{"id":"E","text":"Clonorchis sinensis, for biliary tract inflammation with pigmented gallstones"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Diphyllobothrium latum is the tapeworm acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish, and it competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, producing megaloblastic anemia - the memory hook is DiphylloB12 latum. This association appears in the First Aid parasite hints table and is a frequent test point, so distinguishing it from the other cestode associations is essential. Treatment is praziquantel or niclosamide.',
    '{"A":"Taenia solium does cause neurocysticercosis with seizures, but that mechanism does not involve vitamin B12, and the patient has no cystic CNS lesions.","B":"Echinococcus granulosus does produce calcified hydatid cysts in liver and lungs, but this patient has a macrocytic anemia from a different tapeworm.","D":"Taenia solium is linked to brain cysts and seizures in the First Aid hints table rather than to intestinal competition for cobalamin.","E":"Clonorchis sinensis causes biliary tract inflammation with pigmented gallstones and cholangiocarcinoma, and it is a trematode rather than a tapeworm."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Chlamydia trachomatis',
    'A 19-year-old woman presents with 1 week of dysuria and a mild mucopurulent vaginal discharge. She is sexually active with a new partner and denies fever. Nucleic acid amplification testing returns positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. Her partner has no symptoms but also tests positive.',
    'Which of the following is the best first-line treatment for this patient, assuming she is not pregnant?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Penicillin G"},{"id":"B","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"D","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"id":"E","text":"Ceftriaxone"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Chlamydial infection is treated with doxycycline, and azithromycin is used for pregnant patients. Ceftriaxone is added if concurrent gonorrhea testing is positive, since coinfection is common. Beta-lactams such as penicillin and amoxicillin are ineffective because the chlamydial cell wall lacks classic peptidoglycan due to reduced muramic acid.',
    '{"A":"Penicillin G is the treatment for syphilis and is not effective against Chlamydia.","B":"TMP-SMX is used for Nocardia and as an alternative in macrolide-allergic pertussis, not for chlamydial urethritis.","D":"Amoxicillin is a beta-lactam and is not the recommended chlamydial therapy, related to the organism''s atypical cell wall.","E":"Ceftriaxone is for gonorrhea and is added only if gonococcal coinfection is documented or not excluded."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Chlamydia trachomatis',
    'A 26-year-old man presents with a small, painless ulcer on the penis and a 5-day history of tender swelling of the right groin. Examination shows indurated, matted inguinal lymph nodes, one of which is fluctuant. He denies systemic symptoms. Infection with serotypes L1 to L3 of Chlamydia trachomatis is confirmed.',
    'Which of the following best describes this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Primary syphilis, with a painless chancre and nontender bilateral lymphadenopathy"},{"id":"B","text":"Chancroid, with a painful ragged ulcer and suppurative nodes from Haemophilus ducreyi"},{"id":"C","text":"Genital herpes, with grouped painful vesicles on an erythematous base"},{"id":"D","text":"Trachoma from serotypes A, B, and C with follicular conjunctivitis"},{"id":"E","text":"Lymphogranuloma venereum, with a small painless ulcer progressing to painful ulcerating inguinal buboes"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Chlamydia trachomatis serotypes L1, L2, and L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum, which starts with small painless genital ulcers and progresses to swollen, painful inguinal lymph nodes that ulcerate as buboes. It is treated with doxycycline. The distinction from other causes of genital ulceration rests on the painless initial ulcer with markedly tender, suppurating nodes.',
    '{"A":"Primary syphilis usually produces nontender inguinal adenopathy, and the nodes are not fluctuant buboes; serologic confirmation would be positive for T pallidum.","B":"Chancroid produces a painful, ragged ulcer with purulent exudate, whereas the LGV ulcer here is small and painless.","C":"Herpetic lesions are painful grouped vesicles, not a single painless ulcer with unilateral inguinal buboes.","D":"Serotypes A, B, and C cause chronic follicular conjunctivitis and blindness in resource-limited settings, not genital ulcer disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Chlamydia trachomatis',
    'An 8-day-old infant born by vaginal delivery to an untreated mother presents with bilateral eye discharge and swelling of the eyelids. Examination shows purulent conjunctivitis with redness and edema of the conjunctivae. The infant has no fever and is feeding well. The mother had no prenatal care.',
    'Which of the following organisms most likely causes conjunctivitis in this infant, and which form is responsible for infecting the conjunctival cells?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis, with the reticulate body entering host cells"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis, with the elementary body entering host cells"},{"id":"C","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae, with the pillated form entering host cells"},{"id":"D","text":"Haemophilus influenzae, with the capsular type b polysaccharide entering cells"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus, with the exfoliative toxin entering the conjunctiva"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Chlamydia trachomatis serotypes D through K cause neonatal conjunctivitis at 1 to 2 weeks after birth, later than the 2 to 5 day onset of gonococcal conjunctivitis. Chlamydiae are obligate intracellular organisms that cannot make their own ATP; the small, dense elementary body is the infectious form that enters the cell by endocytosis, and it then transforms into the replicating reticulate body. Inclusion bodies containing reticulate bodies can be seen on Giemsa or fluorescent antibody stained smears.',
    '{"A":"The replicating reticulate body is the intracellular form; the infectious particle that enters cells is the elementary body.","C":"Gonococcal conjunctivitis presents earlier, typically 2 to 5 days after birth, with a hyperacute purulent discharge, and prevention is by erythromycin eye ointment.","D":"Haemophilus causes conjunctivitis and otitis media in older children, but the 1 to 2 week timing and afebrile infant point to chlamydia.","E":"Staphylococcal conjunctivitis is common in neonates but is not associated with a latency of 1 to 2 weeks or with the intracellular life cycle described."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridioides difficile',
    'A 66-year-old woman is hospitalized for aspiration pneumonia and receives 7 days of clindamycin. On day 10 she develops 8 to 10 episodes of watery diarrhea daily, lower abdominal cramping, fever to 38.6°C, and a white blood cell count of 19,000/µL. She is on a proton pump inhibitor. Stool testing by PCR is positive for an organism''s toxin genes, and colonoscopy shows raised yellow-white plaques.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible for this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Clostridioides difficile"},{"id":"B","text":"Bacillus cereus"},{"id":"C","text":"Clostridium botulinum"},{"id":"D","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"E","text":"Clostridium perfringens"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Clostridioides difficile produces toxins A and B, which damage enterocytes and lead to watery diarrhea with pseudomembranous colitis. Infection is typically secondary to antibiotic use, especially clindamycin, ampicillin, cephalosporins, and fluoroquinolones, and it is also associated with proton pump inhibitors. Fulminant infection can cause toxic megacolon, ileus, and shock, and diagnosis is by PCR or antigen detection of one or both toxins in stool.',
    '{"B":"Bacillus cereus causes reheated-rice food poisoning with a brief emetic or diarrheal illness, and it does not produce pseudomembranes.","C":"Clostridium botulinum produces a neurotoxin causing descending flaccid paralysis, with constipation rather than profuse diarrhea.","D":"Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning causes brief vomiting after preformed toxin ingestion, and its antibiotic-associated diarrhea is not the classic pseudomembranous colitis.","E":"Clostridium perfringens causes gas gangrene through α-toxin and a late-onset food poisoning, neither of which produces pseudomembranous colitis after antibiotics."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridioides difficile',
    'A 74-year-old man in the hospital after hip surgery develops watery diarrhea, abdominal distention, and hypotension on day 12 of a cephalosporin course. Abdominal radiograph shows a dilated colon, and stool is positive for C difficile toxin by PCR. He has had two prior episodes treated with oral vancomycin in the past 6 months, and this is now his third recurrence.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient''s recurrent infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Intravenous ceftriaxone"},{"id":"B","text":"Oral ciprofloxacin with metronidazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin, with fecal microbiota transplant considered for repeated recurrence"},{"id":"D","text":"Antiperistaltic agents to reduce diarrhea"},{"id":"E","text":"Loperamide plus a proton pump inhibitor"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Treatment of Clostridioides difficile colitis is oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin. For recurrent cases, treatment options are to repeat the prior regimen or to perform fecal microbiota transplant, which restores colonization resistance after repeated antibiotic courses have destroyed the normal colonic flora.',
    '{"A":"Intravenous ceftriaxone is not effective therapy for colonic C difficile infection and is itself among the antibiotics that predispose to it.","B":"Fluoroquinolones are one of the antibiotic classes associated with C difficile infection, so adding ciprofloxacin with metronidazole is not the recommended regimen.","D":"Antiperistaltic agents can precipitate toxic megacolon by trapping toxin in the colon and are contraindicated in severe colitis.","E":"Loperamide and a proton pump inhibitor would worsen risk, since proton pump inhibitor use is associated with C difficile infection and antimotility drugs risk ileus and megacolon."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium botulinum',
    'A 3-month-old infant is brought in because of poor feeding, weak cry, constipation, and progressive floppiness. He appears alert and has symmetric weakness, ptosis, and reduced gag reflex; his pupils are dilated and react sluggishly. He was fed honey sweetened on a pacifier by a relative. Deep tendon reflexes are reduced, and sensation appears intact.',
    'Which of the following best explains the weakness in this infant?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Blocking of glycine and GABA release in the spinal cord"},{"id":"B","text":"Antibodies to the presynaptic calcium channel at the motor terminal"},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of the acetylcholine receptor at the postsynaptic membrane"},{"id":"D","text":"Direct invasion of anterior horn cells by the organism"},{"id":"E","text":"Inhibition of acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is infant botulism, a floppy baby syndrome caused by ingestion of Clostridium botulinum spores, classically in honey. The organism colonizes the infant gut and produces a heat-labile toxin that damages SNARE proteins, preventing acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction and causing descending flaccid paralysis with bulbar weakness. Sensory deficits are characteristically absent, and the 5 D''s are diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, dyspnea, and descending flaccid paralysis.',
    '{"A":"Blocking glycine and GABA release in the spinal cord produces spastic paralysis with lockjaw and opisthotonos, which is tetanus rather than this flaccid picture.","B":"Antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels cause Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, which improves with repeated effort and is associated with small cell lung cancer in adults.","C":"Loss of postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors causes myasthenia gravis, with fatigable weakness involving ocular muscles; it is immune mediated rather than toxin mediated.","D":"Anterior horn cell invasion occurs with poliovirus, causing asymmetric flaccid paralysis with fever and meningitis, not this afebrile bulbar weakness."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium botulinum',
    'A 52-year-old man attends a family gathering where home-canned green beans from bulging jars were served. Within 36 hours he develops double vision, slurred speech, difficulty swallowing, and progressive weakness that began in the extraocular and bulbar muscles and has moved downward to the limbs. He is afebrile and mentally clear. His sensory examination is completely normal, and he reports no diarrhea.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate management for this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Erythromycin eye ointment to the eyes"},{"id":"B","text":"Human botulinum immunoglobulin"},{"id":"C","text":"Diazepam for muscle spasms and wound debridement"},{"id":"D","text":"Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline prophylaxis"},{"id":"E","text":"Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is food-borne botulism from preformed toxin in improperly canned food that was not adequately sterilized, producing the classic 5 D''s with descending flaccid paralysis and no sensory deficits. Treatment is human botulinum immunoglobulin, which neutralizes circulating toxin before it binds nerve terminals, plus intensive supportive care for respiratory failure. The toxin is heat labile, though the canned food itself should be discarded.',
    '{"A":"Erythromycin eye ointment is used in neonates to prevent gonococcal and chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum, unrelated to systemic intoxication.","C":"Diazepam for muscle spasms with wound debridement is part of tetanus management, where the toxin produces spastic rather than flaccid paralysis.","D":"Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline prophylaxis is used after exposure to aerosolized Bacillus anthracis spores, not for botulinum intoxication.","E":"Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin treats Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis, which presents with watery diarrhea and colitis rather than cranial nerve palsies."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium perfringens',
    'A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes sustains a deep puncture wound of the thigh while gardening. Two days later he has severe thigh pain, bronze discoloration of the skin, tense swelling, crepitus on palpation, and rapid hemodynamic deterioration. Imaging shows gas within the muscle planes, and surgical exploration reveals necrotic, foul-smelling muscle.',
    'Which of the following toxins most directly explains the myonecrosis in this condition?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Tetanospasmin blocking glycine release"},{"id":"B","text":"Toxin A damaging enterocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"Erythrogenic exotoxin activating T cells"},{"id":"D","text":"Edema factor acting as adenylyl cyclase"},{"id":"E","text":"Alpha-toxin, a lecithinase (phospholipase)"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is gas gangrene caused by Clostridium perfringens. Its α-toxin is a lecithinase, a phospholipase that causes myonecrosis and hemolysis, producing soft tissue crepitus and rapid tissue destruction. The mnemonic is that perfringens perforates a gangrenous leg, and spontaneous gas gangrene after hematogenous seeding in a patient with colonic malignancy is caused most often by Clostridium septicum.',
    '{"A":"Tetanospasmin spreads by retrograde axonal transport to block GABA and glycine release from Renshaw cells, producing spastic paralysis such as trismus and opisthotonos, not tissue gas and myonecrosis.","B":"Toxin A is a Clostridioides difficile toxin that damages enterocytes and causes pseudomembranous colitis, an intestinal disease rather than a wound disease.","C":"Erythrogenic exotoxin is a Streptococcus pyogenes product responsible for the scarlet fever rash, not for gas within muscle planes.","D":"Edema factor is a component of anthrax toxin that raises intracellular cAMP and produces edema; it does not cause gas-forming myonecrosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium perfringens',
    'A group of 12 people attending a catered luncheon develop watery diarrhea and abdominal cramping about 11 hours after eating beef stew that had been cooked in a large pot and left to cool slowly at room temperature before serving. None has fever or vomiting. Symptoms resolve within 24 hours in all affected people. Cultures from the stew grow anaerobic gram-positive rods.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this food poisoning?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Direct invasion of the small intestinal mucosa with ulceration"},{"id":"B","text":"Heat-labile enterotoxins activating adenylate cyclase"},{"id":"C","text":"Ingestion of preformed heat-stable enterotoxin in the food"},{"id":"D","text":"Ingestion of vegetative bacteria that produce enterotoxin in the intestine"},{"id":"E","text":"Production of alpha-toxin during intestinal colonization"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'In Clostridium perfringens food poisoning, heavily spore-contaminated food is cooked but left standing too long at below 60°C, allowing spores to germinate into vegetative bacteria that are then ingested. The enterotoxin is produced in the intestine, giving late-onset symptoms at about 10 to 12 hours with resolution in 24 hours.',
    '{"A":"Invasion with mucosal ulceration is characteristic of Shigella and enteroinvasive E coli, which produce fever, tenesmus, and bloody stools.","B":"Heat-labile toxins that activate adenylate cyclase belong to enterotoxigenic E coli and Vibrio cholerae, producing traveler''s diarrhea and rice-water stools respectively.","C":"Preformed heat-stable toxin explains Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning with its 2- to 6-hour onset and prominent vomiting, not this delayed diarrheal illness.","E":"Alpha-toxin causes myonecrosis and hemolysis in gas gangrene, not a self-limited diarrheal illness, and it is not the food poisoning toxin."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium tetani',
    'A 45-year-old landscaper steps on a rusty nail and develops a small, dirty puncture wound. Ten days later he has difficulty opening his mouth, a grimacing facial expression with raised eyebrows, neck stiffness, and painful muscle spasms that arch his back. He is fully alert, and sensation is intact. The wound still contains a small amount of soil.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of the responsible toxin?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 in motor neurons"},{"id":"B","text":"Blocks glycine and GABA release from Renshaw cells after retrograde axonal transport"},{"id":"C","text":"Permanently activates Gs with accumulation of cAMP in muscle"},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibits MAP kinase and induces macrophage apoptosis"},{"id":"E","text":"Cleaves SNARE proteins to prevent acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Tetanus toxin, or tetanospasmin, spreads by retrograde axonal transport to the CNS and blocks release of GABA and glycine from Renshaw cells in the spinal cord, causing unopposed stimulation of motor neurons. The result is spastic paralysis with trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus (raised eyebrows and open grin), and opisthotonos. The organism is noninvasive and remains localized to the wound site.',
    '{"A":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 inhibits protein synthesis and is the mechanism of diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A.","C":"Permanent Gs activation with elevated cAMP is the cholera toxin mechanism, causing secretory diarrhea rather than neuromuscular disease.","D":"MAP kinase inhibition with macrophage apoptosis is the action of lethal factor in anthrax toxin, which produces shock and necrosis rather than muscle spasms.","E":"Cleavage of SNARE proteins to prevent acetylcholine release is the mechanism of botulinum toxin, which produces descending flaccid paralysis rather than spastic paralysis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Clostridium tetani',
    'A 70-year-old farmer who has not seen a physician in 30 years sustains a deep forearm laceration contaminated with soil and manure while repairing a fence. He presents 6 hours after injury. The wound is irrigated and debrided, and the wound edges are approximated. His immunization records cannot be located, and he reports no tetanus booster as an adult.',
    'Which of the following best describes management of tetanus risk in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Diphtheria antitoxin plus erythromycin"},{"id":"B","text":"Tetanus antitoxin with or without a vaccine booster, antibiotics, diazepam for spasms, and wound debridement"},{"id":"C","text":"Human botulinum immunoglobulin and plasmapheresis"},{"id":"D","text":"Wound care only, since tetanus is not transmitted through wounds"},{"id":"E","text":"Oral vancomycin and fidaxomicin"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Management of tetanus risk combines wound debridement to remove the anaerobic environment where spores germinate, tetanus antitoxin with or without a vaccine booster, antibiotics, and diazepam for muscle spasms. The pathogen is noninvasive and stays localized to the wound, so adequate surgical care of the wound is a central part of prevention, and tetanus is prevented by vaccination.',
    '{"A":"Diphtheria antitoxin with erythromycin treats Corynebacterium diphtheriae pharyngeal toxin disease and would not neutralize tetanospasmin.","C":"Human botulinum immunoglobulin is the treatment of botulism, in which the toxin acts at the neuromuscular junction to produce flaccid paralysis.","D":"Tetanus is acquired through contaminated wounds, especially deep puncture wounds and devitalized tissue, so wound care alone without immunization is inadequate for an unvaccinated patient.","E":"Oral vancomycin with fidaxomicin is the treatment of Clostridioides difficile colitis, an antibiotic-associated diarrheal disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Coccidioides immitis',
    'A 27-year-old archaeology student develops fever, cough, and pleuritic chest pain while excavating a site in southern Arizona. Two days later she notes painful red nodules on both shins and complains of aching knees and ankles. Chest imaging shows a small pulmonary infiltrate and a thin-walled cavity.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Aspergillus fumigatus"},{"id":"B","text":"Coccidioides immitis"},{"id":"C","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"},{"id":"D","text":"Blastomyces dermatitidis"},{"id":"E","text":"Paracoccidioides brasiliensis"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Coccidioides immitis is endemic to the southwestern United States and California and is acquired by inhaling arthroconidia from disturbed soil, classically during archaeological excavations or after earthquakes and dust storms. The illness may produce erythema nodosum, described as desert bumps, or erythema multiforme, along with arthralgias known as desert rheumatism. Unlike other systemic dimorphic fungi, it appears in tissue as a spherule filled with endospores rather than as a yeast. It can also disseminate to bone, skin, and the meninges.',
    '{"A":"Aspergillus fumigatus causes invasive pulmonary disease in neutropenic patients and aspergillomas in pre-existing lung cavities, not the self-limited desert illness with erythema nodosum seen here.","C":"Histoplasma capsulatum is endemic to the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys and is associated with bird and bat droppings rather than desert dust exposure.","D":"Blastomyces dermatitidis occurs in the eastern and central United States and the Great Lakes region, producing broad-based budding yeasts and verrucous skin lesions rather than desert rheumatism.","E":"Paracoccidioides brasiliensis is found in Latin America and shows the captain''s wheel pattern of budding yeasts, with a strong male predominance."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Coccidioides immitis',
    'A 38-year-old man who works outdoors in California''s Central Valley is diagnosed with coccidioidomycosis after presenting with pneumonia and erythema multiforme. A biopsy of a lytic rib lesion is performed, and the pathologist examines the tissue for the organism''s tissue-phase morphology.',
    'Which of the following describes the tissue form of Coccidioides?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A cigar-shaped yeast within a granuloma"},{"id":"B","text":"Pseudohyphae and budding yeasts at 37°C"},{"id":"C","text":"Tuberculate macroconidia visible in tissue sections"},{"id":"D","text":"A budding yeast with a thick polysaccharide capsule"},{"id":"E","text":"A large spherule filled with endospores"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The systemic dimorphic fungi grow as molds at 20°C and yeasts at 37°C, but Coccidioides is the exception: in tissue it forms a spherule that is much larger than a red blood cell and is packed with endospores. This distinctive appearance is why the organism is described as a spherule rather than a yeast. Coccidioides can disseminate to bone and skin and can also cause meningitis, for which amphotericin B may be given intrathecally.',
    '{"A":"A cigar-shaped yeast at 37°C describes Sporothrix schenckii, which is inoculated through the skin by a thorn and causes ascending lymphangitis.","B":"Pseudohyphae with budding yeasts at 37°C describes Candida albicans, which is dimorphic in the opposite fashion from the systemic fungi and is identified by germ tube formation.","C":"Tuberculate macroconidia are the mold-phase structures seen when Histoplasma capsulatum is grown in culture, not a tissue form seen in Coccidioides infection.","D":"A thick encapsulated budding yeast is Cryptococcus neoformans, which is not dimorphic and is best seen with India ink or mucicarmine staining."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Corynebacterium diphtheriae',
    'A 6-year-old unvaccinated child who recently immigrated presents with 3 days of low-grade fever, sore throat, and malaise. Examination shows a thick, adherent grayish-white membrane over the tonsils and posterior pharynx that bleeds when scraped, plus marked bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy giving a bull-neck appearance. He is tachycardic, and an ECG shows new arrhythmia.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of the responsible exotoxin?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cleavage of SNARE proteins in the pharyngeal mucosa"},{"id":"B","text":"Increase of intracellular cAMP by adenylyl cyclase activity"},{"id":"C","text":"Polyclonal T-cell activation through MHC class II binding"},{"id":"D","text":"Lecithinase activity degrading phospholipids in cell membranes"},{"id":"E","text":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, inhibiting protein synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Diphtheria toxin inhibits protein synthesis by ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, leading to necrosis in the pharynx, heart, and central nervous system. The result is pseudomembranous pharyngitis with a grayish-white membrane and bull-neck lymphadenopathy, with toxin dissemination causing myocarditis, arrhythmias, and neuropathy. The mnemonic is ABCDEFG: ADP-ribosylation, Beta-prophage, Corynebacterium, Diphtheriae, Elongation Factor 2, Granules.',
    '{"A":"SNARE protein cleavage is the mechanism of botulinum toxin (preventing acetylcholine release) and tetanospasmin (blocking glycine and GABA release) at neural synapses, not a mucosal toxin.","B":"Adenylyl cyclase activity raising cAMP is the edema factor component of anthrax toxin, and a similar mechanism drives cholera toxin and pertussis adenylate cyclase toxin.","C":"Superantigen-mediated polyclonal T-cell activation describes staphylococcal TSST-1 and streptococcal erythrogenic toxin, producing shock or a rash rather than a pharyngeal membrane.","D":"Lecithinase activity degrading phospholipids is the α-toxin of Clostridium perfringens, responsible for gas gangrene and hemolysis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Corynebacterium diphtheriae',
    'A 45-year-old man returns from an area with low immunization coverage and presents with a sore throat, hoarseness, and a gray adherent pharyngeal membrane. The physician suspects diphtheria and sends a pharyngeal swab for culture. The laboratory isolates club-shaped gram-positive rods in angular arrangements with metachromatic blue and red granules on special media, and black colonies grow on cystine-tellurite agar.',
    'Which of the following tests most directly confirms that this isolate produces toxin?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Positive PYR reaction"},{"id":"B","text":"Bile esculin hydrolysis"},{"id":"C","text":"Positive Elek test for toxin production"},{"id":"D","text":"Positive optochin sensitivity disk"},{"id":"E","text":"Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Laboratory diagnosis of Corynebacterium diphtheriae rests on gram-positive club-shaped rods with metachromatic (blue and red) granules and a positive Elek test for toxin. Organisms that carry the β-prophage lysogenized into their genome express the toxin, and the Elek immunodiffusion test demonstrates that toxin production.',
    '{"A":"PYR testing distinguishes group A streptococci (negative results with bacitracin) from group B and other streptococcal groups.","B":"Bile esculin hydrolysis is a test for group D organisms such as Enterococcus and S gallolyticus, unrelated to diphtheria toxin detection.","D":"Optochin sensitivity identifies Streptococcus pneumoniae and has no role in corynebacterial identification.","E":"Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride is a test for Enterococcus and does not apply to corynebacteria, which are not characteristically halotolerant."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Corynebacterium diphtheriae',
    'A 34-year-old woman presents with a 4-day history of sore throat, low-grade fever, and a gray-white adherent tonsillar membrane. She works as a teacher and is fully immunized, but a coworker recently returned from abroad with a similar illness. The hospital laboratory is not able to culture for several hours but the clinical picture is highly suggestive.',
    'Which of the following best describes the recommended management of this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Immediate intubation for airway protection followed by supportive hydration alone"},{"id":"B","text":"Supportive care only, with antibiotics reserved for bacteremia"},{"id":"C","text":"Diphtheria antitoxin with erythromycin or penicillin, and a toxoid vaccine booster if not up to date"},{"id":"D","text":"Vancomycin and surgical debridement of the pharyngeal tissue"},{"id":"E","text":"Antitoxin alone, since antibiotics only prevent secondary bacterial infection"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Treatment of diphtheria is diphtheria antitoxin plus an antibiotic, typically erythromycin or penicillin. Antitoxin neutralizes circulating toxin but does not affect toxin already bound to tissues, so it must be given promptly, and antibiotics eradicate the organism to prevent further toxin production and transmission. A booster toxoid vaccine is given if the patient''s vaccination status is not current.',
    '{"A":"Intubation may be needed if the airway is obstructed, but supportive care alone without antitoxin or antibiotics would allow progression of toxin-mediated injury.","B":"Antibiotics are not reserved for bacteremia; they are part of first-line therapy because they eliminate the toxin-producing organism, and untreated cases can progress to myocarditis and obstruction of the airway.","D":"Vancomycin and debridement fit the management of necrotizing skin and soft-tissue infection, not the toxin-mediated pharyngeal membrane of diphtheria.","E":"Antibiotics are not given only for secondary infection; they treat the primary organism, since continued toxin production drives cardiac and neurologic complications."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Cryptococcus neoformans',
    'A 36-year-old man with untreated HIV infection and a CD4+ count of 55 cells/mm3 presents with two weeks of worsening headache, low-grade fever, and confusion. A lumbar puncture shows elevated opening pressure, lymphocytic pleocytosis, and a clear halo surrounding yeast cells on India ink preparation of the cerebrospinal fluid.',
    'Which of the following best describes the structure responsible for the halo seen on this preparation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A germ tube extending from a yeast cell"},{"id":"B","text":"A cholesterol-rich cell membrane"},{"id":"C","text":"A beta-glucan-rich cell wall"},{"id":"D","text":"A spherule containing endospores"},{"id":"E","text":"A thick polysaccharide capsule"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Cryptococcus neoformans is a heavily encapsulated yeast measuring 5-10 μm with narrow-based budding, and it is not dimorphic. The capsule is made of polysaccharide and appears as a clear halo on India ink staining; it can also be highlighted red by mucicarmine staining of the inner capsule. Latex agglutination for capsular polysaccharide antigen is more sensitive and specific than India ink. The organism is found in soil and pigeon droppings and spreads by inhalation with hematogenous dissemination to the meninges.',
    '{"A":"Germ tube formation at 37°C is the diagnostic test for Candida albicans and has no role in identifying Cryptococcus, which is identified by its capsule.","B":"The fungal membrane contains ergosterol, which is the target of amphotericin B, but the unstained halo on India ink reflects a capsule outside the membrane rather than the membrane itself.","C":"Beta-glucan is a cell wall component targeted by echinocandins, but echinocandins have poor activity against Cryptococcus precisely because the capsule is the key structure here.","D":"Spherules containing endospores are the tissue form of Coccidioides immitis, which is a dimorphic fungus acquired from desert dust rather than pigeon droppings."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Cryptococcus neoformans',
    'A 42-year-old man with AIDS is diagnosed with cryptococcal meningitis after presenting with headache, fever, and altered mental status. His neurologist explains that induction therapy requires two drugs used in combination before transitioning to a single agent for long-term suppression.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate induction regimen for his meningitis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Voriconazole plus caspofungin"},{"id":"B","text":"Fluconazole plus itraconazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Nystatin plus griseofulvin"},{"id":"D","text":"Terbinafine"},{"id":"E","text":"Amphotericin B plus flucytosine"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Cryptococcal meningitis is treated with amphotericin B plus flucytosine, followed by fluconazole for chronic suppression, which is the regimen specifically emphasized for cryptococcal disease. Amphotericin B binds ergosterol and creates membrane pores, while flucytosine is converted by fungal cytosine deaminase to 5-fluorouracil, inhibiting DNA and RNA synthesis; its main toxicity is myelosuppression. Fluconazole is then used for chronic suppression in people living with HIV. Amphotericin B requires hydration and electrolyte monitoring because of nephrotoxicity.',
    '{"A":"Voriconazole is used for Aspergillus, and caspofungin is an echinocandin for invasive aspergillosis and Candida; neither has a standard role in cryptococcal meningitis induction.","B":"Fluconazole is correct later as chronic suppressive therapy, but combining two azoles is not the recommended induction regimen.","C":"Nystatin is a topical polyene used for oral thrush, and griseofulvin treats only superficial dermatophyte infections.","D":"Terbinafine inhibits squalene epoxidase and is used for onychomycosis and other dermatophyte infections; it is not part of the regimen for cryptococcal meningitis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora',
    'A 34-year-old man with untreated HIV infection and a CD4+ count of 40 cells/mm3 presents with 5 weeks of profuse watery diarrhea and an 8-kg weight loss. He has no blood in the stool, no fever, and no abdominal pain. Stool cultures for Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter are negative. His diarrhea began after he stopped filtering his drinking water.',
    'Which of the following stool findings would best confirm the diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Multinucleated trophozoites on stool microscopy"},{"id":"B","text":"Motile trophozoites with punctate cervical hemorrhages"},{"id":"C","text":"Oocysts visible on acid-fast staining of stool"},{"id":"D","text":"Trophozoites containing engulfed red blood cells"},{"id":"E","text":"Flask-shaped ulcers with an anchovy paste exudate"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Cryptosporidium causes severe, prolonged diarrhea in patients with AIDS, whereas immunocompetent hosts usually have a mild, self-limited watery diarrhea. The small round oocysts do not stand out on routine stool microscopy, so acid-fast staining of stool is the classic diagnostic method, supplemented by antigen detection and PCR. Prevention centers on filtering drinking water, and severe or immunocompromised disease is treated with nitazoxanide together with restoration of immune function.',
    '{"A":"Multinucleated trophozoites on stool microscopy indicate Giardia lamblia, which causes malabsorptive fatty diarrhea in immunocompetent campers and hikers.","B":"Motile trophozoites on a wet mount with punctate cervical hemorrhages describe Trichomonas vaginalis and its strawberry cervix, a sexually transmitted vaginitis rather than a diarrheal illness.","D":"Trophozoites containing engulfed RBCs are diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, an invasive colonic ameba that produces bloody dysentery rather than profuse watery diarrhea.","E":"Flask-shaped ulcers with anchovy paste exudate describe amebic colitis and liver abscess, both features of Entamoeba histolytica rather than of a small-bowel coccidian parasite."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora',
    'A 38-year-old woman with a kidney transplant on tacrolimus and prednisone presents in July with 2 weeks of watery diarrhea, nausea, and fatigue after returning from a resort in the Caribbean where she ate fresh salads and fruit rinsed in local water. Stool modified acid-fast stain shows small round acid-fast structures, and stool antigen testing for Cryptosporidium is negative. The organism identified is an acid-fast coccidian that does not respond well to metronidazole.',
    'Which of the following antimicrobials is the most appropriate therapy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amphotericin B"},{"id":"B","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Metronidazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"E","text":"Diethylcarbamazine"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Among the acid-fast, oocyst-forming coccidia that cause watery diarrhea, Cyclospora (like Cystoisospora) responds to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, the same sulfonamide-trimethoprim combination First Aid lists for Pneumocystis treatment and prophylaxis and for Toxoplasma prophylaxis in HIV. Cryptosporidium is the important exception: it is managed with nitazoxanide in severe or immunocompromised disease plus water filtration. Recognizing that the sulfa combination is the difference prevents a mistrial of metronidazole, which has no activity against these coccidia.',
    '{"A":"Amphotericin B is used for severe systemic mycoses, for Naegleria fowleri, and for leishmaniasis, and it has no role against an intestinal coccidian.","C":"Metronidazole covers Giardia, Entamoeba, and Trichomonas, but the acid-fast oocyst-forming coccidia are the classic parasites that do not respond reliably to it.","D":"Praziquantel is an anthelmintic for trematodes and cestodes such as Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia, and it has no antiprotozoal activity.","E":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial worms such as Wuchereria bancrofti and Loa loa, and it plays no part in intestinal protozoan disease."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Culture media: selective and differential',
    'A 24-year-old sexually active man has dysuria and a purulent urethral discharge. A nucleic acid test is positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae. The laboratory also plates the specimen on a medium containing vancomycin, trimethoprim, colistin, and nystatin.',
    'Which medium is described, and what is its purpose?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Thayer-Martin agar, used to selectively culture Neisseria"},{"id":"B","text":"Eosin-methylene blue agar, used to identify E coli"},{"id":"C","text":"Buffered charcoal yeast extract agar, used for Legionella"},{"id":"D","text":"MacConkey agar, used to make lactose fermenters pink"},{"id":"E","text":"Chocolate agar, used to supply factors V and X"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Thayer-Martin agar is the classic selective medium for Neisseria: vancomycin inhibits gram-positive organisms, trimethoprim and colistin inhibit gram-negative organisms other than Neisseria, and nystatin inhibits fungi. It is described in First Aid as "very typically" culturing Neisseria. Chocolate agar, which supplies factors V and X, is the enriched base that lets the fastidious organism grow, while the antibiotics in Thayer-Martin suppress the abundant normal flora of mucosal sites. This layering of enrichment plus selection is the general pattern for culturing fastidious organisms from a colonized site.',
    '{"B":"EMB agar uses eosin and methylene blue to inhibit gram-positive organisms and is used to identify E coli by a green metallic sheen, not for genital cultures.","C":"Buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron grows the \"Ella\" siblings - Brucella, Francisella, Legionella, Pasteurella.","D":"MacConkey agar selects against gram-positive organisms with bile salts and crystal violet and differentiates lactose fermenters by a pink color; it is not designed for Neisseria.","E":"Chocolate agar provides factors V (NAD+) and X (hematin) and is used for H influenzae, and it is also used for Neisseria, but it has no antibiotics and is nonselective."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Culture media: selective and differential',
    'A 26-year-old woman has a urinary tract infection. A urine specimen is plated on a medium containing bile salts and crystal violet, and the colonies that grow are pink. The laboratory reports a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod.',
    'Which medium is being described, and what does the pink color indicate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They cause lactose-fermenting colonies to turn pink for species identification in the lab"},{"id":"B","text":"They inhibit gram-positive organisms, other gram-negative organisms, and fungi to favor Neisseria"},{"id":"C","text":"They neutralize catalase produced by the organism and prevent oxidative damage to it"},{"id":"D","text":"They provide factors V and X, which are required for growth of the organism on this plate"},{"id":"E","text":"They inhibit Neisseria so that the abundant competing normal flora can be identified"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'MacConkey agar is selective and differential: bile salts and crystal violet inhibit gram-positive organisms, and lactose fermentation produces acid that turns the colonies pink once the pH indicator responds. Lactose-fermenting enterics are therefore pink, and nonfermenters remain pale. This single plate often supplies both the Gram-character inference and the sugar fermentation profile, which is why stool and urine workups lean on it. Predicting color requires knowing whether the organism ferments lactose, as E coli does.',
    '{"A":"Lactose fermentation producing a pink color occurs on MacConkey agar through a pH indicator. No such indicator is the selective principle of Thayer-Martin agar.","C":"Catalase converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. These antibiotics do not neutralize it, and catalase production is not the reason competing flora is suppressed here.","D":"Factors V (NAD+) and X (hematin) are nutrients supplied by heated blood in chocolate agar. They support growth of fastidious organisms and are not the antibiotics that suppress competing flora.","E":"The purpose of the antibiotics is the opposite: they inhibit everything except Neisseria, so the organism is enriched rather than suppressed. Normal flora is not identified with this plate."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Culture media: selective and differential',
    'A 68-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and recurrent pneumonia has a respiratory specimen plated. On eosin-methylene blue agar no growth appears; on blood and chocolate agar there is growth. The isolate is a fastidious gram-negative rod that grows best on buffered charcoal yeast extract agar supplemented with cysteine and iron.',
    'Which organism is most likely isolated by the medium described?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Haemophilus influenzae"},{"id":"B","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae"},{"id":"C","text":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},{"id":"D","text":"Legionella pneumophila"},{"id":"E","text":"Bordetella pertussis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron grows Brucella, Francisella, Legionella, and Pasteurella, remembered as the Ella siblings, Bruce, Francis, a legionnaire, and a pasteur (pastor) who built the Sistine (cysteine) chapel of charcoal and iron. Legionella is a primarily intracellular organism that fails to Gram stain well, which is why silver staining and this medium feature in its identification. The negative EMB result is expected because EMB is a gram-negative enteric differential medium, not a medium for a fastidious respiratory organism.',
    '{"A":"H influenzae requires chocolate agar with factors V (NAD+) and X (hematin); it does not require cysteine or iron in charcoal medium.","B":"C diphtheriae grows on tellurite agar or Löffler medium and produces black colonies from tellurite reduction.","C":"M pneumoniae requires Eaton agar containing cholesterol, and it lacks a cell wall; the mnemonic organism here is Legionella.","E":"B pertussis grows on Bordet-Gengou agar with potato extract or on Regan-Lowe medium with charcoal, blood, and antibiotic, not on BCYE."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Culture media: selective and differential',
    'A 19-year-old college freshman is evaluated for fever and a stiff neck. Cerebrospinal fluid shows gram-negative diplococci. The laboratory plates CSF on chocolate agar, where colonies grow, and on a blood-agar-based medium with the antibiotics vancomycin, trimethoprim, colistin, and nystatin, where colonies also grow.',
    'What is the role of the antibiotics in the second medium?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They are obligate intracellular organisms that depend entirely on the host cell for ATP"},{"id":"B","text":"They are normal microbiota of the digestive tract and usually pathogenic only outside it"},{"id":"C","text":"They are strict human pathogens that have no commensal reservoir anywhere in the body"},{"id":"D","text":"They are catalase-positive organisms that evade killing by alveolar macrophages"},{"id":"E","text":"They are encapsulated organisms that are opsonized and cleared by the spleen"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Thayer-Martin agar is chocolate agar base plus vancomycin to inhibit gram-positive organisms, trimethoprim and colistin to inhibit gram-negative organisms other than Neisseria, and nystatin to inhibit fungi. Because respiratory and genital secretions carry abundant normal flora, this antibiotic shield is essential to recover the fastidious diplococcus. CSF itself is sterile, but the same selective medium is used routinely when plating from mucosal or skin sites, and it is described as very typically culturing Neisseria.',
    '{"A":"Obligate intracellular organisms are Rickettsia, Chlamydia, and Coxiella, remembered as staying inside cells when it is Really Chilly and Cold. Oral anaerobes are extracellular.","C":"These organisms are commensals of the mouth and GI tract, not strict pathogens without a commensal reservoir. That is precisely why the disease is described as opportunistic.","D":"Catalase positivity protects organisms such as S aureus and Aspergillus from oxidative killing by macrophages. These anaerobes characteristically lack catalase and superoxide dismutase.","E":"Encapsulated organisms such as S pneumoniae and Klebsiella are opsonized and cleared by the spleen, and asplenic patients are at risk from them. The organisms here are not in that group."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Cytomegalovirus',
    'A 40-year-old man with AIDS has a CD4 count of 35/mm3 and reports 2 weeks of progressive painless loss of vision in the left eye. Fundoscopy shows perivascular retinal hemorrhages with cotton-wool exudates. He has never had Pneumocystis pneumonia.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the pathogen and the mechanism of its disease in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cytomegalovirus retinitis, which typically occurs when the CD4 count falls below 500/mm3"},{"id":"B","text":"Toxoplasma chorioretinitis, which produces a single ring-enhancing retinal lesion"},{"id":"C","text":"Cryptococcal endophthalmitis, associated with India ink positive vitreous"},{"id":"D","text":"BK virus nephropathy with secondary hypertensive retinopathy"},{"id":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus retinitis, which typically occurs when the CD4 count falls below 50/mm3"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'CMV disease in HIV is a CD4 count less than 100/mm3 event, and retinitis characteristically appears below 50/mm3; the mnemonic is sightomegalovirus. Fundoscopy shows hemorrhage and cotton-wool exudates leading to vision loss, and biopsy of affected tissue shows cells with owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions. The CD4-stratified opportunistic infection thresholds are high yield: 500 for oral thrush, 200 for Pneumocystis and Toxoplasma, and 100 for CMV and MAC.',
    '{"A":"A threshold of 500 cells/mm3 is where oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi sarcoma, and HPV-related squamous cell carcinoma appear, not CMV retinitis.","B":"Toxoplasma causes multiple ring-enhancing brain abscesses rather than the perivascular retinal hemorrhages and cotton-wool spots described here.","C":"Cryptococcus causes meningitis with encapsulated yeast on India ink, not the hemorrhagic retinitis pattern shown.","D":"BK virus causes nephropathy in transplant recipients, not retinitis in patients with AIDS."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Cytomegalovirus',
    'A 31-year-old woman is evaluated for 10 days of fever, fatigue, and mild transaminase elevation. She has no significant lymphadenopathy and a normal blood smear. Heterophile antibody testing is negative, but CMV IgM is positive. She is immunocompetent.',
    'Which of the following best accounts for the discordant mononucleosis-like illness and negative heterophile test?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"CMV lacks the CD21 receptor and therefore cannot cause mononucleosis"},{"id":"B","text":"The heterophile test was false negative because EBV was confirmed by a positive Monospot with owl''s eye cells"},{"id":"C","text":"CMV mononucleosis occurs only in immunocompromised patients with CD4 counts below 100"},{"id":"D","text":"The negative Monospot indicates the illness is caused by HSV-1 gingivostomatitis"},{"id":"E","text":"CMV mononucleosis in immunocompetent patients is characteristically Monospot negative"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'CMV causes a mononucleosis-like illness that is heterophile negative (Monospot negative) in immunocompetent patients, unlike EBV, whose mononucleosis is heterophile positive. CMV is transmitted congenitally and by transfusion, sexual contact, saliva, urine, and transplant, and it remains latent in mononuclear cells. In immunocompromised hosts, particularly transplant recipients, CMV causes pneumonia, esophagitis, colitis, retinitis, and encephalitis, remembered as CREEP.',
    '{"A":"CMV does not use CD21, but that does not prevent it from producing a mononucleosis-like syndrome; receptor identity is unrelated to this presentation.","B":"The Monospot is the heterophile antibody test, and it is negative here; the explanation should identify CMV, not restate a positive EBV result.","C":"CMV mononucleosis is most often recognized in immunocompetent young adults; the CD4 below 100 threshold applies to severe end-organ CMV disease in AIDS.","D":"HSV-1 causes gingivostomatitis, keratoconjunctivitis, cold sores, and encephalitis, not a systemic mononucleosis-like illness."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Cytomegalovirus',
    'A newborn is evaluated for microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, and jaundice. Fundoscopy reveals chorioretinitis. Imaging shows periventricular calcifications. The mother had a mononucleosis-like illness during the second trimester, with a negative heterophile antibody test.',
    'Examination of this infant''s affected tissue would most likely reveal which characteristic finding?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Negri bodies in Purkinje cells"},{"id":"B","text":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells in lymph nodes"},{"id":"C","text":"Koilocytes with perinuclear halos on cervical cytology"},{"id":"D","text":"Enlarged cells with intranuclear owl''s eye inclusions"},{"id":"E","text":"Multinucleated giant cells with Cowdry A inclusions on Tzanck smear"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Congenital CMV produces infected cells with characteristic owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions, along with chorioretinitis, periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, and hepatosplenomegaly. CMV is one of the major congenital TORCH infections and is acquired congenitally, through transfusion, sexual contact, saliva, urine, or transplant. The mother''s heterophile-negative mononucleosis-like illness is consistent with primary CMV in pregnancy.',
    '{"A":"Negri bodies are the cytoplasmic inclusions of rabies and would not be found in a congenital TORCH infection.","B":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells are fused lymphocytes seen in measles lymphadenitis, not the cytologic hallmark of CMV.","C":"Koilocytes are the cytopathic effect of human papillomavirus, not of cytomegalovirus.","E":"Cowdry A inclusions and multinucleated giant cells occur with HSV-1, HSV-2, and VZV; congenital HSV typically causes vesicular skin lesions rather than periventricular calcifications."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Dermatophytes',
    'A 9-year-old boy presents with an expanding annular, scaly, pruritic plaque on his scalp with patchy hair loss and broken hairs. Potassium hydroxide examination of plucked hair shows septate hyphae and spores around the hair shaft. He is otherwise healthy.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate oral treatment for this superficial fungal infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Flucytosine"},{"id":"B","text":"Caspofungin"},{"id":"C","text":"Pentamidine"},{"id":"D","text":"Griseofulvin"},{"id":"E","text":"Amphotericin B"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Griseofulvin provides oral treatment of superficial infections and inhibits the growth of dermatophytes causing tinea and ringworm. Its mechanism is interference with microtubule function, disrupting mitosis, and it deposits in keratin-containing tissues such as nails and hair. Important adverse effects include teratogenicity, carcinogenicity, confusion, headaches, a disulfiram-like reaction, and increased cytochrome P-450 and warfarin metabolism. Terbinafine is the alternative, especially for onychomycosis.',
    '{"A":"Flucytosine inhibits DNA and RNA synthesis after conversion to 5-fluorouracil and is used with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis; it does not treat dermatophytes.","B":"Caspofungin inhibits beta-glucan synthesis in the fungal cell wall and is used for invasive aspergillosis and Candida, not for superficial tinea.","C":"Pentamidine is an alternative agent for Pneumocystis pneumonia and is not an antifungal treatment for dermatophytes.","E":"Amphotericin B binds ergosterol and is reserved for serious systemic mycoses, with substantial nephrotoxicity that is unnecessary for a superficial dermatophyte infection."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'DNA viruses overview and DNA virus mnemonics',
    'A 4-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician for multiple small, flesh-colored, dome-shaped papules with central umbilication on the trunk and face. A scraping of one lesion shows large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions within keratinocytes. The child also has mild atopic dermatitis.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative agent?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An enveloped, linear dsDNA virus and the largest DNA virus, belonging to the poxvirus family"},{"id":"B","text":"A naked, linear ssDNA virus that infects erythroid precursor cells"},{"id":"C","text":"An enveloped, partially dsDNA, circular virus with reverse transcriptase activity"},{"id":"D","text":"A naked, linear dsDNA virus that causes febrile pharyngitis and hemorrhagic cystitis"},{"id":"E","text":"A naked, circular dsDNA virus that also causes cervical and oropharyngeal cancer"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The umbilicated flesh-colored papule containing molluscum bodies is molluscum contagiosum, caused by a poxvirus. Poxviruses are enveloped, linear dsDNA viruses and the largest DNA viruses, and they are the classic exception among DNA viruses in replicating in the cytoplasm rather than the nucleus. Smallpox, also a poxvirus, was eradicated worldwide with a live-attenuated vaccine.',
    '{"B":"This describes parvovirus B19, the smallest DNA virus, which causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease and slapped-cheek rash rather than molluscum bodies.","C":"This describes hepatitis B virus, a hepadnavirus with reverse transcriptase; it does not cause skin papules.","D":"This describes adenovirus, which causes pharyngitis, hemorrhagic cystitis, and conjunctivitis but not molluscum contagiosum.","E":"This describes human papillomavirus, a naked circular dsDNA virus causing warts and cervical, anal, penile, and oropharyngeal cancer, not umbilicated molluscum lesions."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'DNA viruses overview and DNA virus mnemonics',
    'A 33-year-old man with untreated HIV infection (CD4 count 60/mm3) develops progressive clumsiness of the right arm and word-finding difficulty over 2 months. MRI shows nonenhancing demyelinating lesions in the subcortical white matter without mass effect. Brain biopsy reveals enlarged oligodendrocytes with glassy intranuclear inclusions.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative virus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An enveloped, linear dsDNA herpesvirus that remains latent in B cells"},{"id":"B","text":"A naked, linear ssDNA virus that infects endothelial cells"},{"id":"C","text":"A positive-sense ssRNA flavivirus transmitted by mosquitoes"},{"id":"D","text":"A negative-sense ssRNA paramyxovirus spread by respiratory droplets"},{"id":"E","text":"A naked, circular dsDNA polyomavirus that also causes nephropathy in kidney transplant recipients"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is caused by JC virus, a polyomavirus: a naked, circular, dsDNA virus of the same family as BK virus. JC virus reactivates in immunocompromised patients such as those with advanced HIV and destroys oligodendrocytes, producing nonenhancing white matter demyelination. The mnemonic pair is JC: Junky Cerebrum and BK: Bad Kidney, since BK virus targets the kidney in transplant patients.',
    '{"A":"This describes Epstein-Barr virus, which causes lymphomas, oral hairy leukoplakia, and mononucleosis but not PML.","B":"This describes parvovirus B19, the smallest DNA virus, which lyse RBC precursors and endothelial cells and does not demyelinate white matter.","C":"This describes a flavivirus such as West Nile or Zika; these cause meningoencephalitis or congenital malformations, not PML.","D":"This describes a paramyxovirus such as measles, which can cause SSPE, a dementing illness that follows wild-type measles years earlier and is not associated with CD4 depletion."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'DNA viruses overview and DNA virus mnemonics',
    'A 7-year-old boy with sickle cell disease presents with 2 days of fever, pallor, and fatigue. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 4 g/dL, reticulocytopenia, and a bone marrow aspirate with giant pronormoblasts but no erythroid maturation. Two weeks earlier he had a bright red rash on both cheeks.',
    'Which of the following best explains his hematologic crisis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Infection of B cells by a herpesvirus using CD21 as its receptor"},{"id":"B","text":"Infection of endothelial cells by a flavivirus causing plasma leakage and hemoconcentration"},{"id":"C","text":"Infection of CD4+ T cells by a retrovirus causing generalized marrow suppression"},{"id":"D","text":"Destruction of infected RBCs by a mosquito-borne protozoan parasite"},{"id":"E","text":"Infection of erythroid precursor cells by a naked ssDNA virus that uses the P antigen as its receptor"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Parvovirus B19 is the smallest DNA virus, a naked, linear ssDNA virus that enters erythroid precursors via the P antigen on RBCs. In patients with high RBC turnover such as sickle cell disease, infection causes transient pure red cell aplasia and an aplastic crisis. The preceding slapped-cheek rash in a child is erythema infectiosum, or fifth disease, the classic presentation in immunocompetent children.',
    '{"A":"Epstein-Barr virus uses CD21 on B cells to cause mononucleosis and lymphoproliferation; it does not produce pure red cell aplasia or a slapped-cheek rash.","B":"Dengue virus produces thrombocytopenia and plasma leakage; it does not selectively destroy erythroid precursors.","C":"HIV depletes CD4+ T cells over years; it does not cause an acute reticulocytopenic crisis with giant pronormoblasts.","D":"Plasmodium falciparum invades RBCs and causes hemolysis, not parvovirus-like marrow arrest, and the presentation would follow mosquito exposure in an endemic area."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'DNA viruses overview and DNA virus mnemonics',
    'A virology lecture presents a table of DNA virus families and asks students to predict which family represents the exception to the standard genome structure and which represents the exception to the standard intracellular site of replication.',
    'Which of the following statements about DNA viruses is correct?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Adenoviruses are enveloped, circular dsDNA viruses that replicate in the nucleus"},{"id":"B","text":"All DNA viruses have dsDNA genomes and replicate in the nucleus without exception"},{"id":"C","text":"All DNA viruses are circular, except the poxviruses and papillomaviruses"},{"id":"D","text":"Parvoviridae is the only ssDNA family and poxvirus is the only DNA virus replicating outside the nucleus"},{"id":"E","text":"Hepadnaviruses are naked, linear dsDNA viruses that replicate in the cytoplasm"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'DNA viruses have dsDNA genomes like our cells, with the single exception of Parvoviridae, which is ssDNA. All are linear except papillomavirus, polyomavirus, and hepadnavirus, which are circular. All are icosahedral and replicate in the nucleus except poxvirus, captured by the mnemonic Pox is out of the box (nucleus).',
    '{"A":"Adenoviruses are naked and linear dsDNA viruses causing pharyngitis, hemorrhagic cystitis, and conjunctivitis.","B":"Parvoviridae is ssDNA, and poxvirus replicates in the cytoplasm, so both halves of this exception-free statement are wrong.","C":"Papillomavirus and polyomavirus are circular, not exceptions to circularity; the circular families are papilloma-, polyoma-, and hepadnavirus.","E":"Hepadnaviruses are enveloped, partially dsDNA and circular, and like other DNA viruses replicate in the nucleus; HBV also carries a reverse transcriptase."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Endotoxin, exotoxin, and superantigens',
    'A 6-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department with fever, a diffuse macular rash, hypotension, and vomiting during a summer camping trip. She has a retained tampon inserted several days earlier. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus, and blistering desquamation develops on her palms and soles.',
    'Which mechanism best explains the diffuse rash, hypotension, and cytokine storm in toxic shock syndrome?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, halting protein synthesis in the host cell"},{"id":"B","text":"Cleavage of SNARE proteins at the presynaptic terminal, blocking neurotransmitter release"},{"id":"C","text":"Cross-linking of the T-cell receptor beta region to MHC class II outside the antigen binding site"},{"id":"D","text":"Overactivation of adenylate cyclase by permanent activation of the Gs protein subunit"},{"id":"E","text":"Phospholipase degradation of host cell membranes, producing myonecrosis and hemolysis"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1) is a superantigen: it cross-links the beta region of the T-cell receptor to MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells outside the antigen-binding site. This bypasses normal antigen specificity and causes overwhelming release of IL-1, IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha, producing fever, rash, and shock. The same organism''s other toxins cause different syndromes: exfoliative toxin causes scalded skin syndrome, and a heat-stable enterotoxin causes food poisoning. S pyogenes erythrogenic exotoxin A produces an analogous toxic shock-like syndrome and scarlet fever.',
    '{"A":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 inactivates protein synthesis and belongs to diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A. It does not produce a cytokine storm.","B":"SNARE cleavage is how tetanospasmin and botulinum toxin block neurotransmitter release, causing spastic and flaccid paralysis respectively. Neither acts as a superantigen.","D":"Permanent Gs activation raising cAMP is the cholera toxin mechanism, producing voluminous rice-water diarrhea rather than shock.","E":"Lecithinase (phospholipase) activity is the C perfringens alpha toxin effect, producing myonecrosis and hemolysis rather than superantigen-mediated cytokine release."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Endotoxin, exotoxin, and superantigens',
    'A 30-year-old woman returns from a trip abroad with a mild sore throat that progresses to a grey pseudomembrane on the tonsils, marked neck swelling, and later myocarditis. The organism is a gram-positive rod, and the toxin responsible is encoded in a lysogenic phage and acts by inactivating elongation factor 2 via ADP-ribosylation.',
    'Which statement about this toxin is correct?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is derived from the cell wall and cannot be made into a toxoid"},{"id":"B","text":"It is heat stable at 100 degrees C for one hour"},{"id":"C","text":"It is a lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharide released only on bacterial lysis"},{"id":"D","text":"It is poorly antigenic and induces no neutralizing antibodies"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a polypeptide whose genes are located on a plasmid or bacteriophage"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The vignette describes diphtheria toxin from Corynebacterium diphtheriae. Exotoxins are polypeptides secreted by certain gram-positive and gram-negative species, with genes located on plasmids or in bacteriophages and very high toxicity (fatal dose on the order of 1 microgram). They are heat labile, strongly antigenic, and convertible to toxoids that are used as vaccines. Diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates and inactivates EF-2, halting protein synthesis in host cells and producing the pseudomembrane, bull neck, and myocarditis.',
    '{"A":"Exotoxins are secreted proteins that can be chemically inactivated into toxoids used as vaccines (e.g., tetanus and diphtheria toxoids); they are not cell wall components.","B":"Heat stability at 100 degrees C for an hour is characteristic of endotoxin (LPS); exotoxins are destroyed rapidly at 60 degrees C, with the exceptions of staphylococcal enterotoxin, E coli heat-stable toxin, and B cereus emetic toxin.","C":"Lipid A is the endotoxin of gram-negative outer membranes, released when cells lyse or by blebs; this toxin is actively secreted by a gram-positive organism.","D":"Exotoxins are strongly antigenic and induce high-titer antibodies called antitoxins; it is endotoxin that is poorly antigenic."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Endotoxin, exotoxin, and superantigens',
    'During a laboratory review of a blood culture bottle with a gram-negative rod, the technician notes that the bacterial suspension remains toxic even after heating the lysate at 100 degrees C for an hour, and that the toxic component cannot be converted into a toxoid.',
    'Which feature of this toxic component best explains this observation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is a small polypeptide organized into an active A subunit and a binding B subunit"},{"id":"B","text":"It is an extremely heat-stable lipid structural component of the outer membrane"},{"id":"C","text":"It is encoded by a bacteriophage gene carried in a lysogenic prophage"},{"id":"D","text":"It is a superantigen that cross-links the TCR beta region to MHC class II"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a secreted polypeptide whose activity is destroyed by gentle heating"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Endotoxin is the lipid A component of lipopolysaccharide in the outer membrane of most gram-negative bacteria, and its genes are on the bacterial chromosome. Because it is a structural lipid rather than a secreted protein, it is extremely heat stable - surviving 100 degrees C for one hour - and it is poorly antigenic, so no toxoid and no vaccine are formed against it. It is released when cells lyse, or from blebs detaching from living cells, and signals through TLR4/CD14 to raise TNF, IL-1, and IL-6.',
    '{"A":"The A-B two-component toxin architecture belongs to many exotoxins (diphtheria, cholera, pertussis, tetanus, botulinum) whose A components are usually ADP ribosyltransferases.","C":"Lysogenic phage-encoded toxins include diphtheria, botulinum, cholera, Shiga, and group A streptococcal erythrogenic toxins, all proteins; LPS genes are chromosomal.","D":"Superantigen activity describes TSST-1 and erythrogenic exotoxin A, which are heat-labile proteins; endotoxin is not a superantigen.","E":"That describes exotoxins, which are destroyed rapidly at 60 degrees C; the lab findings show the opposite pattern."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Endotoxin, exotoxin, and superantigens',
    'A 24-year-old man develops severe watery diarrhea after eating undercooked pork and rice; several hours later he has flushed skin, a scrotal rash with desquamation, and profound hypotension while afebrile early on. A food handler with a hand wound cultured a gram-positive coccus in clusters.',
    'Which toxin from this organism is heat stable, survives boiling, and causes food poisoning with early emesis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Heat-stable enterotoxin"},{"id":"B","text":"Alpha toxin"},{"id":"C","text":"Erythrogenic exotoxin A"},{"id":"D","text":"Exfoliative toxin"},{"id":"E","text":"Toxic shock syndrome toxin-1"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Staphylococcal enterotoxin is the classic heat-stable exotoxin, one of only three exotoxins that survive rapid heating at 60 degrees C (along with E coli heat-stable toxin and the B cereus emetic toxin). Because preformed toxin in food is heat stable, reheating contaminated food does not prevent illness, and vomiting begins rapidly after ingestion. TSST-1 and the other S aureus toxins are heat-labile proteins that cause different syndromes - toxic shock and scalded skin syndrome respectively - which is why knowing the exceptions to exotoxin heat lability is testable knowledge.',
    '{"B":"Alpha toxin is the Clostridium perfringens lecithinase causing gas gangrene and double-zone hemolysis on blood agar.","C":"Erythrogenic exotoxin A is produced by group A Streptococcus and causes scarlet fever and a toxic shock-like syndrome.","D":"Exfoliative toxin causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome by splitting the epidermis, not a food poisoning emesis syndrome.","E":"TSST-1 is the superantigen of tampon-associated toxic shock; it is a heat-labile protein and not responsible for the rapid emesis of staphylococcal food poisoning."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Endotoxin, exotoxin, and superantigens',
    'A 45-year-old woman has fever, rash, and hypotension with a skin infection caused by group A Streptococcus. She also reports a recent scarlet-fever-like illness in her child. The physician is contrasting this picture with the classic lipopolysaccharide response seen in gram-negative sepsis.',
    'Which pair of features distinguishes the streptococcal superantigen syndrome from endotoxin-mediated shock?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is unrelated to lipopolysaccharide, is encoded in a lysogenic phage, is heat labile, and induces antitoxin antibodies"},{"id":"B","text":"It is extremely heat stable at 100 degrees C and signals through TLR4 and CD14 on host macrophages"},{"id":"C","text":"It is a lipid component of the outer membrane and produces DIC through tissue factor activation"},{"id":"D","text":"It is poorly antigenic and so cannot induce the neutralizing antibodies that would protect her"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a peptidoglycan derivative whose toxicity requires activation of the complement cascade"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Erythrogenic exotoxin A of S pyogenes is a phage-encoded exotoxin that acts as a superantigen, causing scarlet fever as well as a toxic shock-like syndrome with fever, rash, and shock. As an exotoxin it is a heat-labile polypeptide, is encoded on a bacteriophage (one of the ABCD''S toxins), and induces high-titer antitoxin antibodies. By contrast, endotoxin is a heat-stable lipid A of the gram-negative outer membrane, is poorly antigenic, is chromosome encoded, and produces its effects through macrophage, complement, and tissue factor activation. Both can produce shock, but the immunologic and biochemical properties separate them cleanly.',
    '{"B":"Extreme heat stability and TLR4/CD14 signaling are properties of lipid A endotoxin. Endotoxin is a lipid of the outer membrane, not a phage-encoded protein.","C":"A lipid outer-membrane component activating tissue factor to cause DIC describes endotoxin. That is a different molecule with different genetics.","D":"Poor antigenicity with no neutralizing antibody is the handicap of endotoxin. Exotoxins are highly antigenic and toxoids are made from them.","E":"The toxin here is a secreted polypeptide exotoxin, not a peptidoglycan derivative, and its superantigen activity does not require complement."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Entamoeba histolytica',
    'A 41-year-old man who emigrated from Mexico 2 years ago presents with 3 weeks of bloody diarrhea, tenesmus, and right upper quadrant pain. He is febrile to 38.4 C. Abdominal CT shows a large hypodense lesion in the right hepatic lobe. Colonoscopy reveals discrete ulcers with undermined edges in the cecum, and a biopsy of the ulcer margin is obtained.',
    'Which of the following findings is most likely to be reported?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Multinucleated trophozoites on stool microscopy"},{"id":"B","text":"Crescent-shaped gametocytes on a blood smear"},{"id":"C","text":"Motile flagellated trophozoites on a wet mount of vaginal discharge"},{"id":"D","text":"Flask-shaped ulcers on colon biopsy"},{"id":"E","text":"Oocysts that stain with acid-fast dye in stool"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Entamoeba histolytica causes amebiasis with bloody diarrhea (dysentery) and liver abscess containing anchovy paste exudate, plus right upper quadrant pain. Histology of the colon biopsy classically shows flask-shaped ulcers, where trophozoites undermine the mucosa. The trophozoite may contain engulfed RBCs and cysts contain up to 4 nuclei - the mnemonic is that Entamoeba Eats Erythrocytes. Because E histolytica is easily confused with the nonpathogenic E dispar, antigen testing, serology, and PCR are also used.',
    '{"A":"Multinucleated trophozoites in stool are the hallmark of Giardia lamblia, which colonizes the small bowel and produces malabsorptive fatty diarrhea rather than bloody dysentery.","B":"Crescent-shaped gametocytes are a blood-smear finding of Plasmodium falciparum, an intraerythrocytic parasite that does not form colonic ulcers or hepatic abscesses.","C":"Motile flagellated trophozoites on a wet mount of vaginal discharge describe Trichomonas vaginalis, which causes vaginitis with a strawberry cervix and never disseminates to the liver.","E":"Acid-fast staining of stool for oocysts identifies Cryptosporidium and the other acid-fast coccidia, which cause watery diarrhea without colonic ulceration or liver abscess."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Entamoeba histolytica',
    'A 30-year-old man is evaluated during a refugee health screening. He feels entirely well, has formed bowel movements once daily, and has no fever, abdominal pain, or weight loss. Stool microscopy shows cysts of Entamoeba histolytica and stool antigen testing is positive. Liver enzymes are normal and abdominal ultrasound shows no abscess.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"B","text":"Sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine"},{"id":"C","text":"Paromomycin"},{"id":"D","text":"Nitazoxanide"},{"id":"E","text":"Ivermectin"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'An asymptomatic cyst passer is treated with paromomycin, a nonabsorbed luminal agent that clears cysts from the colon, prevents later invasive disease, and reduces onward transmission. Metronidazole is reserved for symptomatic invasive amebiasis such as bloody dysentery or a liver abscess, where it acts on tissue trophozoites. First Aid separates these two drugs precisely for this reason: paromomycin for asymptomatic cyst passers, metronidazole for symptomatic disease.',
    '{"A":"Praziquantel treats trematodes and cestodes such as Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia, and has no effect on amebic cysts or trophozoites.","B":"Sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine is the Toxoplasma regimen, targeting an intracellular protozoan of brain and muscle rather than a luminal intestinal ameba.","D":"Nitazoxanide is listed for Cryptosporidium (severe or immunocompromised disease) and giardiasis, and it is not the First Aid drug for an asymptomatic ameba cyst passer.","E":"Ivermectin is the treatment for Strongyloides, Onchocerca volvulus, and scabies, and it does not eradicate Entamoeba from the bowel."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Enterococcus',
    'A 72-year-old man is admitted after elective cystoscopy and transurethral resection of a bladder tumor. He has chronic kidney disease and an indwelling urinary catheter. On postoperative day 5 he develops fever, dysuria, and flank pain. Urine culture grows gram-positive cocci that are catalase negative, PYR positive, nonhemolytic, and able to grow in 6.5% sodium chloride and in bile, with esculin hydrolysis.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"id":"B","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"id":"C","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus"},{"id":"D","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Enterococci (E faecalis and E faecium) are normal colonic microbiota that cause UTI, biliary tract infection, and infective endocarditis, typically after GI or GU procedures. They are catalase negative, PYR positive, and typically nonhemolytic, and they are hardier than streptococci, growing in 6.5% NaCl and in bile, which is the key laboratory discriminator.',
    '{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is an α-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive, bile-soluble diplococcus and is not a urinary pathogen.","C":"Streptococcus gallolyticus is a group D organism linked to colon cancer and infective endocarditis, more often seen with biliary disease and bacteremia than with post-cystoscopy UTI.","D":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus is catalase positive, coagulase negative, and novobiocin resistant, and it is a community-acquired cause of cystitis in young women.","E":"Streptococcus agalactiae is β-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP and hippurate positive, and PYR negative; it does not grow in 6.5% NaCl as reliably as enterococci."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Enterococcus',
    'A 63-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis and multiple prior hospitalizations develops persistent fever and a new murmur. Blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecium that is resistant to vancomycin and ampicillin. She previously received 6 weeks of vancomycin for a catheter-associated infection.',
    'Which of the following best explains why this organism is inherently difficult to treat with penicillin-based therapy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Intrinsic low-level resistance that makes penicillin G alone ineffective"},{"id":"B","text":"Failure to grow in routine culture media"},{"id":"C","text":"Production of a β-lactamase that destroys all penicillins"},{"id":"D","text":"An outer membrane that excludes hydrophilic β-lactams"},{"id":"E","text":"Altered peptidoglycan precursors with d-alanine–d-lactate termini"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Enterococci are intrinsically resistant to penicillin G, so penicillin monotherapy is not adequate; combination therapy with a cell wall agent plus an aminoglycoside is classic for enterococcal endocarditis. VRE are an important cause of healthcare-associated infection, and in this patient prior vancomycin exposure bred resistance in a gut organism that was a normal colonizer.',
    '{"B":"Enterococci grow readily on standard media, including the ability to grow in 6.5% NaCl and bile; culture failure is not the issue here.","C":"β-lactamase production is not the basis of intrinsic enterococcal resistance; the intrinsic mechanism is low-affinity penicillin-binding targets rather than enzymatic drug destruction.","D":"Enterococci are gram-positive organisms without an outer membrane, so porin-based exclusion of β-lactams cannot explain their resistance.","E":"Altered precursors ending in d-alanine–d-lactate are the mechanism of acquired vancomycin resistance, which explains the VRE phenotype but not the intrinsic penicillin resistance."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Epstein-Barr virus',
    'An 18-year-old college freshman presents with 6 days of fever, severe sore throat, and profound fatigue. Examination shows exudative pharyngitis, palatal petechiae, and tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show leukocytosis with 18% atypical lymphocytes and elevated transaminases.',
    'Which of the following best explains the atypical lymphocytes seen on this patient''s peripheral smear?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They are reactive cytotoxic T cells responding to infected B cells"},{"id":"B","text":"They are infected monocytes with owl''s eye inclusions"},{"id":"C","text":"They are blast cells indicating an underlying acute leukemia"},{"id":"D","text":"They are EBV-infected epithelial cells shed from the oropharynx"},{"id":"E","text":"They are EBV-infected B cells proliferating in the circulation"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'EBV infects B cells through the CD21 receptor, using the mnemonic that you must be 21 to drink beer in a Barr. The atypical lymphocytes on the peripheral smear are not infected B cells but reactive cytotoxic T cells responding to the infection. Mononucleosis presents with fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy (especially posterior cervical nodes), and hepatosplenomegaly, and patients should avoid contact sports until resolution because of the risk of splenic rupture.',
    '{"B":"Owl''s eye inclusions occur in cytomegalovirus-infected cells, and monocytes are not the atypical lymphocytes of mononucleosis.","C":"The cells are reactive and polyclonal in the setting of a self-limited viral illness; acute leukemia would show blasts with different morphologic and clinical features.","D":"EBV replicates in oropharyngeal epithelium during transmission, but the atypical circulating cells are lymphoid, not epithelial.","E":"The infected cells are B cells, but the large atypical lymphocytes seen on the smear are the reactive T cells responding to them."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Epstein-Barr virus',
    'A 20-year-old man presents with fever, sore throat, and malaise. Rapid strep testing is negative, but he is prescribed amoxicillin empirically. Two days later he returns with a diffuse maculopapular rash over the trunk and arms. Heterophile antibody testing is positive.',
    'Which of the following best explains his rash?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Direct cytopathic infection of keratinocytes by EBV"},{"id":"B","text":"A streptococcal scarlet fever eruption triggered by coinfection"},{"id":"C","text":"Immune complex deposition in dermal vessels from hepatitis B coinfection"},{"id":"D","text":"Erythema multiforme from HSV-1 reactivation"},{"id":"E","text":"A hypersensitivity reaction to amoxicillin that is characteristically seen in EBV mononucleosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Use of amoxicillin in a patient with presumed streptococcal pharyngitis who actually has EBV mononucleosis can cause a maculopapular rash. The positive heterophile antibody test (Monospot) confirms EBV infection by detecting antibodies that agglutinate sheep or horse RBCs. This drug-induced eruption is a classic First Aid association and a common exam clue that the pharyngitis was viral.',
    '{"A":"EBV does not infect keratinocytes to produce a rash; when a rash appears in mononucleosis it is usually drug related or follows the immune response.","B":"Scarlet fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis with a sandpaper rash and strawberry tongue; the negative rapid strep test and positive heterophile test point elsewhere.","C":"Serum-sickness-like immune complex disease occurs in acute hepatitis B, but this patient''s presentation and heterophile positivity define EBV.","D":"Erythema multiforme from HSV produces targetoid lesions, not a diffuse maculopapular eruption after a specific antibiotic."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Epstein-Barr virus',
    'A 9-year-old boy in equatorial Africa presents with a rapidly growing mandibular mass. Biopsy shows a starry-sky pattern of intermediate-sized B cells with high mitotic activity. Serology is positive for antibodies to Epstein-Barr virus.',
    'Which of the following best describes this malignancy and its association?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Kaposi sarcoma, an endothelial neoplasm from HHV-8"},{"id":"B","text":"Adult T-cell leukemia from HTLV"},{"id":"C","text":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease caused by cytomegalovirus"},{"id":"D","text":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma, seen in Asian adults with EBV"},{"id":"E","text":"Endemic Burkitt lymphoma, a B-cell lymphoma associated with EBV"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'EBV is associated with lymphomas, classically endemic Burkitt lymphoma, in addition to nasopharyngeal carcinoma (especially in Asian adults) and lymphoproliferative disease in transplant patients. Endemic Burkitt lymphoma presents as a jaw mass in African children and is a B-cell neoplasm with a starry-sky histologic pattern. The link is explained by EBV''s tropism for B cells through the CD21 receptor.',
    '{"A":"Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular proliferation of endothelial cells caused by HHV-8 and appears as violaceous plaques or nodules, chiefly in HIV/AIDS and transplant patients.","B":"HTLV causes adult T-cell leukemia via its reverse transcriptase, and the tumor cells are T cells rather than B cells.","C":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease is driven by EBV, not cytomegalovirus, and this child is not a transplant recipient.","D":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is an EBV-associated epithelial malignancy that occurs mainly in Asian adults, so the age and jaw presentation here do not fit."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Epstein-Barr virus',
    'A 21-year-old woman is diagnosed with mononucleosis after 1 week of fever, fatigue, and sore throat. Her examination shows a palpable spleen tip and bilateral posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. She plays on her university''s rugby team and asks whether she can resume practice.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate advice, and why?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"She should avoid contact sports until resolution because of the risk of splenic rupture"},{"id":"B","text":"She should avoid all physical activity indefinitely because EBV latent in B cells reactivates with exercise"},{"id":"C","text":"She requires immediate splenectomy to prevent rupture during the acute illness"},{"id":"D","text":"She should be treated with amoxicillin to shorten the course and prevent splenic involvement"},{"id":"E","text":"She may resume practice immediately because the infection is self-limited and splenic enlargement is not clinically significant"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'EBV mononucleosis produces fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy (especially posterior cervical nodes), and hepatosplenomegaly. Because the spleen is enlarged and friable during the illness, patients should avoid contact sports until resolution because of the risk of splenic rupture. Most cases in immunocompetent teens and young adults are self-limited, and management is supportive.',
    '{"B":"EBV is self-limited in immunocompetent hosts and does not reactivate with exercise; it can reactivate with immunosuppression.","C":"Splenectomy is not indicated for uncomplicated mononucleosis; avoiding trauma until the spleen returns to normal size is the standard approach.","D":"Amoxicillin does not shorten EBV infection and can precipitate a maculopapular rash when given during mononucleosis.","E":"Splenomegaly in mononucleosis is clinically important, and trauma during contact sports can rupture the enlarged spleen."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Escherichia coli',
    'A 4-year-old boy develops 3 days of crampy abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea after a family barbecue where hamburgers were served rare. He is afebrile. He now appears pale and lethargic, with petechiae, and laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.4 g/dL, platelets 62,000/µL, schistocytes on smear, and creatinine 2.1 mg/dL. Stool culture grows E coli that fails to ferment sorbitol.',
    'Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of his current complication?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Shiga toxin damaging endothelium and causing microthrombi, hemolysis, and reduced renal blood flow"},{"id":"B","text":"Invasion of intestinal mucosa with necrosis and inflammation"},{"id":"C","text":"Heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins causing secretory diarrhea"},{"id":"D","text":"Adherence to apical surface flattening villi and preventing absorption"},{"id":"E","text":"P fimbriae adhering to the renal pelvis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is enterohemorrhagic E coli O157:H7 infection complicated by hemolytic-uremic syndrome. Shiga toxin damages endothelium, and microthrombi cause mechanical hemolysis with schistocytes, platelet consumption with thrombocytopenia, and decreased renal blood flow with acute kidney injury. EHEC is associated with hemorrhage, hamburgers, and HUS, and it does not ferment sorbitol unlike other E coli.',
    '{"B":"Mucosal invasion with necrosis and inflammation describes enteroinvasive E coli, which causes a dysentery-like illness mirroring Shigella but does not cause HUS.","C":"Heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins are the mechanism of enterotoxigenic E coli, which causes watery traveler''s diarrhea without invasion or systemic complications.","D":"Adherence with villus flattening and malabsorption is the mechanism of enteropathogenic E coli, which causes infantile watery diarrhea without a toxin.","E":"P fimbriae mediate adherence to uroepithelium in urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis, not the systemic microangiopathy seen with bloody diarrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Escherichia coli',
    'A 26-year-old business traveler to Mexico develops 4 days of watery diarrhea and abdominal cramping 3 days into her trip. She has no fever, no blood in the stool, and no vomiting. She is well hydrated. Stool studies show no leukocytes and no parasites, and the illness is self-limited over the following week.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism responsible for this patient''s diarrhea?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Capsular polysaccharide-driven intestinal inflammation"},{"id":"B","text":"Flattening of villi by tight adherence to enterocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"Heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins without inflammation or invasion"},{"id":"D","text":"Invasive mucosal damage producing dysentery"},{"id":"E","text":"Shiga toxin-mediated endothelial damage"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is traveler''s diarrhea caused by enterotoxigenic E coli, which produces heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins and causes watery diarrhea with no inflammation or invasion. The absence of fever, blood, and fecal leukocytes fits a purely toxigenic, noninvasive mechanism.',
    '{"A":"The K capsule of E coli is associated with pneumonia and neonatal meningitis rather than diarrhea; capsule-driven inflammation is not the mechanism here.","B":"Villus flattening from adherence is the mechanism of enteropathogenic E coli, which typically affects infants and produces malabsorptive diarrhea rather than traveler''s diarrhea.","D":"Invasiveness with mucosal necrosis is characteristic of enteroinvasive E coli and Shigella, which produce fever and bloody dysentery rather than afebrile watery stool.","E":"Shiga toxin from enterohemorrhagic E coli produces bloody diarrhea and hemolytic-uremic syndrome, not afebrile watery diarrhea without blood."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Escherichia coli',
    'A 29-year-old woman presents with 2 days of dysuria, urinary frequency, and suprapubic pain. She has had three similar episodes in the past year, one of which progressed to fever and flank pain. Urine culture grows a lactose-fermenting, indole-positive gram-negative rod that forms pink colonies on MacConkey agar and green-sheen colonies on EMB agar.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors most directly explains her history of upper urinary tract involvement?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"K capsular polysaccharide"},{"id":"B","text":"P fimbriae (P pili)"},{"id":"C","text":"LPS endotoxin"},{"id":"D","text":"Shiga toxin"},{"id":"E","text":"Heat-stable enterotoxin"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'P fimbriae (P pili) of E coli mediate adherence to uroepithelium and are associated with cystitis and pyelonephritis, explaining this patient''s prior episode of fever and flank pain. E coli is the most common cause of uncomplicated UTI, grows as a lactose fermenter with pink colonies on MacConkey and green sheen on EMB, and is indole positive.',
    '{"A":"The K capsule is associated with E coli pneumonia and neonatal meningitis rather than with ascending urinary infection.","C":"LPS endotoxin drives the systemic response of septic shock but does not explain adherence to and ascent through the urinary tract.","D":"Shiga toxin is produced by enterohemorrhagic E coli O157:H7 and causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic-uremic syndrome, not UTI.","E":"Heat-stable enterotoxin belongs to enterotoxigenic E coli and causes watery traveler''s diarrhea, which is a gastrointestinal syndrome."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Escherichia coli',
    'A 3-week-old neonate born at 34 weeks of gestation develops poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability, and a bulging fontanelle. Cerebrospinal fluid culture grows a lactose-fermenting, indole-positive gram-negative rod. Empiric therapy has already been started. The infant''s mother had a complicated delivery with prolonged rupture of membranes.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors of this organism is most strongly associated with neonatal meningitis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Enteropathogenic adherence factors"},{"id":"B","text":"K capsule"},{"id":"C","text":"Indole production"},{"id":"D","text":"Heat-labile enterotoxin"},{"id":"E","text":"P fimbriae"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'The K capsule of E coli is the virulence factor associated with pneumonia and neonatal meningitis, and E coli is a leading cause of neonatal meningitis alongside group B streptococcus. The organism is a lactose-fermenting, indole-positive gram-negative rod, consistent with E coli rather than the other neonatal pathogens.',
    '{"A":"Enteropathogenic adherence factors cause infantile diarrhea through villus flattening and malabsorption, not meningitis.","C":"Indole production is a useful biochemical marker for identifying E coli but is not a virulence factor for meningitis.","D":"Heat-labile enterotoxin causes watery traveler''s diarrhea and plays no role in neonatal central nervous system infection.","E":"P fimbriae mediate urinary tract adherence and pyelonephritis rather than meningeal invasion."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Filarial worms and Toxocara',
    'A 2-year-old boy is brought in after his mother noticed 2 months of pica with eating dirt and hair from the family dog. He was adopted from a rural area of Argentina. Examination shows mild hepatomegaly, and fundoscopy of the right eye shows a white retinal granuloma with reduced fixation. Laboratory studies show eosinophilia of 22% and a mildly elevated alkaline phosphatase level.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Kala-azar with pancytopenia and massive splenomegaly"},{"id":"B","text":"Lymphatic filariasis with lower extremity lymphedema"},{"id":"C","text":"Cutaneous larva migrans with a serpiginous rash"},{"id":"D","text":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity"},{"id":"E","text":"Visceral larva migrans with hepatomegaly and a retinal granuloma"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Visceral larva migrans is caused by Toxocara canis, a dog roundworm whose eggs are ingested from soil contaminated with dog feces, which explains the pica in a toddler who lives with a dog. The larvae do not mature in humans but migrate through the blood and inflame the liver, eyes, central nervous system, and heart, producing visual impairment, seizures, or myocarditis while many patients remain asymptomatic. First Aid lists albendazole as the treatment.',
    '{"A":"Kala-azar with pancytopenia and massive splenomegaly is Leishmania donovani, an intracellular parasite of macrophages spread by the sandfly.","B":"Lymphatic filariasis with lymphedema follows mosquito-borne Wuchereria bancrofti or Brugia malayi infection of lymph nodes, not ingestion of Toxocara eggs.","C":"Cutaneous larva migrans with a serpiginous rash comes from hookworm larvae penetrating the skin, and the eruption stays in the skin rather than producing a retinal granuloma or hepatomegaly.","D":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity describe Onchocerca volvulus river blindness, transmitted by a black fly bite rather than by ingestion of dog-fecal eggs."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Filarial worms and Toxocara',
    'A 45-year-old man who emigrated from Cameroon 3 months ago presents with years of episodic fever and now a progressive swelling of the right leg. He reports sleeping outdoors and being bitten by mosquitoes at night for decades in his home village. Examination shows edema and thickened, warty skin extending from the right thigh to the shin, with discrete inguinal lymph nodes. Laboratory studies show eosinophilia and high IgE, and a circulating filarial antigen test is positive.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient''s condition?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Metronidazole"},{"id":"B","text":"Diethylcarbamazine"},{"id":"C","text":"Ivermectin"},{"id":"D","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"E","text":"Albendazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti or Brugia malayi occurs when adult worms lodge in lymph nodes, causing inflammation and then chronic lymphedema that can progress to elephantiasis, with symptom onset typically after 9 months to a year. The mosquito vector explains the nocturnal outdoor bites. First Aid lists diethylcarbamazine for this disease, the same drug used for Loa loa, whereas ivermectin is the drug for Onchocerca volvulus and Strongyloides.',
    '{"A":"Metronidazole is a protozoal drug for Giardia, Entamoeba, and Trichomonas, and it has no anthelmintic activity against filariae.","C":"Ivermectin is the First Aid drug for Onchocerca volvulus river blindness and for Strongyloides, so it is the wrong filaricide for the elephantiasis-producing Wuchereria species.","D":"Praziquantel treats trematodes and cestodes such as Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia, and it has no effect on filarial nematodes.","E":"Albendazole is used for Echinococcus hydatid cysts and for neurocysticercosis, placing it with tissue cestodes rather than lymphatic filariasis in the First Aid table."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Giardia lamblia',
    'A 28-year-old man returns from a 12-day hiking trip in the Rocky Mountains and reports 2 weeks of intermittent periumbilical cramping, excess belching and flatus, and bulky stools that float and smell foul. He has had no fever and no blood in the stool. During the trip he drank untreated water from mountain streams. He takes no medications and has no allergies. Laboratory studies show no anemia and a normal white blood cell count.',
    'Which of the following findings on stool studies is most likely in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a purulent exudate on colon biopsy"},{"id":"B","text":"Extracellular trypomastigotes on a peripheral blood smear"},{"id":"C","text":"Small oocysts that stain with acid-fast dye on stool microscopy"},{"id":"D","text":"Multinucleated trophozoites seen on stool microscopy"},{"id":"E","text":"Trophozoites containing engulfed red blood cells in the cytoplasm"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Giardia lamblia classically causes bloating, flatulence, and foul-smelling, nonbloody, fatty diarrhea in campers and hikers who ingest cysts from untreated water - a mnemonic framed around fat-rich chocolates for the fatty stools of Giardia. Diagnosis is made by stool microscopy showing multinucleated trophozoites or cysts, by stool antigen detection, or by PCR. Malabsorption of fat produces the floating, greasy stools described here, which is why this patient has no fever and no blood.',
    '{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers on a colon biopsy are the histologic hallmark of invasive amebic colitis from Entamoeba histolytica, and colonoscopy is not the diagnostic route for a small-bowel flagellate.","B":"Extracellular trypomastigotes on a peripheral blood smear are the finding in African sleeping sickness from Trypanosoma brucei, a tsetse fly-borne parasite that causes progressive somnolence rather than diarrhea.","C":"Acid-fast staining of stool for small round oocysts is the classic rapid test for Cryptosporidium, which produces watery diarrhea rather than the malabsorptive, fatty stool described.","E":"A trophozoite containing engulfed RBCs is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, the ameba whose nickname reflects that it eats erythrocytes, and it is associated with bloody dysentery rather than nonbloody diarrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Giardia lamblia',
    'A 9-year-old boy is brought to the clinic in September with 10 days of crampy abdominal pain, excess flatus, and greasy stools that are difficult to flush. He attended a summer camp where he drank untreated water from a creek and from a garden hose. Stool antigen testing confirms Giardia lamblia. He has no drug allergies and takes no medications.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient''s condition?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Praziquantel"},{"id":"B","text":"Ivermectin"},{"id":"C","text":"Metronidazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Paromomycin"},{"id":"E","text":"Nitazoxanide"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'First Aid lists metronidazole, tinidazole, and nitazoxanide as the treatments for giardiasis, and metronidazole is the agent most commonly tested because it also covers Entamoeba histolytica and Trichomonas vaginalis. Cryptosporidium is the high-yield exception: that parasite is not reliably cleared by metronidazole and is managed instead with nitazoxanide in severe or immunocompromised disease plus filtration of drinking water. Paromomycin, in turn, belongs to the asymptomatic ameba cyst passer.',
    '{"A":"Praziquantel is the anthelmintic for Schistosoma, Clonorchis, Taenia, and Diphyllobothrium, and it has no activity against a lumen-dwelling flagellate protozoan.","B":"Ivermectin is used for Strongyloides, Onchocerca volvulus river blindness, and scabies, and it does not eradicate Giardia from the small bowel.","D":"Paromomycin is the luminal agent First Aid recommends for asymptomatic Entamoeba histolytica cyst passers, and it is not a standard first-line treatment for symptomatic giardiasis.","E":"Nitazoxanide is a listed alternative for giardiasis, but it is the preferred drug for Cryptosporidium in severe or immunocompromised disease, so it is not the agent to choose first here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Gram-positive vs gram-negative cell envelope',
    'In a microbiology lab, a student prepares a heat-fixed slide from a colony and performs a Gram stain. After the decolorizer and counterstain steps, the organisms appear red to pink under oil immersion.',
    'Which feature of the bacterial cell envelope explains retention of crystal violet by gram-positive but not gram-negative organisms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The presence of lipoteichoic acid within the outer leaflet of the envelope"},{"id":"B","text":"A lipid-rich mycolic acid layer that repels the decolorizer and resists staining"},{"id":"C","text":"A thick peptidoglycan layer that lies outside the outer membrane of the cell"},{"id":"D","text":"The presence of an outer membrane that traps the crystal violet dye in the periplasmic space"},{"id":"E","text":"A thick peptidoglycan layer that retains crystal violet toward the cell surface"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The Gram stain is the first-line laboratory test in bacterial identification. Bacteria with a thick peptidoglycan layer retain the crystal violet dye and appear purple (gram-positive), whereas bacteria with a thin peptidoglycan layer lose it during decolorization and take up the red or pink counterstain (gram-negative). In gram-negative organisms the peptidoglycan sits in the periplasm, sandwiched between the cytoplasmic membrane and the outer membrane. This structural difference is the reason the Gram reaction correlates with so many clinical choices from empiric therapy to organism identification.',
    '{"A":"Lipoteichoic acid extends from the cytoplasmic membrane to the exterior in gram-positive organisms and induces TNF-alpha and IL-1; it is not an outer-leaflet molecule and does not determine dye retention.","B":"Mycolic acid is the acid-fast lipid of Mycobacteria and Nocardia that makes them poorly Gram-stainable and requires Ziehl-Neelsen; it is not part of the normal gram-positive envelope.","C":"Gram-negative organisms have a thin peptidoglycan in the periplasm and an outer membrane above it; gram-positive organisms have no outer membrane at all.","D":"An outer membrane is exactly why gram-negative organisms lose the dye: it is removed by the decolorizer, and their thin peptidoglycan does not hold crystal violet; it is not what retains it."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Gram-positive vs gram-negative cell envelope',
    'A 34-year-old woman has a deep surgical-site infection after a contaminated wound. The isolate is a gram-negative rod, and the team is reviewing its envelope architecture because a beta-lactamase-producing strain is suspected.',
    'Where in the gram-negative cell envelope does peptidoglycan reside?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Attached only to the cytoplasmic membrane, as a single adherent monolayer"},{"id":"B","text":"In the periplasm, between the cytoplasmic membrane and the outer membrane"},{"id":"C","text":"On the outermost surface, outside the layer of lipopolysaccharide and capsule"},{"id":"D","text":"Within the outer membrane leaflet itself, anchored by the lipid A moiety"},{"id":"E","text":"Between the capsule and the cell wall, as a loose adherent polysaccharide matrix"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'In gram-negative bacteria the peptidoglycan is located in the periplasm, the space between the cytoplasmic membrane and the outer membrane. That periplasm also accumulates components exiting the cell, including hydrolytic enzymes such as beta-lactamases. This architecture explains why gram-negative organisms can secrete beta-lactamases into a compartment that bathes the peptidoglycan, a key element of resistance to penicillins. The outer membrane above contains LPS in its outer leaflet and porins for transport, and the wide periplasmic space is a diagrammatic feature unique to gram-negative organisms.',
    '{"A":"Peptidoglycan is a netlike polymer, not a monolayer on the membrane; the peptidoglycan wall is a sugar backbone cross-linked by transpeptidases.","C":"Outside the surface is the capsule if present; peptidoglycan is always on the inner side of the outer membrane, and LPS (not peptidoglycan) faces the exterior.","D":"Lipid A anchors LPS in the outer leaflet; peptidoglycan is not a lipid and does not intercalate into the membrane leaflet.","E":"That describes a loose matrix, not peptidoglycan: the slime layer is a loose network of polysaccharides that mediates adherence and biofilm formation."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Gram-positive vs gram-negative cell envelope',
    'A 71-year-old man with a hip prosthesis develops a slowly progressive wound infection. The isolate is a gram-positive coccus in clusters, and the laboratory reports that it grows in a wide range of environments and expresses no outer membrane porins.',
    'Which statement best describes a difference in envelope architecture between this organism and a gram-negative rod?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Only the gram-negative organism has a periplasmic space with a wide beta-lactamase compartment"},{"id":"B","text":"Only the gram-negative organism possesses a cytoplasmic membrane bilayer with embedded proteins"},{"id":"C","text":"Only the gram-positive organism possesses lipopolysaccharide in its cell wall envelope"},{"id":"D","text":"Only the gram-negative organism possesses bacterial ribosomes for protein synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"Only the gram-positive organism possesses a peptidoglycan cell wall layer outside its membrane"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Gram-positive organisms have a thick peptidoglycan layer, teichoic and lipoteichoic acids extending from the membrane to the exterior, and no outer membrane. Gram-negative organisms have a thin peptidoglycan in a wide periplasm containing beta-lactamases, plus an outer membrane with LPS and porins. That periplasm-wide, beta-lactamase-rich space is unique to gram-negative organisms and is why gram-negative resistance to penicillins spreads readily. Both groups share the cytoplasmic membrane, where penicillin-binding proteins perform cell wall synthesis.',
    '{"B":"All bacteria have a cytoplasmic membrane, described as a phospholipid bilayer sac with embedded proteins; it is the site of oxidative and transport enzymes and of PBPs.","C":"Lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) is present only in gram-negative organisms, found in the outer membrane; the statement reverses reality.","D":"Ribosomes are universal bacterial structures and the target of many antibiotics; they are not an envelope distinguishing feature.","E":"Peptidoglycan is common to both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms; only the quantity and location differ."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Gram-positive vs gram-negative cell envelope',
    'A 28-year-old man with a ventricular septal defect and dental plaque develops fever and a new murmur. Blood cultures grow a gram-positive coccus in chains that is alpha-hemolytic. The team links the organism''s ability to adhere to damaged endocardium to an envelope surface structure.',
    'Which envelope structure of gram-positive organisms serves as the major site for anchoring cell wall synthesis enzymes and is also where lipoteichoic acid arises?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The outer membrane"},{"id":"B","text":"The capsule"},{"id":"C","text":"The cytoplasmic membrane"},{"id":"D","text":"The periplasm"},{"id":"E","text":"The peptidoglycan peptide side chain"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'The cytoplasmic membrane is a phospholipid bilayer sac with embedded proteins, including penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) and other enzymes, and it is the site of oxidative and transport enzymes. In gram-positive organisms, lipoteichoic acids extend from this membrane all the way to the exterior. Understanding this arrangement explains why beta-lactams that acylate PBPs act at the membrane/wall interface, and why lipoteichoic acid is a proinflammatory surface molecule that is not covered by an outer membrane. It also accounts for the general absence of a periplasmic beta-lactamase compartment in these organisms.',
    '{"A":"An outer membrane is absent from gram-positive organisms; it exists only in gram-negative cells, where LPS sits in the outer leaflet.","B":"The capsule is a discrete polysaccharide layer that protects against phagocytosis; it does not house synthetic enzymes.","D":"A periplasm exists in gram-negative organisms, as the space between cytoplasmic and outer membranes; gram-positive organisms do not have this compartment.","E":"Peptide side chains are the substrate of transpeptidase cross-linking within peptidoglycan, not the membrane site where enzymes and lipoteichoic acids arise."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Haemophilus influenzae',
    'A 3-year-old unvaccinated child presents with high fever, drooling, and severe difficulty swallowing. He sits leaning forward with his neck extended and a muffled voice. Lateral neck radiograph shows a thickened epiglottis producing a thumb sign, and the epiglottis appears cherry red on endoscopy. Blood cultures grow a small gram-negative coccobacillary rod.',
    'Which of the following culture conditions is required to isolate this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Eaton agar containing sterols"},{"id":"B","text":"Chocolate agar, which supplies factors V (NAD+) and X (hematin)"},{"id":"C","text":"Charcoal yeast extract medium with iron and cysteine"},{"id":"D","text":"MacConkey agar incubated at 42 degrees Celsius"},{"id":"E","text":"Alkaline media with a high pH"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillary rod that requires factors V (NAD+) and X (hematin), both provided by chocolate agar. It can also be grown with Staphylococcus aureus, which supplies factor V through red blood cell hemolysis. This organism causes epiglottitis with a cherry-red epiglottis and a thumb sign on lateral neck radiograph, as well as meningitis, otitis media, and pneumonia.',
    '{"A":"Eaton agar with sterols is used for Mycoplasma pneumoniae, which lacks a cell wall.","C":"Charcoal yeast extract with iron and cysteine is the special medium for Legionella pneumophila.","D":"MacConkey agar at 42 degrees Celsius supports Campylobacter jejuni, a curved gram-negative rod causing bloody diarrhea.","E":"Alkaline media is used to select Vibrio cholerae, which grows at high pH."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Haemophilus influenzae',
    'A 1-year-old child with a febrile illness is diagnosed with meningitis. Cerebrospinal fluid culture grows a small gram-negative coccobacillary rod. The child has received no routine immunizations. The organism produces IgA protease and is encapsulated.',
    'Which of the following components is included in the vaccine that prevents this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Type b capsular polysaccharide, polyribosylribitol phosphate, conjugated to a protein carrier"},{"id":"B","text":"Whole-cell heat-inactivated organism administered with alum"},{"id":"C","text":"Type b capsular polysaccharide alone without any protein carrier"},{"id":"D","text":"A toxoid directed against the lipopolysaccharide endotoxin"},{"id":"E","text":"Purified pertussis toxin combined with filamentous hemagglutinin"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccine contains the type b capsular polysaccharide polyribosylribitol phosphate conjugated to diphtheria toxoid or another protein, which makes it immunogenic in young infants. It is given between 2 and 18 months of age. This is why encapsulated type b strains are no longer the leading cause of invasive Haemophilus disease and nontypeable strains predominate.',
    '{"B":"Whole-cell inactivated vaccines are a historical pertussis formulation, not the Haemophilus vaccine, which is a conjugate polysaccharide vaccine.","C":"The polysaccharide alone is poorly immunogenic in infants, which is why it must be conjugated to a carrier protein.","D":"The vaccine targets the capsule rather than the lipopolysaccharide, and a toxoid against endotoxin is not how Haemophilus vaccine works.","E":"Pertussis toxin and filamentous hemagglutinin are components of pertussis vaccines, not of the Haemophilus vaccine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Haemophilus influenzae',
    'A 2-year-old child in daycare has had four episodes of acute otitis media in the past year and one episode of conjunctivitis. He is fully vaccinated, including with the Haemophilus influenzae type b conjugate vaccine. Ear fluid culture grows Haemophilus influenzae, and the laboratory reports the organism is unencapsulated.',
    'Which of the following best explains why this child developed this infection despite vaccination?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Nontypeable unencapsulated strains are now the most common cause of mucosal and invasive Haemophilus infections"},{"id":"B","text":"The vaccine failed because it contains only killed whole organisms"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism produces IgA protease, which directly degrades the capsular polysaccharide vaccine"},{"id":"D","text":"Unencapsulated strains are resistant to the vaccine because they acquire the mecA gene"},{"id":"E","text":"The child must have an underlying complement deficiency that prevents the vaccine response"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Nontypeable, unencapsulated strains are the most common cause of mucosal infections such as otitis media, conjunctivitis, and bronchitis, and they are also a common cause of invasive disease now that the type b capsular vaccine is in use. The vaccine targets only the type b capsule, so it does not protect against unencapsulated strains. Haemophilus also produces IgA protease, which facilitates mucosal colonization.',
    '{"B":"The Haemophilus vaccine is a conjugate polysaccharide-protein vaccine, not a killed whole-organism vaccine.","C":"IgA protease cleaves mucosal antibody and aids colonization; it does not degrade the injected vaccine antigen.","D":"The mecA gene confers methicillin resistance in Staphylococcus aureus by producing an altered penicillin-binding protein and is unrelated to this organism.","E":"No complement deficiency is required; unencapsulated strains simply lack the target of the type b vaccine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Helicobacter pylori',
    'A 46-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of burning epigastric pain that wakes him at night and is relieved by eating. He takes no NSAIDs. Physical examination is notable only for mild epigastric tenderness. Upper endoscopy reveals a 1-cm clean-based ulcer in the duodenal bulb, and a gastric biopsy shows curved gram-negative rods colonizing the antral mucosa.',
    'Which of the following properties of this organism most directly allows it to establish persistent infection in the gastric mucosa?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Production of urease, which generates ammonia and buffers the local acidic environment"},{"id":"B","text":"Production of an ADP-ribosylating exotoxin that permanently activates Gs and raises intracellular cAMP"},{"id":"C","text":"A polysaccharide capsule that prevents opsonization and phagocytosis by neutrophils"},{"id":"D","text":"An exotoxin that inhibits elongation factor 2 and halts host protein synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"The ability to form heat-resistant spores that survive gastric acid and reseed the mucosa"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Helicobacter pylori is a curved, flagellated, gram-negative rod that is catalase, oxidase and urease positive. Its urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes gastric acid in the immediate microenvironment, creating the alkaline niche the organism needs to survive in the stomach. The same enzyme is the basis of the urea breath test, and the fecal antigen test is the other noninvasive diagnostic option. H pylori colonizes mainly the gastric antrum and causes gastritis and peptic ulcer disease, especially duodenal ulcers, and it is a risk factor for gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma.',
    '{"B":"This describes the cholera toxin mechanism. Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the Gs alpha subunit, locking it in the active state and driving secretory diarrhea; H pylori does not produce it.","C":"H pylori does not rely on a polysaccharide capsule. Capsules are the antiphagocytic virulence factor of organisms such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Cryptococcus neoformans.","D":"Inhibition of elongation factor 2 is the mechanism of diphtheria toxin and of Pseudomonas exotoxin A, both of which halt protein synthesis and cause cell death rather than chronic mucosal colonization.","E":"H pylori is a non-spore-forming organism. Heat-resistant spores are characteristic of Bacillus and Clostridium species, not of this curved rod."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Helicobacter pylori',
    'A 52-year-old woman with a duodenal ulcer is found to have Helicobacter pylori infection on biopsy. She has no medication allergies and has never received treatment for this infection. Her physician plans eradication therapy.',
    'Which of the following regimens is the most appropriate initial treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Metronidazole monotherapy for 14 days"},{"id":"B","text":"Bismuth subsalicylate alone until symptoms resolve"},{"id":"C","text":"Doxycycline plus surgical ulcer plication"},{"id":"D","text":"Amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a proton pump inhibitor"},{"id":"E","text":"Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Standard initial therapy for H pylori is triple therapy: amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a proton pump inhibitor. In penicillin-allergic patients metronidazole is substituted for amoxicillin. When there is concern about macrolide resistance, bismuth-based quadruple therapy is used instead. Eradication matters because persistent infection is a risk factor for recurrent peptic ulcer disease, gastric adenocarcinoma, and MALT lymphoma, and curing the infection substantially reduces ulcer recurrence.',
    '{"A":"Metronidazole is a component of H pylori regimens rather than an effective single agent. Monotherapy reliably fails to eradicate the organism and promotes resistance.","B":"Bismuth subsalicylate alone does not eradicate H pylori. Bismuth is used as one component of quadruple therapy, always combined with two antibiotics and a proton pump inhibitor.","C":"Doxycycline is used in quadruple therapy for some resistant regimens and in rickettsial disease, but it is not a standard H pylori agent, and ulcer plication is not indicated for uncomplicated duodenal ulcer disease.","E":"Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is a regimen for serious gram-positive and gram-negative systemic infections. It is not used for H pylori, an organism that responds to oral acid-suppressing triple therapy."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Helicobacter pylori',
    'A 61-year-old man from Colombia presents with early satiety, weight loss, and epigastric pain. Endoscopy shows a large irregular ulcerated mass in the gastric body, and biopsy reveals adenocarcinoma with surrounding chronic gastritis and numerous curved gram-negative rods. He asks what the connection is between the infection and the tumor.',
    'Chronic infection with this organism is most strongly associated with which of the following malignancies?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma"},{"id":"B","text":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},{"id":"C","text":"Colorectal adenocarcinoma"},{"id":"D","text":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},{"id":"E","text":"Gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Chronic H pylori infection drives persistent gastric mucosal inflammation and is a recognized risk factor for both gastric adenocarcinoma and gastric MALT lymphoma. The organism colonizes mainly the antrum and causes chronic gastritis and peptic ulcer disease, and long-standing infection can progress through atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia to adenocarcinoma. Eradication of the organism can induce regression of low-grade MALT lymphoma, which illustrates how directly the infection drives that particular tumor.',
    '{"A":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma is associated with tobacco and alcohol use, achalasia, and hot beverage consumption rather than with H pylori. In fact, gastric colonization may be inversely associated with esophageal adenocarcinoma.","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma arises in the setting of chronic hepatitis B or C infection, aflatoxin exposure, or cirrhosis. H pylori is not a hepatic oncogen.","C":"Colorectal adenocarcinoma is linked to adenomatous polyposis syndromes, Lynch syndrome, and inflammatory bowel disease. The classic bacterial association for colonic malignancy is Streptococcus gallolyticus bacteremia, not H pylori.","D":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is associated with smoking, chronic pancreatitis, and inherited syndromes such as BRCA and PALB2 mutations. H pylori is not an established cause."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Helicobacter pylori',
    'A 38-year-old woman undergoes evaluation for dyspepsia. Rather than proceed straight to endoscopy, her physician orders a noninvasive test in which she swallows a labeled substrate and exhaled breath is analyzed afterward. The result is positive, and she is treated for a gastric infection.',
    'This breath test detects which activity of the causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Hydrogen sulfide production on TSI agar"},{"id":"B","text":"Urease-mediated cleavage of urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide"},{"id":"C","text":"Oxidase activity in the respiratory chain"},{"id":"D","text":"Catalase-mediated breakdown of hydrogen peroxide"},{"id":"E","text":"Lactose fermentation with acid production"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'The urea breath test relies on the organism''s high urease activity. The patient swallows urea labeled with carbon-13 or carbon-14, and if H pylori is present its urease splits the urea into ammonia and labeled carbon dioxide, which is absorbed and exhaled. Exhaled labeled CO2 therefore indicates active infection. The fecal antigen test is the other common noninvasive option. Both tests exploit features that make this organism uniquely adapted to the stomach.',
    '{"A":"Hydrogen sulfide production on TSI agar identifies organisms such as Salmonella and Proteus. It has no role in breath testing and is not a feature of H pylori.","C":"H pylori is oxidase positive, but oxidase activity is not what a breath test measures; it is a laboratory characteristic used to help identify the organism in culture.","D":"H pylori is catalase positive, but catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide rather than a swallowed labeled substrate, so it produces no detectable breath signal.","E":"H pylori does not ferment lactose. Lactose fermentation is the distinguishing feature of organisms such as Escherichia coli and Klebsiella on MacConkey agar."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis A and B',
    'A 27-year-old man presents with 5 days of fever, nausea, anorexia, and right upper quadrant discomfort, followed by dark urine and jaundice. ALT is 1450 U/L and bilirubin is 5.2 mg/dL. He returned 4 weeks ago from a trip to Central America and recalls eating raw shellfish at a beach restaurant. Serology is positive for IgM anti-HAV and negative for HBsAg.',
    'Which of the following best explains why hepatitis A is not destroyed by gastric acid and small bowel contents?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It establishes a chronic carrier state that shields it from digestive enzymes"},{"id":"B","text":"It is a hepadnavirus whose DNA polymerase repairs acid-induced capsid damage"},{"id":"C","text":"It enters through the respiratory tract and only later reaches the liver hematogenously"},{"id":"D","text":"It is an enveloped picornavirus that is stabilized by the lipid bilayer at low pH"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a naked virus that lacks an envelope, resisting the gut, whereas hepatitis B is parenterally transmitted"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Hepatitis A is a naked positive-sense ssRNA picornavirus, and naked viruses are not destroyed by the gut; the mnemonic is that the vowels (A and E) hit your bowels. Its transmission is fecal-oral, classically via shellfish, contaminated water, travelers, and day care outbreaks, and it causes acute self-limited hepatitis with IgM anti-HAV as the best acute marker and no carrier state.',
    '{"A":"HAV has no carrier state; chronic carriage is a feature of hepatitis B and C, not A.","B":"HAV is a picornavirus, not a hepadnavirus; HAV has no DNA polymerase or reverse transcriptase.","C":"HAV transmission is fecal-oral; respiratory spread is not a described route.","D":"HAV is nonenveloped; a lipid envelope would make it more, not less, vulnerable to acid and detergents."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis A and B',
    'A 62-year-old man is seen for fatigue and mild jaundice. Laboratory studies show positive HBsAg, positive IgG anti-HBc, positive anti-HBe, and negative IgM anti-HBc. His ALT is mildly elevated. He has been followed for 3 years with persistently positive HBsAg.',
    'Which of the following best interprets this serologic pattern?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Chronic hepatitis B with low transmissibility, indicated by anti-HBe positivity"},{"id":"B","text":"Resolved hepatitis B with no residual virus"},{"id":"C","text":"Hepatitis D superinfection with suppressed HBV markers"},{"id":"D","text":"Acute hepatitis B in the window period, with IgM anti-HBc pending"},{"id":"E","text":"Immunity from prior hepatitis B vaccination alone"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Persistent HBsAg beyond 6 months indicates chronic infection, and IgG anti-HBc confirms prior exposure; IgM anti-HBc would mark acute infection. Anti-HBe appears when HBeAg is cleared and indicates low transmissibility with a better prognosis. The HBeAg/anti-HBe axis reflects active viral replication, since HBeAg is secreted into the circulation and is not part of the mature virion.',
    '{"B":"Resolved infection would show anti-HBs and anti-HBc positivity with negative HBsAg; persistent HBsAg means ongoing infection.","C":"HDV superinfection would show HDV markers such as anti-HDV; it does not produce isolated anti-HBe positivity as the explanation for chronic HBsAg carriage.","D":"During the window period HBsAg has cleared and IgM anti-HBc may be the sole marker; here HBsAg and IgG anti-HBc are both positive and the pattern has persisted for years.","E":"Vaccination produces anti-HBs without anti-HBc, because anti-HBc requires infection with intact virus, not the surface antigen vaccine."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis A and B',
    'A 24-year-old woman who works as a nurse sustains a needlestick injury from a patient known to have chronic hepatitis B. She has no record of vaccination and her baseline serology shows negative HBsAg, negative anti-HBs, and negative anti-HBc.',
    'Which of the following serologic results would document successful hepatitis B vaccination rather than natural infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Positive anti-HBs with negative anti-HBc"},{"id":"B","text":"Positive anti-HBc (IgG) with negative anti-HBs"},{"id":"C","text":"Positive HBeAg with negative anti-HBs"},{"id":"D","text":"Positive HBsAg with positive IgM anti-HBc"},{"id":"E","text":"Positive IgM anti-HAV with negative anti-HBs"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Anti-HBs indicates immunity to hepatitis B from vaccination or from recovery after infection, and it protects against reinfection. Vaccination uses surface antigen only, so anti-HBc never appears; anti-HBc requires infection with whole virus. Isolated anti-HBs therefore marks vaccine-induced immunity, whereas anti-HBs plus anti-HBc marks recovery from natural infection.',
    '{"B":"Anti-HBc positivity indicates infection with intact virions rather than vaccination, and without anti-HBs this pattern suggests remote or chronic infection rather than immunity.","C":"HBeAg reflects active viral replication and high transmissibility; it is never a marker of immunity from vaccine.","D":"HBsAg with IgM anti-HBc indicates acute hepatitis B infection, which is the outcome to be prevented, not evidence of protection.","E":"IgM anti-HAV is the best test for acute hepatitis A and says nothing about hepatitis B immunity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis A and B',
    'A 30-year-old woman presents with fever, arthralgias, and a urticarial rash, followed 1 week later by jaundice. Serology shows HBsAg positive, IgM anti-HBc positive, anti-HBs negative, and HBeAg positive. Liver biopsy shows hepatocytes with a granular eosinophilic ground-glass appearance.',
    'Which of the following best explains the ground-glass hepatocyte appearance and the high transmissibility in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Iron deposition in periportal hepatocytes secondary to chronic hemolysis"},{"id":"B","text":"Accumulation of hepatitis B surface antigen within infected hepatocytes, with HBeAg indicating active replication"},{"id":"C","text":"Macrovesicular steatosis with lymphoid aggregates typical of hepatitis C"},{"id":"D","text":"Councilman bodies from hepatocyte apoptosis, which are specific to hepatitis A"},{"id":"E","text":"Cytopathic destruction of hepatocytes directly by HBsAg, without a T-cell response"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'In hepatitis B, the ground-glass hepatocyte appearance results from accumulation of surface antigen within infected hepatocytes, and injury is mediated by cytotoxic T cells rather than direct cytopathic effect. HBeAg is secreted into the circulation, indicates active viral replication, and therefore predicts high transmissibility and a poorer prognosis. The serum-sickness-like prodrome of fever, arthralgias, and rash is a classic early HBV presentation.',
    '{"A":"Iron deposition with periportal hepatocytes describes hemochromatosis, which is unrelated to acute icteric hepatitis B.","C":"Lymphoid aggregates with focal macrovesicular steatosis describe hepatitis C histology, not the ground-glass change of hepatitis B.","D":"Councilman bodies are eosinophilic apoptotic globules seen in hepatitis A and yellow fever; they are not the ground-glass finding.","E":"HBV is not directly cytopathic; cytotoxic T cells mediate the hepatocyte damage, which is why immunosuppression can produce high viral loads with little inflammation."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis A and B',
    'A 34-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B is evaluated for proteinuria and edema. Renal biopsy shows a membranous glomerulonephropathy pattern. He also reports episodes of myalgias and skin nodules, and biopsy of a small artery shows transmural inflammation consistent with polyarteritis nodosa.',
    'Which of the following best accounts for these extrahepatic findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Essential mixed cryoglobulinemia due to chronic hepatitis C"},{"id":"B","text":"Immune complex deposition associated with chronic hepatitis B"},{"id":"C","text":"Direct cytopathic infection of renal podocytes and medium arteries by HBV"},{"id":"D","text":"Aplastic anemia caused by parvovirus B19 coinfection"},{"id":"E","text":"Leukocytoclastic vasculitis related to hepatitis C with B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Chronic hepatitis B is associated with membranous glomerulonephritis, which is more common than membranoproliferative GN, and with polyarteritis nodosa, a necrotizing vasculitis of medium vessels. These extrahepatic manifestations are immune complex mediated rather than a direct viral cytopathic effect. Hepatitis C shows the mirror-image pattern: membranoproliferative GN is more common than membranous and essential mixed cryoglobulinemia is classic.',
    '{"A":"Essential mixed cryoglobulinemia and an increased risk of B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma are hepatitis C associations, not hepatitis B.","C":"These lesions reflect circulating immune complex deposition; HBV does not directly infect podocytes or medium-sized arteries to cause this pattern.","D":"Aplastic anemia is a described hematologic manifestation of hepatitis B, but it does not explain membranous nephropathy or polyarteritis nodosa.","E":"Leukocytoclastic vasculitis is listed under hepatitis C extrahepatic manifestations, along with the lymphoma risk."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis C, D, and E',
    'A 41-year-old woman with a history of injection drug use is found to have an ALT of 88 U/L on routine screening. HCV RNA is detectable, and the same viral genotype persists on repeat testing 6 months later. She has never cleared the virus.',
    'Which of the following best explains why most patients with hepatitis C fail to clear the virus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"HCV lacks 3''-5'' exonuclease proofreading activity, so its envelope proteins undergo continuous antigenic variation"},{"id":"B","text":"HCV integrates into the host genome and becomes invisible to cytotoxic T cells"},{"id":"C","text":"HCV is a defective virus that requires hepatitis B surface antigen to replicate"},{"id":"D","text":"HCV replicates in the nucleus, where neutralizing antibodies cannot reach it"},{"id":"E","text":"HCV encodes a reverse transcriptase that generates mutations during DNA synthesis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The HCV RNA-dependent RNA polymerase lacks 3''-5'' exonuclease activity, so it cannot proofread and makes frequent errors. This produces antigenic variation of the envelope proteins, and host antibody production lags behind the emergence of new mutant strains. As a result, the majority of infected people develop stable chronic hepatitis C, which may progress to cirrhosis or hepatocellular carcinoma.',
    '{"B":"HCV is a flavivirus that replicates through an RNA intermediate in the cytoplasm; it does not integrate into host DNA like a retrovirus.","C":"That description fits hepatitis D, the defective deltavirus that requires HBsAg as its envelope; HCV replicates independently.","D":"HCV replicates in the cytoplasm as an RNA virus, and the escape mechanism is antigenic variation rather than nuclear sequestration.","E":"HCV does not encode reverse transcriptase; that enzyme belongs to retroviruses and hepatitis B virus."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis C, D, and E',
    'A 29-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester in South Asia develops jaundice and becomes encephalopathic over 5 days. Laboratory studies show an ALT of 2100 U/L, INR of 2.8, and positive IgM anti-HEV. Testing for HAV, HBV, HCV, and HDV is negative.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely outcome of this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"High mortality from fulminant hepatitis in pregnancy"},{"id":"B","text":"Complete dependence on hepatitis B surface antigen for entry into hepatocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"Chronic hepatitis in the majority of those infected"},{"id":"D","text":"Resolution with lifelong immunity and no risk of recurrence"},{"id":"E","text":"Chronic carriage with eventual cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Hepatitis E is a naked positive-sense ssRNA hepevirus spread fecal-orally, especially by contaminated water in epidemic outbreaks in parts of Asia, Africa, and the Middle East. It has no carrier state and does not cause chronic disease, but in pregnant patients, particularly in the third trimester, it can cause fulminant hepatitis with high mortality, reflected here by encephalopathy and a markedly elevated INR.',
    '{"B":"Dependence on HBsAg describes hepatitis D, a defective deltavirus, not the independently replicating hepatitis E virus.","C":"Chronic infection develops in the majority of hepatitis C patients and in some hepatitis B patients, but not with HEV.","D":"Fulminant hepatitis in pregnancy makes this a high-risk rather than benign course, and this patient already has encephalopathy and coagulopathy.","E":"HEV does not cause chronic hepatitis or hepatocellular carcinoma; it has no carrier state, which distinguishes it from HBV and HCV."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis C, D, and E',
    'A 38-year-old man with a history of injection drug use and known HBsAg-positive chronic hepatitis B presents with rapidly worsening jaundice, increasing ascites, and hepatic encephalopathy. Serology is positive for IgM anti-HDV. Six months earlier his liver enzymes were only mildly elevated.',
    'Which of the following best explains the fulminant course in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Acute hepatitis E superimposed on chronic hepatitis B"},{"id":"B","text":"Hepatitis C superinfection causing essential mixed cryoglobulinemia"},{"id":"C","text":"Reactivation of hepatitis A with aplastic anemia"},{"id":"D","text":"HDV coinfection with HBV, which typically produces a long incubation period and less severe disease"},{"id":"E","text":"HDV superinfection of established hepatitis B, which carries a worse prognosis than coinfection"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Hepatitis D is a defective circular negative-sense ssRNA deltavirus that depends on HBsAg produced by replicating hepatitis B virus to form its envelope and infect hepatocytes. When HDV superinfects a patient who already has chronic hepatitis B, the incubation period is short and the prognosis is worse, with an accelerated course to cirrhosis and fulminant hepatitis, than with simultaneous coinfection.',
    '{"A":"Acute hepatitis E can cause fulminant disease, especially in pregnancy, but this patient''s IgM anti-HDV identifies the explanation for the flare directly.","B":"Hepatitis C superinfection causes chronic hepatitis and mixed cryoglobulinemia, not an acute fulminant deterioration with positive HDV IgM.","C":"Hepatitis A does not produce chronic infection or reactivation, and aplastic anemia is not part of HDV-related deterioration.","D":"Coinfection means simultaneous acquisition of HDV and HBV and has a long incubation period with a less severe course; this patient''s established hepatitis B indicates superinfection."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Hepatitis C, D, and E',
    'A 55-year-old woman with chronic hepatitis C presents with palpable purpura on the lower legs, arthralgias, and numbness in both feet. Laboratory studies show a positive rheumatoid factor, low complement, and circulating cryoglobulins. She also has new proteinuria.',
    'Which of the following best accounts for this constellation of findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Membranous nephropathy due to hepatitis B immune complexes"},{"id":"B","text":"Lichen planus of the skin secondary to hepatitis C, without renal involvement"},{"id":"C","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with chronic hepatitis B"},{"id":"D","text":"Essential mixed cryoglobulinemia with membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis in hepatitis C"},{"id":"E","text":"Sporadic porphyria cutanea tarda from hepatitis B"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Hepatitis C extrahepatic manifestations are grouped by organ system: hematologic effects include essential mixed cryoglobulinemia, an increased risk of B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, ITP, and autoimmune hemolytic anemia; renal disease is more often membranoproliferative GN than membranous GN; vascular disease includes leukocytoclastic vasculitis. Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis links the purpura, neuropathy, low complement, and glomerulonephritis in one explanation.',
    '{"A":"Membranous nephropathy precedes membranoproliferative GN in hepatitis B, not in hepatitis C, where the reverse hierarchy applies.","B":"Lichen planus is a hepatitis C skin association, but this patient has clear systemic vasculitic and renal disease that requires explanation.","C":"Polyarteritis nodosa is the hepatitis B vascular manifestation and affects medium vessels with myalgias and skin nodules rather than cryoglobulinemic purpura.","E":"Porphyria cutanea tarda is listed among hepatitis C dermatologic manifestations, not hepatitis B, and it does not explain the renal or neurologic findings."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Herpes simplex virus',
    'A 48-year-old man presents with 2 days of fever, confusion, and a fluent aphasia. He has no focal motor deficits. Brain MRI shows hyperintensity in the medial temporal lobes and insula. CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, elevated protein, and a normal glucose. Herpes simplex virus-1 PCR of the CSF is positive.',
    'Which of the following best explains why this particular syndrome is attributed to HSV-1?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic encephalitis, characteristically affecting the temporal lobes with altered mental status, seizures, or aphasia"},{"id":"B","text":"HSV-1 is an enveloped DNA virus that acquires its envelope from the nuclear membrane, which explains its tropism"},{"id":"C","text":"HSV-1 latency in mononuclear cells leads to intranuclear owl''s eye inclusions in neurons"},{"id":"D","text":"HSV-1 is the agent of the classic aseptic meningitis syndrome more often than HSV-2"},{"id":"E","text":"HSV-1 reactivates from the sacral ganglia and spreads along sacral nerve roots to the temporal lobe"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic encephalitis, classically presenting with altered mental status, seizures, and aphasia and targeting the temporal lobes. It is transmitted by respiratory secretions and saliva and remains latent in the trigeminal ganglia, which is why reactivation can seed the anterior and middle cranial fossa. CSF PCR is the test of choice for herpes encephalitis.',
    '{"B":"Herpesviruses do acquire enveloped from the nuclear membrane, but that is true of all herpesviruses and does not explain temporal lobe tropism.","C":"Owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions are the hallmark of cytomegalovirus; HSV and VZV show Cowdry A inclusions instead.","D":"Viral meningitis is more common with HSV-2 than HSV-1; HSV-1 is distinguished by encephalitis.","E":"Sacral ganglia latency belongs to HSV-2, which causes herpes genitalis, neonatal herpes, and viral meningitis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Herpes simplex virus',
    'A 26-year-old woman presents with a single 3-mm erythematous swelling of the distal index finger that appeared 2 days after she bit her nail carelessly; she is a dental hygienist. It is exquisitely tender and has a cluster of small vesicles that later coalesced into a coalescing crust.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and its agent?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Bacterial paronychia from Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"B","text":"Herpangina from coxsackievirus"},{"id":"C","text":"Gianotti-Crosti syndrome from hepatitis B"},{"id":"D","text":"Herpetic whitlow from HSV-1 or HSV-2"},{"id":"E","text":"Erythema multiforme from HSV-1"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Herpetic whitlow is a painful vesicular lesion of the distal finger caused by inoculation of HSV-1 (or HSV-2) into broken skin, classically in healthcare workers such as dental hygienists who are exposed to oral secretions. It is one of the recognized HSV-1 clinical syndromes in First Aid, alongside gingivostomatitis, keratoconjunctivitis, herpes labialis, temporal lobe encephalitis, esophagitis, and erythema multiforme.',
    '{"A":"Bacterial paronychia also affects the nail fold, but the clustered clear vesicles and occupational exposure to saliva in the absence of purulent drainage point to a viral etiology.","B":"Herpangina is an oropharyngeal disease with mouth blisters and fever caused by coxsackievirus, not a digital lesion.","C":"Gianotti-Crosti is a papular acrodermatitis associated with viral infection, not a localized vesicle of the fingertip.","E":"Erythema multiforme is a targetoid cutaneous eruption that can follow HSV, but it is a generalized rash rather than a localized vesicular finger lesion."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Herpes simplex virus',
    'A 22-year-old woman presents with a painful labial vesicle that ruptured and crusted over 3 days. She reports previous similar episodes triggered by sun exposure. The clinician considers the classic bedside test for a viral vesicle.',
    'A Tzanck smear of an opened vesicle in this patient would most likely show which of the following?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Multinucleated giant cells with Cowdry A intranuclear inclusions"},{"id":"B","text":"Cells with owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions"},{"id":"C","text":"Negri bodies in the cytoplasm of neurons"},{"id":"D","text":"Koilocytes with perinuclear halos"},{"id":"E","text":"Molluscum bodies in keratinocytes"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The Tzanck test is a smear of an opened skin vesicle that detects multinucleated giant cells, which are commonly seen with HSV-1, HSV-2, and VZV. Intranuclear eosinophilic Cowdry A inclusions are also seen in these infections. The test is now outdated: PCR of skin lesions is the diagnostic test of choice.',
    '{"B":"Owl''s eye inclusions are characteristic of cytomegalovirus-infected cells, not of the multinucleated giant cells of HSV or VZV.","C":"Negri bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions of rabies virus found in Purkinje cells of the cerebellum and hippocampal neurons.","D":"Koilocytes with perinuclear halos are the cytopathic effect of human papillomavirus on squamous epithelium.","E":"Molluscum bodies are intracytoplasmic inclusions in keratinocytes of molluscum contagiosum, a poxvirus lesion."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Histoplasma capsulatum',
    'A 29-year-old spelunker develops fever, dry cough, fatigue, and night sweats two weeks after exploring bat-inhabited caves in the Mississippi River Valley. Chest imaging shows hilar lymphadenopathy and a patchy infiltrate. Liver function tests are mildly elevated and the platelet count is low.',
    'Which of the following findings is most characteristic of this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Budding yeasts arranged in a captain''s wheel pattern"},{"id":"B","text":"Septate hyphae branching at acute angles on silver stain"},{"id":"C","text":"Broad-based budding yeasts the same size as a red blood cell"},{"id":"D","text":"Macrophages filled with small yeasts smaller than a red blood cell"},{"id":"E","text":"Large spherules containing endospores within giant cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Histoplasma capsulatum is acquired by inhaling spores from soil contaminated with bird or bat droppings, classically in caves and in the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys. On tissue staining, macrophages are stuffed with small yeasts that are smaller than a red blood cell, which explains the pancytopenia and hepatosplenomegaly caused by reticuloendothelial involvement. Histoplasma ''hides'' within macrophages. Urine and serum antigen testing is the preferred diagnostic test for disseminated disease.',
    '{"A":"The captain''s wheel arrangement of budding yeasts describes Paracoccidioides brasiliensis, a Latin American organism affecting males far more than females.","B":"Septate hyphae branching at acute angles describe Aspergillus fumigatus growing as a mold, which causes invasive pulmonary disease in neutropenic hosts.","C":"Broad-based budding of a yeast the size of a red blood cell is Blastomyces dermatitidis, which is characteristic of the eastern and central United States rather than cave exposure.","E":"Spherules filled with endospores indicate Coccidioides immitis, which is acquired from dust in the southwestern United States, not from bat droppings."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Histoplasma capsulatum',
    'A 44-year-old man with AIDS and a CD4+ count of 40 cells/mm3 presents with fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly, and pancytopenia. Examination reveals shallow ulcers on the palate and tongue. Blood cultures are negative, and the clinical team wants the fastest sensitive test for disseminated disease.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic method in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cytology looking for broad nonseptate hyphae"},{"id":"B","text":"India ink preparation of cerebrospinal fluid"},{"id":"C","text":"Methenamine silver stain looking for disc-shaped yeasts"},{"id":"D","text":"Culture on Sabouraud agar showing tuberculate macroconidia"},{"id":"E","text":"Urine and serum antigen testing"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is disseminated histoplasmosis: fever, hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia from macrophage stuffing, and the classic palatal and tongue ulcers in an immunocompromised host. Urine and serum antigen testing is the recommended diagnostic approach because it is rapid and sensitive for disseminated disease. The organism is a dimorphic fungus that is a mold at 20°C and a yeast at 37°C, so culture may be slow. Antigen testing explains the emphasis on it as the diagnostic test of choice rather than histology alone.',
    '{"A":"Broad non-septate hyphae branching at wide angles identify Mucor and Rhizopus, which cause angioinvasion in diabetic ketoacidosis rather than hepatosplenic disease.","B":"India ink highlights the thick polysaccharide capsule of Cryptococcus neoformans; although Cryptococcus can also infect AIDS patients, it typically presents with meningoencephalitis.","C":"Disc-shaped yeasts on methenamine silver staining of lung tissue identify Pneumocystis jirovecii, which causes diffuse interstitial pneumonia rather than hepatosplenomegaly.","D":"Tuberculate macroconidia on culture do identify Histoplasma, but culture requires prolonged incubation and is less useful when rapid diagnosis of disseminated disease is needed."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Histoplasma capsulatum',
    'A 26-year-old man who works on a construction crew in the Ohio River Valley develops fever, chills, dry cough, and chest tightness 10 days after demolishing an old barn that housed a large bird roost. Several co-workers have similar symptoms. Biopsy of a cervical lymph node shows granulomas.',
    'Which of the following best explains why so many of his co-workers became ill?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They were bitten by infected ticks at the work site"},{"id":"B","text":"The organism is spread person-to-person by respiratory droplets"},{"id":"C","text":"They inhaled spores aerosolized from contaminated soil and droppings"},{"id":"D","text":"The organism was introduced through skin breaks by thorns"},{"id":"E","text":"They ingested contaminated undercooked pork"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Multiple people developing the same illness after a shared environmental exposure is a classic outbreak clue for histoplasmosis. The fungus grows in soil enriched by bird and bat droppings, and demolition or excavation aerosolizes the spores, which are then inhaled and deposit in the lungs before spreading to the reticuloendothelial system. This is why the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys and activities such as cave exploration, barn cleaning, and construction are emphasized. Systemic mycoses are not transmitted person-to-person, so shared inhalation of a common environmental source is the explanation.',
    '{"A":"Tick bite transmission fits rickettsial and Borrelia infections, which produce rash and systemic symptoms rather than granulomatous pulmonary disease and lymphadenopathy.","B":"Unlike tuberculosis, the systemic dimorphic fungi cannot be transmitted from person to person, which is why an infected patient does not need respiratory isolation.","D":"Inoculation of spores through a thorn is the route for Sporothrix schenckii (rose gardener''s disease), which produces ascending nodular lymphangitis rather than a shared respiratory outbreak.","E":"Ingestion of undercooked pork is the classic route for tapeworm cysts such as Taenia solium, and it does not fit a respiratory illness in an entire work crew."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Histoplasma capsulatum',
    'A 35-year-old man from the Ohio River Valley presents with fever, cough, and painful red nodules on his shins. Chest imaging shows a patchy infiltrate with hilar adenopathy. A fungal culture is grown at 20°C for identification, and the laboratory reports characteristic mold-phase structures.',
    'Which of the following is the classic culture finding for this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Spherules containing endospores"},{"id":"B","text":"Broad-based budding yeasts"},{"id":"C","text":"Cigar-shaped yeasts"},{"id":"D","text":"Multiple buds in a captain''s wheel pattern"},{"id":"E","text":"Tuberculate macroconidia"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'At the cooler temperature of 20°C, Histoplasma capsulatum grows as a mold and produces tuberculate macroconidia, which is the classic culture identification. The same organism is a yeast at 37°C in human tissue, where it hides inside macrophages as small yeasts smaller than a red blood cell. Fever, cough, and erythema nodosum in a patient from the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys fit acute pulmonary histoplasmosis. Urine and serum antigen testing is used for rapid diagnosis, especially in disseminated disease.',
    '{"A":"Spherules filled with endospores are the tissue and culture morphology of Coccidioides immitis, the desert organism of the southwestern United States.","B":"Broad-based budding yeasts are typical of Blastomyces dermatitidis, which causes verrucous skin lesions and bone disease in the eastern and central United States.","C":"Cigar-shaped yeasts at 37°C identify Sporothrix schenckii, which is inoculated by a thorn and causes nodules along draining lymphatics.","D":"A captain''s wheel pattern of multiple buds around a parent cell is the hallmark of Paracoccidioides brasiliensis, endemic to Latin America."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'HIV virology and treatment',
    'A 29-year-old man is newly diagnosed with HIV infection. His CD4 count is 520/mm3 and viral load is 68,000 copies/mL. He previously had controlled hypertension and no other medical problems. The clinician discusses combination antiretroviral therapy.',
    'Which of the following drug pairs correctly matches the HIV protein with the drug that inhibits it?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Zidovudine inhibits integrase, and dolutegravir inhibits reverse transcriptase"},{"id":"B","text":"Enfuvirtide inhibits reverse transcriptase, and maraviroc inhibits protease"},{"id":"C","text":"Lamivudine inhibits gp120 attachment, and tenofovir blocks gp41 fusion"},{"id":"D","text":"Enfuvirtide inhibits gp41-mediated fusion, and maraviroc blocks the CCR5 coreceptor"},{"id":"E","text":"Raltegravir inhibits protease, and ritonavir inhibits integrase"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'HIV entry requires gp120 attachment to CD4 and a coreceptor, followed by gp41-mediated fusion. Enfuvirtide blocks gp41 fusion and maraviroc blocks CCR5, so both act at entry; the other steps are reverse transcription (inhibited by nucleoside and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors), integration (inhibited by integrase inhibitors such as raltegravir and dolutegravir), and maturation (blocked by protease inhibitors). The pol gene encodes reverse transcriptase, integrase, and protease through cleavage of the gag-pol polyprotein.',
    '{"A":"Zidovudine is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor and dolutegravir is an integrase inhibitor, so both assignments are wrong.","B":"Enfuvirtide acts on gp41 fusion rather than reverse transcriptase, and maraviroc blocks the CCR5 coreceptor rather than protease.","C":"Lamivudine is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor and tenofovir is a nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor; neither targets gp120 or gp41.","E":"Raltegravir is an integrase inhibitor and ritonavir is a protease inhibitor often used as a pharmacokinetic booster; this pairing is reversed."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'HIV virology and treatment',
    'A 36-year-old man presents with 2 weeks of fever, sore throat, fatigue, and a diffuse maculopapular rash after a new sexual partner. His HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody combination immunoassay is negative, but HIV-1 nucleic acid testing is positive with a high viral load.',
    'Which of the following best explains the discordant test results?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The p24 antigen component of the immunoassay is only detectable after seroconversion is complete"},{"id":"B","text":"The antigen/antibody immunoassay may miss early HIV disease if performed within the first 2 weeks of infection, but NAAT detects viremia in antibody-negative patients"},{"id":"C","text":"The negative immunoassay proves the NAAT result is a false positive from a laboratory error"},{"id":"D","text":"The NAAT result reflects maternal antibody transfer rather than true infection"},{"id":"E","text":"The confirmatory test for a positive immunoassay is a Western blot, which is why the initial test was negative"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody immunoassays detect p24 capsid antigen and IgG and IgM antibodies to HIV-1/2 with very high sensitivity and specificity, but they may miss early disease if the test is done within the first 2 weeks of infection. NAAT is a qualitative, sensitive method to detect HIV viremia in antibody-negative patients, so it establishes acute HIV infection here. The acute retroviral syndrome with fever, pharyngitis, and rash fits this window.',
    '{"A":"p24 antigen is detectable before antibodies appear, which is precisely why antigen/antibody combination assays shorten the window period.","C":"A negative antibody test during acute infection is expected, and a positive NAAT in this clinical setting is diagnostic rather than a laboratory artifact.","D":"Maternally transferred antibody is a concern in infants, where the recommendation is to use HIV viral load testing instead; this patient is an adult.","E":"Western blot is no longer recommended by the CDC for confirmatory testing; a positive screening test is followed by an HIV-1/2 antibody differentiation immunoassay."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'HIV virology and treatment',
    'A 42-year-old man with untreated HIV infection has a CD4 count of 180/mm3. He presents with 3 weeks of dry cough, exertional dyspnea, and fatigue. Chest radiograph shows bilateral perihilar interstitial infiltrates, and high-resolution CT shows ground-glass opacities.',
    'Which of the following CD4 count thresholds is most appropriate for initiating prophylaxis against this pathogen?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"CD4 count below 100/mm3"},{"id":"B","text":"CD4 count below 50/mm3"},{"id":"C","text":"CD4 count below 750/mm3"},{"id":"D","text":"CD4 count below 200/mm3"},{"id":"E","text":"CD4 count below 500/mm3"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The patient has Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, which appears when the CD4 count falls below 200/mm3 and produces ground-glass opacities on chest imaging. Other diseases in this tier include Histoplasma capsulatum infection, HIV dementia and HIV-associated nephropathy, JC virus reactivation causing progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, disseminated HHV-8 Kaposi sarcoma, and B-cell lymphoma. Prophylaxis is triggered at the same 200/mm3 threshold.',
    '{"A":"The CD4 below 100/mm3 tier includes Bartonella bacillary angiomatosis, Candida esophagitis, CMV disease (CREEP), Cryptococcus meningitis, Cryptosporidium diarrhea, EBV CNS lymphoma, and MAC.","B":"A CD4 count below 50/mm3 is when Mycobacterium avium complex disease is most common, along with CMV retinitis; PCP prophylaxis begins earlier.","C":"A threshold of 750/mm3 is above the normal lower limit of 500 cells/mm3 and is not an AIDS-defining threshold.","E":"The CD4 below 500/mm3 tier includes oral thrush from Candida albicans, EBV oral hairy leukoplakia, HHV-8 Kaposi sarcoma, HPV squamous cell carcinoma, and increased tuberculosis reactivation risk."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'HIV virology and treatment',
    'A 51-year-old man with AIDS and a CD4 count of 30/mm3 presents with headache, fever, and confusion. MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions, and serology for Toxoplasma is positive. Two months later he develops severe headache and meningismus, and CSF India ink staining shows encapsulated yeast.',
    'Which of the following best explains why he developed these opportunistic infections?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Loss of B cells eliminates antibody production, which is the sole cause of these infections"},{"id":"B","text":"A decline in CD4+ T cells reactivates past infections and permits dissemination of bacterial and fungal infections and non-Hodgkin lymphomas"},{"id":"C","text":"HIV directly infects astrocytes and fungal organisms, causing their proliferation"},{"id":"D","text":"Reverse transcriptase inhibitors cause immunosuppression that predisposes to these infections"},{"id":"E","text":"gp120 binding to CCR5 on macrophages destroys the macrophages and eliminates phagocytosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'A declining CD4+ T cell count leads to reactivation of past infections such as tuberculosis, HSV, and shingles, dissemination of bacterial and fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis, and non-Hodgkin lymphomas. Specific thresholds map to specific pathogens: below 200/mm3 Toxoplasma causes ring-enhancing brain abscesses and PCP causes ground-glass opacities, and below 100/mm3 Cryptococcus causes meningitis with encapsulated yeast on India ink. HIV also binds CD4 plus CCR5 on macrophages early and CXCR4 on T cells late.',
    '{"A":"HIV targets CD4+ T cells primarily, and cell-mediated immunity rather than B-cell antibody production is the dominant defense against these opportunistic pathogens.","C":"HIV infects CD4-expressing cells such as T cells and macrophages; it does not infect astrocytes or fungal organisms to make them proliferate.","D":"Reverse transcriptase inhibitors treat HIV and improve immune function; drug-related immunosuppression is not the mechanism of these AIDS-defining infections.","E":"gp120 binding to CD4 and a coreceptor allows viral entry into macrophages; it does not eliminate phagocytosis as the explanation for Toxoplasma and Cryptococcus."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'HIV virology and treatment',
    'A 27-year-old man who has sex with men is seen for prevention counseling. He is HIV negative. His partner is HIV positive and has an undetectable viral load on antiretroviral therapy. The clinician reviews methods of pre-exposure prophylaxis and explains differences in viral entry.',
    'Which of the following best explains why a homozygous CCR5 mutation confers resistance to HIV infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"HIV requires CD4 plus either CCR5 on macrophages or CXCR4 on T cells for entry, and CCR5 loss blocks entry of early macrophage-tropic virus"},{"id":"B","text":"CCR5 is the enzyme that reverse transcribes the HIV genome, so its loss prevents provirus formation"},{"id":"C","text":"CCR5 is the same molecule as CD4, so mutation eliminates the primary receptor entirely"},{"id":"D","text":"CCR5 mutation enhances gp41 fusion so the virus enters but cannot integrate"},{"id":"E","text":"CCR5 mutation increases CCR5 surface expression and traps virions outside the cell"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'HIV binds CD4 as well as a coreceptor, either CCR5 on macrophages during early infection or CXCR4 on T cells during late infection. A homozygous CCR5 mutation confers immunity, whereas a heterozygous mutation produces a slower disease course, because the missing coreceptor prevents entry of macrophage-tropic virus. This is the rationale behind maraviroc, a CCR5 antagonist that blocks the same step.',
    '{"B":"Reverse transcription is performed by the pol gene product reverse transcriptase, not by the chemokine receptor CCR5.","C":"CD4 and CCR5 are distinct surface molecules; CD4 is the primary receptor and CCR5 is a coreceptor used in addition to it.","D":"The mutation reduces functional coreceptor availability and prevents entry; it does not enhance gp41-mediated fusion.","E":"The mutation reduces or eliminates functional CCR5 on the cell surface rather than increasing expression, and virions are blocked at entry rather than trapped."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Human herpesvirus 6 and 8',
    'A 14-month-old boy is brought in after 4 days of high fever up to 40.2 degrees C, during which he had a febrile seizure. Today the fever abruptly resolved and a diffuse blanching macular rash appeared, beginning on the trunk and spreading to the extremities. He is otherwise well appearing and the family has no travel history.',
    'Which of the following best describes the virus responsible for this illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rubella virus, causing postauricular lymphadenopathy and a rash spreading centrifugally"},{"id":"B","text":"Human herpesvirus 8, transmitted by sexual contact and associated with violaceous plaques"},{"id":"C","text":"Human herpesvirus 6, spread by saliva and causing roseola infantum in children under 2 years"},{"id":"D","text":"Varicella-zoster virus, causing a vesicular rash in crops"},{"id":"E","text":"Measles virus, causing Koplik spots and a rash beginning at the head and neck"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum) is caused by HHV-6, spread by saliva, and presents with several days of high fever that can cause seizures, followed by a diffuse macular rash that starts on the trunk and spreads to the extremities in children under 2 years old. The mnemonic is that roseola gives fever first and rosy rash later. HHV-7 is a less common cause of the same illness, and the disease is self-limited.',
    '{"A":"Rubella causes postauricular lymphadenopathy and a fine maculopapular rash starting on the face, and it is a togavirus-like matonavirus rather than a herpesvirus.","B":"HHV-8 is sexually transmitted, causes Kaposi sarcoma with dark violaceous vascular plaques, and is not a cause of febrile exanthem in toddlers.","D":"Chickenpox produces a pruritic vesicular rash with lesions at several stages simultaneously, not a macular rash appearing after defervescence.","E":"Measles follows fever with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and Koplik spots, and the rash starts at the head and neck rather than on the trunk."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Human papillomavirus',
    'A 23-year-old woman presents with a cluster of soft, fleshy, cauliflower-like papules around the vaginal introitus. She is otherwise healthy and her cervical cytology is normal. The lesions are diagnosed clinically as condyloma acuminatum.',
    'Which HPV serotypes are most likely responsible for these lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Serotypes 5 and 8"},{"id":"B","text":"Serotypes 16 and 18"},{"id":"C","text":"Serotypes 6 and 11"},{"id":"D","text":"Serotypes 1 and 2"},{"id":"E","text":"Serotypes 31, 33, and 45"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'HPV serotypes 6 and 11 are low-risk types associated with anogenital warts, condyloma acuminatum, whereas serotypes 16 and 18 are the high-risk types associated with cervical, anal, penile, and oropharyngeal cancer. HPV is a naked, circular dsDNA papillomavirus that infects squamous epithelium and produces koilocytes with perinuclear halos on cytology.',
    '{"A":"Serotypes 5 and 8 are cutaneous types associated with epidermodysplasia-like lesions, not genital warts.","B":"Serotypes 16 and 18 are the high-risk oncogenic types associated with cervical and oropharyngeal cancer, not with condyloma acuminatum.","D":"Serotypes 1 and 2 are associated with common cutaneous warts, not the anogenital warts described here.","E":"Serotypes 31, 33, and 45 are additional high-risk oncogenic types rather than the wart-producing low-risk types."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Human papillomavirus',
    'A 44-year-old woman with HIV infection has a CD4 count of 420/mm3. She presents for routine cervical cancer screening. Her most recent screening was 5 years ago and showed no abnormality. Her HIV disease is well controlled on antiretroviral therapy.',
    'Which of the following best explains why HPV-related malignancy risk is increased in HIV infection despite a CD4 count above 200/mm3?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"HPV causes Kaposi sarcoma in patients with HIV when the CD4 count falls below 500"},{"id":"B","text":"HPV becomes oncogenic only after coinfection with HHV-8, which reactivates at CD4 counts below 500"},{"id":"C","text":"HPV directly infects and depletes CD4+ T cells, so malignancy appears at any CD4 count"},{"id":"D","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma at sites of sexual contact occurs with CD4 counts below 500/mm3, reflecting impaired cell-mediated control of HPV"},{"id":"E","text":"HPV is an enveloped virus that integrates into host DNA more efficiently when CD4 cells are low"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'In the CD4 stratification of HIV-associated disease, HPV-related squamous cell carcinoma at sites of sexual contact (most commonly anus, cervix, oropharynx) appears in the CD4 less than 500/mm3 tier, alongside oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi sarcoma, and tuberculosis reactivation. The mechanism is loss of cell-mediated immune surveillance that normally contains HPV infection and its oncogenic effects. Higher-risk oncogenic serotypes such as 16 and 18 drive the malignant transformation.',
    '{"A":"Kaposi sarcoma is caused by HHV-8, appearing as a separate CD4 less than 500 item, and is not an HPV lesion.","B":"HPV oncogenesis depends on high-risk serotypes and impaired immune surveillance, not on coinfection with HHV-8.","C":"HPV infects squamous epithelium; it is HIV, not HPV, that infects and depletes CD4+ T cells.","E":"HPV is a naked circular dsDNA virus; envelope status is unrelated to integration frequency, and HPV does not require an envelope for entry."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Human papillomavirus',
    'A 19-year-old woman has a routine cervical cytology specimen that shows squamous cells with enlarged, irregular nuclei surrounded by a clear perinuclear halo. HPV DNA testing is positive for a high-risk serotype. The clinician wishes to explain the biology behind the finding.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative virus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An enveloped, linear dsDNA virus that latencies in sacral ganglia"},{"id":"B","text":"A naked, linear ssDNA virus that infects RBC precursors"},{"id":"C","text":"A partially dsDNA circular hepadnavirus with reverse transcriptase"},{"id":"D","text":"A circular negative-sense ssRNA deltavirus requiring HBsAg"},{"id":"E","text":"A naked, circular dsDNA virus that produces koilocytes and can cause cervical cancer"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Human papillomavirus is a naked, circular, dsDNA virus that infects squamous epithelium, producing koilocytes with perinuclear halos, and is associated with warts and with cervical, anal, penile, and oropharyngeal cancer. High-risk serotypes 16 and 18 carry the oncogenic risk, whereas 1, 2, 6, and 11 are associated with warts. Circularity places HPV among the three circular DNA families along with polyomavirus and hepadnavirus.',
    '{"A":"This describes herpes simplex virus, an enveloped linear dsDNA virus that latencies in sensory ganglia and does not produce koilocytes.","B":"This describes parvovirus B19, the ssDNA virus that infects erythroid precursors; it does not transform cervical epithelium.","C":"This describes hepatitis B virus, which targets hepatocytes and is not the cause of koilocytic change on cervical cytology.","D":"This describes hepatitis D virus, which depends on HBV and infects the liver, not cervical squamous cells."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Immunoglobulin and antibody responses to bacteria',
    'A 30-year-old man with a history of untreated streptococcal pharyngitis presents 3 weeks later with migratory polyarthritis, carditis, and subcutaneous nodules. Anti-streptolysin O (ASO) titers are elevated, confirming recent infection.',
    'Which immunoglobulin-related mechanism best explains his acute rheumatic fever?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Polysaccharide-induced, T-cell-independent activation of B cells without T-cell help"},{"id":"B","text":"Molecular mimicry between streptococcal M protein and human cardiac myosin heavy chains"},{"id":"C","text":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation with release of histamine and other mediators"},{"id":"D","text":"Cleavage of secretory IgA by a bacterial protease at the mucosal surface of the pharynx"},{"id":"E","text":"Binding of the IgG Fc region by a protein displayed on the bacterial cell surface"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'M protein is a group A streptococcal surface protein that prevents phagocytosis and shares sequence homology with human cardiac myosin, so antibodies raised against it cross-react with the heart in a classic example of molecular mimicry underlying acute rheumatic fever. The present illness follows pharyngitis by weeks, which fits a post-infectious type II hypersensitivity mechanism rather than acute toxin injury. For diagnosis, antibodies against streptolysin O (ASO) indicate recent infection, and these should not be confused with the immune complexes that cause post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.',
    '{"A":"T-cell-independent polysaccharide responses explain why an unmodified polysaccharide antigen cannot be presented to T cells; conjugation to carrier protein fixes this, and it is unrelated to carditis.","C":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is the basis of immediate-type hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis, not of rheumatic fever.","D":"IgA cleavage by bacterial proteases (S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, Neisseria) promotes mucosal colonization but does not cause cardiac autoimmunity.","E":"Binding the Fc region of IgG describes Protein A of S aureus, an antiphagocytic strategy, not the post-streptococcal autoimmune process."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Immunoglobulin and antibody responses to bacteria',
    'A 2-year-old child with recurrent pneumococcal meningitis is being considered for vaccination. He previously received a pure polysaccharide vaccine and mounted a poor, short-lived response without class switching. His physician proposes the conjugated vaccine instead.',
    'Why does conjugating the capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein improve the immune response?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It allows the antigen to be presented to T cells, enabling T-cell help and immunologic memory"},{"id":"B","text":"It converts the polysaccharide antigen into a toxoid that induces protective antitoxin in the host"},{"id":"C","text":"It prevents opsonization of the bacteria so that the antigen persists longer in circulation"},{"id":"D","text":"It converts the polysaccharide antigen into a superantigen that drives massive cytokine release"},{"id":"E","text":"It degrades the capsule so that macrophages can no longer phagocytize the whole organisms"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'A polysaccharide antigen alone cannot be presented to T cells, so it stimulates a T-cell-independent response with limited class switching and memory, which is poor in this young child. Capsular polysaccharide plus or minus a protein carrier can serve as a vaccine antigen, and conjugating capsule antigens to a carrier protein enhances immunogenicity by recruiting T-cell help. This is exactly the logic behind conjugate vaccines against encapsulated organisms, which are also the vaccines needed by asplenic patients because such organisms are opsonized and then cleared by the spleen. The capsule itself functions as an antiphagocytic virulence factor, so antibody against it must be effective before disease.',
    '{"B":"Toxoids are inactivated exotoxins used to induce antitoxins for tetanus and diphtheria; a polysaccharide is not a toxin.","C":"Preventing opsonization would impair clearance and is the effect of capsules as virulence factors, not of conjugation.","D":"Superantigens bypass antigen specificity by cross-linking TCR and MHC class II and cause shock; that is the opposite of the goal of a vaccine.","E":"The capsule is deliberately retained as the antigen; the conjugate design exploits, rather than destroys, the antiphagocytic polysaccharide."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Influenza virus',
    'In early spring a novel influenza A strain emerges that contains a mixture of gene segments from swine, avian, and human influenza viruses. It spreads globally within months, and mortality is far higher than in previous flu seasons. Epidemiologists attribute the emergence to an event in a coinfected host cell.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism responsible for this pandemic strain?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Antigenic shift from reassortment of RNA segments when one cell is coinfected by two different segmented influenza viruses"},{"id":"B","text":"Phenotypic mixing of influenza with an unrelated virus, changing the hemagglutinin coat"},{"id":"C","text":"Complementation between a mutant influenza virus and a wild-type paramyxovirus"},{"id":"D","text":"Recombination of homologous DNA segments within the host nucleus"},{"id":"E","text":"Antigenic drift from random point mutations in hemagglutinin, producing minor changes and local seasonal outbreaks"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Influenza is an orthomyxovirus with a segmented negative-sense ssRNA genome, so coinfection of one cell by two different strains permits reassortment of whole RNA segments. This produces a dramatically different virus with major antigenic change, leading to global pandemics; sudden shift is more deadly than gradual drift. The 2009 novel H1N1 pandemic arose through complex reassortment of human, swine, and avian genes.',
    '{"B":"Phenotypic mixing creates a virion with another virus''s surface proteins without changing the genome, so the change would not be heritable or globally sustained.","C":"Complementation rescues a defective protein in one virus but does not generate a new pandemic strain with a mixed segment composition.","D":"Influenza has an RNA genome, so there are no DNA segments to recombine; recombination by crossing over applies to DNA viruses with homologous sequences.","E":"Antigenic drift is the accumulation of random mutations in hemagglutinin or neuraminidase that produces minor changes and local seasonal epidemics, not a pandemic with a novel segment constellation."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Influenza virus',
    'A 70-year-old man presents in January with 2 days of fever, myalgias, and a dry cough during a community outbreak of influenza A. He is otherwise healthy and is not severely ill. The clinician discusses antiviral therapy active against influenza.',
    'Which of the following drugs acts by blocking release of progeny virions from infected respiratory cells?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Palivizumab, a monoclonal antibody against the F protein"},{"id":"B","text":"Acyclovir, which inhibits viral DNA polymerase"},{"id":"C","text":"Oseltamivir, a neuraminidase inhibitor"},{"id":"D","text":"Amantadine, which blocks uncoating and viral entry"},{"id":"E","text":"Ribavirin, which inhibits RNA synthesis broadly"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Influenza virions carry two surface antigens: hemagglutinin binds sialic acid and promotes entry, and neuraminidase promotes release of progeny virions. Neuraminidase inhibitors such as oseltamivir and zanamivir therefore trap new virions at the cell surface. Remember hemagglutinin lets the virus in and neuraminidase sends the virus away; treatment of influenza is supportive with or without a neuraminidase inhibitor.',
    '{"A":"Palivizumab is a monoclonal antibody against the RSV fusion protein used to prevent RSV pneumonia in premature infants.","B":"Acyclovir requires viral thymidine kinase and inhibits herpesvirus DNA polymerase; it has no activity against an RNA virus like influenza.","D":"Amantadine blocks uncoating of influenza A by interfering with the M2 ion channel, which acts at entry rather than at release, and resistance is widespread.","E":"Ribavirin is used for other viral infections such as RSV and hepatitis C, not as standard influenza therapy."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Influenza virus',
    'A 58-year-old woman with diabetes recovers from influenza but returns 6 days later with recurrent high fever, productive cough, and pleuritic chest pain. Chest imaging shows a new lobar consolidation. Sputum Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains.',
    'Which of the following pathogens most commonly causes fatal bacterial superinfection after influenza?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae"},{"id":"B","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia"},{"id":"C","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii and cytomegalovirus"},{"id":"D","text":"Bordetella pertussis and Legionella pneumophila"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Haemophilus influenzae"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Patients with influenza are at risk for fatal bacterial superinfection, most commonly Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Haemophilus influenzae. Influenza damages the respiratory epithelium and exposes adhesion sites that allow bacterial adherence and invasion, producing a biphasic illness with improvement followed by recrudescent fever and consolidation. This is the reason secondary bacterial pneumonia is a leading cause of influenza-associated death.',
    '{"A":"Atypical organisms such as Mycoplasma and Chlamydia pneumoniae cause slowly progressive pneumonia in young adults and are not the classic post-influenza superinfection trio.","B":"Pseudomonas and Stenotrophomonas are nosocomial organisms in patients with prior antibiotic exposure or structural lung disease, not the classic post-influenza pathogens.","C":"Pneumocystis and CMV cause pneumonia in immunocompromised patients, not in an immunocompetent host recovering from influenza.","D":"Pertussis causes paroxysmal cough without fever, and Legionella causes a water-source-associated pneumonia with hyponatremia and GI symptoms."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Influenza virus',
    'A public health official explains that the influenza vaccine must be reformulated each year. Two formulations are available: an intramuscular killed vaccine and an intranasal live attenuated vaccine containing a temperature-sensitive mutant that replicates in the nose but not in the lung.',
    'Which of the following best explains why annual reformulation is necessary?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The killed vaccine induces only innate immunity and must be readministered for that reason"},{"id":"B","text":"Reassortment of influenza with paramyxoviruses creates a new virus every year"},{"id":"C","text":"The vaccine loses potency during storage so a fresh batch must be produced each year"},{"id":"D","text":"Frequent point mutations in hemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes cause antigenic drift, so the vaccine must match the strains likely to circulate"},{"id":"E","text":"Influenza virus switches from RNA to DNA genomes each season"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The influenza vaccine is reformulated annually to contain the viral strains most likely to appear during the upcoming season, because the virus undergoes rapid genetic change. Random mutation in hemagglutinin or neuraminidase genes produces minor antigenic changes that occur frequently and cause local seasonal outbreaks. Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid and promotes entry, whereas neuraminidase promotes release of progeny virions.',
    '{"A":"The killed vaccine induces adaptive antibody responses against hemagglutinin and neuraminidase; the reason for annual dosing is antigenic change, not absence of adaptive immunity.","B":"Reassortment occurs among influenza strains with segmented genomes, not between influenza and paramyxoviruses.","C":"Annual reformulation reflects strain change rather than loss of potency during storage of a single vaccine composition.","E":"Influenza is an orthomyxovirus with a negative-sense, segmented ssRNA genome and does not switch to a DNA genome."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Klebsiella, Enterobacter, Serratia',
    'A 54-year-old man with a long history of heavy alcohol use and poorly controlled diabetes mellitus presents with fever, productive cough, and pleuritic chest pain. Sputum is thick, dark red, and gelatinous. Chest radiograph shows lobar consolidation with a bulging minor fissure. Sputum culture grows a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod with very mucoid colonies on MacConkey agar.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Klebsiella pneumoniae"},{"id":"B","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},{"id":"C","text":"Haemophilus influenzae"},{"id":"D","text":"Legionella pneumophila"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Klebsiella pneumoniae is an intestinal gram-negative rod that causes lobar pneumonia, particularly in patients with heavy alcohol use or impaired host defenses. It produces abundant polysaccharide capsules, giving very mucoid colonies and the classic dark red currant jelly sputum, and can cause abscesses of the lung and liver. The mnemonic ABCDEs of Klebsiella covers aspiration pneumonia, abscesses, currant jelly sputum, diabetes mellitus, and ethanol overuse.',
    '{"B":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a non-lactose fermenter, oxidase positive, and associated with burns, catheters, cystic fibrosis, and hospital-acquired pneumonia.","C":"Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus requiring factors V and X, and it causes epiglottitis, otitis media, and meningitis.","D":"Legionella is a gram-negative rod that stains poorly, needs charcoal yeast extract with iron and cysteine, and causes pneumonia with hyponatremia and elevated transaminases.","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is a gram-positive, encapsulated, alpha-hemolytic diplococcus associated with rusty sputum, not mucoid lactose-fermenting gram-negative colonies."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Klebsiella, Enterobacter, Serratia',
    'A 72-year-old woman with an indwelling urinary catheter and recent prolonged hospitalization develops fever and flank pain. Urine culture grows a gram-negative rod that forms pink colonies on MacConkey agar and is identified as Enterobacter species. The laboratory notes the isolate is resistant to several cephalosporins.',
    'Which of the following best explains the basis for identifying this group of organisms as lactose fermenters and why they pose a therapeutic problem?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lactose fermentation is detected only by H2S production on TSI agar"},{"id":"B","text":"Lactose fermentation produces pink colonies on MacConkey agar, and these organisms are associated with multidrug resistance"},{"id":"C","text":"Lactose fermentation produces black colonies on MacConkey agar, and the organisms are always susceptible to ampicillin"},{"id":"D","text":"Lactose non-fermentation produces pink colonies, and these organisms are oxidase positive"},{"id":"E","text":"Lactose fermentation produces a green sheen on EMB agar specifically for Enterobacter"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Fermentation of lactose produces pink colonies on MacConkey agar, and the lactose-fermenting enteric bacteria include Citrobacter, E coli, Enterobacter, Klebsiella, and Serratia. The mnemonic is that MacConkey is for the CEEKS milk. Laboratory statements also note multidrug resistance among these organisms, which is reflected in healthcare-associated infections and antimicrobial resistance.',
    '{"A":"H2S production on TSI agar distinguishes organisms such as Salmonella from Shigella and is unrelated to lactose fermentation.","C":"Lactose fermentation yields pink rather than black colonies on MacConkey, and these organisms are not reliably susceptible to ampicillin.","D":"Lactose non-fermenters are the organisms that appear colorless on MacConkey, and Enterobacter is not a classic oxidase-positive organism.","E":"The green sheen on EMB agar is characteristic of E coli, not of Enterobacter."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Legionella pneumophila',
    'A 68-year-old man who smokes and has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease returns from a cruise and presents with 3 days of high fever, confusion, watery diarrhea, and cough. Chest radiograph shows a dense unilateral lobar consolidation. Laboratory studies show sodium of 127 mEq/L and elevated transaminases. Attempted culture of sputum on standard blood agar grows nothing.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic approach for the responsible organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Culture on MacConkey agar incubated at 42 degrees Celsius"},{"id":"B","text":"Cold agglutinin titer and culture on Eaton agar"},{"id":"C","text":"Urine antigen testing, with culture on charcoal yeast extract medium supplemented with iron and cysteine"},{"id":"D","text":"Culture on chocolate agar supplemented with factors V and X"},{"id":"E","text":"Acid-fast staining of sputum followed by culture on Lowenstein-Jensen medium"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Legionella pneumophila is a gram-negative rod that stains poorly on Gram stain, so a silver stain is used, and it grows on charcoal yeast extract medium supplemented with iron and cysteine. It is detected by the presence of antigen in urine, which is rapid and useful in severe pneumonia. Labs classically show hyponatremia and elevated transaminases, and outbreaks are linked to environmental water sources such as air conditioning systems, hot water tanks, cruise ships, and nursing homes.',
    '{"A":"Growth on MacConkey agar at 42 degrees Celsius describes Campylobacter jejuni, a cause of bloody diarrhea rather than lobar pneumonia.","B":"Cold agglutinins and Eaton agar relate to Mycoplasma pneumoniae, which causes atypical pneumonia rather than lobar consolidation with hyponatremia.","D":"Chocolate agar with factors V and X is the required medium for Haemophilus influenzae, not Legionella.","E":"Acid-fast staining with Lowenstein-Jensen culture is the approach for mycobacteria such as M tuberculosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Legionella pneumophila',
    'A 55-year-old woman living in a nursing home develops 2 days of fever, myalgias, headache, and dry cough. She is hemodynamically stable and her chest radiograph shows only a minimal infiltrate. Several other residents of the same facility have had similar self-limited illnesses. Environmental investigation reveals growth of Legionella pneumophila in the facility''s hot water system.',
    'Which of the following best describes the transmission of this infection and the likely diagnosis in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Tick bite transmission, causing a rickettsial pneumonia"},{"id":"B","text":"Inhalation of aerosols from sheep amniotic fluid, causing Q fever"},{"id":"C","text":"Aerosol transmission from contaminated water causing severe lobar pneumonia with shock"},{"id":"D","text":"Aerosol transmission from contaminated water with no person-to-person spread, causing Pontiac fever"},{"id":"E","text":"Direct person-to-person transmission, causing Legionnaires'' disease"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Legionella is transmitted by aerosol from environmental water sources such as air conditioning systems and hot water tanks, with outbreaks linked to cruise ships and nursing homes, and there is no person-to-person transmission. Pontiac fever is the mild flulike form, whereas Legionnaires'' disease is the severe pneumonia with GI and CNS symptoms. This patient''s self-limited illness in a cluster of residents fits Pontiac fever.',
    '{"A":"Rickettsial pneumonias such as Q fever are acquired other ways; tick-borne rickettsioses typically cause rash and vasculitis rather than outbreaks traced to a water system.","B":"Q fever follows aerosol exposure to cattle or sheep amniotic fluid, and the environmental source here is a building water system.","C":"This describes Legionnaires'' disease, which is a severe lobar pneumonia with shock, not the mild syndrome seen in this clinically stable patient.","E":"Legionnaires'' disease is the severe form, and the source explicitly states there is no person-to-person transmission."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Legionella pneumophila',
    'A 72-year-old man with diabetes mellitus is admitted with severe pneumonia and requires intensive care support for hypotension. He has a history of tobacco use and chronic lung disease. Given his severity of illness and the concern for a water-borne atypical pathogen, his team selects antibiotic coverage for the most likely organism.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic class for this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Vancomycin"},{"id":"B","text":"A macrolide or a quinolone"},{"id":"C","text":"A beta-lactam with clavulanate"},{"id":"D","text":"Aminoglycoside monotherapy"},{"id":"E","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Legionella pneumophila is treated with a macrolide or a quinolone. Risk factors for severe disease include older age, tobacco smoking, and chronic lung disease, and the organism causes Legionnaires'' disease with pneumonia, fever, and GI and CNS symptoms. Beta-lactams are not the treatment of choice, even though they are common coverage for typical pneumonia pathogens.',
    '{"A":"Vancomycin targets gram-positive organisms such as MRSA and is not active therapy for Legionella.","C":"Beta-lactams act on the cell wall and are used for typical pneumonia pathogens such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, not for Legionella.","D":"Aminoglycoside monotherapy is avoided because of poor performance in acidic environments and is not used for Legionella pneumonia.","E":"TMP-SMX is used for Nocardia and as a pertussis alternative, not for Legionnaires'' disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Leptospira',
    'A 22-year-old college student returns from a surfing trip in Hawaii and presents with 5 days of fever, severe myalgias of the calves, headache, and eye discomfort in bright light. Examination shows bilateral conjunctival erythema without discharge. Laboratory studies show elevated bilirubin and creatinine, and urinalysis shows mild proteinuria.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely route of acquisition of this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Bite of an Ixodes deer tick"},{"id":"B","text":"Inhalation of aerosols from cattle amniotic fluid"},{"id":"C","text":"Sexual contact with an infected partner"},{"id":"D","text":"Ingestion of unpasteurized milk"},{"id":"E","text":"Exposure to water contaminated with animal urine"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Leptospira interrogans is a spirochete with hook-shaped ends found in water contaminated with animal urine, and leptospirosis is prevalent among surfers and in tropical regions such as Hawaii. Illness features flulike symptoms, myalgias classically of the calves, jaundice, and photophobia with conjunctival suffusion, which is erythema without exudate. Severe cases with jaundice and azotemia constitute Weil disease.',
    '{"A":"The Ixodes tick transmits Borrelia burgdorferi, Anaplasma, and Babesia, and produces erythema migrans rather than conjunctival suffusion and calf myalgias.","B":"Aerosols of cattle or sheep amniotic fluid transmit Coxiella burnetii, which causes Q fever with pneumonia and hepatitis.","C":"Leptospirosis is not a sexually transmitted infection; the characteristic exposure is contaminated recreational or environmental water.","D":"Unpasteurized dairy is the classic source of Brucella, which causes undulant fever and arthralgias."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Leptospira',
    'A 47-year-old rice farmer develops high fever, severe jaundice, muscle tenderness, and bleeding gums 10 days after wading in flooded fields. Laboratory studies show blood urea nitrogen of 70 mg/dL, creatinine of 3.4 mg/dL, and anemia. He becomes hypotensive and is admitted to the intensive care unit.',
    'Which of the following best describes this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Q fever with hepatitis and culture-negative endocarditis"},{"id":"B","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever with vasculitic rash and hyponatremia"},{"id":"C","text":"Secondary syphilis with disseminated rash and lymphadenopathy"},{"id":"D","text":"Brucellosis with undulant fever and noncaseating granulomas"},{"id":"E","text":"Weil disease, the severe icterohemorrhagic form of leptospirosis with liver and kidney dysfunction"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Weil disease is the severe icterohemorrhagic form of leptospirosis, with jaundice and azotemia from liver and kidney dysfunction, fever, hemorrhage, and anemia. It follows exposure to water contaminated with animal urine, typical of agricultural or recreational flooding exposures. Jaundice with acute kidney injury and hemorrhage in a patient with the flulike prodrome of leptospirosis is the classic picture.',
    '{"A":"Q fever is acquired by inhaling aerosols from cattle or sheep amniotic fluid and typically causes pneumonia or hepatitis without jaundice-predominant renal failure.","B":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever presents with a petechial rash starting at wrists and ankles with vasculitis, not this icteric hemorrhagic renal syndrome after water exposure.","C":"Secondary syphilis features a maculopapular palm-and-sole rash and condylomata lata, not acute jaundice and azotemia.","D":"Brucellosis is acquired from unpasteurized dairy, produces undulant fever and arthralgias, and is not the classic cause of this severe icteric syndrome."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Listeria monocytogenes',
    'A 28-year-old woman at 30 weeks of gestation presents with 2 days of fever, myalgias, and mild watery diarrhea. She reports eating soft unpasteurized cheese and cold deli meats at a party last week. Fetal heart tracing is concerning for distress, and ultrasound shows decreased amniotic fluid. Blood cultures grow a gram-positive rod that grows well at refrigeration temperature.',
    'Which of the following best explains how this organism spreads from cell to cell while avoiding antibody?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"IgA protease cleaving mucosal antibodies at the placental interface"},{"id":"B","text":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 in host cells"},{"id":"C","text":"Formation of heat-resistant endospores that survive in macrophages"},{"id":"D","text":"Polypeptide capsule of poly d-glutamate preventing opsonization"},{"id":"E","text":"Actin polymerization producing rocket tails for direct cell-to-cell movement"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Listeria monocytogenes is a facultative intracellular rod that forms rocket tails by polymerizing host actin, allowing it to move within the cytoplasm and spread directly into adjacent cells across membranes without ever entering the extracellular space, thereby avoiding antibody. Listeriolysin generates pores in the phagosome so the organism can escape into the cytoplasm, and it survives cold enrichment in refrigerated foods.',
    '{"A":"IgA protease is produced by pneumococcus, Neisseria, and Haemophilus to degrade mucosal antibody, and it is not the mechanism of placental or cell-to-cell spread.","B":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is the mechanism of diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A; Listeria does not use it to enter neighboring cells.","C":"Listeria does not form endospores; spore formation is the hallmark of Bacillus and Clostridium species, and spores are not the mechanism of intracellular spread.","D":"A poly d-glutamate polypeptide capsule is characteristic of Bacillus anthracis and does not mediate the actin-based motility described."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Listeria monocytogenes',
    'A 68-year-old man with a kidney transplant on chronic immunosuppression presents with 3 days of fever, headache, and confusion. He has no focal neurologic deficit and no rash. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein and normal glucose. Blood cultures grow a gram-positive rod that shows tumbling motility in broth.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"id":"B","text":"Vancomycin plus ceftazidime"},{"id":"C","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"D","text":"Ampicillin"},{"id":"E","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is Listeria monocytogenes meningitis in an immunocompromised older adult, and the treatment is ampicillin. Listeria is a facultative intracellular gram-positive rod that causes meningitis in immunocompromised patients, neonates, and older adults, and it also grows readily at refrigeration temperatures, which is why cold deli meats and unpasteurized dairy are the classic sources.',
    '{"A":"Ceftriaxone is the backbone of empiric meningitis therapy for pneumococcus and meningococcus but is not reliably active against Listeria, which is why ampicillin is added for high-risk patients.","B":"Vancomycin plus ceftazidime covers gram-positive and gram-negative hospital organisms, but vancomycin is not a first-line agent for listerial meningitis and ceftazidime does not cover it either.","C":"Doxycycline is used for rickettsial disease, borreliosis, and chlamydia, none of which produce this picture, and it is not the drug of choice for Listeria.","E":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the treatment for Nocardia and for Pneumocystis, and although it has activity against Listeria it is reserved for penicillin-allergic patients rather than being first line."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Listeria monocytogenes',
    'A 33-year-old woman who is 22 weeks pregnant has a spontaneous abortion. Placental cultures grow a gram-positive rod that is catalase positive. Histology of the products of conception shows microabscesses and granulomatous inflammation. She recalls eating refrigerated smoked seafood and unpasteurized cheese several times during the pregnancy.',
    'Which of the following best accounts for this organism''s propensity to cause fetal loss in pregnancy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Toxin-mediated destruction of the placenta by an ADP-ribosylating exotoxin"},{"id":"B","text":"Hematogenous spread from a gram-negative mucosal infection"},{"id":"C","text":"Direct ascending infection from the vaginal canal only"},{"id":"D","text":"Antibody-mediated complement activation on the trophoblast"},{"id":"E","text":"Transplacental transmission after ingestion of contaminated food"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Listeria monocytogenes is acquired by ingestion of unpasteurized dairy products and cold deli meats and can also be transmitted transplacentally and by vaginal transmission during birth. In pregnant patients it causes amnionitis, sepsis, and spontaneous abortion, and in the fetus and neonate it produces granulomatosis infantiseptica. Its ability to grow at refrigeration temperatures, called cold enrichment, is why refrigerated ready-to-eat foods are the classic vehicles.',
    '{"A":"Listeria does not cause fetal loss through an ADP-ribosylating exotoxin; that mechanism belongs to diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A, which are not listerial products.","B":"Listeria is a gram-positive rod, not a gram-negative mucosal organism, and its intracellular lifestyle is central to its placental tropism rather than a gram-negative endotoxin mechanism.","C":"Vaginal transmission during birth can occur but does not explain infection in the second trimester; transplacental spread from ingested contaminated food accounts for this presentation.","D":"Complement activation on trophoblasts is not described as the mechanism of listerial placentitis; the organism invades cells directly and spreads cell to cell while avoiding antibody."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Measles, mumps, and rubella',
    'A 5-year-old unvaccinated boy presents with 3 days of fever, cough, coryza, and red eyes. On examination of the buccal mucosa, the physician sees small bright red spots with blue-white centers. A maculopapular rash appears the next day, starting at the head and neck and spreading downward.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the causative virus and its family?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Measles virus, a picornavirus with a positive-sense ssRNA genome"},{"id":"B","text":"Measles (rubeola) virus, a paramyxovirus with a negative-sense ssRNA genome"},{"id":"C","text":"Rubella virus, a matonavirus causing postauricular lymphadenopathy"},{"id":"D","text":"Parainfluenza virus, a paramyxovirus causing a barking cough and stridor"},{"id":"E","text":"Varicella-zoster virus, a herpesvirus causing a vesicular rash in crops"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Measles (rubeola) is a paramyxovirus, an enveloped negative-sense ssRNA virus. The classic prodrome is fever with the 4 C''s: cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and Koplik spots, which are bright red spots with blue-white centers on the buccal mucosa. One to two days after Koplik spots, a maculopapular rash appears at the head and neck and spreads downward.',
    '{"A":"The virus is a paramyxovirus with a negative-sense ssRNA genome, not a positive-sense picornavirus.","C":"Rubella causes a milder illness with postauricular lymphadenopathy and a rash that spreads centrifugally from the face, without the pronounced cough, coryza, and Koplik spots described.","D":"Parainfluenza causes acute laryngotracheobronchitis (croup) with a seal-like barking cough, inspiratory stridor, and a steeple sign on x-ray.","E":"Varicella-zoster virus causes chickenpox with a pruritic vesicular rash, not a maculopapular rash preceded by Koplik spots."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Measles, mumps, and rubella',
    'A 24-year-old man has a history of measles at age 6 during a period of low vaccination coverage. Over the past 3 months he has developed progressive personality change, declining school and work performance, and myoclonic jerks. He becomes demented and develops autonomic dysfunction, and brain biopsy shows viral RNA in neurons.',
    'Which of the following best explains this complication?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, occurring years after wild-type measles infection and progressing to death"},{"id":"B","text":"Acute post-measles encephalitis, which typically appears within days of the rash"},{"id":"C","text":"Giant cell pneumonia from measles in an immunocompetent adult"},{"id":"D","text":"Congenital rubella syndrome with sensorineural deafness and cataracts"},{"id":"E","text":"Reye syndrome triggered by aspirin use during measles"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis is a late complication of measles that occurs years after the acute illness and presents with personality changes, dementia, autonomic dysfunction, and death. Acute post-measles encephalitis is different: it occurs in about 1 in 1000 cases and appears within a few days of the rash. The MMR vaccine prevents both by preventing wild-type infection.',
    '{"B":"Acute encephalitis after measles has symptoms within a few days of the rash, whereas this patient''s decline began many years after infection.","C":"Giant cell pneumonia is rare except in immunosuppressed patients and presents as acute respiratory failure, not a progressive demyelinating neurologic illness.","D":"Congenital rubella syndrome is a congenital TORCH disease with deafness, cataracts, and patent ductus arteriosus, unrelated to a childhood measles infection.","E":"Reye syndrome is an acute illness with hepatic dysfunction and encephalopathy in children recovering from a viral illness who took aspirin, not a late neurodegenerative process in adults."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Measles, mumps, and rubella',
    'A 20-year-old unvaccinated male college student presents with 3 days of fever, bilateral parotid swelling that obscures the angle of the jaw, and testicular pain with swelling of the right testis. He is concerned about his future fertility.',
    'Which of the following best describes this infection and its classic complication?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Mumps virus with epididymitis, which does not affect spermatogenesis"},{"id":"B","text":"Rubella with postauricular lymphadenopathy and arthralgias"},{"id":"C","text":"Parainfluenza virus with croup and salivary gland involvement"},{"id":"D","text":"Measles with parotitis and Koplik spots"},{"id":"E","text":"Mumps virus with orchitis, which can cause sterility especially after puberty"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Mumps is a paramyxovirus that causes parotitis, orchitis, aseptic meningitis, and pancreatitis, remembered as mumps making the parotid glands and testes as big as pom-poms. Orchitis can cause sterility, especially when it occurs after puberty. Mumps is now uncommon because of the effectiveness of the MMR vaccine.',
    '{"A":"Epididymitis alone is not the described syndrome, and mumps orchitis is specifically recognized as a cause of sterility after puberty.","B":"Rubella causes a mild illness with postauricular lymphadenopathy, arthralgias, and a centrifugal rash; it does not produce the parotitis and orchitis pattern.","C":"Parainfluenza causes croup with a barking cough and stridor; it does not cause parotitis or orchitis.","D":"Measles produces Koplik spots and a descending maculopapular rash, not parotitis with orchitis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Measles, mumps, and rubella',
    'A newborn is evaluated for a faint blue-violet papular eruption on the trunk and limbs, along with cataracts, sensorineural hearing loss, and a continuous machinery murmur at the left upper sternal border. Echocardiography confirms a patent ductus arteriosus. The mother had a febrile illness with a rash and postauricular lymphadenopathy during the first trimester.',
    'Which of the following best describes the classic triad of this congenital syndrome?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Microcephaly, subcortical calcifications, and ocular anomalies from congenital Zika virus"},{"id":"B","text":"Hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, and intracranial calcifications from congenital Toxoplasma"},{"id":"C","text":"Vesicular skin lesions, chorioretinitis, and encephalitis from congenital HSV-2"},{"id":"D","text":"Sensorineural deafness, cataracts, and patent ductus arteriosus from congenital rubella"},{"id":"E","text":"Microcephaly, periventricular calcifications, and chorioretinitis from congenital cytomegalovirus"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Congenital rubella is a TORCH infection with the classic triad of sensorineural deafness, cataracts, and patent ductus arteriosus. Infection during the first trimester is most dangerous, and the rash, postauricular lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias of maternal rubella are characteristic. The blueberry muffin appearance reflects dermal extramedullary hematopoiesis.',
    '{"A":"Congenital Zika causes microcephaly with subcortical calcifications and ocular and motor abnormalities; it does not produce cataracts with patent ductus arteriosus.","B":"Congenital toxoplasmosis causes hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, and scattered intracranial calcifications, and it is a protozoan rather than a virus.","C":"Congenital HSV-2 presents with vesicular skin lesions, keratoconjunctivitis, and encephalitis, typically acquired perinatally rather than producing a PDA.","E":"Congenital CMV produces periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, and chorioretinitis with owl''s eye inclusions, not the deafness, cataracts, and PDA triad."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Mechanisms of antibiotic resistance',
    'A 33-year-old woman with a urinary tract infection from E coli is treated with ampicillin but does not improve. The laboratory confirms production of an enzyme that hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring, and the organism is susceptible to a combination containing a beta-lactamase inhibitor.',
    'Which mechanism of resistance does this represent?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Target modification at the ribosomal level"},{"id":"B","text":"Increased efflux with decreased uptake"},{"id":"C","text":"Enzymatic inactivation (drug cleavage)"},{"id":"D","text":"Bypass of a metabolic pathway"},{"id":"E","text":"Reduced drug activation by a bacterial kinase"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Beta-lactamase (penicillinase) cleaves the beta-lactam ring of penicillin G and V, penicillinase-sensitive penicillins, cephalosporins, and carbapenems, a mechanism labeled enzymatic inactivation - drug cleavage in the resistance diagram. Because the enzyme is a secreted hydrolase, it can also protect neighboring organisms, and in gram-negative organisms it accumulates in the periplasm. Beta-lactamase inhibitors restore activity, while penicillinase-resistant penicillins, later-generation cephalosporins, and carbapenems evade hydrolysis structurally. This is why altered PBP affinity matters clinically too: it defeats even enzyme-resistant agents.',
    '{"A":"Ribosomal target modification (e.g., rRNA methylation) affects macrolides and linezolid, not beta-lactam susceptibility.","B":"Increased efflux and decreased uptake apply to tetracyclines, sulfonamides, and fluoroquinolones; they do not hydrolyze a drug.","D":"Pathway bypass describes sulfonamide resistance through an alternative enzyme with low drug affinity.","E":"Reduced drug activation is not the operative mechanism here; aminoglycoside resistance more typically involves drug-modifying enzymes or altered uptake."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Mechanisms of antibiotic resistance',
    'A 47-year-old man with a hospital-acquired pneumonia is treated with a fluoroquinolone but fails to improve. The isolate shows reduced intracellular drug accumulation and mutations in the gene encoding the drug''s enzyme target. The laboratory describes resistance as multifactorial.',
    'Which two resistance mechanisms are described in this isolate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pathway bypass plus methylation of the ribosomal target site in the 50S subunit"},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced drug activation by a bacterial kinase plus alteration of the outer membrane porin channels"},{"id":"C","text":"Drug target modification of the drug''s target enzyme, plus increased efflux and decreased uptake"},{"id":"D","text":"Complete loss of the drug target from the genome plus spontaneous loss of the resistance plasmid"},{"id":"E","text":"Drug cleavage by a hydrolyzing enzyme plus drug modification by acetylation inside the cell"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'First Aid''s resistance diagram groups mechanisms as enzymatic inactivation (drug cleavage for beta-lactams; drug modification for aminoglycosides and chloramphenicol), drug target modification (rRNA methylation for linezolid and macrolides, altered PBPs for penicillinase-resistant penicillins and cephalosporins, altered enzymes for sulfonamides and fluoroquinolones), and increased efflux with decreased uptake (tetracyclines, sulfonamides, fluoroquinolones). This patient''s isolate shows altered target plus reduced accumulation, the classic paired mechanism for fluoroquinolones. Because horizontal gene transfer - transformation, conjugation, transduction, and transposition - is the main route by which resistance spreads between bacteria, multidrug resistance often combines several of these mechanisms in one strain.',
    '{"A":"Pathway bypass concerns folate synthesis and sulfonamides, and ribosome methylation concerns macrolides and linezolid; neither fits DNA gyrase-targeted drugs.","B":"Kinase-mediated reduced activation is not the fluoroquinolone mechanism described, and LPS change is not the relevant envelope effect here.","D":"Loss of target would remove the drug''s effect but does not explain reduced accumulation, and plasmid curing is not a described resistance mechanism in the diagram.","E":"Enzymatic inactivation by cleavage or modification applies to beta-lactams and aminoglycosides/chloramphenicol, not to the fluoroquinolone case described."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Mucor and Rhizopus',
    'A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes presents with two days of facial pain, periorbital swelling, and blurred vision. Examination shows a black necrotic eschar on the palate and impaired extraocular movement. His glucose is 520 mg/dL, arterial pH is 7.18, and serum ketones are positive. Sinus biopsy shows broad, irregular, non-septate hyphae branching at wide angles invading blood vessel walls.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management in addition to urgent surgical debridement and correction of the metabolic derangement?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Amphotericin B"},{"id":"B","text":"Fluconazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Voriconazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Caspofungin"},{"id":"E","text":"Terbinafine"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Mucormycosis is caused by Mucor and Rhizopus species and classically occurs in patients with diabetic ketoacidosis or neutropenia. Spores are inhaled, fungi proliferate in blood vessel walls, penetrate the cribriform plate, and invade the brain, producing rhinocerebral disease, frontal lobe abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis, and the black necrotic eschar on the face. Treatment requires urgent surgical debridement plus amphotericin B or isavuconazole. The broad, non-septate hyphae branching at wide or right angles are the key histologic clue.',
    '{"B":"Fluconazole is an azole used for Candida infections and chronic cryptococcal suppression but lacks reliable activity against Mucorales.","C":"Voriconazole is first-line for Aspergillus, which is the organism with septate hyphae branching at acute 45-degree angles rather than this wide-angle pattern.","D":"Caspofungin inhibits beta-glucan synthesis in the cell wall and is used for invasive aspergillosis and Candida, but echinocandins are not the standard therapy for mucormycosis.","E":"Terbinafine inhibits squalene epoxidase and treats dermatophyte infections of skin and nails only."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Mucor and Rhizopus',
    'A 49-year-old woman with acute leukemia is undergoing induction chemotherapy and has been neutropenic for 12 days. She develops facial swelling, headache, and a dark necrotic lesion over the nasal ala. A biopsy of the nasal mucosa reveals irregular, ribbon-like hyphae without cross-walls that branch at nearly right angles.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Invasive aspergillosis"},{"id":"B","text":"Sporotrichosis"},{"id":"C","text":"Candidal esophagitis"},{"id":"D","text":"Cryptococcal meningitis"},{"id":"E","text":"Mucormycosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The broad, non-septate, irregular hyphae branching at wide or right angles indicate Mucor or Rhizopus species, and the dark necrotic eschar on the face with headache in a neutropenic patient is the classic rhinocerebral presentation. Fungi invade blood vessel walls, penetrate the cribriform plate, and can cause cavernous sinus thrombosis and cranial nerve deficits. These patients are often both neutropenic and acidotic after treatment, and the treatment is surgical debridement plus amphotericin B or isavuconazole.',
    '{"A":"Invasive aspergillosis shows septate hyphae branching at acute 45-degree angles and typically causes pulmonary nodules in neutropenic patients rather than a facial necrotic eschar with right-angle branching.","B":"Sporotrichosis is caused by Sporothrix schenckii, a dimorphic fungus inoculated by a thorn that produces a cigar-shaped yeast and ascending nodular lymphangitis.","C":"Candidal esophagitis produces odynophagia and white plaques in immunocompromised patients and shows pseudohyphae rather than broad non-septate hyphae.","D":"Cryptococcal meningitis presents with headache and encephalitis in patients with low CD4 counts and shows encapsulated yeasts with a clear halo on India ink."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium leprae',
    'A 44-year-old man who works as a wildlife rehabilitator in Texas presents with several years of progressive painless burns and ulcers on his hands and feet, which he cannot feel when he touches hot objects. Examination shows diffuse thickening of the skin of the face with prominent earlobes and eyebrows, and multiple nodular lesions over the trunk and extremities. Skin biopsy shows numerous acid-fast bacilli.',
    'Which of the following best explains the distribution of this patient''s lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The organism replicates in sweat glands, producing local skin destruction only"},{"id":"B","text":"The organism prefers cooler temperatures, so it infects skin and superficial nerves of the extremities and face"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism spreads hematogenously to the central nervous system, causing sensory loss centrally"},{"id":"D","text":"Bacterial toxins destroy motor neurons with a length-dependent pattern"},{"id":"E","text":"Immune complexes deposit in the small vessels of acral skin, causing ischemic neuropathy"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Mycobacterium leprae is an acid-fast bacillus that prefers cool temperatures, so it infects skin and superficial nerves, producing the classic glove-and-stocking loss of sensation that leads to unrecognized trauma, burns, and ulcers. This patient''s diffuse facial infiltration with thickened skin and nodular lesions is the leonine facies of the lepromatous form. The armadillo is a noted reservoir in the United States.',
    '{"A":"The distribution reflects temperature preference broadly across skin and nerves, not selective tropism for sweat glands.","C":"Leprosy does not cause central nervous system disease; the sensory loss results from infection of cool superficial peripheral nerves.","D":"The neuropathy is sensory and results from direct nerve infection, not from a toxin that kills motor neurons.","E":"Immune-complex vasculitis is not the mechanism; the lesions reflect direct bacillary infiltration of skin and nerves."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium leprae',
    'A 36-year-old man from a region with endemic leprosy has a small number of well-demarcated, hypopigmented, hairless plaques on the trunk. Sensation within the plaques is diminished. Skin biopsy shows few organisms and a predominantly Th1-pattern lymphocytic infiltrate with granulomas. He is diagnosed with the tuberculoid form of leprosy.',
    'Which of the following best characterizes the immunologic basis of the tuberculoid form of leprosy compared with the lepromatous form?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"High cell-mediated immunity with a largely Th1 response and a low bacterial load"},{"id":"B","text":"Low cell-mediated immunity with a largely Th2 response and a high bacterial load"},{"id":"C","text":"Absent antibody production with immune-complex mediated nerve destruction"},{"id":"D","text":"Neutrophil-predominant inflammation with abscess formation in peripheral nerves"},{"id":"E","text":"Autoantibodies against myelin basic protein causing demyelinating neuropathy"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The tuberculoid form is characterized by high cell-mediated immunity with a largely Th1 response and a low bacterial load, which limits disease to a few hypoesthetic, hairless skin plaques. The lepromatous form is the opposite: low cell-mediated immunity, a largely Th2 response, high bacterial burden, diffuse skin involvement, and leonine facies. Many cases fall between the two extremes.',
    '{"B":"This describes exactly the lepromatous pole, which is diffuse, communicable, and potentially lethal because of the high bacterial load.","C":"Antibody production is not absent in tuberculoid leprosy; the protective arm here is cell-mediated immunity, and nerve injury is from bacilli and inflammation rather than immune complexes.","D":"Leprosy lesions are granulomatous with lymphocytes and macrophages, not neutrophil-rich abscesses.","E":"This describes an autoimmune demyelinating process such as Guillain-Barre, not leprosy, in which nerve damage follows direct bacillary involvement."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium leprae',
    'A 52-year-old man with lepromatous leprosy and extensive nodular skin lesions is started on therapy chosen for his high bacterial burden. His brother, who has only a few hypoesthetic plaques and is diagnosed with tuberculoid leprosy, is started on a simpler regimen.',
    'Which of the following best explains why clofazimine is added to this patient''s regimen?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Clofazimine replaces dapsone when dapsone resistance develops in the tuberculoid form"},{"id":"B","text":"Clofazimine provides rapid relief of nerve pain in tuberculoid disease"},{"id":"C","text":"Clofazimine is a vaccine adjuvant given with BCG in lepromatous disease"},{"id":"D","text":"The lepromatous form has a high bacterial burden and is treated with rifampin and dapsone plus clofazimine"},{"id":"E","text":"Clofazimine is needed to treat coexisting tuberculosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Tuberculoid leprosy is treated with dapsone and rifampin, whereas clofazimine is added for the lepromatous form, which carries a much higher bacterial load and is communicable. The mnemonic given is that dapsone and rifampin cover the tuberculoid form while the lepromatous form needs the additional agent. Clofazimine is not an antituberculous drug.',
    '{"A":"The added agent is for the lepromatous form''s higher burden; tuberculoid disease is treated with dapsone and rifampin alone.","B":"Clofazimine is not an analgesic and does not treat nerve pain; neuropathic pain in leprosy is managed separately.","C":"BCG is a vaccine given before exposure and is not combined with clofazimine as an adjuvant in treatment.","E":"Clofazimine is an antileprosy agent; coexisting tuberculosis would require standard antituberculous drugs such as isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis',
    'A 42-year-old man who recently emigrated from a country with a high burden of tuberculosis presents with 3 months of low-grade fevers, drenching night sweats, an 8-kg weight loss, and a productive cough with blood-streaked sputum. Chest radiograph shows a cavitary lesion in the apex of the right upper lobe. Sputum is processed for acid-fast staining and culture.',
    'Which of the following properties of Mycobacterium tuberculosis best explains its detection by the acid-fast stain but not by routine Gram stain?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An outer membrane with lipopolysaccharide that takes up the safranin counterstain"},{"id":"B","text":"A polysaccharide capsule that prevents penetration of both crystal violet and carbol fuchsin"},{"id":"C","text":"A deficiency of muramic acid that renders the organism unable to retain any stain"},{"id":"D","text":"A waxy lipid-rich cell wall containing mycolic acids that retains carbol fuchsin despite acid-alcohol decolorization"},{"id":"E","text":"A thick peptidoglycan layer with teichoic acids that traps crystal violet-iodine complexes"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Mycobacteria are acid-fast rods: their cell wall is rich in mycolic acids and other waxy lipids, so once carbol fuchsin is driven in with heat it resists decolorization by acid-alcohol and the bacilli appear as pink rods. Gram stain does not reliably demonstrate them for the same reason. This waxy wall also makes the organisms slow-growing. Cord factor and sulfatides are additional surface lipids important for virulence.',
    '{"A":"A lipopolysaccharide-containing outer membrane is the gram-negative arrangement and is why gram-negative organisms decolorize and take up safranin, the opposite of what is described here.","B":"Although mycobacteria possess surface glycolipids, it is the waxy mycolic acid wall that is stained, not a capsule that excludes stain; a capsule is not the basis of acid-fast staining.","C":"Reduced muramic acid with no classic peptidoglycan describes the chlamydial cell wall, which is why beta-lactams fail against Chlamydia, not why mycobacteria stain with carbol fuchsin.","E":"That describes a classic gram-positive wall; thick peptidoglycan with teichoic acids retains crystal violet in the Gram stain but has nothing to do with acid-fastness."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis',
    'A 29-year-old woman with a positive tuberculin skin test and an abnormal chest radiograph is diagnosed with active pulmonary tuberculosis. She is started on a multidrug regimen. Two weeks later she reports that her medications are making her urine and other secretions orange, and she asks whether this is dangerous.',
    'Which of the following changes in her regimen would most seriously undermine the principle that tuberculosis must be treated with multiple agents simultaneously?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Advising her to take the medications on an empty stomach"},{"id":"B","text":"Eliminating the agent that causes orange discoloration of body fluids and continuing the other drugs"},{"id":"C","text":"Adding pyridoxine to prevent peripheral neuropathy during therapy"},{"id":"D","text":"Continuing the regimen for a minimum of several months after sputum conversion"},{"id":"E","text":"Monitoring liver enzymes and visual acuity while on treatment"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'The source emphasizes that M tuberculosis is often resistant to multiple drugs, which is why it is treated with combination therapy rather than a single agent; stopping one drug of a combination leaves the organism exposed to fewer agents and selects for resistance. The orange discoloration is a benign, expected effect of rifampin and is not a reason to modify therapy. Rifampin is a cornerstone of TB treatment, so removing it would both weaken the regimen and risk resistance.',
    '{"A":"Empty-stomach dosing helps absorption and is appropriate; it is a pharmacokinetic instruction, not a change in the number of active agents.","C":"Pyridoxine is given to prevent isoniazid-related peripheral neuropathy, an appropriate adjunct that does not weaken the regimen.","D":"Prolonging therapy well past culture and symptom resolution is standard for TB because the organism is slow-growing and persisting bacilli are difficult to eradicate; it strengthens rather than undermines treatment.","E":"Hepatotoxicity from isoniazid and rifampin and ethambutol-related optic neuritis are real adverse effects that warrant monitoring, which supports safe multi-agent therapy."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis',
    'A 55-year-old man with psoriasis has been treated with a TNF-alpha inhibitor for 2 years. He now presents with fever, night sweats, weight loss, and hemoptysis. Chest radiograph reveals a fibrocaseous cavitary lesion in the lung apices. Sputum grows Mycobacterium tuberculosis.',
    'Which of the following best explains why this patient developed disease in the lung apices?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Aerophilic bacteria deposited selectively on the pleural surface"},{"id":"B","text":"Resistance of mycobacteria to alveolar macrophage killing in the lower lobes only"},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of cell-mediated immunity together with the organism''s preference for the most highly oxygenated lung tissue"},{"id":"D","text":"Direct spread of bacteria from the hilar lymph nodes into the upper lobes"},{"id":"E","text":"Preferential lymphatic drainage of the upper lobes into the thoracic duct"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'TB reactivation risk is highest in immunocompromised patients, including those on TNF-alpha inhibitors, HIV, and transplant immunosuppression. The source attributes the apical predilection of reactivation disease to the bacteria being highly aerobic, so the well-ventilated apices are the favored site of cavitary disease. Blocking TNF-alpha also impairs granuloma maintenance, allowing dormant organisms to resume growth.',
    '{"A":"The location is not pleural-directed deposition but intraparenchymal growth in highly oxygenated apical tissue.","B":"Organisms are not confined to lower lobes; primary infection typically involves the mid-to-lower lobes and the apices are the site of reactivation, the opposite of this statement.","D":"Hilar node involvement is part of the primary Ghon complex; secondary reactivation arises from endogenous reseeding, not from contiguous nodal spread into the apices.","E":"Regional lymphatic drainage does not explain the apical location of reactivation lesions and is not the mechanism cited."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis',
    'A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection and a CD4+ count of 60 cells/mm3 has fever and weight loss. Chest radiograph shows diffuse small nodules throughout both lungs, and biopsy of a cervical lymph node shows caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells whose nuclei are arranged in a horseshoe pattern at the periphery. A tuberculin skin test is negative.',
    'Which of the following best explains the negative tuberculin skin test in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Depletion of CD4+ T cells prevents the delayed hypersensitivity reaction required for a positive test"},{"id":"B","text":"Prior BCG vaccination neutralized the purified protein derivative"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism has switched from an acid-fast to a non-acid-fast form"},{"id":"D","text":"Miliary spread produces tuberculin antigen that is neutralized in the bloodstream"},{"id":"E","text":"TNF-alpha release prevents induration at the injection site"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'A positive PPD reflects prior or current infection and is a delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction requiring functional CD4+ T cells. In profoundly immunocompromised patients, especially those with low CD4+ counts, the test is falsely negative, so a negative PPD cannot exclude infection. The enormous bacterial burden with hematogenous dissemination in miliary tuberculosis also reflects failed cellular immunity.',
    '{"B":"BCG vaccination causes false-positive PPDs, not false negatives; the interferon-gamma release assay is the test noted for fewer false positives from BCG.","C":"Mycobacteria remain acid-fast; failure of the host response, not a change in staining, explains the negative test.","D":"There is no neutralization of antigen by circulating tuberculin; the problem is an absent T-cell response.","E":"TNF-alpha promotes granuloma formation and is part of the normal response; it does not suppress induration in this setting."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis',
    'A 6-year-old girl who recently moved from a country with endemic tuberculosis has a positive tuberculin skin test performed as part of screening. She has no fever, cough, weight loss, or night sweats, and her chest radiograph is normal. Records show she received the BCG vaccine as an infant. The public health clinic is choosing the next confirmatory test.',
    'Which of the following is the main advantage of the interferon-gamma release assay over the tuberculin skin test in this child?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It identifies multidrug-resistant strains of M tuberculosis"},{"id":"B","text":"It remains reliable in patients with profound CD4+ depletion"},{"id":"C","text":"It quantifies the size of the induration at 48 to 72 hours"},{"id":"D","text":"It has fewer false-positive results attributable to prior BCG vaccination"},{"id":"E","text":"It detects active cavitary disease before symptoms begin"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The source states that the interferon-gamma release assay has fewer false positives from BCG vaccination than the PPD, because it uses antigens more specific to M tuberculosis rather than purified protein derivative that cross-reacts with BCG. Both tests reflect immunologic memory of infection and do not distinguish latent from active disease. Confirmation of active disease still requires imaging, microbiology, and clinical assessment.',
    '{"A":"Neither test characterizes drug susceptibility; that requires culture with susceptibility testing or molecular resistance testing of the isolate.","B":"Both tests rely on T-cell responses and can be falsely negative in severe immunosuppression, so this is not a relative advantage.","C":"Induration measurement is specific to the tuberculin skin test; the IGRA is a blood assay without a skin reaction.","E":"Neither test distinguishes latent infection from active disease; active disease is diagnosed by symptoms, imaging, and microbiologic studies such as sputum culture."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae',
    'A 20-year-old college student living in a dormitory presents with 2 weeks of low-grade fever, headache, and a persistent dry nonproductive cough that has gradually worsened. He has not been ill enough to miss classes. Chest radiograph shows a patchy bilateral interstitial infiltrate that looks more severe than his overall appearance. Sputum Gram stain shows no organisms.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of the most appropriate treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An aminoglycoside that binds the 30S ribosomal subunit"},{"id":"B","text":"A sulfonamide that inhibits folic acid synthesis"},{"id":"C","text":"A macrolide that targets the 50S ribosomal subunit"},{"id":"D","text":"A beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins to block cell wall synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"A glycopeptide that binds the cell wall D-Ala-D-Ala precursors"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a classic cause of atypical walking pneumonia with an insidious onset, headache, nonproductive cough, patchy interstitial infiltrate, and a chest radiograph that looks worse than the patient. It lacks a cell wall, so cell-wall drugs such as penicillins and cephalosporins are ineffective; macrolides, doxycycline, or fluoroquinolones are the treatments. Macrolides act by inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis at the 50S ribosome.',
    '{"A":"Aminoglycosides are used for gram-negative aerobic infections and are not the recommended therapy for atypical pneumonia.","B":"Sulfonamides block folate synthesis and are used for Nocardia and as a pertussis alternative, not for Mycoplasma.","D":"Beta-lactams require a cell wall; Mycoplasma has none, so penicillins are ineffective despite being standard therapy for typical pneumonia pathogens such as Streptococcus pneumoniae.","E":"Glycopeptides such as vancomycin bind D-Ala-D-Ala precursors in the cell wall, which Mycoplasma lacks, so this drug class has no target."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae',
    'A 17-year-old recruit at a military training camp develops fever, sore throat, and a nonproductive cough. Chest radiograph shows a diffuse interstitial infiltrate. Laboratory studies show a high titer of IgM autoantibodies that agglutinate red blood cells at cold temperatures, and a relative lymphocytosis. There is no growth on routine blood agar.',
    'Which of the following best explains this patient''s laboratory findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Antibodies against the gram-negative cell wall lipopolysaccharide cross-react with red blood cells"},{"id":"B","text":"IgG antibodies against chlamydial elementary bodies agglutinate erythrocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"IgA protease produced by the organism cleaves erythrocyte surface antigens"},{"id":"D","text":"The organism adsorbs to red cells through its capsule and causes hemagglutination"},{"id":"E","text":"Cold agglutinins are IgM antibodies that agglutinate red blood cells in mycoplasma infection"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'A high titer of cold agglutinins, which are IgM antibodies that can agglutinate red blood cells, is a characteristic laboratory finding in Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall, so it is not seen on Gram stain and appears pleomorphic, and it is grown on Eaton agar because its membrane requires sterols for stability. Outbreaks occur in military recruits, prisons, and colleges.',
    '{"A":"Mycoplasma has no cell wall or lipopolysaccharide, which is also why it does not stain with Gram stain.","B":"Cold agglutinins in this setting are IgM autoantibodies, and chlamydial infection is not the cause of this respiratory picture.","C":"IgA protease is produced by organisms such as Haemophilus influenzae and Neisseria to cleave secretory IgA, not to alter red cells.","D":"The agglutination is antibody-mediated autoimmune cross-reactivity, not direct bacterial adherence through a capsule."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae',
    'A 22-year-old woman presents with a low-grade fever, a dry cough, and an erythematous macular rash over her trunk. Her chest radiograph shows patchy infiltrates. Sputum cultures on standard blood and MacConkey agar show no growth after 48 hours, and a Gram stain of respiratory secretions is unrevealing.',
    'Which of the following properties of the organism best explains why it is not identified by routine culture and Gram stain?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It requires cysteine and iron supplementation for growth"},{"id":"B","text":"It grows only within the cytoplasm of host cells and cannot be cultured at all"},{"id":"C","text":"It lacks a cell wall and requires sterols in its membrane, growing poorly on routine media"},{"id":"D","text":"It is an obligate anaerobe that cannot survive atmospheric oxygen"},{"id":"E","text":"It requires factors V and X that are absent from blood agar"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Mycoplasma pneumoniae has no cell wall, so it takes up neither Gram stain nor the cell-wall-directed antibiotics, and appears pleomorphic on specialized preparations. Its bacterial membrane contains sterols for stability, so it grows on specialized media such as Eaton agar rather than routine plates. This unusual structure is also why the chest radiograph commonly looks more severe than the patient''s presentation.',
    '{"A":"Charcoal yeast extract with iron and cysteine is the required medium for Legionella pneumophila, which also stains poorly with Gram stain but is a gram-negative rod.","B":"Unlike chlamydiae, Mycoplasma can be grown on cell-free media such as Eaton agar; it is not an obligate intracellular organism.","D":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae is aerobic and is not an obligate anaerobe; the poor growth reflects nutritional requirements rather than oxygen intolerance.","E":"Factors V and X are required by Haemophilus influenzae, which grows on chocolate agar and does appear on Gram stain as a small gram-negative coccobacillary rod."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae',
    'A 22-year-old man presents with 3 days of dysuria and a purulent urethral discharge. He is afebrile and has no rash. A urethral smear shows neutrophils containing intracellular gram-negative diplococci. He reports a new sexual partner 2 weeks ago and has never been tested for sexually transmitted infections.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis"},{"id":"C","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"id":"D","text":"Escherichia coli"},{"id":"E","text":"Neisseria meningitidis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus that is often seen intracellularly within neutrophils and produces IgA proteases. It is sexually and perinatally transmitted and causes gonorrhea, septic arthritis, neonatal conjunctivitis at 2 to 5 days after birth, pelvic inflammatory disease, and Fitz-Hugh–Curtis syndrome, and it is diagnosed with NAAT.',
    '{"B":"Chlamydia trachomatis causes nongonococcal urethritis, but it is an obligate intracellular organism that does not stain on Gram as gram-negative diplococci and characteristically produces cytoplasmic inclusions.","C":"Treponema pallidum is a spirochete visualized by dark-field or fluorescent microscopy and produces a painless chancre, not a purulent urethral discharge.","D":"Escherichia coli is a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod causing urinary tract infection, not a diplococcus seen inside neutrophils in urethral discharge.","E":"Neisseria meningitidis is also a gram-negative diplococcus but is transmitted by respiratory and oral secretions and causes meningococcemia and meningitis rather than urethritis."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae',
    'A 24-year-old woman presents with 5 days of lower abdominal pain, fever, and purulent vaginal discharge. She has worsening pain in the right upper quadrant with pleuritic features on inspiration. Pelvic examination shows cervical motion tenderness and adnexal tenderness, and NAAT of a vaginal swab is positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of the right upper quadrant pain in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cholangitis from ascending biliary infection"},{"id":"B","text":"Inflammation of the liver capsule and adjacent peritoneum"},{"id":"C","text":"Gonococcal endocarditis with hepatic emboli"},{"id":"D","text":"Liver abscess from portal vein bacteremia"},{"id":"E","text":"Hematogenous seeding of the gallbladder"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is Fitz-Hugh–Curtis syndrome, in which gonococcal pelvic inflammatory disease spreads within the peritoneum to produce inflammation of the liver capsule, causing right upper quadrant pleuritic pain. Gonococci cause PID and Fitz-Hugh–Curtis syndrome in addition to urethritis, septic arthritis, and neonatal conjunctivitis at 2 to 5 days after birth.',
    '{"A":"Ascending biliary infection in the setting of obstruction typically involves enteric organisms such as E coli and would present with jaundice and cholestasis.","C":"Gonococcal endocarditis is rare and causes valvular findings and systemic emboli, not isolated perihepatic pain following pelvic infection.","D":"A liver abscess would show a focal fluid collection rather than thin adhesions between the liver capsule and diaphragm, and it is not the classic complication described.","E":"Gallbladder seeding would not be expected from an ascending genital infection and would produce focal gallbladder findings rather than perihepatic inflammation after PID."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae',
    'A 23-year-old man is diagnosed with uncomplicated gonococcal urethritis. He is treated with a single intramuscular dose of ceftriaxone. The clinic''s molecular testing for chlamydial coinfection has not yet resulted, and the clinician is deciding whether to add additional antibiotics. The team also discusses why there is no gonococcal vaccine.',
    'Which of the following best explains the current approach to treatment and prevention in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Treat with single-dose ceftriaxone and add doxycycline if chlamydial coinfection is not excluded, since no vaccine exists because of antigenic variation of pilus proteins"},{"id":"B","text":"Manage with erythromycin eye ointment alone"},{"id":"C","text":"Immunize with a polysaccharide conjugate vaccine after treatment"},{"id":"D","text":"Use penicillin G monotherapy because resistance is not described"},{"id":"E","text":"Provide rifampin prophylaxis to all sexual contacts without treating the patient"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Gonorrhea is treated with a single intramuscular dose of ceftriaxone, and if chlamydial coinfection has not been excluded by molecular testing, doxycycline is added. No gonococcal vaccine exists because of antigenic variation of pilus proteins, unlike meningococcus, for which a vaccine is available. Condoms reduce sexual transmission, and erythromycin eye ointment in the newborn prevents neonatal blindness.',
    '{"B":"Erythromycin eye ointment is a neonatal preventive measure against ophthalmia neonatorum, not a treatment for established adult urethritis.","C":"There is no gonococcal vaccine because pilus proteins vary antigenically, so vaccination is not part of management.","D":"Penicillin G monotherapy is the treatment for syphilis caused by Treponema pallidum, not for gonorrhea, and gonococcal penicillin resistance is well recognized.","E":"Rifampin prophylaxis for contacts applies to meningococcal and Haemophilus disease; the patient himself requires antibiotic treatment for gonorrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria meningitidis',
    'A 19-year-old college freshman living in a crowded dormitory develops fever to 39.5°C, severe headache, neck stiffness, and photophobia over 12 hours. He is confused and hypotensive. Examination shows a rapidly spreading petechial and purpuric rash with early gangrenous changes of the toes. Cerebrospinal fluid Gram stain shows gram-negative diplococci.',
    'Which of the following laboratory findings best distinguishes this organism from Neisseria gonorrhoeae?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Acid production from both maltose and glucose"},{"id":"B","text":"Growth on Thayer-Martin agar"},{"id":"C","text":"Fermentation of glucose only"},{"id":"D","text":"Absence of a polysaccharide capsule"},{"id":"E","text":"Intracellular location within neutrophils"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Meningococci produce acid from both maltose and glucose, whereas gonococci produce acid from glucose only, which is the classic biochemical separation. Neisseria meningitidis is encapsulated, is transmitted by respiratory and oral secretions, and thrives among people in close quarters such as army barracks and college dorms, causing meningococcemia with petechiae and meningitis.',
    '{"B":"Both Neisseria species grow on selective Thayer-Martin agar, so this does not distinguish between them.","C":"Glucose-only acid production is the pattern of Neisseria gonorrhoeae, the organism that causes gonorrhea, PID, and neonatal conjunctivitis.","D":"Meningococci have a polysaccharide capsule, which is the basis of the vaccine and of its antiphagocytic virulence; lack of a capsule is a feature of gonococci.","E":"Being intracellular within neutrophils is characteristic of N gonorrhoeae in urethral smear, whereas meningococci are more often seen free in cerebrospinal fluid."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria meningitidis',
    'A 22-year-old army recruit in basic training is admitted with fever, headache, and a petechial rash. Within hours he becomes profoundly hypotensive and unresponsive to fluid resuscitation. Laboratory studies show hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, and eosinophilia with a low cortisol. He dies despite broad-spectrum antibiotics and intensive care.',
    'Which of the following best describes the complication responsible for his terminal deterioration?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Toxic shock syndrome from a superantigen"},{"id":"B","text":"Endocarditis with septic emboli to the skin"},{"id":"C","text":"Pseudomembranous colitis from antecedent antibiotics"},{"id":"D","text":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome with acute hemorrhagic adrenal insufficiency"},{"id":"E","text":"Fitz-Hugh–Curtis syndrome with perihepatitis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is acute hemorrhagic adrenal insufficiency caused by massive meningococcal sepsis, producing destruction of the adrenal glands with hypotension refractory to fluids and the electrolyte and glucose derangements described. Meningococcemia also produces petechial hemorrhages and gangrene of the toes, and prophylaxis of close contacts with rifampin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone is required.',
    '{"A":"Toxic shock syndrome from a superantigen is caused by staphylococcal TSST-1 or streptococcal toxins and does not produce the specific adrenal hemorrhage and cortisol deficiency seen here.","B":"Endocarditis with septic emboli usually involves gram-positive organisms such as S aureus or viridans streptococci and produces Osler nodes or Janeway lesions rather than fulminant adrenal failure.","C":"Pseudomembranous colitis is toxin-mediated Clostridioides difficile disease after antibiotic exposure and presents with diarrhea and colitis, not with purpura fulminans.","E":"Fitz-Hugh–Curtis syndrome is perihepatitis complicating gonococcal or chlamydial pelvic inflammatory disease, with right upper quadrant pain and violin-string adhesions, not adrenal hemorrhage."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Neisseria meningitidis',
    'A 20-year-old college student is diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis and is started on intravenous ceftriaxone. The county health department is notified. His roommate, who shares a bathroom and eating utensils with him, has no symptoms and is at risk for invasive disease in the coming days.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention for the asymptomatic roommate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"No intervention, since he is asymptomatic"},{"id":"B","text":"Intrapartum penicillin prophylaxis"},{"id":"C","text":"Erythromycin eye ointment"},{"id":"D","text":"Diphtheria antitoxin and a vaccine booster"},{"id":"E","text":"Rifampin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone prophylaxis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Close contacts of a patient with meningococcal disease should receive prophylaxis with rifampin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone to eradicate nasopharyngeal carriage and prevent secondary cases. Transmission of N meningitidis is via respiratory and oral secretions, and disease is more common among individuals in close quarters such as college dormitories and army barracks.',
    '{"A":"Asymptomatic nasopharyngeal carriers are the reservoir for transmission, so observation alone would leave the roommate at substantial risk during the incubation period.","B":"Intrapartum penicillin prophylaxis prevents neonatal group B streptococcal disease and is irrelevant to a college roommate of a meningitis patient.","C":"Erythromycin eye ointment prevents neonatal gonococcal and chlamydial conjunctivitis and has no role in meningococcal prophylaxis.","D":"Diphtheria antitoxin plus a booster is the management of Corynebacterium diphtheriae infection, a gram-positive rod disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Ascaris, hookworms, Strongyloides',
    'A 6-year-old boy in a rural village presents with 3 days of dry cough and wheezing, followed by crampy abdominal discomfort. His mother reports that he passed a large, pale, cylindrical worm in his stool yesterday. A chest radiograph obtained during the cough illness shows patchy migratory infiltrates, and a complete blood count shows eosinophilia. He has been playing in a sandbox and has eaten dirt.',
    'Which of the following best describes the route by which this organism infects humans?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A female mosquito bite that deposits infective larvae"},{"id":"B","text":"Ingestion of eggs in food contaminated with soil"},{"id":"C","text":"Ingestion of larvae encysted in striated muscle"},{"id":"D","text":"Cercariae from a snail intermediate host penetrating skin"},{"id":"E","text":"Larvae in soil that penetrate intact skin"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Ascaris lumbricoides is the giant roundworm, transmitted by the fecal-oral route through ingestion of eggs from contaminated soil, and it belongs to the ingested group in the First Aid EATTT mnemonic. The eggs are knobby-coated and oval. In heavier infection, larvae migrate through the lungs to the alveoli and produce Loeffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia and cough, which explains this child''s chest findings. Other complications include obstruction at the ileocecal valve, biliary obstruction, and intestinal perforation.',
    '{"A":"A female mosquito bite transmits the filarial worms Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia malayi, which cause lymphatic filariasis and lymphedema rather than stool-passed worms.","C":"Larvae encysted in striated muscle are the transmission form of Trichinella spiralis from undercooked pork, causing myalgias and periorbital edema rather than intestinal worms with eosinophilic pneumonia.","D":"Cercariae from a snail intermediate host penetrate skin in Schistosoma infection, which produces portal hypertension or bladder carcinoma instead of a large intestinal roundworm.","E":"Larvae in soil that penetrate intact skin are the route for Strongyloides stercoralis and the hookworms Ancylostoma and Necator, the group First Aid groups under the SANd mnemonic."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Ascaris, hookworms, Strongyloides',
    'A 9-year-old boy in a rural village in sub-Saharan Africa presents with fatigue, pica, and poor school performance. He walks barefoot on sandy soil around his home and near a beach. Examination shows pale conjunctivae and tachycardia. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL, mean corpuscular volume 68 fL, a low ferritin level, and elevated platelets. Stool microscopy shows ova consistent with a blood-feeding intestinal worm, and stool occult blood is positive.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely consequence of this child''s infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Microcytic anemia from chronic intestinal blood loss"},{"id":"B","text":"Lower extremity elephantiasis with a female mosquito bite history"},{"id":"C","text":"A pruritic serpiginous rash on the foot"},{"id":"D","text":"Rectal prolapse following chronic loose stools"},{"id":"E","text":"Myalgias with periorbital edema after eating pork"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Hookworms such as Ancylostoma species and Necator americanus enter through the skin when larvae penetrate a bare foot walking on contaminated beach or soil. The adult worms attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic microcytic iron deficiency anemia, which is what this child has. First Aid lists iron deficiency anemia as the hint association for Ancylostoma and Necator, and treatment is albendazole or pyrantel pamoate.',
    '{"C":"A pruritic serpiginous rash is cutaneous larva migrans, which is also caused by hookworm larvae, but it is a self-limited skin phase rather than the cause of severe anemia and pica.","D":"Rectal prolapse with chronic loose stools is the classic complication of heavy Trichuris trichiura infection, a whipworm that does not suck blood from the intestinal wall.","E":"Myalgias with periorbital edema after eating undercooked pork indicate trichinosis from Trichinella spiralis, whose larvae encyst in muscle and do not cause chronic iron deficiency.","B":"Elephantiasis with a mosquito bite history is lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti or Brugia malayi, which obstructs lymphatics rather than causing anemia."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Ascaris, hookworms, Strongyloides',
    'A 55-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation and started on high-dose prednisone. One week later he develops fever, dry cough with blood-streaked sputum, diffuse abdominal pain, and a rapidly spreading petechial rash on the abdomen and thighs. He worked on farms in Southeast Asia 30 years ago. Laboratory studies show eosinophilia and gram-negative bacteremia, and stool microscopy shows small, slender larvae rather than eggs.',
    'Which of the following organisms most likely explains this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ancylostoma species"},{"id":"B","text":"Enterobius vermicularis"},{"id":"C","text":"Trichuris trichiura"},{"id":"D","text":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},{"id":"E","text":"Strongyloides stercoralis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Strongyloides stercoralis enters through the skin when larvae in soil penetrate it, and alone among these nematodes it can complete its life cycle inside the host, so autoinfection can persist for decades after the initial exposure. When cellular immunity declines, especially with corticosteroids or HTLV-1 infection, accelerated autoinfection causes hyperinfection syndrome with gastrointestinal, pulmonary, and cutaneous symptoms, and gut bacteria can be carried into the bloodstream. Diagnosis is by rhabditiform larvae in the stool, and treatment is ivermectin.',
    '{"A":"Ancylostoma hookworms share the skin-penetration route but produce chronic iron deficiency anemia from blood feeding and do not establish host-internal replication.","B":"Enterobius vermicularis causes nocturnal perianal pruritus in children, is diagnosed by the tape test for eggs, and does not cause hyperinfection or bacteremia.","C":"Trichuris trichiura is ingested as eggs and causes loose stools, anemia, and rectal prolapse in children, with eggs rather than larvae seen in stool.","D":"Ascaris also migrates through the lungs and causes eosinophilia, but it cannot replicate within the host, so corticosteroids cannot trigger an autoinfection-driven hyperinfection syndrome."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Ascaris, hookworms, Strongyloides',
    'A 47-year-old man who lived in rural Cambodia as a child presents with 3 months of intermittent sharp epigastric pain with nausea, a dry cough, and a pruritic rash over the buttocks and lower back. He reports frequent loose stools and has had multiple courses of antibiotics without improvement. Laboratory studies show eosinophilia, and stool microscopy shows rhabditiform larvae.',
    'Which of the following best characterizes this organism''s clinical spectrum?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rectal prolapse with chronic loose stools in a child"},{"id":"B","text":"Symptoms of duodenitis, cough, and pruritic skin rash in one patient"},{"id":"C","text":"Microcytic anemia with a serpiginous rash on the foot"},{"id":"D","text":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity"},{"id":"E","text":"Myalgias, periorbital edema, and fever after undercooked pork"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Strongyloides stercoralis is the threadworm, and First Aid emphasizes that it can produce gastrointestinal symptoms such as duodenitis, pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis, and cutaneous symptoms such as pruritus. This multiorgan spectrum occurs because larvae penetrate the skin, migrate through the lungs, and mature in the small bowel, and in immunosuppression accelerated autoinfection produces hyperinfection syndrome. Larvae in soil penetrate the skin, and rhabditiform larvae are seen in the stool.',
    '{"A":"Rectal prolapse with chronic loose stools in a child is the classic Trichuris trichiura picture, a whipworm acquired by ingesting eggs and diagnosed by eggs in stool rather than larvae.","C":"Microcytic anemia with a serpiginous rash describes hookworm infection, which shares the skin entry route but produces blood-loss anemia and does not cause this combined duodenitis-pulmonary-cutaneous picture.","D":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity describe Onchocerca volvulus and river blindness, transmitted by the black fly rather than by soil larvae.","E":"Myalgias with periorbital edema and fever after undercooked pork describe trichinosis from Trichinella spiralis, whose larvae encyst in striated muscle."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Ascaris, hookworms, Strongyloides',
    'A 10-year-old girl in Brazil is found to have Ascaris lumbricoides eggs on a school screening stool examination. Her neighborhood also has a high rate of hookworm infection in children, and an outreach team is treating soil-transmitted helminths in the community while a local patient is being treated for Onchocerca volvulus river blindness.',
    'Which of the following best summarizes the First Aid anthelmintic associations for these organisms?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Praziquantel as the treatment for all three organisms"},{"id":"B","text":"Ivermectin as the first-line agent for Ascaris and Trichuris"},{"id":"C","text":"Metronidazole as the treatment for Strongyloides and hookworm"},{"id":"D","text":"albendazole, with ivermectin as the first-line agent for Strongyloides"},{"id":"E","text":"Pyrantel pamoate as the first-line agent for Ascaris and hookworm"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'First Aid lists albendazole as the treatment for Ascaris lumbricoides and Trichuris trichiura, with pyrantel pamoate added as an alternative for Enterobius and hookworm. Strongyloides stercoralis is treated with ivermectin or albendazole, and ivermectin is also the drug for Onchocerca volvulus river blindness. The high-yield distinction is between the albendazole-first intestinal nematodes and the ivermectin-first tissue and filarial worms.',
    '{"A":"Praziquantel treats trematodes and cestodes such as Schistosoma, Clonorchis, Taenia, and Diphyllobothrium, and it has no activity against nematodes.","B":"Ivermectin is the first-line agent for Strongyloides and Onchocerca rather than for Ascaris and Trichuris, which are albendazole-first in First Aid.","C":"Metronidazole is a protozoal drug for Giardia, Entamoeba, and Trichomonas, and it has no anthelmintic activity.","E":"Pyrantel pamoate appears as an alternative for Enterobius and hookworm, but First Aid lists albendazole for Ascaris, so this reverses the emphasis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Nematodes: Enterobius and Trichuris',
    'A 6-year-old girl is brought in by her mother for 3 weeks of intense itching around the anus that wakes her at night and causes irritability and poor sleep. She shares a bedroom with two younger siblings, and one of them has similar itching. Examination shows excoriations and mild erythema of the perianal skin. Stool microscopy is negative for ova and parasites.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely presenting complaint of this child''s infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Biliary colic with pigmented gallstones"},{"id":"B","text":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity"},{"id":"C","text":"Nocturnal perianal pruritus"},{"id":"D","text":"Microcytic anemia with pica"},{"id":"E","text":"Right upper quadrant pain with an anchovy paste aspirate"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Enterobius vermicularis, the pinworm, causes anal pruritus that is worse at night, and it is most common in children aged 5-10. The female worm migrates to the perianal skin to deposit eggs, so stool microscopy is often negative and the diagnosis is made with the tape test, which visualizes the eggs. Transmission is fecal-oral and treatment is albendazole or pyrantel pamoate, along with treatment of household contacts.',
    '{"A":"Biliary colic with pigmented gallstones and cholangiocarcinoma is the Clonorchis sinensis picture after eating undercooked fish, not a pinworm presentation in a child.","B":"Subcutaneous nodules with loss of visual acuity describe Onchocerca volvulus river blindness, a filarial worm transmitted by a black fly rather than by the fecal-oral route.","D":"Microcytic anemia with pica is the result of hookworm blood feeding at the intestinal wall, and pinworms do not suck blood or cause iron deficiency.","E":"Right upper quadrant pain with an anchovy paste aspirate describes an amebic liver abscess from Entamoeba histolytica, a protozoan complication unrelated to perianal pruritus."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Normal flora and opportunistic infections',
    'A 72-year-old man has a witnessed aspiration event after a stroke, and 3 days later develops fever, productive cough, and a right lower lobe infiltrate with a foul-smelling sputum sample. Anaerobic culture grows organisms from the mouth.',
    'Which statement best explains why these organisms are pathogenic in this setting?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Clostridium perfringens, a catalase-negative spore-forming anaerobic bacillus"},{"id":"B","text":"Listeria monocytogenes, a catalase-positive facultative intracellular rod"},{"id":"C","text":"Burkholderia cepacia, a catalase-positive gram-negative rod forming biofilms"},{"id":"D","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae, a catalase-positive gram-positive rod forming black colonies"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, a catalase-negative alpha-hemolytic gram-positive diplococcus"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Anaerobes such as Clostridioides, Clostridium, Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, and Actinomyces israelii are normal microbiota of the GI tract and are typically pathogenic elsewhere; aspiration pneumonia is the classic example. They are often foul smelling from short-chain fatty acids, produce gas in tissue, are difficult to culture, and lack catalase and/or superoxide dismutase. Aminoglycosides are ineffective against them because these drugs require oxygen to enter the bacterial cell. The concept to carry forward is that many bacterial infections are opportunism by resident flora when a barrier is breached.',
    '{"A":"Encapsulated organisms such as S pneumoniae, Klebsiella, and H influenzae are opsonized and cleared by the spleen; these anaerobes are not in that group and the mechanism does not match.","B":"Obligate intracellular organisms are Rickettsia, Chlamydia, and Coxiella (\"stay inside cells when it is Really Chilly and Cold\"); they are not oral anaerobes.","C":"The organisms here are commensals of the mouth and GI tract that cause disease only when they reach protected sites; aspiration gives them such access.","D":"Catalase positivity is a feature of organisms such as S aureus, Candia, Pseudomonas, Nocardia, and Aspergillus, and it protects against oxidative killing, not a mechanism of aspiration pathogenicity."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Normal flora and opportunistic infections',
    'A 41-year-old woman with chronic granulomatous disease has recurrent infections including liver abscesses and pneumonia. The microbiology laboratory reports growth of a catalase-positive organism on buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron.',
    'Which organism fits this pattern of catalase positivity and the described culture requirement?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Listeria monocytogenes"},{"id":"B","text":"Burkholderia cepacia"},{"id":"C","text":"Clostridium perfringens"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"id":"E","text":"Legionella pneumophila"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Legionella is catalase positive (as are Candida, Pseudomonas, Nocardia, Bordetella pertussis, Burkholderia cepacia, H pylori, Aspergillus, staphylococci, Serratia, Listeria, and E coli, mnemonic "Notoriously Big Bubbling HASSLE") and it grows on buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron. Patients with chronic granulomatous disease, who lack NADPH oxidase and cannot generate the oxidative burst, are particularly susceptible to catalase-positive organisms; mycobacterial and Legionella exposure are both described in that population. The methylotrophs here rely on cysteine, which is why the mnemonic hints at the Sistine chapel.',
    '{"A":"Listeria is catalase positive and a facultative intracellular organism, but it grows on routine media as a motile gram-positive rod and needs no charcoal-yeast-extract medium.","B":"Burkholderia cepacia is catalase positive, but its classic association is with respiratory colonization in cystic fibrosis and hospital outbreaks, not the cysteine- and iron-supplemented medium named.","C":"Clostridium perfringens is an anaerobe that lacks catalase and grows on anaerobic media; it causes gas gangrene via phospholipase-laden alpha toxin, which is a separate pathogenesis.","D":"S pneumoniae is catalase negative and would be identified as a lancet-shaped gram-positive diplococcus causing pneumonia, sinusitis, and meningitis, not grown on BCYE."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Paracoccidioides brasiliensis',
    'A 47-year-old man who immigrated from Brazil 8 years ago presents with chronic cough, weight loss, and painful oropharyngeal ulcers. Biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation containing large budding yeasts with multiple buds radiating from a central parent cell.',
    'Which of the following organisms best explains these findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},{"id":"B","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii"},{"id":"C","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"},{"id":"D","text":"Coccidioides immitis"},{"id":"E","text":"Paracoccidioides brasiliensis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Paracoccidioides brasiliensis is a dimorphic fungus endemic to Latin America. In tissue it shows budding yeasts with multiple buds arranged around a central parent cell in a ''captain''s wheel'' pattern, and the organisms are much larger than a red blood cell. The disease resembles blastomycosis but has a marked male predominance. A history of residence in or travel to Latin America is the key geographic clue, and itraconazole is an appropriate azole for localized disease.',
    '{"A":"Cryptococcus neoformans is a heavily encapsulated yeast with narrow-based budding and has no geographic restriction to Latin America; it typically causes meningitis in immunocompromised hosts.","B":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a yeastlike fungus that causes diffuse interstitial pneumonia in AIDS patients and appears as disc-shaped yeasts on methenamine silver stain.","C":"Histoplasma capsulatum shows small intracellular yeasts within macrophages and is endemic to the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys, not Latin America.","D":"Coccidioides immitis is the desert organism of the southwestern United States and forms spherules with endospores rather than captain''s wheel budding yeasts."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 31-year-old man returns from a 3-week trip to sub-Saharan Africa and presents with 4 days of fevers that spike irregularly and are associated with chills, headache, and vomiting. He took no chemoprophylaxis and did not sleep under a bed net. Examination shows a temperature of 39.8 C, scleral icterus, and splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show anemia, thrombocytopenia, elevated indirect bilirubin, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase.',
    'Which of the following morphologies on blood smear is the classic finding in the species most likely responsible for this patient''s severe presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Trophozoites with Schüffner stippling in enlarged RBCs"},{"id":"B","text":"Band-form trophozoites with a basket appearance"},{"id":"C","text":"Rosettes of 8-24 merozoites in mature schizonts"},{"id":"D","text":"Ring trophozoites in a headphone shape within normal-sized RBCs"},{"id":"E","text":"Crescent-shaped gametocytes visible on a thin smear"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Plasmodium falciparum causes irregular, often high fevers and is the species responsible for severe and life-threatening malaria, because it can invade RBCs of all ages and reach very high parasitemia. First Aid illustrates falciparum as a headphone-shaped ring trophozoite within the RBC, with crescent-shaped gametocytes later in the cycle. Its parasitized RBCs can occlude capillaries in the brain, kidneys, and lungs, producing cerebral malaria, renal failure, and hypoxemia.',
    '{"A":"Schüffner stippling with enlarged infected RBCs belongs to Plasmodium vivax and ovale, the tertian species with a 48-hour fever cycle and hypnozoites.","B":"Band-form trophozoites with a basket-like appearance are the classic description of Plasmodium malariae, the quartan species with a 72-hour fever cycle.","C":"Rosettes of 8-24 merozoites are mature schizonts of Plasmodium malariae, and such mature forms are not the expected finding with this severe clinical picture.","E":"Crescent-shaped gametocytes do belong to Plasmodium falciparum, but they are the gametocyte stage rather than the trophozoite ring that First Aid uses as the classic intraerythrocytic finding."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 26-year-old woman presents with 1 week of fevers every other day, headache, and malaise after returning from 2 months in rural Guatemala. She received no chemoprophylaxis. Examination shows a temperature of 39.1 C and a spleen tip palpable 2 cm below the costal margin. Laboratory studies show mild anemia and normal renal and hepatic function. Peripheral blood smear shows enlarged infected RBCs containing trophozoites with fine red granules in the cytoplasm, and no crescent-shaped forms are seen.',
    'Which of the following regimens is most appropriate for this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Intravenous artesunate alone"},{"id":"B","text":"Atovaquone plus azithromycin"},{"id":"C","text":"Mefloquine plus doxycycline"},{"id":"D","text":"Chloroquine alone"},{"id":"E","text":"Chloroquine plus primaquine"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'The combination of a 48-hour tertian fever cycle with enlarged infected RBCs and Schüffner stippling identifies Plasmodium vivax or Plasmodium ovale, the species that form a dormant hypnozoite in the liver. Chloroquine clears the blood-stage parasites, but only primaquine eliminates the hypnozoites and prevents relapses, so primaquine is added to chloroquine. Primaquine requires testing for G6PD deficiency because it causes oxidative hemolysis in deficient patients.',
    '{"A":"Intravenous artesunate is reserved for life-threatening malaria such as severe falciparum disease with impaired consciousness, and this patient has uncomplicated tertian malaria.","B":"Atovaquone plus azithromycin is the treatment for Babesia microti, the Ixodes tick-borne parasite with a Maltese cross, not for a hypnozoite-forming Plasmodium.","C":"Mefloquine plus doxycycline targets chloroquine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum, and neither agent eliminates hepatic hypnozoites.","D":"Chloroquine alone abolishes the current attack but leaves hepatic hypnozoites intact, so relapses occur after the drug level wanes."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 41-year-old engineer who worked for 3 months in western Thailand presents with daily fevers, cough, and confusion. Examination shows a temperature of 40.1 C and disorientation to place and time. Laboratory studies show parasitemia affecting 9% of RBCs, blood glucose of 45 mg/dL, creatinine of 3.4 mg/dL, and a high lactate level. He is admitted to the intensive care unit, and intravenous therapy is being selected.',
    'Which of the following findings is the most important reason that this patient requires intravenous artesunate rather than oral chloroquine?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Discovery that the patient is G6PD deficient"},{"id":"B","text":"A history of sulfa drug allergy"},{"id":"C","text":"A pregnancy at 9 weeks of gestation"},{"id":"D","text":"Hypoglycemia and acute kidney injury with cerebral involvement"},{"id":"E","text":"Realization that the infection is chloroquine-resistant"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'First Aid directs intravenous quinine or artesunate for life-threatening malaria, and artesunate is preferred because it is more effective and better tolerated. The features that make malaria life threatening include cerebral involvement, hypoglycemia, renal impairment, and high parasitemia, all of which this patient has. Knowing which features push a patient from oral to parenteral therapy is the high-yield distinction, and artesunate still necessitates checking for G6PD deficiency because of the hemolysis risk.',
    '{"A":"G6PD deficiency must be known before artesunate is given because these agents can cause oxidative hemolysis, but it is a drug safety consideration rather than the reason intravenous therapy is required.","B":"A sulfa allergy matters for sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine, an antifolate antimalarial, and is unrelated to the decision to use parenteral therapy.","C":"Pregnancy influences drug selection and some agents are preferred or avoided, but gestation alone does not make uncomplicated malaria life threatening.","E":"Chloroquine resistance is common in this region, but resistance status alone does not establish that the disease is life threatening."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 34-year-old woman is seen in a travel clinic before a 4-week trip to sub-Saharan Africa. She has a history of intolerance to several antibiotics, and she has limited access to G6PD testing. Her physician plans a chemoprophylactic agent that also treats rickettsial infections and covers chloroquine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum.',
    'Which of the following is the route of transmission for the pathogen this prophylaxis is intended to prevent?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Undercooked freshwater fish"},{"id":"B","text":"A tsetse fly bite"},{"id":"C","text":"A female Anopheles mosquito bite"},{"id":"D","text":"A female black fly bite"},{"id":"E","text":"Cysts in untreated drinking water"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'The prophylaxis described is doxycycline, which First Aid lists together with mefloquine and atovaquone-proguanil as the regimen for resistant Plasmodium falciparum. Malaria is transmitted by the bite of the female Anopheles mosquito. Infection of RBCs produces the cyclic fevers, anemia, and splenomegaly of malaria, and a peripheral blood smear allows identification of the infecting species.',
    '{"D":"A female black fly transmits Onchocerca volvulus, causing black skin nodules and river blindness, which is unrelated to antimalarial prophylaxis.","E":"Cysts in untreated drinking water transmit Giardia lamblia and Entamoeba histolytica, producing malabsorptive diarrhea or bloody dysentery and liver abscess.","A":"Undercooked freshwater fish transmits Diphyllobothrium latum larvae or Clonorchis sinensis, causing megaloblastic anemia or biliary disease rather than cyclical fevers.","B":"A tsetse fly transmits Trypanosoma brucei, producing African sleeping sickness with lymphadenopathy and somnolence that no antimalarial prevents."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 38-year-old man returned from Nigeria 3 weeks ago and reports fevers that spike every 2-3 days, defervesce, and then recur. Between the fever spikes he feels relatively well. A blood smear shows intraerythrocytic ring trophozoites at low parasitemia. His physician explains that repeated attacks can occur because the adaptive immune response clears one wave of parasites but is outpaced by the next.',
    'Which of the following mechanisms best explains this recurring pattern of parasitemia and fever?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Hypnozoites reactivate in the liver on a fixed monthly schedule"},{"id":"B","text":"Repeated ingestion of new parasite cysts produces cyclical liver release of trophozoites"},{"id":"C","text":"Sequestration of infected RBCs in capillaries acts as a progressively worsening reservoir of drug-resistant parasite"},{"id":"D","text":"Antigenic variation produces parasite surface proteins that escape antibody control, so fevers recur"},{"id":"E","text":"Drug-induced hemolysis accounts for the repeating febrile episodes during therapy"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Antigenic variation is the reason that immunity to malaria requires repeated exposure and why a person can suffer repeated attacks. The parasite expresses variant surface proteins that evade the antibodies that cleared the previous wave, so parasitemia rises again and the synchronous rupture of infected RBCs produces another fever spike. First Aid emphasizes this same mechanism for the recurring fever of Trypanosoma brucei, which is the classic comparison point.',
    '{"B":"Repeated ingestion of new cysts is a reinfection mechanism, and it does not explain recurring fevers within a single episode in which no new exposure occurred.","C":"Cytoadherence and rosetting of infected RBCs cause microvascular obstruction and severe disease, but they do not explain the recurrent wave of parasitemia after each antibody response.","E":"Drug-induced hemolysis is a complication of primaquine and other oxidant drugs in G6PD deficiency and would not produce cyclical fever with renewed parasitemia.","A":"Hypnozoite reactivation is a relapsing mechanism specific to Plasmodium vivax and ovale, and it does not follow a fixed monthly schedule."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Plasmodium and malaria',
    'A 19-year-old student is seen after returning from a summer in an endemic region of Central America. She reports a distinct pattern of fever that returns every other day. On physical examination she has splenomegaly, and her blood smear shows increased RBC size with fine granules throughout the cytoplasm of infected cells, findings consistent with a tertian species.',
    'Which of the following best characterizes the fever pattern and lifecycle feature of the species described by these smear findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Spikes every 48 hours with a dormant hypnozoite stage in the liver"},{"id":"B","text":"Spikes every 24 hours with multiple rings per RBC"},{"id":"C","text":"Continuous fever with Maltese cross forms and no fever-free interval"},{"id":"D","text":"Spikes every 48 hours with band-form trophozoites and no liver stage"},{"id":"E","text":"Spikes every 72 hours with crescent-shaped gametocytes"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale cause tertian malaria, with fever cycles every 48 hours and a dormant hypnozoite form in the liver. The hypnozoite is why relapses can occur months after the initial infection seems to resolve, and it is the reason primaquine is added to chloroquine to prevent relapse. Vivax and ovale also show Schüffner stippling in enlarged infected RBCs.',
    '{"B":"A 24-hour fever cycle with multiple rings per RBC is not the First Aid description for vivax or ovale, and multiply infected cells are more characteristic of falciparum.","C":"Continuous fever with Maltese cross forms describes Babesia microti, a tick-borne parasite rather than a mosquito-borne Plasmodium species.","D":"Band-form trophozoites belong to Plasmodium malariae, which has a quartan 72-hour cycle, so this option pairs the wrong periodicity with the wrong morphology.","E":"A 72-hour quartan cycle with crescent-shaped gametocytes mixes two different species, since quartan fever belongs to Plasmodium malariae and crescents belong to Plasmodium falciparum."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Pneumocystis jirovecii',
    'A 31-year-old man with untreated HIV infection presents with three weeks of progressive exertional dyspnea, dry cough, and fever. His oxygen saturation falls from 96% to 88% with ambulation, and his CD4+ count is 90 cells/mm3. Chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates, and high-resolution CT shows patchy ground-glass opacities.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii"},{"id":"B","text":"Coccidioides immitis"},{"id":"C","text":"Aspergillus fumigatus"},{"id":"D","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},{"id":"E","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Pneumocystis jirovecii is a yeastlike fungus that causes Pneumocystis pneumonia, a diffuse interstitial pneumonia seen in immunosuppression, particularly AIDS with a low CD4+ count. It classically produces diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities and pneumatoceles on chest imaging, with progressive dyspnea on exertion and marked exertional hypoxemia. It is diagnosed by bronchoalveolar lavage or lung biopsy showing disc-shaped yeasts with methenamine silver staining or fluorescent antibody. Treatment and prophylaxis use TMP-SMX.',
    '{"B":"Coccidioides causes a focal pneumonitis or cavity in patients with dust exposure in the southwestern United States and shows spherules with endospores on biopsy.","C":"Aspergillus produces nodular or cavitary lesions in neutropenic patients and shows septate hyphae with acute-angle branching rather than diffuse ground-glass disease.","D":"Cryptococcus mainly causes meningitis and pneumonia in immunocompromised patients, and its hallmark is a thick capsule seen with India ink or mucicarmine.","E":"Histoplasma typically follows bird or bat droppings exposure in river valleys and shows macrophages stuffed with small yeasts and portal-of-entry pulmonary infiltrates."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Pneumocystis jirovecii',
    'A 45-year-old man with HIV infection has a CD4+ count of 185 cells/mm3 and is asymptomatic. He takes no antiretroviral therapy and has had no opportunistic infections. His physician wants to start prophylaxis against Pneumocystis pneumonia at the correct threshold.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate prophylaxis for this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"B","text":"Aerosolized amphotericin B"},{"id":"C","text":"Azithromycin"},{"id":"D","text":"Isoniazid"},{"id":"E","text":"Fluconazole"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Prophylaxis against Pneumocystis pneumonia should begin when the CD4+ cell count falls below 200 cells/mm3 in people living with HIV, and at a CD4+ count below 100 cells/mm3 TMP-SMX also covers toxoplasmosis. TMP-SMX is the preferred agent, with pentamidine, dapsone, and atovaquone as alternatives for treatment and prophylaxis. At 185 cells/mm3 this patient has crossed the treatment threshold and needs prophylaxis now. TMP-SMX is also the first-line treatment for active PCP.',
    '{"B":"Inhaled or aerosolized pentamidine is an alternative agent, but amphotericin B is used for serious systemic mycoses such as mucormycosis and cryptococcal meningitis, not for PCP prophylaxis.","C":"Azithromycin is a macrolide used for atypical respiratory pathogens and Mycobacterium avium complex prophylaxis, not for Pneumocystis.","D":"Isoniazid is used to treat and prevent reactivation of latent tuberculosis infection, which is a separate consideration in HIV patients.","E":"Fluconazole is an azole used for Candida infections and chronic suppressive therapy for cryptococcal meningitis, not for Pneumocystis prophylaxis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Poliovirus and enteroviruses',
    'A 4-year-old boy in an area with incomplete vaccination coverage develops fever and headache, followed by asymmetric weakness of the left leg with diminished reflexes and no sensory loss. CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose. Stool culture grows an enterovirus.',
    'Which of the following best describes the prevention of this disease?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Postexposure rabies immunoglobulin combined with killed vaccine"},{"id":"B","text":"Neuraminidase inhibitors started within 48 hours of symptom onset"},{"id":"C","text":"Inactivated poliovirus vaccine given parenterally (Salk) or live attenuated oral vaccine (Sabin)"},{"id":"D","text":"Palivizumab given monthly during respiratory season"},{"id":"E","text":"A live attenuated vaccine containing temperature-sensitive mutants given intranasally"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Poliovirus is a naked positive-sense ssRNA picornavirus and an enterovirus, prevented by the inactivated parenteral Salk vaccine (IPV) or the live attenuated oral Sabin vaccine (OPV). Its characteristic disease is asymmetric flaccid paralysis with fever and meningeal signs from anterior horn cell destruction. Other enteroviruses such as echovirus and coxsackievirus also cause aseptic meningitis.',
    '{"A":"Rabies postexposure prophylaxis with wound cleaning, killed vaccine, and immunoglobulin is used for rabies exposure, not enterovirus infection.","B":"Neuraminidase inhibitors such as oseltamivir treat influenza by blocking virion release and are inactive against picornaviruses.","D":"Palivizumab is a monoclonal antibody against the RSV fusion protein for premature infants and has no role in poliovirus prevention.","E":"The intranasal temperature-sensitive live vaccine is the live attenuated influenza vaccine, not a poliovirus vaccine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Poliovirus and enteroviruses',
    'A 6-year-old boy presents with fever, sore throat, and several small painful vesicles with erythematous halos on the posterior pharynx and tonsillar pillars. Two days later, a few similar vesicles appear on his palms and soles. A classmate had a similar illness last week.',
    'Which of the following viruses is the most likely cause?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Parainfluenza virus, causing croup with a barking cough"},{"id":"B","text":"Rhinovirus, causing the common cold"},{"id":"C","text":"Coxsackievirus, a picornavirus causing herpangina and hand, foot, and mouth disease"},{"id":"D","text":"Herpes simplex virus-1, causing gingivostomatitis"},{"id":"E","text":"Epstein-Barr virus, causing exudative pharyngitis with posterior cervical adenopathy"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Coxsackievirus is a naked positive-sense ssRNA picornavirus that causes herpangina with mouth blisters and fever, hand, foot, and mouth disease, aseptic meningitis, myocarditis, and pericarditis. The distribution of lesions on the posterior pharynx and tonsillar pillars plus the subsequent palmar and plantar vesicles is the classic hand, foot, and mouth pattern. Picornaviruses include poliovirus, echovirus, rhinovirus, coxsackievirus, and hepatitis A, remembered as PERCH on a peak.',
    '{"A":"Parainfluenza virus causes croup with a seal-like barking cough and subglottic narrowing, not mucosal vesicles.","B":"Rhinovirus causes rhinorrhea and nasal congestion of the common cold without vesicular oral or acral lesions.","D":"HSV-1 gingivostomatitis produces vesicles and ulcers on the anterior oral mucosa and lips, and it does not cause acral palmar and plantar lesions.","E":"EBV mononucleosis produces exudative pharyngitis with posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly, not vesicular oral and acral lesions."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa',
    'A 30-year-old man with 60% total body surface area burns develops fever 5 days after admission. Examination shows a black necrotic ulceration with a violaceous rim in unburned skin and several similar lesions on the trunk. Blood cultures grow a non-lactose-fermenting, oxidase-positive gram-negative rod with a grapelike odor.',
    'Which of the following bacterial products most directly accounts for the necrotic cutaneous lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Exotoxin A, which inactivates elongation factor 2"},{"id":"B","text":"Pertussis toxin, which disables the inhibitory Gi protein"},{"id":"C","text":"Shiga toxin, which damages endothelial cells and causes microthrombi"},{"id":"D","text":"Alpha-toxin lecithinase, which causes myonecrosis and hemolysis"},{"id":"E","text":"Tracheal cytotoxin, which destroys ciliated respiratory epithelium"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is ecthyma gangrenosum from Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia, a rapidly progressive necrotic cutaneous lesion typically seen in immunocompromised patients such as burn victims. Exotoxin A is one of the PEEP virulence factors and inactivates elongation factor 2, injuring tissue in the setting of invasive disease. Pseudomonas is also known for its mucoid polysaccharide capsule, endotoxin, and green pigment.',
    '{"B":"Pertussis toxin is produced by Bordetella pertussis and disables Gi, raising cAMP; it causes whooping cough rather than necrotic skin lesions.","C":"Shiga toxin is produced by Shigella dysenteriae and enterohemorrhagic E coli and causes hemolytic-uremic syndrome.","D":"Lecithinase alpha-toxin is from Clostridium perfringens and causes gas gangrene and hemolysis, not the lesions of ecthyma gangrenosum.","E":"Tracheal cytotoxin is a Bordetella pertussis virulence factor that destroys respiratory ciliated epithelium."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa',
    'A 24-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis has had repeated hospitalizations for pneumonia. Sputum cultures repeatedly grow a mucoid, oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod that is resistant to many antibiotics. Her physician is concerned about chronic airway infection driven by biofilm formation.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors of this organism contributes most to chronic pneumonic infection in cystic fibrosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"An IgA protease that cleaves mucosal antibodies"},{"id":"B","text":"A mucoid polysaccharide capsule that supports biofilm formation"},{"id":"C","text":"Protein A, which binds the Fc portion of IgG"},{"id":"D","text":"An M protein that inhibits phagocytosis and resembles host myosin"},{"id":"E","text":"A poly-D-glutamate polypeptide capsule"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is aerobic, motile, catalase positive, oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenting, and frequently found in water; it has a grapelike odor. Its mucoid polysaccharide capsule may contribute to chronic pneumonia in cystic fibrosis through biofilm formation, and this is why mucoid strains are so difficult to eradicate. The source lists cystic fibrosis among the PSEUDOMONAS associations.',
    '{"A":"IgA protease is produced by Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria species, and Streptococcus pneumoniae, not by Pseudomonas.","C":"Protein A is a Staphylococcus aureus virulence factor that binds Fc-IgG and inhibits complement activation and phagocytosis.","D":"M protein is a Streptococcus pyogenes factor that inhibits phagocytosis and is structurally similar to myosin, contributing to rheumatic fever.","E":"A poly-D-glutamate polypeptide capsule belongs to Bacillus anthracis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa',
    'A 71-year-old man in the intensive care unit with a urinary catheter and recent broad-spectrum antibiotics develops fever and hypotension. Blood and urine cultures grow an aerobic, oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod. The infectious disease consultant recommends combination therapy with an antipseudomonal beta-lactam and notes that aminoglycoside monotherapy should be avoided.',
    'Which of the following best explains why aminoglycoside monotherapy is avoided in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Aminoglycosides are inactivated by the mucoid capsule of Pseudomonas"},{"id":"B","text":"Aminoglycosides bind 50S ribosomes and are bacteriostatic against Pseudomonas"},{"id":"C","text":"Aminoglycosides induce chromosomal AmpC beta-lactamase expression"},{"id":"D","text":"Aminoglycosides perform poorly in acidic environments despite antipseudomonal activity"},{"id":"E","text":"Aminoglycosides lack activity against aerobic gram-negative rods entirely"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The source notes that despite antipseudomonal activity, aminoglycoside monotherapy is avoided because of poor performance in acidic environments. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is noted to have increased virulence in acidic environments, so monotherapy risks failure at infected sites. Appropriate agents include antipseudomonal penicillins with beta-lactamase inhibitors, third- and fourth-generation cephalosporins, monobactams, fluoroquinolones, and carbapenems.',
    '{"A":"The mucoid capsule contributes to biofilm formation, but it is not the reason the source gives for avoiding aminoglycoside monotherapy.","B":"Aminoglycosides bind the 30S ribosomal subunit and are bactericidal, not 50S-binding bacteriostatic agents.","C":"The reason given is poor activity in acidic environments, not induction of AmpC beta-lactamase.","E":"Aminoglycosides do have activity against aerobic gram-negative rods, which are precisely the organisms they cover; the issue is the site-related poor performance."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rabies',
    'A 34-year-old hiker is bitten on the hand by a bat while camping. The bite is small and he washes it with soap and water. He has never received rabies vaccine. He presents to the emergency department 6 hours later and is otherwise asymptomatic.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rabies immunoglobulin alone, because vaccine cannot be given after exposure"},{"id":"B","text":"Observation only, because the long incubation period allows treatment once symptoms begin"},{"id":"C","text":"Wound cleaning plus killed rabies vaccine and rabies immunoglobulin, an example of passive-active immunity"},{"id":"D","text":"Wound cleaning alone, because bat bites rarely transmit rabies"},{"id":"E","text":"Live attenuated rabies vaccine alone, without immunoglobulin"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Postexposure prophylaxis for rabies is wound cleaning plus immunization with killed vaccine and rabies immunoglobulin, which is an example of passive-active immunity. Rabies virus binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and travels to the CNS by retrograde axonal transport via dynein motors, with an incubation period of weeks to months, so prophylaxis must be given before symptoms appear. In the United States, infection is more commonly from bat, raccoon, and skunk bites than from dog bites.',
    '{"A":"Vaccination is appropriate after exposure and must be combined with immunoglobulin in previously unvaccinated people to provide passive antibodies while active immunity develops.","B":"Once symptoms begin, rabies is almost universally fatal, so prophylaxis must be given during the incubation period rather than withheld.","D":"Bat exposure is a recognized rabies risk in the United States, and wound cleaning alone is inadequate after a bite from a potentially rabid animal.","E":"Rabies postexposure prophylaxis uses killed vaccine; a live attenuated rabies vaccine is not appropriate, and immunoglobulin is also indicated for previously unvaccinated patients."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rabies',
    'A 47-year-old man from a rural area dies of an illness characterized by agitation, photophobia, hydrophobia, hypersalivation, and progressive paralysis leading to coma. He had been bitten by a stray dog 3 months earlier and never sought care. Autopsy examines the cerebellum and hippocampus.',
    'Which of the following findings is most characteristic at autopsy?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Negri bodies, cytoplasmic inclusions in Purkinje cells and hippocampal neurons"},{"id":"B","text":"Owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions in mononuclear cells"},{"id":"C","text":"Cowdry A intranuclear inclusions with multinucleated giant cells"},{"id":"D","text":"Councilman bodies in the liver"},{"id":"E","text":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells in paracortical lymph node regions"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Rabies virus is a bullet-shaped rhabdovirus with a negative-sense ssRNA genome. Negri bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions commonly found in Purkinje cells of the cerebellum and in hippocampal neurons. The progression of disease is fever and malaise, then agitation, photophobia, hydrophobia, and hypersalivation, then paralysis, coma, and death.',
    '{"B":"Owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions are typical of cytomegalovirus infection, not rabies.","C":"Cowdry A inclusions with multinucleated giant cells are seen on Tzanck smears in HSV-1, HSV-2, and VZV infection.","D":"Councilman bodies are eosinophilic apoptotic globules seen in the liver in hepatitis A and yellow fever.","E":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells are fused lymphocytes in measles lymphadenitis, a different paramyxovirus infection."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Respiratory syncytial virus and parainfluenza',
    'A 5-month-old born at 29 weeks'' gestation is admitted in winter with 2 days of nasal congestion followed by rapid breathing, retractions, and wheezing. Chest examination shows diffuse fine crackles and expiratory wheeze. Nasal aspirate is positive for respiratory syncytial virus by PCR.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate prophylaxis for high-risk infants against this pathogen?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Acyclovir, which inhibits herpesvirus DNA polymerase"},{"id":"B","text":"Palivizumab, a monoclonal antibody against the viral F protein"},{"id":"C","text":"Oseltamivir, a neuraminidase inhibitor"},{"id":"D","text":"Rotavirus vaccine, which cross-protects against pneumoviruses"},{"id":"E","text":"Live attenuated influenza vaccine given intranasally"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Palivizumab is a monoclonal antibody against the F (fusion) protein of respiratory syncytial virus and prevents pneumonia caused by RSV infection in premature infants. The F protein causes respiratory epithelial cells to fuse and form multinucleated cells, which is the cytopathic hallmark of the paramyxoviruses. Remember palivizumab for paramyxovirus prophylaxis in preemies.',
    '{"A":"Acyclovir acts on herpesvirus DNA polymerase and requires viral thymidine kinase; RSV is an RNA virus and is unaffected.","C":"Oseltamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor for influenza and has no activity against RSV, which is a pneumovirus/paramyxovirus.","D":"Rotavirus vaccine prevents infantile gastroenteritis from a dsRNA reovirus and provides no cross-protection against respiratory paramyxoviruses.","E":"The live attenuated intranasal influenza vaccine contains a temperature-sensitive mutant for influenza, not RSV, and is not used for RSV prevention."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Respiratory syncytial virus and parainfluenza',
    'A 2-year-old boy presents with 2 days of a harsh, seal-like barking cough, hoarse voice, and inspiratory stridor that worsen at night. He has a low-grade fever and rhinorrhea. A lateral neck radiograph shows narrowing of the upper trachea and subglottis with a steeple sign.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the causative agent and its transmission?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Coxsackievirus, which causes herpangina with mouth blisters"},{"id":"B","text":"Measles virus, which causes laryngotracheobronchitis with Koplik spots"},{"id":"C","text":"Parainfluenza virus, a paramyxovirus spread by respiratory droplets and contact"},{"id":"D","text":"Respiratory syncytial virus, which causes bronchiolitis in infants under 6 months"},{"id":"E","text":"Influenza virus, which produces a steeple sign in children with croup"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Acute laryngotracheobronchitis, or croup, is caused by parainfluenza viruses, which are enveloped negative-sense ssRNA paramyxoviruses spread by respiratory droplets and contact. The illness produces a seal-like barking cough and inspiratory stridor, and narrowing of the upper trachea and subglottis gives the characteristic steeple sign on x-ray. Parainfluenza virus also carries hemagglutinin and neuraminidase surface antigens.',
    '{"A":"Coxsackievirus causes herpangina with oral blisters and hand, foot, and mouth disease, not subglottic narrowing with stridor.","B":"Measles produces Koplik spots with a descending maculopapular rash; it is not the classic cause of croup.","D":"RSV causes bronchiolitis in babies with wheezing and crackles, and the steeple sign on x-ray is the radiologic signature of croup rather than bronchiolitis.","E":"Influenza causes abrupt fever, myalgias, and cough, and croup is classically attributed to parainfluenza rather than influenza."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rhinovirus and coronaviruses',
    'A 25-year-old woman presents in September with 3 days of clear rhinorrhea, sneezing, nasal congestion, and mild sore throat without fever. Her coworker recovered from an identical illness the previous week. There are more than 100 serologic types of the responsible virus.',
    'Which of the following explains why this virus causes only upper respiratory illness rather than infecting the GI tract like other viruses in its family?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"It is acid labile and is destroyed by stomach acid, unlike the other picornaviruses"},{"id":"B","text":"It is an enveloped virus that is destroyed by gastric detergent-like bile salts"},{"id":"C","text":"It requires the P antigen on RBCs to enter cells"},{"id":"D","text":"It replicates only in the nucleus, which is absent in intestinal cells"},{"id":"E","text":"It is a dsRNA virus that is inactivated by intestinal proteases"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Rhinovirus is a picornavirus: a nonenveloped positive-sense ssRNA virus with more than 100 serologic types that uses ICAM-1 as its receptor, remembered as I came to see the rhino. It is acid labile and destroyed by stomach acid, so unlike other picornaviruses such as poliovirus, echovirus, and coxsackievirus, it does not infect the GI tract. It produces the common cold with rhinorrhea, hence rhino has a runny nose.',
    '{"B":"Rhinovirus is nonenveloped, so a lipid bilayer is not present to be destroyed by bile salts; acid lability is the mechanism.","C":"The P antigen on RBCs is the parvovirus B19 receptor, not the rhinovirus receptor, which is ICAM-1.","D":"Rhinoviruses, like other RNA viruses, replicate in the cytoplasm; nuclear replication is the exception of influenza and retroviruses.","E":"Picornaviruses have single-stranded positive-sense RNA genomes, not dsRNA, which is the property of reoviruses."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rhinovirus and coronaviruses',
    'A 57-year-old man develops fever, myalgias, sore throat, and loss of smell and taste, followed by progressive dyspnea and hypoxemia. Chest imaging shows bilateral peripheral ground-glass opacities. He later develops a pulmonary embolism. Nasopharyngeal PCR is positive for SARS-CoV-2.',
    'Which of the following best describes the entry mechanism of this virus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Binding to the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction"},{"id":"B","text":"Spike protein binding to the ACE2 receptor, highly expressed in lungs, intestines, heart, and kidneys"},{"id":"C","text":"gp120 binding to CD4 and a chemokine coreceptor on T cells"},{"id":"D","text":"Hemagglutinin binding to sialic acid on respiratory epithelial cells"},{"id":"E","text":"Attachment to ICAM-1 on nasal epithelial cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'SARS-CoV-2 is a positive-sense ssRNA coronavirus that enters host cells when its spike protein attaches to the ACE2 receptor, which is highly expressed in the lungs, intestines, heart, and kidneys. Clinical features include fever, myalgia, anosmia, and dysgeusia, and severe cases develop pneumonia, hypercoagulability with DVT, PE, or stroke, and organ failure. mRNA and viral vector vaccines work by presenting the spike protein to induce anti-spike antibodies.',
    '{"A":"Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors mediate rabies virus binding at the neuromuscular junction before retrograde axonal transport.","C":"This describes HIV entry, with gp120 docking to CD4 and CCR5 or CXCR4; HIV is a retrovirus, not a coronavirus.","D":"Hemagglutinin binding to sialic acid is how influenza virus enters cells, using a different receptor entirely.","E":"ICAM-1 is the rhinovirus receptor, remembered as I came to see the rhino, and rhinovirus causes the common cold rather than COVID-19."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Rickettsia and Ehrlichia',
    'A 9-year-old boy from North Carolina presents in June with 4 days of high fever, severe headache, and myalgias. His mother reports a tick bite 1 week ago. Examination shows a blanching erythematous rash on the wrists and ankles that has begun to spread toward the trunk, and several petechiae on the palms.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate empiric treatment while awaiting confirmatory serology?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"id":"B","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"},{"id":"C","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"D","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"id":"E","text":"Azithromycin"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is Rocky Mountain spotted fever due to Rickettsia rickettsii, transmitted by the Dermacentor tick and most common in the South Atlantic states including North Carolina. The classic triad is headache, fever, and rash from vasculitis, with the rash starting at the wrists and ankles and spreading to the trunk, palms, and soles. Rickettsial diseases are treated with doxycycline.',
    '{"A":"Ceftriaxone is a cephalosporin used for meningitis and gonorrhea, not for rickettsial infection.","B":"TMP-SMX is used for Nocardia and as an alternative for pertussis in macrolide allergy, and sulfonamides may worsen rickettsial disease.","D":"Amoxicillin is a cell-wall active beta-lactam and does not cover Rickettsia, which is an obligate intracellular organism.","E":"Azithromycin is used for atypical organisms such as Mycoplasma and Chlamydia, not as first-line therapy for rickettsial disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Rickettsia and Ehrlichia',
    'A 63-year-old man from a rural area presents with fever and headache after a tick bite. Peripheral blood smear shows inclusion bodies within monocytes. A separate patient with a similar febrile illness after a tick bite has inclusions within granulocytes on smear.',
    'Which of the following correctly matches the organisms to these findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects monocytes, whereas Anaplasma phagocytophilum infects granulocytes"},{"id":"B","text":"Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects granulocytes, whereas Anaplasma phagocytophilum infects monocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"Both organisms infect erythrocytes and produce hemolytic anemia"},{"id":"D","text":"Both organisms infect lymphocytes, and the inclusions are viral nucleocapsids"},{"id":"E","text":"Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects monocytes, whereas Anaplasma forms inclusions in platelets only"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'The mnemonic MEGA distinguishes the two: monocytes indicate ehrlichiosis, and granulocytes indicate anaplasmosis. Ehrlichia chaffeensis produces morulae, which are mulberrylike inclusions, in monocytes, while Anaplasma phagocytophilum produces morulae in granulocytes. Both are tick-borne and treated with doxycycline.',
    '{"B":"This reverses the correct pairing; the source explicitly assigns monocytes to Ehrlichia and granulocytes to Anaplasma.","C":"Neither organism infects erythrocytes; the intracellular targets are monocytes and granulocytes respectively.","D":"The morulae are bacterial microcolonies within leukocytes, not viral inclusions in lymphocytes.","E":"Anaplasma infects granulocytes, including neutrophils, not platelets."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Rickettsia and Ehrlichia',
    'A 40-year-old veterinarian presents with 2 weeks of fever, severe headache, dry cough, and right upper quadrant discomfort. She reports assisting with calving on a dairy farm. Chest radiograph shows a patchy infiltrate and liver enzymes are elevated. Blood cultures are negative, but echocardiography reveals a vegetation, and serology for a fastidious gram-negative coccobacillus is positive.',
    'Which of the following best describes the organism responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rickettsia prowazekii, transmitted person to person by the human body louse"},{"id":"B","text":"Ehrlichia chaffeensis, transmitted by the Lone Star tick, infecting monocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"Brucella abortus, ingested in unpasteurized dairy products from cattle"},{"id":"D","text":"Coxiella burnetii, inhaled in aerosols from cattle or sheep amniotic fluid, with no arthropod vector"},{"id":"E","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii, transmitted by the Dermacentor tick, causing a palm-and-sole rash"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Q fever is caused by Coxiella burnetii, which has no arthropod vector and is inhaled as aerosols from cattle or sheep amniotic fluid, classically in farmers and veterinarians assisting with parturition. It presents with headache, cough, flulike symptoms, pneumonia, and hepatitis, and is a common cause of culture-negative endocarditis. Its mnemonic emphasizes a Quite Complicated bug with no rash and no vector.',
    '{"A":"Epidemic typhus is louse-borne and human-to-human, producing a centrally starting rash that spares the palms and soles.","B":"Ehrlichiosis is tick-borne, does not have a cattle amniotic fluid reservoir, and is not a classic cause of endocarditis.","C":"Brucellosis is acquired mainly from unpasteurized dairy and causes undulant fever, but it is not the classic cause of culture-negative endocarditis described here.","E":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever is tick-borne and produces a characteristic palm-and-sole vasculitic rash, which is absent here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'RNA viruses overview and RNA virus mnemonics',
    'A student is memorizing which RNA viruses carry positive-sense genomes equivalent to mRNA versus negative-sense genomes that must be transcribed first. She is told to remember the phrase about drinking flavored Corona and eating hippie California pickles.',
    'Which of the following groups of viruses all have positive-sense ssRNA genomes?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Poxvirus, hepadnavirus, and herpesvirus"},{"id":"B","text":"Retrovirus, togavirus, flavivirus, coronavirus, hepevirus, calicivirus, picornavirus"},{"id":"C","text":"Arenavirus, bunyavirus, paramyxovirus, orthomyxovirus, filovirus, rhabdovirus"},{"id":"D","text":"Orthomyxovirus, paramyxovirus, and reovirus"},{"id":"E","text":"Reovirus, rotavirus, and rhabdovirus"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Positive-sense ssRNA, which behaves like mRNA and can be translated directly, includes the retro-, toga-, flavi-, corona-, hepe-, calici-, and picornaviruses; the mnemonic is drinking flavored Corona and eating hippie California pickles at a retro toga party. Negative-sense viruses are the arena-, bunya-, paramyxo-, orthomyxo-, filo-, and rhabdoviruses, which must bring their own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.',
    '{"A":"These are DNA viruses; poxvirus and herpesvirus are dsDNA and hepadnavirus is partially dsDNA.","C":"This is exactly the negative-sense list; these virions package RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to make a positive-sense message first.","D":"Orthomyxovirus (influenza) and paramyxovirus are negative-sense; reovirus is the one RNA family with dsRNA.","E":"Reovirus has a dsRNA genome and rhabdovirus (rabies) is negative-sense ssRNA; neither is positive-sense."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'RNA viruses overview and RNA virus mnemonics',
    'A first-year student is asked why influenza virus and retrovirus are singled out when learning that RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm. A classmate answers with the phrase retro flu is outta cyt (sight).',
    'Which of the following best explains the exceptions to cytoplasmic replication among RNA viruses?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Both replicate in the cytoplasm but assemble their virions in the nucleus before budding at the plasma membrane"},{"id":"B","text":"Influenza virus replicates in the nucleus because it requires host splicing machinery, and retrovirus integrates proviral DNA into the host genome in the nucleus"},{"id":"C","text":"Both replicate exclusively in mitochondria to evade host immunity"},{"id":"D","text":"Both are DNA viruses misclassified as RNA viruses"},{"id":"E","text":"Both are naked viruses whose capsids must be disassembled in the nucleus"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'All RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm except the retrovirus and influenza virus. Retroviruses use reverse transcriptase to make dsDNA, which integrates into the host genome, a nuclear event. Influenza virus, an orthomyxovirus, transcribes and replicates its genome in the nucleus, using host nuclear functions. The mnemonic is Retro flu is outta cyt (sight).',
    '{"A":"Influenza mRNAs are made in the nucleus and retroviral provirus is nuclear DNA; the nuclear step is genome transcription and integration, not merely assembly.","C":"Neither virus replicates in mitochondria; influenza uses the host nucleus and retroviruses the host chromosomal DNA.","D":"Influenza is an enveloped negative-sense ssRNA orthomyxovirus and HIV is a positive-sense ssRNA retrovirus with reverse transcriptase; neither is a DNA virus.","E":"Influenza is enveloped with a helical nucleocapsid and retrovirus is enveloped with an icosahedral core; capsid disassembly location is not the reason for nuclear replication."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'RNA viruses overview and RNA virus mnemonics',
    'During a review of viral genome architecture, a student is given four RNA virus families and asked to identify which have segmented genomes, remembering the mnemonic BOAR. She is also told that all RNA viruses are single-stranded except one family.',
    'Which of the following pairings of RNA virus families with their distinguishing genome features is correct?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Orthomyxovirus has an unsegmented positive-sense genome"},{"id":"B","text":"Paramyxovirus has a segmented dsRNA genome"},{"id":"C","text":"Picornavirus has a fragmented negative-sense genome packaged as multiple segments"},{"id":"D","text":"Reoviridae is the only dsRNA family, and the segmented families are bunya-, orthomyxo-, arena-, and reoviruses"},{"id":"E","text":"Flaviviridae has a dsRNA genome and a circular, segmented arrangement"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'All RNA viruses have ssRNA genomes except Reoviridae, which has dsRNA, and reovirus is also the rotavirus family that causes infantile gastroenteritis. Segmented genomes occur in the BOAR families: bunya-, orthomyxo-, arena-, and reoviruses. Segmentation matters because it permits reassortment, the mechanism of antigenic shift in influenza.',
    '{"A":"Orthomyxovirus (influenza) is negative-sense and multisegmented, which is precisely why it can reassort and shift.","B":"Paramyxoviruses such as measles, mumps, RSV, and parainfluenza are negative-sense and unsegmented ssRNA viruses.","C":"Picornaviruses have a single linear positive-sense ssRNA genome translated into one large polyprotein cleaved by viral proteases.","E":"Flaviviruses such as hepatitis C, yellow fever, dengue, West Nile, and Zika have linear positive-sense ssRNA genomes and are not segmented."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rotavirus and Norovirus',
    'A 14-month-old girl in day care presents in January with 3 days of watery diarrhea, vomiting, and low-grade fever. She has had seven stools per day and is mildly dehydrated. Stool antigen testing is positive for rotavirus. She has no history of intussusception.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative virus and the recommended prevention?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A segmented dsRNA reovirus, preventable by routine vaccination of all infants except those with a history of intussusception or SCID"},{"id":"B","text":"A naked positive-sense ssRNA calicivirus, preventable by a vaccine given at 2 months"},{"id":"C","text":"An enveloped negative-sense ssRNA paramyxovirus, preventable with palivizumab"},{"id":"D","text":"A naked linear ssDNA virus, preventable by hand hygiene alone because no vaccine exists"},{"id":"E","text":"A segmented negative-sense ssRNA orthomyxovirus, preventable by an annual seasonal vaccine"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Rotavirus is a naked segmented dsRNA reovirus and the most important global cause of infantile gastroenteritis, with a winter peak and outbreaks in day care centers and kindergartens. Villous destruction with atrophy leads to decreased absorption of sodium and loss of potassium. The CDC recommends routine vaccination of all infants except those with a history of intussusception, a rare adverse effect of rotavirus vaccination, or SCID.',
    '{"B":"Norovirus is the calicivirus that causes viral gastroenteritis in outbreaks of older children and adults, and no vaccine is routinely given in infancy.","C":"Palivizumab prophylaxis targets RSV pneumonia in premature infants, not gastroenteritis.","D":"Rotavirus is a dsRNA virus, not a ssDNA virus, and an effective vaccine is part of the routine infant schedule.","E":"Orthomyxovirus describes influenza, and the annual vaccine prevents respiratory rather than enteric disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Rotavirus and Norovirus',
    'Within 36 hours, 22 of 60 passengers on a cruise ship develop explosive watery diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal cramps lasting about 2 days. Stool studies are negative for bacteria and parasites. The ship''s water supply and a shared buffet are suspected sources.',
    'Which of the following best identifies the most likely pathogen?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Poliovirus, an enterovirus that causes aseptic meningitis and flaccid paralysis"},{"id":"B","text":"Norovirus, a naked positive-sense ssRNA calicivirus spread by contaminated food and water"},{"id":"C","text":"Rotavirus, a segmented dsRNA reovirus that primarily affects infants in day care"},{"id":"D","text":"Hepatitis A virus, a naked positive-sense ssRNA picornavirus causing jaundice"},{"id":"E","text":"Coxsackievirus, a picornavirus causing herpangina and hand, foot, and mouth disease"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Norovirus is a naked positive-sense ssRNA calicivirus and the leading cause of epidemic viral gastroenteritis in outbreaks among older children and adults, especially in closed settings such as cruise ships, and it spreads by contaminated food and water. Because it is nonenveloped, it survives stomach acid and environmental exposure. The short incubation and rapid attack rate with negative bacterial studies fit this diagnosis.',
    '{"A":"Poliovirus causes aseptic meningitis and asymmetric flaccid paralysis, and it is now rare because of vaccination.","C":"Rotavirus is the most important cause of infantile gastroenteritis and peaks in winter among young children, not in adult cruise-ship outbreaks.","D":"Hepatitis A is spread fecal-orally through shellfish and contaminated water but produces jaundice and hepatitis, not explosive watery diarrhea.","E":"Coxsackievirus causes herpangina with mouth blisters and hand, foot, and mouth disease, not a foodborne diarrheal outbreak."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Salmonella',
    'A 31-year-old man returns from a 3-week trip to South Asia and presents with 10 days of stepwise rising fever, headache, abdominal pain, and constipation followed by diarrhea. His temperature is 39.4°C while his pulse is only 78/min. Examination shows a faint blanching pink rash over the trunk and hepatosplenomegaly. Blood culture grows a gram-negative rod that produces H2S.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Yersinia enterocolitica"},{"id":"B","text":"Shigella sonnei"},{"id":"C","text":"Campylobacter jejuni"},{"id":"D","text":"Vibrio cholerae"},{"id":"E","text":"Salmonella typhi"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is typhoid fever caused by Salmonella typhi, with a salmon-colored truncal macular rash, abdominal pain, fever with pulse-temperature dissociation, and later gastrointestinal ulceration and hemorrhage. S typhi has a human-only reservoir, spreads hematogenously, produces H2S, has flagella, and is treated with ceftriaxone or a fluoroquinolone.',
    '{"A":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a pleomorphic rod with bipolar staining that causes pseudoappendicitis and reactive arthritis, typically acquired from pork or pet feces.","B":"Shigella sonnei does not produce H2S, has no flagella, does not spread hematogenously, and causes bacillary dysentery with tenesmus and bloody mucoid stools.","C":"Campylobacter jejuni is a curved organism that grows at 42°C with polar flagella, causes bloody diarrhea, and produces no H2S; it is linked to Guillain-Barré syndrome.","D":"Vibrio cholerae is a comma-shaped organism that grows in alkaline media and produces profuse rice-water diarrhea rather than a febrile systemic illness with a rash."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Salmonella',
    'A 34-year-old woman has recovered from an acute diarrheal illness after a trip abroad, but she continues to have intermittent low-grade fevers and abdominal discomfort 6 months later. Stool cultures intermittently grow Salmonella typhi. Imaging shows gallstones, and she recalls having a similar febrile illness many years earlier.',
    'Which of the following best explains the site of her persistent organism carriage?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Renal pelvis colonization with struvite stone formation"},{"id":"B","text":"Small intestinal colonization by organisms that produce enterotoxin"},{"id":"C","text":"Gallbladder colonization"},{"id":"D","text":"Colonic mucosal invasion with cell-to-cell spread"},{"id":"E","text":"Peyer patch invasion with regional lymph node latency"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Salmonella typhi establishes a chronic carrier state with gallbladder colonization, which is why long-term shedding and intermittent bacteremia persist after acute illness. Unlike most other Salmonella, S typhi has humans as its only reservoir, and the 4 F''s of fingers, flies, food, and feces describe its transmission.',
    '{"A":"Renal pelvis colonization with struvite stones is the mechanism of Proteus mirabilis infection, driven by urease production and an alkaline urine pH.","B":"Enterotoxin-driven small intestinal colonization causes acute watery diarrhea and does not produce chronic carriage; S typhi does not use an enterotoxin to establish persistence.","D":"Cell-to-cell spread without hematogenous dissemination is the defining behavior of Shigella, not of Salmonella, which spreads hematogenously.","E":"Invasion of Peyer patches occurs in acute Salmonella infection, but chronic carriage persists in the gallbladder rather than in lymph nodes."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Salmonella',
    'A 7-year-old boy presents with fever, abdominal cramps, and nonbloody diarrhea that began 2 days after his family bought a pet turtle for his terrarium. He handles the turtle daily and does not always wash his hands. Stool culture grows a gram-negative rod that produces H2S on TSI agar and is oxidase negative.',
    'Which of the following statements about the responsible organism is correct?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Antibiotics shorten the duration of fecal excretion"},{"id":"B","text":"It requires a very low infectious dose because it is acid stable"},{"id":"C","text":"It is a non-lactose fermenter that can be acquired from pets, poultry, and eggs"},{"id":"D","text":"It is a non-lactose fermenter with humans as its only reservoir"},{"id":"E","text":"It has no flagella and does not produce H2S"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is nontyphoidal Salmonella infection, and reptiles and poultry are common sources, so a pet turtle is a classic exposure. Salmonella species other than S typhi have both human and animal reservoirs, are non-lactose fermenters that produce H2S, are oxidase negative, are flagellated, and require a relatively high infectious dose because they are acid labile.',
    '{"A":"Antibiotics in nontyphoidal Salmonella prolong rather than shorten the duration of fecal excretion; antibiotics shorten shedding in Shigella.","B":"A low infectious dose with acid stability is characteristic of Shigella; Salmonella requires a large inoculum because it is inactivated by gastric acid.","D":"Human-only reservoir is a feature of S typhi and Shigella; nontyphoidal Salmonella is zoonotic, as this turtle exposure illustrates.","E":"Nonflagellated, H2S-negative organisms describe Shigella, not Salmonella, which swims with flagella and produces H2S."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Shigella',
    'A 32-year-old man develops high fever, crampy lower abdominal pain, and frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus, along with a persistent urge to defecate even when the rectum is empty. He ate at a restaurant where a food handler had diarrheal illness. Stool culture grows a gram-negative rod that is oxidase negative, non-lactose fermenting, and does not produce H2S or flagella.',
    'Which of the following best explains the small inoculum needed to transmit this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Hematogenous spread before intestinal invasion"},{"id":"B","text":"Urease production neutralizing gastric acid"},{"id":"C","text":"Production of a Vi capsular polysaccharide"},{"id":"D","text":"Acid stability allowing survival through the stomach"},{"id":"E","text":"Formation of heat-resistant endospores"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is bacillary dysentery caused by Shigella, with crampy abdominal pain, tenesmus, and bloody mucoid stools. Shigella has a low infectious dose because it is acid stable and resistant to gastric acids, and invasion of M cells of Peyer patches is key to its pathogenicity. It spreads cell to cell without hematogenous dissemination, does not produce H2S or flagella, and its reservoir is humans only.',
    '{"A":"Shigella spreads from cell to cell without hematogenous spread, which is the opposite of Salmonella; hematogenous spread would not explain the low infectious dose.","B":"Urease production is a feature of Proteus mirabilis and Helicobacter pylori; it is not how Shigella survives gastric acid, and H pylori creates local alkaline microenvironments rather than neutralizing the stomach.","C":"A Vi capsule belongs to Salmonella typhi, which has a high infectious dose because it is acid labile; Shigella has no such capsule.","E":"Shigella does not form spores; spore formation belongs to Bacillus and Clostridium species."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Shigella',
    'A 28-year-old man presents with 2 days of fever, abdominal cramps, tenesmus, and 10 to 12 bloody mucoid stools per day. He is otherwise healthy and well hydrated. Stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. The patient asks whether antibiotics will help him recover faster and whether they will affect how long he remains contagious.',
    'Which of the following statements about this infection is correct?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Antibiotics shorten the duration of fecal excretion and illness"},{"id":"B","text":"Antibiotics eliminate the human carrier state in the gallbladder"},{"id":"C","text":"Antibiotics are contraindicated because they precipitate hemolytic-uremic syndrome"},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotics are not indicated in immunocompetent individuals and only prolong shedding"},{"id":"E","text":"Antibiotics lengthen fecal excretion, similar to Salmonella infection"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'In Shigella infection, antibiotics shorten the duration of fecal excretion, in contrast to Salmonella, where antibiotics prolong shedding. Shigella has humans as its only reservoir, requires a very small inoculum because it is acid stable, and causes bacillary dysentery through invasion of M cells; antibiotic treatment reduces both illness duration and transmission.',
    '{"B":"Gallbladder carriage is a feature of Salmonella typhi; Shigella infection does not establish a chronic biliary carrier state.","C":"Antibiotic-precipitated hemolytic-uremic syndrome is a concern in enterohemorrhagic E coli O157:H7 infection, not a reason to withhold antibiotics in shigellosis.","D":"Antibiotics are not indicated in immunocompetent patients with uncomplicated Salmonella gastroenteritis, not with shigellosis, where treatment shortens illness and shedding.","E":"Lengthened fecal excretion with antibiotics is a feature of Salmonella infection, and the source text contrasts that directly with Shigella, in which shedding is shortened."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Specialized transduction and lysogenic conversion',
    'A 3-year-old child with bloody diarrhea develops pallor, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. Stool culture grows Shiga toxin-producing E coli O157:H7. The laboratory notes that the toxin gene is carried on a temperate bacteriophage integrated into the bacterial chromosome.',
    'Which process allowed this bacterial strain to acquire the Shiga toxin gene?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lysogenic conversion after specialized transduction"},{"id":"B","text":"Conjugation through the sex pilus"},{"id":"C","text":"Generalized transduction packaging a random fragment"},{"id":"D","text":"Transposition of a transposon into the chromosome"},{"id":"E","text":"Transformation with naked DNA from lysed cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'A lysogenic (temperate) phage integrates its DNA into the bacterial chromosome. When the prophage later excises, it may take flanking bacterial genes with it; when the excised DNA is a phage-borne toxin gene and the recipient expresses it, the phenomenon is lysogenic conversion. The five classic phage-encoded toxins are the ABCD''S: group A streptococcal erythrogenic toxin, Botulinum toxin, Cholera toxin, Diphtheria toxin, and Shiga toxin. In Shiga toxin-producing E coli, infection by a toxin-carrying phage converted a harmless commensal into a pathogen that causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic-uremic syndrome through toxin-mediated injury, even though EHEC does not invade host cells.',
    '{"B":"Conjugation transfers plasmid DNA through a sex pilus and does not insert phage DNA into the chromosome.","C":"Generalized transduction carries random chromosomal fragments from a packaging error; it would not deliver a specific phage-encoded toxin gene.","D":"A transposon jump does not require phage integration; the Shiga toxin gene here is explicitly phage encoded.","E":"Transformation is uptake of naked DNA by competent organisms such as S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and Neisseria; it does not involve a temperate phage."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Specialized transduction and lysogenic conversion',
    'A 70-year-old woman with non-Hodgkin lymphoma treated with rituximab develops profound hypogammaglobulinemia, and her ability to mount antitoxin antibody responses is markedly reduced. She has not been immunized against tetanus or diphtheria in decades. She is exposed to a patient with diphtheria and asks whether she can be protected.',
    'Why does prior vaccination with a toxoid provide protection against the systemic effects of this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Generalized transduction packaging a random chromosomal fragment in a capsid"},{"id":"B","text":"An excision event of a lysogenic phage during specialized transduction"},{"id":"C","text":"Transposition of a mobile genetic element into the bacterial chromosome"},{"id":"D","text":"Toxoids are live attenuated organisms that outcompete the wild type"},{"id":"E","text":"Transformation with naked DNA released from the lysed bacterial cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Exotoxins are strongly antigenic and induce high-titer antibodies called antitoxins, and heat- or chemically-inactivated exotoxins (toxoids) are used as vaccines for tetanus and diphtheria. Protection is therefore humoral and depends on an intact B-cell compartment, which is exactly why rituximab-induced hypogammaglobulinemia markedly blunts it. Diphtheria toxin inactivates elongation factor 2 by ADP-ribosylation, and antitoxin neutralizes the secreted toxin; killing of the organism requires antibiotics. This pairing of toxoid vaccines with antitoxin therapy is a classic board association.',
    '{"A":"A polysaccharide antigen alone cannot be presented to T cells; that is why capsule antigens must be conjugated to carrier proteins, and toxoids are proteins anyway.","C":"Lipid A is endotoxin and is neither the basis of toxoids nor a protective antigen, since endotoxin is poorly antigenic.","D":"Toxoid vaccines contain no living organism; they rely on an inactivated toxin to elicit antitoxin, so colonization is not their mechanism.","E":"Toxoid vaccines are chemically inactivated protein toxins, not phage particles, and they work through humoral antitoxin, not interferon."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Spores and sporulation',
    'A 9-month-old infant is brought in with poor feeding, generalized weakness, constipation, and a weak cry. The family reports giving him raw honey. The infant remains afebrile with normal mental status. The organisms cultured are gram-positive bacilli that form heat-resistant spores and grow only under anaerobic conditions.',
    'Which structure/feature best explains the environmental persistence of this organism in the honey?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lipopolysaccharide in the outer membrane that resists heat"},{"id":"B","text":"An S layer that inactivates hydrogen peroxide"},{"id":"C","text":"M protein that prevents opsonization"},{"id":"D","text":"A keratinlike spore coat with dipicolinic acid in its core"},{"id":"E","text":"A thick polysaccharide capsule that resists desiccation"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Some gram-positive bacteria form spores when nutrients are limited; the spore is a specialized structure with a keratinlike coat and a core containing dipicolinic acid, which confers heat resistance, plus peptidoglycan and DNA. Spores lack metabolic activity and resist dehydration, heat, and chemicals, which is why honey can harbor Clostridium botulinum spores that germinate in the immature infant gut and produce toxin in vivo. This is infant botulism, distinct from foodborne botulism, in which preformed toxin from canned foods is ingested. Killing such spores requires autoclaving, and hydrogen peroxide and iodine are also sporicidal.',
    '{"A":"LPS confers endotoxin-mediated toxicity in gram-negative organisms; it is extremely heat stable, but it is not the dormant, much more heat-resistant structure described here.","B":"The slime layer is a loose polysaccharide network for surface adherence and biofilm formation; it has no peroxide-inactivating function.","C":"M protein is a group A streptococcal surface protein that interferes with phagocytosis and shows molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin; it is unrelated to spore survival.","E":"The capsule protects against phagocytosis and complement-mediated lysis; it is not the structure responsible for extreme heat and chemical resistance of the dormant form."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Spores and sporulation',
    'A 38-year-old man who injects drugs presents with a rapidly progressive, crepitant, painful leg wound with skin necrosis and gas in the tissue. The organism isolated is a gram-positive bacillus that forms spores, and blood agar shows double-zone hemolysis.',
    'Which organism and spore-associated feature best matches this picture?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Clostridium botulinum - spore former that produces a protease that cleaves SNARE proteins"},{"id":"B","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes - non-spore-forming organism with streptolysin O"},{"id":"C","text":"Bacillus anthracis - catalase-negative spore former with a polypeptide capsule"},{"id":"D","text":"Clostridium perfringens - spore former whose alpha toxin is a phospholipase"},{"id":"E","text":"Clostridioides difficile - spore former that produces two toxins causing pseudomembranous colitis"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Clostridium perfringens is a spore-forming, gram-positive anaerobe whose alpha toxin is a phospholipase (lecithinase) that degrades tissue and cell membranes, producing myonecrosis with gas gangrene and the double-zone hemolysis seen on blood agar. Spore formation among the gram-positive bacilli includes B anthracis, B cereus, C botulinum, C difficile, C perfringens, and C tetani, memorized as "Autoclave to kill Bacillus and Clostridium." The crepitant, rapidly progressive wound with gas in tissue is the signature of a gas-producing anaerobe, and spores explain the organism''s resistance to routine cleaning of contaminated wounds.',
    '{"A":"C botulinum produces a SNARE-cleaving toxin causing flaccid paralysis, not myonecrosis and gas.","B":"S pyogenes is not a spore former; its streptolysin O lyses red cells and contributes to beta-hemolysis, but the spore-forming requirement excludes it.","C":"B anthracis causes anthrax with a polypeptide capsule and edema-inducing toxin, not gas gangrene with double-zone hemolysis.","E":"C difficile spores cause pseudomembranous colitis after antibiotics; it does not cause a necrotizing limb wound."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Stains: Gram, Giemsa, PAS, Ziehl-Neelsen, India ink',
    'A 44-year-old man with HIV and a CD4 count of 60 cells/mm3 presents with a headache and fever. Cerebrospinal fluid shows encapsulated budding yeasts. India ink preparation demonstrates clear halos around the organisms.',
    'Which stain would also allow visualization of this organism''s thick polysaccharide capsule, appearing as red halos?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Mucicarmine stain"},{"id":"B","text":"Periodic acid-Schiff stain"},{"id":"C","text":"Silver stain"},{"id":"D","text":"Giemsa stain"},{"id":"E","text":"Ziehl-Neelsen stain"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'India ink preparations rely on the ability of a thick polysaccharide capsule to exclude the colloidal carbon particles, leaving a clear halo around Cryptococcus neoformans. Mucicarmine can also be used, staining this polysaccharide capsule red. Both are capsule-directed methods and are complementary rather than interchangeable in textbook usage. Recognizing the India ink halo with the classic HIV-associated meningitis syndrome is one of the highest-yield stain-organism pairings in microbiology.',
    '{"B":"PAS stains glycogen and mucopolysaccharides and is used for Whipple disease (Tropheryma whipplei); it is not the classic capsule stain.","C":"Silver stain is used for Helicobacter pylori, Legionella, Bartonella henselae, and fungi such as Coccidioides, Pneumocystis, and Aspergillus; it does not selectively highlight capsular material.","D":"Giemsa stains H pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, Trypanosoma, and Plasmodium; it is used for blood and tissue smears, not for capsular halos.","E":"Ziehl-Neelsen (carbol fuchsin) stains acid-fast organisms such as Mycobacteria and Nocardia by binding mycolic acid, not a polysaccharide capsule."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Stains: Gram, Giemsa, PAS, Ziehl-Neelsen, India ink',
    'A 29-year-old man with a chronic cough, night sweats, and weight loss has a sputum smear that is negative with the standard Gram stain but shows elongated red bacilli when an acid-fast preparation is used. He is suspected to have Mycobacterium tuberculosis.',
    'Which property allows this organism to be visualized with the Ziehl-Neelsen stain?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Absence of a cell wall, resulting in pleomorphic forms"},{"id":"B","text":"A polysaccharide capsule that excludes counterstain"},{"id":"C","text":"Primarily intracellular growth that concentrates dye"},{"id":"D","text":"High lipid content from mycolic acid in the cell wall"},{"id":"E","text":"A thick peptidoglycan layer that retains crystal violet"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The Ziehl-Neelsen (carbol fuchsin) stain exploits the high lipid content of the mycobacterial cell wall, enabling retention of carbol fuchsin despite acid-alcohol decolorization. The organisms photographed this way include Mycobacteria and Nocardia, and the stain also demonstrates protozoa such as Cryptosporidium oocysts. Mycobacteria belong to the classic list of organisms that do not Gram stain well because of their high lipid content. In practice, auramine-rhodamine fluorescence is often used for screening because it is inexpensive and more sensitive.',
    '{"A":"Absence of a cell wall describes Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma, which do not Gram stain well; mycobacteria do have a wall, but it is waxy and lipid rich.","B":"A capsule is not the reason: capsular material excludes ink in India ink preparations and does not confer acid fastness.","C":"Intracellular location does not explain the stain: the organisms that fail to Gram stain partly because they are primarily intracellular include Legionella, Rickettsia, Chlamydia, Bartonella, Anaplasma, and Ehrlichia.","E":"Crystal violet retention is the Gram stain mechanism and relies on thick peptidoglycan; mycobacteria typically stain poorly because of their lipid-rich wall."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Stains: Gram, Giemsa, PAS, Ziehl-Neelsen, India ink',
    'A 55-year-old man with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and malabsorption has small-bowel biopsies showing foamy macrophages packed with PAS-positive granules in the lamina propria. The pathologist requests a specific stain to highlight the organism.',
    'Which stain best demonstrates the responsible organism, Tropheryma whipplei?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A thick polysaccharide capsule that excludes dye and prevents counterstaining"},{"id":"B","text":"Primarily intracellular growth that concentrates dye within the host cytoplasm"},{"id":"C","text":"A thick peptidoglycan layer that retains crystal violet through decolorization"},{"id":"D","text":"Absence of a cell wall, resulting in pleomorphic forms that resist staining"},{"id":"E","text":"High lipid content from mycolic acid in the cell wall, which repels the stain"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Whipple disease from Tropheryma whipplei is classically diagnosed with periodic acid-Schiff staining, which highlights the glycogen and mucopolysaccharide content of the organisms accumulating in small-bowel macrophages. PAS positivity in foamy macrophages of the lamina propria is the classic histologic clue and explains the malabsorptive syndrome. The organism is not acid fast, is not encapsulated, and is not a fungus, which eliminates the other commonly used special stains.',
    '{"A":"Giemsa is used for H pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, Trypanosoma, and Plasmodium, not for this organism.","B":"Silver stain highlights H pylori, Legionella, Bartonella henselae, and fungi such as Coccidioides, Pneumocystis, and Aspergillus.","C":"Ziehl-Neelsen is for acid-fast bacilli and Cryptosporidium oocysts; T whipplei is not acid fast.","E":"India ink is a negative stain for thick polysaccharide capsules such as Cryptococcus neoformans, a fungal organism."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Stains: Gram, Giemsa, PAS, Ziehl-Neelsen, India ink',
    'A 33-year-old man presents with a painless penile chancre, and dark-field examination of the exudate reveals spirochetes. A routine Gram stain of the same specimen shows no organisms.',
    'Which pair correctly matches an organism from the "do not Gram stain well" list with the reason?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Mycoplasma - cell wall has a high lipid content"},{"id":"B","text":"Legionella - no cell wall and thus cannot retain dye"},{"id":"C","text":"Chlamydia - thick peptidoglycan that overstains"},{"id":"D","text":"Treponema - too thin to be visualized on Gram stain"},{"id":"E","text":"Rickettsia - presence of an outer membrane that excludes dye"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The classic list of organisms that do not Gram stain well is remembered with "These Little Microbes May Unfortunately Lack Real Color But Are Everywhere." Treponema and Leptospira are too thin to be visualized; Mycobacteria have a high-lipid-content cell wall; Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma have no cell wall; and Legionella, Rickettsia, Chlamydia, Bartonella, Anaplasma, and Ehrlichia are primarily intracellular, with Chlamydia also lacking classic peptidoglycan because of defective muramic acid. Syphilis is therefore diagnosed with dark-field microscopy or fluorescent antibody stains such as FTA-ABS. Knowing why each organism fails the Gram stain, rather than just the list, is what the question tests.',
    '{"A":"Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma lack a cell wall altogether; the high-lipid-cell-wall reason belongs to Mycobacteria.","B":"Legionella belongs among the primarily intracellular organisms that fail to Gram stain well; it does have a cell wall, and the no-cell-wall reason belongs to Mycoplasma.","C":"Chlamydia lack classic peptidoglycan because of a defect in muramic acid synthesis and are primarily intracellular, and also are not thick-walled overstainers.","E":"Rickettsia fails to Gram stain because it is primarily intracellular, not because of an outer membrane; Neisseria, which are gram-negative, stain fine."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus aureus',
    'A 23-year-old woman presents with 2 days of fever to 39.8°C, diffuse erythematous rash, vomiting, and watery diarrhea. She is hypotensive with a systolic pressure of 78 mm Hg and appears confused. She has been using a superabsorbent vaginal tampon continuously for the past 5 days during a heavy menstrual period. Laboratories show AST and ALT above 200 U/L, an elevated bilirubin, and rising creatinine.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which the responsible toxin produces this illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Permanent activation of Gs with accumulation of intracellular cAMP"},{"id":"B","text":"Binding of MHC class II and the T-cell receptor with polyclonal T-cell activation"},{"id":"C","text":"Proteolytic cleavage of SNARE proteins at the neuromuscular junction"},{"id":"D","text":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, halting protein synthesis"},{"id":"E","text":"Sphingomyelinase activity that degrades keratinocyte desmosomes"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome, classically associated with prolonged tampon use or nasal packing. TSST-1 is a superantigen: it bridges MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and the T-cell receptor outside the normal peptide groove, causing massive polyclonal T-cell activation and cytokine release. The result is fever, vomiting, diarrhea, rash with desquamation, shock, and end-organ failure, with elevated AST, ALT, and bilirubin.',
    '{"A":"Permanent activation of Gs with elevated cAMP is how cholera toxin and heat-labile enterotoxin of ETEC work, producing profuse secretory diarrhea rather than a superantigen-driven shock state.","C":"SNARE protein cleavage is the mechanism of the clostridial toxins, botulinum toxin (blocks acetylcholine release, flaccid paralysis) and tetanospasmin (blocks glycine and GABA release, spastic paralysis), not of TSST-1.","D":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 halts protein synthesis and is the mechanism of diphtheria toxin and of Pseudomonas exotoxin A; it is not the mechanism of a superantigen.","E":"Degradation of desmosomes by a sphingomyelinase-like activity belongs to staphylococcal exfoliative toxin, which causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, not the shock and multiorgan failure described here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus aureus',
    'Several members of a family develop nausea, forceful vomiting, and nonbloody diarrhea about 3 hours after a church picnic where sliced ham sat unrefrigerated all afternoon. They have no fever and recover within a day. The ham was reheated thoroughly before being served, and Gram stain of the leftover meat shows gram-positive cocci in clusters.',
    'Which of the following best explains why reheating the food did not prevent the illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The toxin is a superantigen that requires host MHC class II for activity"},{"id":"B","text":"The enterotoxin is heat stable and is not destroyed by cooking"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism survives as heat-resistant endospores that germinate in the gut"},{"id":"D","text":"The toxin is produced in the small intestine after ingestion of live bacteria"},{"id":"E","text":"The toxin is inactivated by gastric acid, so it acts only in the colon"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning. The enterotoxin is preformed in the food and is heat stable, so cooking or reheating does not destroy it. The short incubation period of about 2 to 6 hours followed by nonbloody diarrhea and prominent emesis reflects direct toxin action rather than infection.',
    '{"A":"Superantigen activity against MHC class II describes TSST-1, the cause of toxic shock syndrome, which presents with shock and multiorgan failure rather than brief self-limited vomiting and diarrhea.","C":"Heat-resistant endospores that germinate after cooking are the mechanism of Bacillus cereus (reheated rice) and of Clostridium perfringens, not of this cluster-forming coccus.","D":"Toxin produced in the gut after ingestion of live organisms is the mechanism of Clostridium perfringens food poisoning, which has a longer incubation of about 10 to 12 hours and no prominent vomiting.","E":"Staphylococcal enterotoxin acts quickly after ingestion in the upper gut; the classic toxin that is acid labile and requires a large inoculum is Vibrio cholerae enterotoxin."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus aureus',
    'A 26-year-old man injected heroin into his left arm 5 days ago. He now has fever and a fluctuant, tender, warm nodule at the injection site that drains thick pus. A wound culture grows catalase-positive, coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in clusters. The laboratory reports that the isolate is resistant to methicillin.',
    'Which of the following best explains the mechanism of methicillin resistance in this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Sequestration of the drug by a thickened polysaccharide capsule"},{"id":"B","text":"Production of a β-lactamase that hydrolyzes the methicillin ring"},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of peptidoglycan cross-links with d-alanine–d-lactate"},{"id":"D","text":"Altered penicillin-binding proteins encoded by the mecA gene"},{"id":"E","text":"Porin mutations that exclude the drug from the periplasm"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'The organism is Staphylococcus aureus (catalase positive, coagulase positive, gram-positive cocci in clusters), and the abscess is a classic staphylococcal lesion formed because coagulase walls the organism off in a fibrin clot. MRSA resistance results from altered penicillin-binding proteins conferred by the mecA gene, a change that no β-lactam inhibitor can overcome.',
    '{"A":"A polysaccharide capsule interferes with phagocytosis rather than drug access; encapsulated staphylococci remain fully susceptible to methicillin if their PBPs are unaltered.","B":"β-lactamase hydrolysis underlies penicillin resistance in most staphylococci, but methicillin was designed to resist this enzyme, so resistance to it requires a different target-based mechanism.","C":"Peptidoglycan cross-links built from d-alanine–d-lactate are the mechanism of vancomycin resistance in vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE) and in VRSA, not the mechanism of methicillin resistance.","E":"Porin loss is a gram-negative strategy for limiting entry of small hydrophilic drugs, relevant to carbapenem resistance in Enterobacteriaceae; staphylococci are gram-positive and lack an outer membrane."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus aureus',
    'A 40-year-old man with acute myelogenous leukemia is neutropenic after chemotherapy. He develops high fever, chills, and rapidly enlarging, painful pustular skin lesions with surrounding erythema. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters, and the isolate shows zones of clearing that coalesce when placed near a meticillin-containing disk.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors of this organism most directly explains the severe necrotizing skin findings and neutropenia-related tissue damage?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Capsular hyaluronic acid inhibiting complement deposition"},{"id":"B","text":"Protein A binding the Fc region of IgG"},{"id":"C","text":"Coagulase converting fibrinogen to fibrin around the colony"},{"id":"D","text":"Exfoliative toxin cleaving desmosomes in the stratum granulosum"},{"id":"E","text":"Panton-Valentine leukocidin killing leukocytes and causing tissue necrosis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Some Staphylococcus aureus strains release Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL), a pore-forming toxin that kills leukocytes and causes tissue necrosis. It is a marker of community-associated MRSA and is associated with recurrent necrotizing skin and soft-tissue infections, matching these rapidly enlarging necrotic lesions.',
    '{"A":"Hyaluronic acid capsule is characteristic of Streptococcus pyogenes rather than S aureus, and it inhibits phagocytosis rather than lysing white cells.","B":"Protein A binds the Fc region of IgG to inhibit complement activation and phagocytosis, which promotes immune evasion but does not lyse leukocytes or directly produce necrosis.","C":"Coagulase generates the fibrin wall that turns a staphylococcal infection into a walled-off abscess; it is a defining diagnostic feature but not a cytolytic toxin.","D":"Exfoliative toxin causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome by targeting the superficial epidermis, producing diffuse peeling rather than focal necrotizing pustules."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus aureus',
    'A 2-year-old boy is brought in with 2 days of a tender, red, rapidly spreading rash with large flaccid bullae, multiple areas of superficial peeling, and fever. He has had no mucosal involvement and seems otherwise alert and hydrated. Petechiae and purpura are absent. Cultures taken from the unroofed bullae are sterile, but cultures of the nasopharynx grow coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in clusters.',
    'Which of the following best explains the negative bulla culture in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The organism requires chocolate agar and factors V and X for growth"},{"id":"B","text":"The disease is mediated by a toxin acting at a distant site rather than by infection at the bulla"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism is cleared rapidly from the skin by neutrophil influx"},{"id":"D","text":"The organism is an obligate anaerobe that fails to grow in routine culture"},{"id":"E","text":"The bullae represent a superficial dermatophyte infection with secondary blistering"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome. Colonization of the nasopharynx or umbilicus releases exfoliative toxin, which travels hematogenously to the skin and cleaves superficial desmosomes, producing flaccid bullae and diffuse peeling. Because the epidermis itself is not infected, fluid from the blisters is sterile, so the diagnosis rests on clinical findings plus cultures from the primary colonized site.',
    '{"A":"Chocolate agar with factors V and X is required by Haemophilus influenzae, and this disease is far more consistent with toxin-mediated staphylococcal skin involvement than with Haemophilus.","C":"Neutrophil clearance would not render the blister fluid sterile; instead, the absence of organisms reflects a toxin-mediated mechanism in which bacteria are not present in the lesion.","D":"Staphylococci are facultative anaerobes that grow readily in routine culture; an obligate anaerobe in this clinical setting would suggest Clostridium, which does not produce this picture.","E":"A dermatophyte infection causes scaly, annular plaques with central clearing; it does not cause flaccid bullae or diffuse superficial peeling in a febrile toddler."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus epidermidis and saprophyticus',
    'A 68-year-old man had a mechanical mitral valve replacement 8 weeks ago. He now has 3 weeks of low-grade fever, malaise, and a new systolic murmur. Four of four blood culture sets grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive, coagulase negative, and urease positive. Transesophageal echocardiography shows a vegetation on the prosthetic ring.',
    'Which of the following best explains this organism''s tropism for prosthetic material?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Protein A binding the Fc portion of IgG"},{"id":"B","text":"Secretion of exfoliative toxin that damages the endocardium"},{"id":"C","text":"Coagulase-mediated fibrin clot formation around the valve"},{"id":"D","text":"Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride bile-containing medium"},{"id":"E","text":"Production of adherent biofilms on implanted surfaces"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is Staphylococcus epidermidis, a coagulase-negative, novobiocin-sensitive member of the normal skin microbiota. It infects prosthetic devices such as artificial heart valves, hip implants, and intravenous catheters by producing adherent biofilms. It is also the classic blood culture contaminant, but growth in multiple sets with a vegetation is genuine infection.',
    '{"A":"Protein A is a virulence factor of Staphylococcus aureus used to inhibit complement activation and phagocytosis, and its presence supports S aureus rather than this coagulase-negative organism.","B":"Exfoliative toxin causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, a superficial epidermal disease; it is produced by some S aureus strains and has no role in biofilm-associated endocarditis.","C":"Coagulase is absent in S epidermidis, and the coagulase-produced fibrin clot is what makes S aureus abscesses; a negative coagulase test is precisely how this isolate was distinguished.","D":"Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride with bile is the resilience test for Enterococcus, a different gram-positive coccus entirely, and it does not explain biofilm formation."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus epidermidis and saprophyticus',
    'A 21-year-old sexually active woman presents with 2 days of burning on urination, urinary frequency, and suprapubic discomfort. She has no fever, flank pain, vaginal discharge, or history of instrumentation. Urinalysis shows pyuria and nitrite positivity, and urine culture grows more than 100,000 colonies of gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive, coagulase negative, urease positive, and resistant to novobiocin.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"id":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"id":"C","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"id":"D","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"E","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Staphylococcus saprophyticus is part of the normal female genital tract and perineal microbiota. It is coagulase negative and uniquely novobiocin resistant, and it is the second most common cause of uncomplicated UTI in young women after E coli, matching this uncomplicated cystitis in a sexually active young woman.',
    '{"A":"Streptococcus agalactiae is a β-hemolytic, bacitracin-resistant, catalase-negative coccus of the vaginal tract whose classic disease is neonatal sepsis and meningitis, not cystitis.","B":"Staphylococcus epidermidis is also coagulase negative and urease positive, but it is novobiocin sensitive and causes device and catheter infections rather than community-acquired cystitis in young women.","D":"Staphylococcus aureus is coagulase positive and, while able to cause urinary infection, is a far less common cause of uncomplicated cystitis in a healthy young woman.","E":"Enterococcus faecalis is catalase negative, PYR positive, and typically nonhemolytic, and it causes UTI mainly after instrumentation or in healthcare settings; it also grows in 6.5% NaCl."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Staphylococcus epidermidis and saprophyticus',
    'A 55-year-old woman in the intensive care unit has a central venous catheter in place after surgery. On hospital day 9 she develops fever and hypotension. Blood drawn from the catheter hub and from a peripheral vein both grow the same gram-positive cocci; the laboratory reports growth only in the catheter specimen initially, and the organism produces abundant slime on the catheter tip after removal. The isolate is catalase positive, coagulase negative, novobiocin sensitive, and does not ferment mannitol.',
    'Which of the following best distinguishes this isolate from Staphylococcus aureus?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"β-hemolysis on blood agar"},{"id":"B","text":"Coagulase negativity and inability to ferment mannitol"},{"id":"C","text":"Formation of cocci in clusters"},{"id":"D","text":"Growth at 37°C in ambient air"},{"id":"E","text":"Production of catalase"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Both S epidermidis and S aureus are gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive, so the discriminating tests are coagulase and mannitol fermentation. S epidermidis is coagulase negative and does not ferment mannitol, whereas S aureus is coagulase positive and ferments mannitol. Novobiocin sensitivity further separates S epidermidis from novobiocin-resistant S saprophyticus.',
    '{"A":"β-hemolysis is common in S aureus and is not the reliable discriminator, since some coagulase-negative staphylococci can also show hemolysis depending on the strain.","C":"Both organisms form clusters, which is the shared feature that identifies them as staphylococci in the first place rather than distinguishing them.","D":"Both are facultative anaerobes that grow readily at body temperature in ordinary atmosphere, so growth under these conditions has no discriminatory value.","E":"Catalase is produced by essentially all staphylococci, so a positive catalase reaction cannot separate the two species; it separates staphylococci from streptococci."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Sterilization and disinfection',
    'A hospital reprocessing unit must sterilize surgical instruments that may be contaminated with Clostridium perfringens spores from a wound debridement performed the night before. The staff is reviewing the parameters for reliable sterilization.',
    'Which condition is required to reliably kill bacterial spores on surgical equipment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Washing the instruments with soap and hot water, then drying them fully"},{"id":"B","text":"Boiling the instruments at 100 degrees C for 10 minutes in water"},{"id":"C","text":"Soaking the instruments in 70% ethanol for 30 minutes at room temperature"},{"id":"D","text":"Autoclaving by steaming at 121 degrees C for 15 minutes at 15 psi"},{"id":"E","text":"Exposing the instruments to ultraviolet light for 20 minutes after cleaning"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Spores lack metabolic activity and are highly resistant to heat and chemicals because their core contains dipicolinic acid; routine disinfection and boiling will not reliably kill them. Autoclaving - steaming at 121 degrees C for 15 minutes at a pressure of 15 psi - is the standard method, summarized by the mnemonic abc (Autoclave to kill Bacillus and Clostridium). Hydrogen peroxide and iodine-based agents are also sporicidal, which is why they appear among chemical sterilization and high-level disinfection options. The pressure is essential because it raises the steam temperature above boiling.',
    '{"A":"Soap and hot water achieve mechanical removal and detergent action but no microbial kill, and they have no effect on heat-resistant spore structures.","B":"Boiling at 100 degrees C kills vegetative bacteria but is not reliably sporicidal; the missing element is the pressure that raises steam temperature, which is exactly why autoclaving works.","C":"70% ethanol is a bactericidal alcohol that is active against many vegetative organisms but is not dependable against spores, so it cannot sterilize a contaminated instrument.","E":"UV irradiation damages nucleic acid on exposed surfaces only; it does not penetrate instruments and has no reliable sporicidal activity, so it cannot be used here."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Sterilization and disinfection',
    'An endoscopy unit is choosing an agent for a heat-sensitive flexible endoscope that was exposed to a patient with inadequate bowel preparation and possible spore contamination. Chemical disinfection is required because the device cannot be autoclaved.',
    'Which chemical agent is sporicidal and appropriate for high-level disinfection of heat-sensitive instruments?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Isopropyl alcohol"},{"id":"B","text":"Quaternary ammonium compounds"},{"id":"C","text":"Glutaraldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Soap and water"},{"id":"E","text":"70% ethanol"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Because spores resist heat and chemicals through their dipicolinic acid-rich core, chemical killing requires intrinsically sporicidal agents. Glutaraldehyde provides so-called cold sterilization for heat-sensitive equipment such as endoscopes, and hydrogen peroxide and iodine-based agents are likewise sporicidal. Alcohols are effective against vegetative organisms including many mycobacteria but not against spores, which is why they cannot be substituted here. The distinction between disinfection and sterilization hinges on whether the method reliably destroys spores.',
    '{"A":"Isopropyl alcohol shares the limitations of ethanol: rapid killing of vegetative forms but no dependable spore activity and no high-level disinfection claim.","B":"Quaternary ammonium compounds are low-level disinfectants that are active against many vegetative bacteria but do not kill spores and have limited mycobacterial activity.","D":"Soap and water performs mechanical removal and detergent action only; it is cleaning, not disinfection, and has no sporicidal action.","E":"Ethanol at 70% is a bactericidal alcohol that inactivates many vegetative organisms, but it is not reliably sporicidal and is not used for sterilization."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus agalactiae',
    'A 30-year-old woman at 20 weeks of gestation has a vaginal swab performed because of preterm contractions. The culture grows β-hemolytic colonies on blood agar, and the organism is bacitracin resistant, camp positive, hippurate positive, and PYR negative.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely isolated?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"id":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"id":"C","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"id":"E","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Streptococcus agalactiae, or group B strep, colonizes the vagina and is β-hemolytic but bacitracin resistant, distinguished from group A by a positive CAMP test (its CAMP factor enlarges the hemolysis produced by S aureus), a positive hippurate test, and a negative PYR. Group B is for Babies: it causes pneumonia, meningitis, and sepsis in neonates.',
    '{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is an α-hemolytic lancet-shaped diplococcus that is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, and it does not cause vaginal colonization.","C":"Streptococcus gallolyticus is a group D gut organism linked to bacteremia, infective endocarditis, and colon cancer rather than to perinatal vaginal colonization.","D":"Streptococcus pyogenes is also β-hemolytic but is bacitracin sensitive and PYR positive, and it is not the classic colonizer of the birth canal.","E":"Enterococcus faecalis is typically nonhemolytic, catalase negative, PYR positive, and able to grow in 6.5% NaCl; it is not β-hemolytic or hippurate positive."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus agalactiae',
    'A 36-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation has a routine prenatal visit. She is asymptomatic and has no vaginal symptoms. She had a previous infant who developed group B streptococcal sepsis at 2 weeks of age. The team is deciding how to implement recommended screening and prevention.',
    'Which of the following is the recommended approach to preventing neonatal group B streptococcal disease in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rifampin prophylaxis for all household contacts after delivery"},{"id":"B","text":"Screening by serum antibody titer and no antibiotic prophylaxis"},{"id":"C","text":"Vaginal and rectal swab culture at 35–37 weeks, with intrapartum penicillin or ampicillin if positive"},{"id":"D","text":"Administration of oral penicillin for the final 4 weeks of pregnancy"},{"id":"E","text":"Erythromycin eye ointment applied to the newborn at delivery"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Pregnant patients are screened at 35 to 37 weeks of gestation with rectal and vaginal swabs, and those with a positive culture receive intrapartum penicillin or ampicillin prophylaxis. This intrapartum approach targets vertical transmission during labor, which is when neonates acquire group B strep and develop pneumonia, meningitis, and sepsis.',
    '{"A":"Rifampin prophylaxis for close contacts is used for meningococcal and Haemophilus influenzae type b disease, and it plays no role in group B strep prevention.","B":"There is no antibody titer-based screening for group B strep; culture-based screening at 35 to 37 weeks is the standard of care.","D":"Oral penicillin late in pregnancy is not the recommended strategy; prophylaxis is given intrapartum because colonization can recur and because intrapartum exposure is the moment of transmission.","E":"Erythromycin eye ointment prevents gonococcal and chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum, a different mechanism for a different neonatal infection entirely."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus agalactiae',
    'A 5-day-old infant born at term after an uncomplicated vaginal delivery develops lethargy, poor feeding, temperature instability, and grunting. He has a bulging fontanelle and a seizure. Cerebrospinal fluid shows neutrophilic pleocytosis with elevated protein and low glucose. Culture grows gram-positive cocci in chains. The mother''s records show that she was not screened prenatally and had an intrapartum fever.',
    'Which of the following best explains this organism''s key virulence feature in neonates?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A polypeptide capsule of poly d-glutamate"},{"id":"B","text":"IgA protease that cleaves mucosal antibody"},{"id":"C","text":"A polysaccharide capsule that confers virulence"},{"id":"D","text":"Production of a superantigen that causes toxic shock"},{"id":"E","text":"Obligate intracellular growth with actin-based motility"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is group B streptococcal late-onset disease in a neonate, with meningitis in the setting of unscreened maternal colonization. The polysaccharide capsule confers virulence for S agalactiae by interfering with phagocytosis. Group B strep mainly causes pneumonia, meningitis, and sepsis in babies, which is why intrapartum prophylaxis is targeted to positive screens.',
    '{"A":"A poly d-glutamate polypeptide capsule is the antiphagocytic capsule of Bacillus anthracis, not of streptococci, whose capsules are polysaccharide.","B":"IgA protease is produced by Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria species, and Haemophilus influenzae to degrade mucosal antibody, but it is not described as the virulence basis of group B disease.","D":"Superantigen-mediated toxic shock is a feature of Staphylococcus aureus TSST-1 and of Streptococcus pyogenes TSS-like illness, and it produces shock rather than neonatal meningitis.","E":"Obligate intracellular growth with actin-based motility describes Listeria monocytogenes, another neonatal pathogen but one transmitted through contaminated dairy, cold deli meats, or the birth canal."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae',
    'A 58-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and a history of splenectomy after trauma presents with a 2-day history of fever, rigors, productive cough, and pleuritic chest pain. He is tachypneic and hypoxic. Chest radiograph shows lobar consolidation of the right lower lobe, and his sputum is described as rusty. Blood culture grows α-hemolytic lancet-shaped diplococci that are optochin sensitive and bile soluble.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Enterococcus faecium"},{"id":"B","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"id":"C","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"id":"D","text":"Viridans group streptococci"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Streptococcus pneumoniae is an α-hemolytic, lancet-shaped diplococcus that is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, and it produces rusty sputum with lobar pneumonia. Patients with functional or anatomic hyposplenia or asplenia are predisposed to overwhelming pneumococcal infection, and pneumococcal pneumonia is frequently severe in patients with chronic lung disease.',
    '{"A":"Enterococcus faecium is catalase negative and grows in 6.5% NaCl with bile; it causes UTI, biliary infection, and endocarditis, not community-acquired lobar pneumonia.","B":"Streptococcus pyogenes is β-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, and PYR positive; pneumonia is not its classic presentation, which is pharyngitis and skin infection.","D":"Viridans streptococci are also α-hemolytic but are optochin resistant and bile insoluble, and they cause endocarditis rather than acute lobar pneumonia.","E":"Staphylococcus aureus is a catalase-positive, coagulase-positive coccus in clusters, and it classically causes pneumonia after influenza infection rather than rusty-sputum lobar pneumonia."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae',
    'A 24-year-old man with sickle cell disease and functional asplenia presents with 8 hours of fever to 40°C, confusion, and neck stiffness. He is hemodynamically unstable and requires fluids. He has had no pneumococcal vaccination in the last decade. Cerebrospinal fluid shows neutrophilic pleocytosis, high protein, and low glucose, and Gram stain shows α-hemolytic lancet-shaped diplococci.',
    'Which of the following best explains why this patient is so susceptible to invasive infection with this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Loss of IgA protease production by respiratory mucosa"},{"id":"B","text":"Impaired T-cell help due to low CD4 count"},{"id":"C","text":"Deficiency of complement C5 to C9 membrane attack complex"},{"id":"D","text":"Impaired neutrophil oxidative burst from NADPH oxidase deficiency"},{"id":"E","text":"Absence of splenic macrophage clearance of encapsulated organisms"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Anatomic or functional hyposplenia or asplenia predisposes patients to pneumococcal infection because the spleen clears encapsulated organisms through opsonin-dependent macrophage phagocytosis. Pneumococcus is heavily encapsulated and invasive infection is prevented mainly by capsular antibody and splenic function, which is the rationale for pneumococcal vaccination in asplenic patients.',
    '{"A":"Pneumococcus itself makes IgA protease, so loss of mucosal IgA protease is not the mechanism; it is a virulence factor rather than a host deficiency.","B":"Low CD4 counts predispose to pneumococcal disease in advanced HIV, but the mechanism in asplenia is splenic clearance of encapsulated organisms, not impaired T-cell help.","C":"Terminal complement deficiency predisposes to recurrent Neisseria bacteremia and meningococcemia, not specifically to encapsulated pneumococcal disease.","D":"Defective NADPH oxidase causes chronic granulomatous disease, with catalase-positive organism infections such as S aureus and Aspergillus, and it is not the defect produced by splenectomy."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae',
    'A 3-year-old boy in daycare has had 4 episodes of acute otitis media in the past year, each with fever, ear pain, and a bulging erythematous tympanic membrane. He is otherwise healthy and has no immunodeficiency. The pediatrician discusses pneumococcal vaccination, noting the current regimens given in early childhood.',
    'Which of the following statements best describes the forms of pneumococcal vaccine available?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"No vaccine exists because of antigenic variation of pili"},{"id":"B","text":"Only a live attenuated oral formulation is available"},{"id":"C","text":"Only a polysaccharide vaccine is available, given after age 18"},{"id":"D","text":"A toxoid vaccine against the pneumolysin exotoxin is used"},{"id":"E","text":"Both conjugate formulations (PCV13, PCV15, PCV20) and a polysaccharide formulation (PPSV23) are available"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Pneumococcal vaccines are available in both conjugate (PCV13, PCV15, PCV20) and polysaccharide (PPSV23) formulations. The conjugate vaccines are used in young children and the polysaccharide vaccine in older or high-risk patients. Because pneumococcal disease is capsule dependent, all of these vaccines target capsular polysaccharide.',
    '{"A":"Absence of a vaccine because of pilus antigenic variation is the situation with Neisseria gonorrhoeae, whereas pneumococcal vaccines exist and target the capsule.","B":"There is no live attenuated oral pneumococcal vaccine; the vaccine-preventable organism with oral and intramuscular vaccine formulations is Salmonella typhi.","C":"A polysaccharide vaccine exists, but conjugate formulations are equally available and are the ones used in infants and toddlers.","D":"There is no pneumolysin toxoid vaccine in use; toxoid vaccines are designed against the toxins of diphtheria, tetanus, and pertussis."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae',
    'A 34-year-old woman presents with 4 days of maxillary facial pain, purulent nasal discharge, and fever. She has a history of recurrent sinusitis and one prior episode of pneumococcal meningitis. The microbiology laboratory is working up a respiratory isolate that is α-hemolytic and grows as lancet-shaped diplococci on blood agar.',
    'Which of the following laboratory characteristics is most consistent with this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Optochin sensitive and bile soluble"},{"id":"B","text":"Optochin resistant and bile insoluble"},{"id":"C","text":"Bacitracin sensitive with positive PYR"},{"id":"D","text":"Growth in 6.5% NaCl with esculin hydrolysis"},{"id":"E","text":"Novobiocin resistance with urease positivity"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Streptococcus pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, which distinguishes it from viridans streptococci, the other α-hemolytic group. It is encapsulated and lacks virulence without its capsule. Its classic clinical profile is MOPS: meningitis, otitis media, pneumonia, and sinusitis.',
    '{"B":"Optochin resistance with bile insolubility identifies viridans group streptococci, which live in the oropharynx and cause dental caries and endocarditis, and they would not be identified with these pneumococcal findings.","C":"Bacitracin sensitivity with PYR positivity identifies Streptococcus pyogenes, a β-hemolytic group A organism rather than an α-hemolytic diplococcus.","D":"Growth in 6.5% NaCl with esculin hydrolysis is characteristic of Enterococcus and group D organisms, which are typically nonhemolytic and catalase negative.","E":"Novobiocin resistance with urease positivity is the signature of Staphylococcus saprophyticus, a coagulase-negative coccus in clusters from the urinary tract."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pyogenes',
    'An 8-year-old girl develops sore throat and fever, and a throat swab grows β-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive cocci in chains. Two weeks later she returns with migratory polyarthritis of the knees and ankles, a new apical systolic murmur, and subcutaneous nodules over the elbows. Echocardiography shows vegetations on the mitral valve with valve leaflet thickening.',
    'Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of the cardiac findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptolysin O pores causing direct myocyte lysis"},{"id":"B","text":"Direct bacterial invasion of the valve by organisms from the pharynx"},{"id":"C","text":"Deposition of circulating immune complexes on the valve endothelium"},{"id":"D","text":"Erythrogenic exotoxin causing a delayed hypersensitivity reaction"},{"id":"E","text":"Antibodies to M protein cross-reacting with host myosin in the heart"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is acute rheumatic fever following group A streptococcal pharyngitis. M protein is structurally similar to host proteins such as myosin, so antibodies raised against it can cross-react with cardiac tissue, producing carditis, valvular damage, migratory polyarthritis, subcutaneous nodules, and chorea. The classic sequence is that pharyngitis caused by this organism can lead to rheumatic fever, and the immune reaction, not live bacteria, damages the valve.',
    '{"A":"Streptolysin O is a pore-forming hemolysin important for the ASO antibody response used in diagnosis, but it is not responsible for the immune cross-reactivity that damages heart valves.","B":"Direct invasion of the valve is the mechanism of infective endocarditis, typically caused by viridans streptococci, S aureus, or enterococci; acute rheumatic fever is a post-infectious immune process with sterile valves.","C":"Circulating immune complex deposition on endothelium is the mechanism of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, which follows skin infection or pharyngitis and presents with hematuria and hypertension rather than valvular disease.","D":"Erythrogenic exotoxin mediates scarlet fever through a toxin-driven rash, strawberry tongue, and circumoral pallor, and it is not the mechanism of rheumatic valvulitis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pyogenes',
    'A 6-year-old boy has a 2-day history of sore throat, fever to 38.9°C, and cervical lymphadenopathy. Examination shows a white-yellow tonsillar exudate. On the second day a fine, blanching, sandpaperlike rash appears on his chest and spreads to the extremities, sparing the palms and soles. His tongue is bright red with prominent papillae, and there is pallor around the mouth.',
    'Which of the following virulence factors most directly produces the rash in this illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptokinase"},{"id":"B","text":"Hyaluronic acid capsule"},{"id":"C","text":"Erythrogenic exotoxin"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptolysin O"},{"id":"E","text":"M protein"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is scarlet fever, in which group A streptococcal pharyngitis plus erythrogenic toxin produces a fine, blanching, sandpaperlike rash that spares the palms and soles, along with a strawberry tongue and circumoral pallor. Erythrogenic exotoxin is the toxin that drives these findings, and treating the underlying pharyngitis resolves them.',
    '{"A":"Streptokinase activates plasminogen to plasmin to dissolve fibrin and aid tissue spread; it contributes to invasion rather than to the rash.","B":"The hyaluronic acid capsule inhibits phagocytosis and is a marker of invasive potential, not the mediator of the cutaneous eruption.","D":"Streptolysin O is a pore-forming hemolysin used diagnostically through the anti-streptolysin O titer, but the titer marks exposure rather than causing the rash.","E":"M protein is the major antiphagocytic surface protein whose cross-reactivity with myosin underlies rheumatic fever; it does not directly produce the exanthem."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pyogenes',
    'A 44-year-old man with a small scrape on his shin develops severe, increasing leg pain out of proportion to exam findings over 12 hours, then dusky skin, bullae, systemic toxicity, and hypotension. Surgical exploration shows necrosis extending along fascial planes. Tissue Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and the isolate is β-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, and PYR positive.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"B","text":"Clostridium septicum"},{"id":"C","text":"Vibrio vulnificus"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"id":"E","text":"Clostridium perfringens"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is necrotizing fasciitis caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, a toxigenic manifestation of group A streptococcal disease. The bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive β-hemolytic coccus in chains identifies group A strep, which also causes a toxic shock-like syndrome associated with a painful skin infection, in contrast to the tampon-associated presentation of staphylococcal toxic shock.',
    '{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus produces coagulase-positive cocci in clusters, and its toxic shock presentation is associated with tampon use or nasal packing rather than a painful necrotizing skin infection.","B":"Clostridium septicum causes spontaneous gas gangrene through hematogenous seeding in patients with colonic malignancy, which does not fit this post-traumatic presentation.","C":"Vibrio vulnificus is a gram-negative marine bacillus that causes wound infection after seawater exposure in patients with high serum iron, and it would appear as gram-negative rods.","E":"Clostridium perfringens is a gram-positive rod whose α-toxin (lecithinase) causes myonecrosis with crepitus; the gram-positive cocci in chains here do not fit a clostridial species."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pyogenes',
    'A 7-year-old girl in a summer camp develops a cluster of small, golden-yellow, crusted lesions around her nose and lips. There is no fever and no systemic illness. The lesions enlarge slowly and some coalesce. Culture of the crust grows a β-hemolytic organism that is bacitracin sensitive.',
    'Which of the following terms best describes the characteristic appearance of these lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Diffuse petechial eruption of the distal extremities"},{"id":"B","text":"Violaceous, dusky plaques with an eschar"},{"id":"C","text":"Honey-crusted lesions"},{"id":"D","text":"Flaccid bullae with superficial peeling"},{"id":"E","text":"Erythematous flush with a well-demarcated raised border"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'This is impetigo from Streptococcus pyogenes, characterized by honey-crusted lesions. Impetigo is a pyogenic group A streptococcal skin manifestation, and strains causing impetigo can later induce acute glomerulonephritis. Erysipelas, a related pyogenic group A strep skin infection, is what produces a raised, well-demarcated border.',
    '{"A":"A diffuse petechial eruption of the distal extremities suggests meningococcemia, which carries petechiae and gangrene of the toes rather than crusted perioral lesions.","B":"Dusky plaques with an eschar suggest ecthyma gangrenosum from Pseudomonas bacteremia or a necrotic process such as anthrax eschar, not typical honey-crusted impetigo.","D":"Flaccid bullae with superficial peeling describe staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, mediated by exfoliative toxin rather than by this crusted lesion.","E":"A raised, well-demarcated erythematous border is the description of erysipelas, which is a deeper group A streptococcal dermal infection and is often associated with lymphatic involvement."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Streptococcus pyogenes',
    'A 10-year-old boy is brought to the clinic in January with sore throat, fever, and enlarged tender anterior cervical nodes. The clinician plans same-visit testing to decide whether antibiotics are warranted. The family asks how quickly a result can be obtained, and the office has both a rapid in-office kit and standard culture available.',
    'Which of the following best describes the diagnostic approach to streptococcal pharyngitis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Culture on blood agar with results in 48 hours, or a rapid antigen detection assay with in-office results"},{"id":"B","text":"Growth on bile esculin agar with hydrolysis of esculin"},{"id":"C","text":"NAAT of urine for capsular polysaccharide antigen"},{"id":"D","text":"Bile solubility and optochin sensitivity testing on a sputum sample"},{"id":"E","text":"Serum anti-streptolysin O titer measured at the time of acute illness"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Streptococcal pharyngitis is diagnosed from a throat swab using an antigen detection assay, which gives rapid in-office results, or by culture on blood agar, which takes about 48 hours. Both approaches identify the β-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive group A organism, and prompt diagnosis supports treatment that prevents rheumatic fever.',
    '{"B":"Bile esculin hydrolysis is a test for group D organisms such as Enterococcus and S gallolyticus; it does not identify group A strep.","C":"Urine antigen testing is classically used for Legionella pneumophila and pneumococcus in pneumonia; group A pharyngitis is diagnosed from the throat, not the urine.","D":"Bile solubility and optochin sensitivity identify Streptococcus pneumoniae, an α-hemolytic diplococcus, and sputum is not the specimen for pharyngitis diagnosis.","E":"The anti-streptolysin O titer is a marker of recent infection and rises later in the course; it is used for complications such as rheumatic fever, not for acute diagnosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Systemic dimorphic mycoses overview',
    'A 40-year-old man from the Ohio River Valley presents with fever, cough, and weight loss. A lung biopsy shows granulomas, and his physician considers a systemic dimorphic fungal infection rather than tuberculosis.',
    'Which of the following properties best explains why the systemic dimorphic fungi are considered dimorphic?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They grow as a mold at 20°C and as a yeast at 37°C"},{"id":"B","text":"They are strict molds in both the environment and human tissue"},{"id":"C","text":"They grow as hyphae in soil and as spherules in tissue at every temperature"},{"id":"D","text":"They grow as a yeast at 20°C and as a mold at 37°C"},{"id":"E","text":"They are strict yeasts that form a capsule only at body temperature"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Dimorphism refers to temperature-dependent growth: at the cooler ambient temperature of 20°C these fungi grow as molds, and at the warmer 37°C of human tissue they convert to yeasts. Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides, Paracoccidioides, and Sporothrix all follow this cold = mold, heat = yeast rule. Coccidioides is the classic exception, becoming a spherule rather than a yeast in tissue. Knowing the rule explains why the organism in soil or culture looks like a mold, while the organism seen on tissue histology is a yeast form.',
    '{"B":"Numerous important fungi are molds only in the environment; the systemic fungi convert to yeast at body temperature, so a strict mold would describe non-dimorphic environmental fungi rather than these organisms.","C":"Spherules with endospores are characteristic of Coccidioides in tissue specifically; the other dimorphic fungi are yeasts at 37°C, and only Coccidioides uses a spherule as its tissue phase.","D":"This reverses the rule. The cooler environmental temperature produces the mold form, and the warmer host temperature produces the yeast form, not the other way around.","E":"A thick capsule and narrow-based budding describe Cryptococcus neoformans, which is not dimorphic and grows as a yeast at both temperatures."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Mycology', 'Systemic dimorphic mycoses overview',
    'A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with disseminated histoplasmosis involving the lungs and liver. His wife asks whether she needs separate screening and whether he should be isolated from their children. He has no other diagnosed immune deficiency.',
    'Which of the following statements best describes the transmissibility and tissue response of systemic mycoses such as his?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"They cause diffuse necrosis without granuloma formation because they lack cell walls"},{"id":"B","text":"They are transmitted by tick bite, similar to rickettsial diseases"},{"id":"C","text":"They are acquired by direct skin inoculation, which is the only route of infection"},{"id":"D","text":"They form granulomas but cannot be transmitted from person to person"},{"id":"E","text":"They are spread person-to-person by respiratory droplets and require airborne isolation"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Systemic mycoses such as histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, and coccidioidomycosis produce granulomatous inflammation that histologically resembles tuberculosis. However, unlike tuberculosis, they are not transmitted from person to person; they are acquired from the environment by inhalation of spores or arthroconidia. This is why a patient with disseminated disease does not need respiratory isolation and does not pose a risk to household contacts. This parallel to tuberculosis, with a key difference in transmissibility, is exactly what First Aid emphasizes.',
    '{"A":"Systemic fungi have chitin-containing cell walls and typically do produce granulomas, so this option wrongly excludes granulomatous inflammation, which is a key mimic of tuberculosis.","B":"Tick-borne transmission applies to rickettsial infections and Borrelia, while the systemic mycoses are acquired by inhaling spores from contaminated soil rather than by vector bite.","C":"Direct cutaneous inoculation is the route for Sporothrix schenckii (rose gardener''s disease); the major systemic mycoses are inhaled rather than inoculated into the skin.","E":"Respiratory droplet spread with a need for airborne isolation describes Mycobacterium tuberculosis, not the systemic dimorphic fungi, which are acquired from environmental sources such as soil and dust."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Food-borne and water-borne pathogens',
    'A 28-year-old man develops nausea, vomiting, and crampy abdominal pain 3 hours after a church picnic where he ate potato salad that had sat unrefrigerated in the sun for several hours. He has no fever and no diarrhea. His symptoms resolve completely within 6 hours without treatment. Stool cultures are negative, but the public health team suspects a preformed toxin in the mayonnaise-based food.',
    'Which organism most likely produced the toxin responsible for this illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Listeria monocytogenes"},{"id":"B","text":"Salmonella species"},{"id":"C","text":"Clostridium perfringens"},{"id":"D","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"E","text":"Vibrio parahaemolyticus"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'This is classic Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning: a preformed exotoxin in mayonnaise-containing food produces vomiting and cramps within a few hours, and the illness resolves quickly on its own. First Aid''s rule is that S aureus and B cereus food poisoning start quickly and end quickly because they are exotoxin-mediated rather than requiring infection and replication. Meats, mayonnaise, and custard are the classic S aureus vehicles.',
    '{"A":"Listeria comes from deli meats and soft cheeses and causes invasive disease in pregnant, elderly, and immunocompromised patients rather than self-limited explosive vomiting.","B":"Salmonella comes from poultry, meat, and eggs and produces an inflammatory diarrheal illness with fever over days, not a 3-hour toxin syndrome.","C":"C perfringens is linked to reheated meat and causes a later-onset diarrheal illness, not an explosive vomiting illness a few hours after mayonnaise-based food.","E":"V parahaemolyticus comes from raw or undercooked seafood, not from a mayonnaise potato salad, and typically causes diarrheal illness."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Food-borne and water-borne pathogens',
    'A 4-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department with 2 days of bloody diarrhea and severe abdominal cramps after a family barbecue. She develops pallor, oliguria, and petechiae. Laboratory studies show anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury with schistocytes on the peripheral smear. Stool culture grows a lactose-negative, sorbitol-negative E coli.',
    'Which of the following best explains this patient''s renal failure?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Shiga toxin-mediated endothelial injury causing microangiopathy"},{"id":"B","text":"Preformed exotoxin in undercooked meat causing vomiting within 2 hours of ingestion"},{"id":"C","text":"Production of a toxin that ADP-ribosylates Gs and increases intestinal cAMP"},{"id":"D","text":"Invasion of the colonic mucosa with subsequent bacteremia and renal abscess formation"},{"id":"E","text":"Heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins causing secretory diarrhea without mucosal invasion"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is hemolytic-uremic syndrome caused by enterohemorrhagic E coli O157:H7, which is acquired from undercooked meat and produces Shiga toxin. Shiga toxin damages endothelium, producing the triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. First Aid lists EHEC O157:H7 under the bloody diarrhea causes as the Shiga toxin producer that can cause HUS, and the organism is a lactose-negative, sorbitol-negative E coli.',
    '{"B":"A preformed exotoxin with vomiting within a couple hours describes S aureus or B cereus food poisoning, which are self-limited and do not cause renal failure.","C":"ADP-ribosylation of Gs with increased cAMP describes cholera toxin from V cholerae, which produces profuse rice-water diarrhea, not bloody diarrhea with HUS.","D":"Invasion of colonic mucosa describes enteroinvasive E coli, which causes a dysentery-like illness without Shiga toxin-mediated HUS.","E":"The LT and ST enterotoxins describe enterotoxigenic E coli, the cause of travelers'' diarrhea, which is watery and nonbloody and does not cause HUS."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Food-borne and water-borne pathogens',
    'A 34-year-old woman presents in the third trimester of pregnancy with fever, myalgias, and a blood culture that grows a gram-positive rod. She reports eating soft queso fresco and deli turkey throughout pregnancy. The fetus shows decreased movement, and an ultrasound reveals hydrops. In addition to the maternal illness, the neonatology team is concerned about transmission of this organism to the newborn.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely organism in this case?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Clostridium botulinum and raw honey spores"},{"id":"B","text":"Salmonella and undercooked poultry or eggs"},{"id":"C","text":"Vibrio vulnificus and shellfish wounds"},{"id":"D","text":"Listeria monocytogenes from soft cheese"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus and preformed toxin"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Listeria monocytogenes from soft cheese is acquired from deli meats and soft cheeses, is a gram-positive rod, and has a special predilection for pregnant patients, in whom it can cause bacteremia and fetal infection or loss. First Aid lists Listeria among the organisms causing food-borne illness and also among the causes of neonatal meningitis along with group B streptococci and E coli. Pregnancy-associated invasion is a signature association for this organism.',
    '{"A":"V vulnificus causes wound infection and sepsis after contact with contaminated water or shellfish, typically in patients with liver disease.","B":"C botulinum comes from improperly canned foods or raw honey spores and causes symmetric descending flaccid paralysis, not a febrile bacteremia in pregnancy.","C":"Salmonella comes from poultry, meat, and eggs and causes a diarrheal illness with fever; it is not the characteristic soft-cheese organism of pregnancy.","E":"S aureus food poisoning is a preformed toxin illness with rapid vomiting and no bacteremia, and it is not associated with soft cheese in pregnancy."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Food-borne and water-borne pathogens',
    'A 52-year-old man with cirrhosis develops fever, bullous skin lesions on both legs, and septic shock 1 day after wading in the Gulf of Mexico and cleaning crabs with a cut on his hand. Blood cultures grow a curved gram-negative rod that is oxidase positive and requires salt for growth. He has no diarrhea.',
    'Which organism best fits this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Vibrio cholerae"},{"id":"B","text":"Vibrio parahaemolyticus"},{"id":"C","text":"Aeromonas hydrophila"},{"id":"D","text":"Vibrio vulnificus"},{"id":"E","text":"Campylobacter jejuni"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Vibrio vulnificus predominantly causes wound infections after contact with contaminated water or shellfish and can produce rapidly progressive bullous cellulitis and fulminant sepsis in patients with chronic liver disease. First Aid lists V parahaemolyticus and V vulnificus together as raw or undercooked seafood organisms but specifically notes that V vulnificus predominantly causes wound infections from contact with contaminated water or shellfish. Cirrhosis is the classic host risk factor for severe disease.',
    '{"A":"V cholerae produces the classic rice-water diarrhea from an enterotoxin that raises cAMP; it does not typically cause bullous wound infection with sepsis.","B":"V parahaemolyticus is associated with diarrheal gastroenteritis after raw or undercooked seafood rather than rapidly progressive wound infection in cirrhosis.","C":"Aeromonas is a freshwater gram-negative rod that can cause wound infection, but the marine shellfish exposure, bullous lesions, and cirrhosis point to Vibrio vulnificus.","E":"Campylobacter jejuni causes bloody diarrhea from poultry and pets and is a curved organism, but it is not associated with saltwater wounds or cirrhosis-related sepsis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Food-borne and water-borne pathogens',
    'A 30-year-old medical student returns from a 2-week trip with 4 days of profuse watery diarrhea. She has no fever, no blood in the stool, and no leukocytes on stool smear. She drank tap water and ate street food. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped organism, and the laboratory notes that the organism produces toxins that increase intestinal cyclic AMP and cyclic GMP.',
    'Which of the following organisms produces the heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins described?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Entamoeba histolytica"},{"id":"B","text":"Clostridioides difficile"},{"id":"C","text":"Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli"},{"id":"D","text":"Enterohemorrhagic E coli O157:H7"},{"id":"E","text":"Shigella species"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Enterotoxigenic E coli is the classic cause of travelers'' diarrhea and produces a heat-labile toxin (LT) and a heat-stable toxin (ST) that drive secretory, noninflammatory watery diarrhea without fever or blood. First Aid lists ETEC under watery diarrhea with exactly this toxin description. The absence of fecal leukocytes and of systemic symptoms fits a purely enterotoxin-mediated process.',
    '{"A":"E histolytica is a protozoan that causes amebic dysentery and liver abscess, not a toxigenic watery diarrhea of travelers.","B":"C difficile causes pseudomembranous colitis with watery diarrhea in patients exposed to antibiotics or proton pump inhibitors, not classic travelers'' diarrhea.","D":"EHEC O157:H7 is a bloody diarrhea pathogen that produces Shiga toxin and can cause HUS; it does not produce the LT and ST enterotoxins.","E":"Shigella is a lactose-negative organism with a very low infectious dose that invades colonic mucosa and causes bacillary dysentery with blood and pus, not toxin-only watery diarrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Hospital-acquired and device-related infections',
    'A 58-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia has had a tunneled central venous catheter in place for 5 weeks. She develops fever and chills with each catheter flush, and the skin over the catheter exit site is erythematous and indurated. Blood cultures drawn from the catheter lumen grow a coagulase-negative, novobiocin-sensitive gram-positive coccus in clusters. Blood cultures from a peripheral vein are negative.',
    'Which organism is most likely responsible for this catheter-related infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"id":"B","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},{"id":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"id":"D","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Staphylococcus epidermidis is the dominant skin commensal and the classic organism forming biofilms on long-term intravascular catheters, producing a coagulase-negative staphylococcal bacteremia that is often culture-positive from the line and negative peripherally. The healthcare-associated infection table lists S aureus and S epidermidis as the organisms of intravascular catheters, with S epidermidis favored for long-term devices. Vancomycin covers this organism because most are methicillin resistant.',
    '{"B":"P aeruginosa is associated with mechanical ventilation and burn wounds, producing sweet-smelling green sputum, not long-term catheter biofilm infection typically.","C":"E faecalis is associated with indwelling lines and urinary catheters as a healthcare-associated, often drug-resistant organism, but the culture here is a catalase-positive, coagulase-negative coccus in clusters.","D":"S saprophyticus is a novobiocin-resistant cause of UTI in young sexually active women, not a catheter bacteremia organism.","E":"S aureus is the other catheter-associated organism and would be a strong candidate, but S epidermidis is specifically favored in long-term catheters, and S aureus is coagulase positive."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Hospital-acquired and device-related infections',
    'A 43-year-old man has been mechanically ventilated for 10 days in the intensive care unit after a motor vehicle collision. He develops fever, copious purulent sputum, and a new right lower lobe infiltrate on chest radiograph. Gram stain of endotracheal aspirate shows gram-negative rods, and the culture has a fruity odor with a blue-green pigment on agar.',
    'Which organism is most likely responsible for this late-onset ventilator-associated pneumonia?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},{"id":"B","text":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},{"id":"C","text":"Moraxella catarrhalis"},{"id":"D","text":"Haemophilus influenzae"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Late-onset ventilator-associated pneumonia is classically caused by P aeruginosa, Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, or S aureus, and Pseudomonas is identified by its sweet or fruity odor and blue-green pigment. First Aid''s healthcare-associated table lists mechanical ventilation and endotracheal intubation with late-onset P aeruginosa as a leading pathogen, and notes increased sputum production and the sweet odor. Gram-negative rods with a blue-green pigment are the signature.',
    '{"B":"M pneumoniae is an atypical organism that cannot be seen on Gram stain and causes community-acquired \"walking pneumonia\" in young adults.","C":"M catarrhalis is a gram-negative diplococcus associated with COPD exacerbations and otitis media, not late ventilator-associated pneumonia.","D":"H influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus associated with COPD exacerbations and epiglottitis, without a characteristic blue-green pigment.","E":"S pneumoniae is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia and would not explain a blue-green pigmented gram-negative rod."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Hospital-acquired and device-related infections',
    'Two days after admission for an exacerbation of heart failure, a 73-year-old woman develops profuse watery diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a white blood cell count of 19,000 per microliter. She received intravenous ceftriaxone on admission and takes a proton pump inhibitor at home. Flexible sigmoidoscopy shows yellow-white plaques on the colonic mucosa, and stool testing is positive for a toxin-producing anaerobe.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely offending organism, and which risk factors in the history support it?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Enterotoxigenic E coli, supported by recent foreign travel"},{"id":"B","text":"C difficile, with antibiotic and PPI exposure"},{"id":"C","text":"Norovirus, supported by a community outbreak of vomiting"},{"id":"D","text":"Vibrio cholerae, supported by raw seafood ingestion"},{"id":"E","text":"Clostridium perfringens, supported by reheated meat consumption"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is pseudomembranous colitis from Clostridioides difficile, a healthcare-associated infection whose risk factors in First Aid are antibiotic use and proton pump inhibitors. It produces watery diarrhea with leukocytosis and, occasionally, bloody diarrhea, and pseudomembranes are seen on endoscopy. Treatment is oral vancomycin, which is bacteriostatic against C difficile and is not absorbed, so it acts in the lumen.',
    '{"A":"ETEC causes travelers'' diarrhea via LT and ST enterotoxins and is acquired abroad, without pseudomembranes or association with antibiotic use.","C":"Norovirus is the most common cause of gastroenteritis outbreaks in developed countries but does not cause pseudomembranous colitis.","D":"V cholerae produces rice-water diarrhea after contaminated seafood or water, not lesions on sigmoidoscopy or a link to antibiotics.","E":"C perfringens causes food poisoning from reheated meat and gas gangrene; it does not produce antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Hospital-acquired and device-related infections',
    'A 66-year-old man with a spinal cord injury has had an indwelling urinary catheter for 3 weeks. He develops fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness. Urinalysis shows many leukocytes and leukocyte esterase, and urine culture grows an organism that is highly motile and swarms across the agar plate. Imaging reveals a large stone in the renal pelvis.',
    'Which of the following best explains the formation of this stone?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Production of a red pigment by Serratia marcescens causes pigment stone formation"},{"id":"B","text":"Capsular polysaccharide from Klebsiella acts as a nidus for cholesterol stones"},{"id":"C","text":"Urease from Proteus raises urine pH, forming struvite stones"},{"id":"D","text":"Blue-green pigment from Pseudomonas aeruginosa nucleates uric acid crystals"},{"id":"E","text":"Lactose fermentation by E coli acidifies urine and precipitates calcium oxalate"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Proteus mirabilis is one of the classic organisms of catheter-associated UTI (the PEcK group with E coli and Klebsiella), and it produces urease, which splits urea to ammonia and raises urine pH, promoting struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stone formation. First Aid notes both the swarming motility on agar and the association with struvite stones. The catheter is the device-related risk factor that permits these organisms to ascend.',
    '{"A":"Serratia marcescens may produce a red pigment and is often healthcare-associated and drug resistant, but it is not associated with struvite stones.","B":"Klebsiella is a catheter-associated UTI organism with a large mucoid capsule and viscous colonies, but struvite stone formation is attributed to urease-producing Proteus.","D":"Pseudomonas is associated with blue-green pigment and healthcare-associated infection but not with urease-driven struvite stones.","E":"E coli is the leading cause of UTI, but it is not a urease producer and is not the classic struvite stone organism; it ferments lactose on MacConkey agar."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Normal microbiota by body site',
    'A 41-year-old woman is admitted with fever, hypotension, and abdominal pain 3 days after a sigmoid colectomy for diverticular disease. She received perioperative cefazolin. Blood cultures grow a gram-negative anaerobe, and the surgical team notes that the perforation involved the distal colon. The infectious disease consultant explains that the organism is the dominant anaerobe of the colonic microbiota and therefore a predictable source of intra-abdominal infection after colonic perforation.',
    'Which organism is the dominant member of the colonic microbiota in this context?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Lactobacillus species"},{"id":"B","text":"Streptococcus mutans"},{"id":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"id":"D","text":"Bacteroides fragilis"},{"id":"E","text":"Escherichia coli"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'In the colon, Bacteroides fragilis outnumbers Escherichia coli as the dominant microbiota member (B fragilis > E coli). Because colonic perforation spills this resident flora, B fragilis is a predictable pathogen in intra-abdominal abscess and post-surgical anaerobic infection, which is why anaerobic coverage such as metronidazole is used. The microbiota table pairs each body site with its dominant organism, and the colon is the site where B fragilis dominates.',
    '{"A":"Lactobacillus is the dominant organism of the vagina, where it keeps the pH acidic, not of the colon.","B":"S mutans dominates dental plaque and drives caries, not the colonic lumen.","C":"S epidermidis is the dominant skin commensal and a classic catheter biofilm organism, not a colonic anaerobe.","E":"E coli is present in the colon but is not the dominant member; the normal microbiota table lists B fragilis ahead of E coli. E coli is the leading cause of UTI, not the dominant colonic anaerobe."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Normal microbiota by body site',
    'A 19-year-old woman presents with vulvar pruritus and a thick, white, cottage cheese-like discharge. Wet mount shows pseudohyphae, and vaginal pH is 4.2. She recently completed a course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for sinusitis. Her gynecologist notes that the acidic vaginal environment is normally maintained by the dominant resident organism, and that antibiotic disruption of that flora predisposed her to this infection.',
    'Which organism is the dominant member of the normal vaginal microbiota?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Group B streptococci"},{"id":"B","text":"Bacteroides fragilis"},{"id":"C","text":"Lactobacillus species"},{"id":"D","text":"Viridans group streptococci"},{"id":"E","text":"Candida albicans"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Lactobacillus is the dominant organism of the normal vagina, and it maintains the acidic pH that suppresses overgrowth of yeast and other organisms. The vaginal microbiota is also commonly colonized by E coli and group B streptococci, which matter for ascending UTI and for neonatal transmission respectively. Antibiotic suppression of lactobacilli removes that colonization resistance and permits Candida overgrowth, exactly the sequence in this patient.',
    '{"A":"Group B streptococci colonize the vagina but are not dominant; their significance is neonatal meningitis and pneumonia after vertical transmission.","B":"B fragilis is the dominant colonic anaerobe, not a normal vaginal dominant organism; its role is intra-abdominal infection after colonic perforation.","D":"Viridans group streptococci are the dominant organisms of the oropharynx, where they also cause dental plaque and subacute endocarditis.","E":"Candida is the pathogen causing this patient''s vulvovaginitis, not the normal dominant vaginal organism that keeps her asymptomatic."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Normal microbiota by body site',
    'A 6-year-old boy has multiple cavities in his primary molars. His dentist explains that the bacteria most responsible for dental caries adhere to the tooth surface, ferment dietary sucrose, and produce acid that demineralizes enamel. The same organisms can enter the bloodstream after dental extraction and cause subacute infective endocarditis in children with unrepaired congenital heart lesions.',
    'Which organism is the dominant microbiota member of dental plaque and the principal driver of caries?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"id":"B","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"id":"C","text":"Streptococcus mutans"},{"id":"D","text":"Bacteroides fragilis"},{"id":"E","text":"Neisseria meningitidis"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Dental plaque is dominated by Streptococcus mutans, which produces glucans from sucrose via glucosyltransferases, adheres to enamel, and generates lactic acid that demineralizes tooth structure. The microbiota table explicitly lists S mutans as the dominant organism of dental plaque. Viridans group streptococci broadly dominate the oropharynx and are the classic cause of subacute endocarditis after dental procedures.',
    '{"A":"S epidermidis dominates the skin and is the classic long-term intravascular catheter biofilm organism, not a caries pathogen.","B":"S aureus colonizes the nose and is a leading cause of wound infection and bacteremia; it is not the dominant plaque organism.","D":"B fragilis dominates the colon and causes anaerobic intra-abdominal infection, and it does not colonize tooth surfaces.","E":"N meningitidis colonizes the nasopharynx and causes meningitis and meningococcemia, not dental caries."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Prion diseases',
    'A 63-year-old man is brought to clinic by his wife because of 4 months of rapidly progressive memory loss, personality change, and unsteady gait. His wife reports sudden jerking movements of his arms that occur mostly when he is startled. He worked for many years as a shepherd and ate traditional meat products. MRI shows cortical ribboning, and CSF is positive for 14-3-3 protein. He dies 5 months after symptom onset.',
    'Which of the following best describes the molecular event responsible for this patient''s disease?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Alpha-helical prion protein converts to a beta-pleated, protease-resistant form"},{"id":"B","text":"Prion protein gene mutation causing complete absence of the normal cellular prion protein"},{"id":"C","text":"Expansion of a CAG trinucleotide repeat within the huntingtin gene"},{"id":"D","text":"Accumulation of amyloid-beta peptide derived from cleavage of the amyloid precursor protein"},{"id":"E","text":"Hyperphosphorylation of tau protein leading to intracellular neurofibrillary tangles"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'This is sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, the classic prion disease: rapidly progressive dementia with ataxia and startle myoclonus, with death usually within months to a year. Prion disease results from conversion of the normal predominantly alpha-helical prion protein (PrPc) into a beta-pleated form (PrPsc); PrPsc resists protease degradation and templates the conversion of more PrPc, producing spongiform encephalopathy. Accumulated PrPsc is the transmissible agent, which is why CNS tissue and BSE-contaminated food can transmit disease.',
    '{"B":"Prion disease reflects gain of a toxic, transmissible PrPsc conformation, not loss of PrPc; the normal protein is converted, not deleted, and the disease is transmissible.","C":"CAG repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene causes Huntington disease, which presents with chorea, psychiatric change, and autosomal dominant inheritance, not a rapidly progressive dementia with myoclonus.","D":"Amyloid-beta accumulation with plaques is the hallmark of Alzheimer disease, which evolves over years with an insidious amnestic onset rather than the subacute course and startle myoclonus seen here.","E":"Tau hyperphosphorylation and neurofibrillary tangles are seen in Alzheimer disease and other tauopathies, not in prion disease, which is defined by PrPsc deposition and spongiform change."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Prion diseases',
    'A 22-year-old man in a remote highland community develops progressive ataxia, tremors, and emotional lability over several months, followed by dementia and death within a year. Epidemiological study of his community finds that the disease occurs almost exclusively among members of a group that, until recently, practiced ritual endocannibalism as part of mourning ceremonies.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Kuru"},{"id":"B","text":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"},{"id":"C","text":"Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"},{"id":"D","text":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy"},{"id":"E","text":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Kuru is the acquired prion disease described in tribal populations that practiced human cannibalism; CNS-related tissue is the vehicle of transmission, and the illness is a cerebellar-predominant spongiform encephalopathy with ataxia, tremor, and progressive dementia. It is one of the classic three prion diseases in First Aid alongside sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease and bovine spongiform encephalopathy.',
    '{"B":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is caused by JC polyomavirus reactivation in immunosuppressed patients and produces focal white matter demyelination rather than a transmissible spongiform process.","C":"Variant CJD is acquired from food contaminated by BSE-infected animal products, so it is linked to beef consumption and mad cow disease, not to ritual cannibalism.","D":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy is the prion disease of cattle, also called mad cow disease; it is the animal source that can transmit variant CJD to humans rather than a human illness.","E":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis follows measles infection years earlier in childhood and shows myoclonic jerks with EEG periodic complexes, not a dietary-ritual epidemiological pattern."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Prion diseases',
    'A hospital sterilizing services unit is revising its protocols after a patient with confirmed Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease underwent a brain biopsy. The surgical instruments were processed with the standard autoclave cycle used for all other surgical trays. The infection control committee asks whether that cycle was adequate and what distinguishes the prion agent from conventional pathogens.',
    'Which of the following statements about the prion agent is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A protein conformation that resists standard autoclaving"},{"id":"B","text":"It is a bacterium that lacks a cell wall and therefore resists beta-lactam antibiotics"},{"id":"C","text":"It is a self-replicating nucleic acid that is destroyed by protease digestion"},{"id":"D","text":"It is a fungus that is killed by ethylene oxide but survives pressurized steam"},{"id":"E","text":"It is an RNA virus that is inactivated by standard autoclaving but not by alcohols"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'PrPsc is a misfolded protein conformation, and it resists protease degradation and standard sterilizing procedures including standard autoclaving, which is why prion-contaminated instruments require special decontamination protocols. Its resistance to proteases is itself part of the diagnostic concept: PrPsc survives proteinase K digestion while normal PrPc does not. The transmissible agent is the abnormal protein itself, not a nucleic acid genome.',
    '{"B":"Prions are not bacteria; the absence of a cell wall describes Mycoplasma and does not explain protease resistance or autoclave resistance.","C":"No self-replicating nucleic acid has been identified in prions; the infectious entity is protease-resistant protein, so protease digestion does not destroy infectivity.","D":"Prions are not fungi. Fungi are killed by pressurized steam, whereas the prion protein is the entity that survives standard autoclaving.","E":"Prions are not viruses and contain no RNA genome; standard autoclaving is exactly the procedure that may fail to reliably inactivate them."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Sexually transmitted infections',
    'A 24-year-old man presents with a single painful genital ulcer with a yellow-gray exudate and tender, fluctuant inguinal lymph nodes that have begun to drain. He is uncircumcised and reports a new partner 1 week ago. Dark-field microscopy of the ulcer exudate is negative for spirochetes, and PCR for herpes simplex virus is negative.',
    'Which organism is most likely responsible for this lesion?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Klebsiella granulomatis"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1-L3"},{"id":"C","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae"},{"id":"D","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"id":"E","text":"Haemophilus ducreyi"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid: a painful genital ulcer with exudate and tender inguinal adenopathy that may suppurate. The First Aid mnemonic is that chancroid is so painful you "do cry" (ducreyi). The paired features of a painful ulcer plus tender, draining nodes distinguish it from the painless chancre of syphilis and from the painless ulcer with painful buboes of lymphogranuloma venereum.',
    '{"A":"K granulomatis causes granuloma inguinale, a painless beefy red ulcer that bleeds easily on contact, with Donovan bodies on microscopy.","B":"C trachomatis L1-L3 causes lymphogranuloma venereum with painless genital ulcers and painful buboes, so the ulcer itself should not be painful with exudate.","C":"N gonorrhoeae causes urethritis and purulent discharge rather than an exudative ulcer with inguinal adenopathy.","D":"T pallidum causes a painless chancre with rubbery, nontender regional lymphadenopathy; a painful exudative ulcer with suppurating nodes is not primary syphilis."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Sexually transmitted infections',
    'A 21-year-old woman presents with 2 days of fever, severe pelvic pain, and purulent cervical discharge. She has cervical motion tenderness and adnexal tenderness on bimanual examination. Nucleic acid amplification testing returns positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. The clinician explains that this organism is the most common bacterial sexually transmitted infection in the United States and often presents subacutely, and that treatment must cover both chlamydia and gonorrhea.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for the chlamydial component of this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"id":"B","text":"Doxycycline"},{"id":"C","text":"Metronidazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Acyclovir"},{"id":"E","text":"Benzathine penicillin G"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Tetracyclines are the agents of choice for chlamydial infection: doxycycline is first line for chlamydial urethritis and cervicitis, and it is also an alternative when a longer course is needed. Tetracyclines accumulate intracellularly and are therefore highly effective against Chlamydia and Rickettsia. PID requires coverage of both pathogens, so ceftriaxone is added for gonorrhea while doxycycline covers C trachomatis.',
    '{"A":"Ceftriaxone is the treatment for gonorrhea, including disseminated infection, but a beta-lactam is not the primary agent for the intracellular chlamydial organism.","C":"Metronidazole covers anaerobes and Trichomonas and is not the drug for Chlamydia.","D":"Acyclovir treats herpes simplex virus genital infection and has no activity against chlamydia.","E":"Benzathine penicillin G is the treatment for syphilis, given to cover T pallidum at each stage."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Sexually transmitted infections',
    'A 31-year-old man presents with multiple painless, beefy red ulcers on the genitals that bleed readily when examined. He is originally from a rural area of a developing country, and the lesions have enlarged slowly over 3 weeks without significant pain. Biopsy shows large macrophages containing bipolar-staining inclusions within the cytoplasm.',
    'Which organism is most likely responsible?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Calymmatobacterium-like Donovan body-forming fungi"},{"id":"B","text":"Haemophilus ducreyi"},{"id":"C","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"id":"D","text":"Klebsiella granulomatis"},{"id":"E","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D-K"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Granuloma inguinale (donovanosis) is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis, formerly Calymmatobacterium granulomatis, which produces painless, beefy red ulcers that bleed readily on contact. The diagnostic finding is the cytoplasmic Donovan body with bipolar staining seen on microscopy of macrophages. First Aid notes the disease is uncommon in the US.',
    '{"A":"Donovan bodies are intracellular bacteria within macrophages, not fungal structures; there is no fungal agent in the differential for this presentation.","B":"H ducreyi produces painful ulcers with exudate and tender nodes, not painless beefy red bleeding ulcers.","C":"T pallidum produces a single painless indurated chancre with a clean base and rubbery lymphadenopathy, with spirochetes on dark-field examination rather than Donovan bodies.","E":"C trachomatis D-K causes urethritis, cervicitis, and reactive arthritis, while serovars L1-L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum rather than ulcerative donovanosis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Sexually transmitted infections',
    'A 27-year-old woman presents with recurrent painful blisters and shallow ulcers on the vulva. She had a similar episode 6 months ago, and each episode is accompanied by bilateral tender inguinal lymphadenopathy and a prodrome of tingling. PCR from a swab of the lesion is positive for herpes simplex virus type 2. Her new partner asks about prevention and about whether the drug eradicates the virus.',
    'Which of the following statements best characterizes therapy for this infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Acyclovir resistance arises primarily from mutations in viral DNA polymerase rather than thymidine kinase"},{"id":"B","text":"Acyclovir is activated by viral thymidine kinase and has no effect on latent virus in sensory ganglia"},{"id":"C","text":"Acyclovir eradicates latent virus from the sacral dorsal root ganglia, preventing all future recurrences"},{"id":"D","text":"Acyclovir is activated by cellular kinases only and therefore is equally active against CMV"},{"id":"E","text":"Foscarnet requires no kinase activation and is the first-line drug for recurrent genital herpes"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Acyclovir is a guanosine analog that is monophosphorylated by HSV/VZV thymidine kinase and therefore is phosphorylated only in infected cells, which limits host toxicity. The triphosphate then preferentially inhibits viral DNA polymerase by chain termination. Because it acts on replicating virus, it has no effect on latent forms of HSV and VZV, so it shortens and suppresses episodes but does not cure infection.',
    '{"A":"Resistance to acyclovir most commonly arises from mutated viral thymidine kinase, which prevents the first phosphorylation step; altered DNA polymerase is the resistance mechanism for foscarnet.","C":"Acyclovir has no effect on latent HSV, and latent virus in the sacral ganglia persists, which is why recurrences continue after treatment.","D":"Acyclovir is not activated by cellular kinases alone; the requirement for viral thymidine kinase is the basis of its selectivity, and CMV lacks the thymidine kinase needed to activate guanosine analogs, so acyclovir has no activity against CMV.","E":"Foscarnet is reserved for acyclovir-resistant HSV and for CMV retinitis when ganciclovir fails; it is not first-line therapy for routine recurrent genital herpes."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Sexually transmitted infections',
    'A 29-year-old woman presents with a painless ulcer on the cervix incidentally found during a routine pelvic examination. She recalls a painless genital sore 3 weeks earlier that resolved. Rapid plasma reagin is positive, and dark-field microscopy of a lesion exudate shows slender coiled organisms. She is not pregnant and has no drug allergies.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Oral doxycycline for 7 days"},{"id":"B","text":"Intramuscular ceftriaxone single dose"},{"id":"C","text":"Oral metronidazole"},{"id":"D","text":"Topical podophyllin"},{"id":"E","text":"Benzathine penicillin G IM once"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is primary syphilis from Treponema pallidum, and the treatment of choice is benzathine penicillin G given intramuscularly. Penicillin G is the prototype beta-lactam and is active against spirochetes, mainly T pallidum. The painless chancre with regional lymphadenopathy is the defining presentation of primary syphilis in the STI table.',
    '{"A":"Doxycycline is used for chlamydial infection; tetracyclines are an alternative in penicillin-allergic syphilis but are not first-line therapy.","B":"Ceftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin used for gonorrhea, meningitis, and disseminated Lyme disease; it is not the standard syphilis regimen.","C":"Metronidazole treats Trichomonas, bacterial vaginosis, and anaerobes, and it has no role in syphilis.","D":"Podophyllin is a topical treatment for condylomata acuminata from HPV, not for a treponemal ulcer."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Vertical transmission and congenital infections',
    'A neonate is noted at birth to have microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, and thrombocytopenia with a petechial rash. Head ultrasound shows calcifications in a periventricular distribution, and fundoscopy reveals chorioretinitis. Maternal serology is positive for cytomegalovirus IgG and IgM, and the infant''s urine CMV PCR is positive.',
    'Which pattern of findings is most consistent with this congenital infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Hydrops fetalis from parvovirus B19 with pure red cell aplasia"},{"id":"B","text":"Diffuse intracranial calcifications with hydrocephalus and a classic triad of chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and calcifications"},{"id":"C","text":"Periventricular calcifications with hearing loss and chorioretinitis"},{"id":"D","text":"Cataracts, deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus"},{"id":"E","text":"Saber shins, notched teeth, and eighth cranial nerve deafness"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Congenital CMV characteristically produces periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, hearing loss, seizures, petechial rash, and chorioretinitis; the First Aid mnemonic is CMV = Chorioretinitis, Microcephaly, periVentricular calcifications. Hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and growth restriction are the nonspecific features shared by many TORCH infections. CMV is acquired by the mother through sexual contact or organ transplant and is usually asymptomatic in her.',
    '{"A":"Parvovirus B19 does cause hydrops fetalis, but it does not produce the calcifications, chorioretinitis, or periventricular pattern described in this infant.","B":"Diffuse intracranial calcifications with hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis is the classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis, which gives scattered calcifications rather than periventricular ones.","D":"Cataracts, deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus form the classic triad of congenital rubella, remembered as \"I heart ruby earrings.\"","E":"Saber shins, notched (Hutchinson) teeth, saddle nose, and CN VIII deafness are the stigmata of congenital syphilis."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Vertical transmission and congenital infections',
    'A newborn is evaluated for a classic triad of findings: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and diffuse intracranial calcifications. The mother, who immigrated from Central America, reports that she has several cats at home and routinely eats undercooked pork. She had no symptoms during pregnancy other than an occasional painless cervical lymph node.',
    'Which organism is most likely responsible for this infant''s findings?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rubella virus"},{"id":"B","text":"Cytomegalovirus"},{"id":"C","text":"Toxoplasma gondii"},{"id":"D","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"id":"E","text":"Listeria monocytogenes"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Congenital toxoplasmosis classically presents with the triad of chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications, and may also show a blueberry muffin rash. The mother acquires the organism from cat feces or undercooked meat and is usually asymptomatic, or has only painless lymphadenopathy. Both exposures are present in this patient''s history.',
    '{"A":"Rubella produces cataracts, deafness, and congenital heart disease such as PDA, and its maternal illness includes rash, lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias.","B":"CMV produces periventricular calcifications with microcephaly and hearing loss rather than the classic hydrocephalus and diffuse calcification triad of toxoplasmosis.","D":"Congenital syphilis produces facial abnormalities, saber shins, and CN VIII deafness, and often stillbirth or hydrops fetalis.","E":"Listeria is a cause of neonatal meningitis and sepsis but is not associated with the calcification and chorioretinitis triad."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Vertical transmission and congenital infections',
    'A neonate is born with cloudy corneas, cataracts, deafness, and a systolic murmur with a continuous machinery-like murmur on examination. Echocardiography confirms a patent ductus arteriosus. The mother reports a rash with joint pains during the first trimester but did not seek care. There is also a small purpuric rash on the infant''s trunk described as blueberry muffin.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative agent?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"id":"B","text":"Rubella virus"},{"id":"C","text":"Herpes simplex virus type 2"},{"id":"D","text":"Toxoplasma gondii"},{"id":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Congenital rubella produces the classic triad of eye abnormalities (cataracts), ear abnormalities (deafness), and congenital heart disease, classically patent ductus arteriosus; the First Aid mnemonic is "I (eye) heart ruby (rubella) earrings." A blueberry muffin rash may also occur. The mother acquires rubella via respiratory droplets and shows rash, lymphadenopathy, and polyarthritis or polyarthralgia.',
    '{"A":"Congenital syphilis produces rhinitis, notched teeth, saddle nose, saber shins, and CN VIII deafness rather than cataracts with PDA.","C":"HSV-2 is acquired mainly during vaginal delivery and causes meningoencephalitis and herpetic vesicular lesions rather than cataracts and patent ductus arteriosus.","D":"Toxoplasma causes chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and diffuse calcifications, not cataracts and congenital heart disease.","E":"CMV causes periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, and hearing loss, but cataracts and PDA are not part of its classic syndrome."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Vertical transmission and congenital infections',
    'A 2-day-old infant born by vaginal delivery after prolonged rupture of membranes becomes lethargic and feeds poorly, then develops seizures. CSF shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and blood cultures are positive for the same organism. The mother had a positive rectovaginal screening culture during pregnancy and received no intrapartum prophylaxis because she presented in advanced labor.',
    'Which of the following statements about this infection is most accurate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The organism is the most common cause of neonatal pneumonia after acquisition from contaminated water aerosols"},{"id":"B","text":"The organism is a leading cause of meningitis in adolescents and young adults due to nasopharyngeal carriage"},{"id":"C","text":"The organism is acquired from soft cheese and deli meats in the third trimester"},{"id":"D","text":"Vaccination against this organism is part of the routine childhood schedule, eliminating neonatal disease"},{"id":"E","text":"Group B strep and E coli cause neonatal meningitis, declining with prophylaxis"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Streptococcus agalactiae (group B streptococci) is grouped with E coli and Listeria monocytogenes as an important cause of neonatal meningitis and sepsis, acquired perinatally from the colonized birth canal; transmission is via vaginal delivery. First Aid''s systems section notes that the incidence of group B streptococcal meningitis in neonates has fallen greatly because of screening and antibiotic prophylaxis in pregnancy. Empiric neonatal meningitis therapy includes ampicillin when Listeria is suspected plus ceftriaxone and vancomycin.',
    '{"A":"Water aerosols are the route for Legionella, which causes pneumonia in adults with exposure to contaminated water systems, not neonatal infection.","B":"The leading causes of meningitis in adolescents and young adults are S pneumoniae and N meningitidis, for which nasopharyngeal carriage is relevant; group B strep is a neonatal pathogen.","C":"Acquisition from soft cheese and deli meats describes Listeria, which is also a neonatal meningitis cause but is not the group B strep organism described by the culture.","D":"There is no routine childhood vaccine against group B streptococci; the decline in disease comes from maternal screening and intrapartum prophylaxis with penicillin G or ampicillin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Zoonotic infections and bioterrorism agents',
    'A 34-year-old laboratory technician in a biodefense research facility develops fever, a painless black necrotic ulcer on the forearm, and profound nonpitting edema of the surrounding tissue with painless regional lymphadenopathy. Two days later he has dyspnea, widened mediastinum on chest radiograph, and pleural effusions. He works with spore-forming gram-positive rods cultured from soil samples.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely agent, and which feature most supports it?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Yersinia pestis; the buboes and hemoptysis of pneumonic plague"},{"id":"B","text":"Francisella tularensis; the tick bite and ulceroglandular tularemia"},{"id":"C","text":"Brucella species; the undulant fever with sacroiliitis"},{"id":"D","text":"Clostridium botulinum; the symmetric descending flaccid paralysis"},{"id":"E","text":"B anthracis; painless eschar and a widened mediastinum"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Cutaneous anthrax from Bacillus anthracis begins as a painless papule that ulcerates into a black eschar with marked edema, and inhalation anthrax produces a widened mediastinum with hemorrhagic mediastinitis after spores are inhaled. B anthracis is a spore-forming gram-positive rod and is one of the classic bioterrorism agents because of its ability to be aerosolized as spores. Its anthrax toxin acts by mimicking adenylate cyclase.',
    '{"A":"Y pestis causes bubonic plague with painful buboes and pneumonic plague with hemoptysis; it does not produce a black eschar with a widened mediastinum.","B":"Tularemia from F tularensis produces an ulceroglandular illness after tick or rabbit exposure without a black eschar or mediastinal widening.","C":"Brucellosis produces undulant fever with sacroiliitis after unpasteurized dairy exposure rather than eschar and mediastinal hemorrhage.","D":"Botulism produces symmetric descending flaccid paralysis from a neurotoxin, not a cutaneous eschar or a mediastinal mass."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Zoonotic infections and bioterrorism agents',
    'A 45-year-old man from a rural area of New Mexico presents with 6 days of fever, chills, and a painful left inguinal lymph node the size of a golf ball. He notes that a prairie dog near his cabin died of a similar wasting illness and that he was bitten by fleas while camping. He has a productive cough with blood-streaked sputum, and Gram stain shows bipolar-staining gram-negative rods.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Anthrax"},{"id":"B","text":"Brucellosis"},{"id":"C","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever"},{"id":"D","text":"Tularemia"},{"id":"E","text":"Plague"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Yersinia pestis causes plague and is transmitted by fleas, with rats and prairie dogs serving as reservoirs. Bipolar-staining gram-negative rods with a safety-pin appearance are the classic morphology, and the painful enlarged node is a bubo; progression to pneumonic plague causes hemoptysis. Plague is one of the classic bioterrorism agents.',
    '{"A":"Anthrax causes a painless black eschar and mediastinal widening, and B anthracis is a large spore-forming gram-positive rod rather than a gram-negative rod.","B":"Brucellosis produces undulant fever with back pain after unpasteurized dairy consumption and does not give buboes or pneumonic spread.","C":"RMSF from R rickettsii is tick-borne and produces a rash spreading from the wrists and ankles to the trunk with headache and fever, not suppurating buboes.","D":"Tularemia from F tularensis follows tick, rabbit, or deer-fly exposure and produces ulceroglandular disease, not the flea-prairie dog reservoir pattern with bipolar rods of plague."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Zoonotic infections and bioterrorism agents',
    'A 38-year-old veterinarian who assists with sheep and cattle births in a rural county develops a 2-week history of high fever, severe retro-orbital headache, dry cough, and right upper quadrant tenderness. Liver enzymes are mildly elevated. Several farmers in the region had similar febrile illnesses after assisting with lambing, and one reported that the amniotic fluid may have aerosolized in the barn.',
    'Which organism is the most likely cause of this cluster of illness?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Coxiella burnetii"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlamydophila psittaci"},{"id":"C","text":"Leptospira species"},{"id":"D","text":"Francisella tularensis"},{"id":"E","text":"Brucella species"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Coxiella burnetii causes Q fever and is transmitted by aerosols of cattle or sheep amniotic fluid, making obstetric work with livestock the classic exposure, and it produces fever, severe headache, and an atypical pneumonia with hepatitis-like liver enzyme elevation. The organism is an obligate intracellular bacterium and is highly infectious by the aerosol route, which matters for laboratory and biodefense handling. Brucella, also a livestock zoonosis, is instead acquired from unpasteurized dairy or direct tissue contact and gives undulant fever.',
    '{"B":"C psittaci causes psittacosis with pneumonia after exposure to parrots and other birds, not livestock parturition.","C":"Leptospirosis is acquired from animal urine in water and produces fever, myalgias, and jaundice, not an amniotic-fluid aerosol outbreak.","D":"F tularensis is transmitted by ticks, rabbits, and deer flies and causes ulceroglandular tularemia, not an outbreak tied to lambing.","E":"Brucellosis produces undulant fever with sacroiliitis and back pain after unpasteurized dairy or direct tissue contact; it is not the organism specifically associated with amniotic fluid aerosols."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Zoonotic infections and bioterrorism agents',
    'A 52-year-old dairy farmer presents with 3 weeks of undulant fever, drenching night sweats, low back pain, and weight loss. He drinks unpasteurized milk from his herd and has helped deliver calves bare-handed. MRI of the spine shows inflammation at the L4-L5 level. Blood cultures are persistently negative on routine media but eventually grow a slow-growing gram-negative coccobacillus.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Yersinia pestis"},{"id":"B","text":"Coxiella burnetii"},{"id":"C","text":"Brucella species"},{"id":"D","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"},{"id":"E","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Brucella causes brucellosis (undulant fever) and is acquired by ingestion of unpasteurized dairy or by inhalation of and contact with infected animal tissue or fluids, making dairy farmers and veterinarians the classic hosts. The classic complication is sacroiliitis and vertebral osteomyelitis with back pain, and the organism is a slow-growing gram-negative coccobacillus requiring prolonged culture. It is one of the zoonotic bacteria and one of the classic bioterrorism agents.',
    '{"A":"Y pestis is flea-borne and produces buboes or pneumonic plague rather than undulant fever with sacroiliitis after milk ingestion.","B":"C burnetii causes Q fever through aerosolized amniotic fluid and produces pneumonia and hepatitis without the extensive vertebral osteomyelitis.","D":"M tuberculosis causes Pott disease, which also affects the spine, but it presents with a positive tuberculin test and acid-fast bacilli rather than a gram-negative coccobacillus after dairy exposure.","E":"S aureus is the most common cause of vertebral osteomyelitis overall, but it would grow promptly as coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in clusters, and it does not explain the unpasteurized dairy exposure."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Systems Microbiology', 'Zoonotic infections and bioterrorism agents',
    'A 61-year-old man presents with 3 days of fever, headache, myalgias, and a rash that began on the wrists and ankles and now involves the trunk, palms, and soles. He recalls a tick bite after a hiking trip. Laboratory studies show hyponatremia, thrombocytopenia, and elevated transaminases. He is started on a tetracycline-class antibiotic empirically before the serology returns.',
    'Which of the following statements best explains why this empiric choice is appropriate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Rickettsia are extracellular gram-positive cocci, so a cell wall inhibitor such as vancomycin is required"},{"id":"B","text":"Rickettsia are fungi, so ergosterol-binding polyenes should be used rather than antibacterials"},{"id":"C","text":"Rickettsia lack ribosomes, so protein synthesis inhibitors have no activity and only fluoroquinolones work"},{"id":"D","text":"Rickettsia are intracellular, so tetracyclines accumulate inside"},{"id":"E","text":"Rickettsia are anaerobes, so metronidazole is the agent of choice"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Rocky Mountain spotted fever from Rickettsia rickettsii produces the classic triad of headache, fever, and rash, with the rash starting at the wrists and ankles and spreading to the trunk, palms, and soles. Rickettsia are obligate intracellular bacteria, and tetracyclines accumulate intracellularly, which is why doxycycline is the treatment for all rickettsial diseases, including RMSF. First Aid''s palm-and-sole mnemonic groups RMSF with Coxsackievirus A hand-foot-mouth disease and secondary syphilis.',
    '{"A":"Rickettsia are neither extracellular nor gram-positive cocci; they are obligate intracellular organisms, which is why a cell wall inhibitor such as vancomycin would fail against them.","B":"Rickettsia are bacteria with peptidoglycan-containing walls, not fungi, and treatment is with antibacterials such as doxycycline.","C":"Rickettsia have bacterial (70S) ribosomes and are susceptible to protein synthesis inhibitors including tetracyclines and chloramphenicol.","E":"Rickettsia are not treated with metronidazole, which is used for anaerobes and protozoa such as Trichomonas and Giardia."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma gondii',
    'A 33-year-old man with untreated HIV infection and a CD4+ count of 55 cells/mm3 presents with 3 weeks of headache, low-grade fever, and progressive confusion. He was born in France and emigrated 5 years ago. MRI of the brain shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions with surrounding edema scattered through both hemispheres, and the lesions are multiple rather than solitary.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely cause of these lesions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cytomegalovirus"},{"id":"B","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},{"id":"C","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"},{"id":"D","text":"JC virus"},{"id":"E","text":"Toxoplasma gondii"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Reactivation of latent Toxoplasma gondii bradyzoite cysts in the setting of advanced HIV produces brain abscesses that are characteristically multiple ring-enhancing lesions on MRI, and First Aid notes that this occurs once the CD4+ count falls below about 100 cells/mm3. The two main alternatives in the differential are CNS lymphoma, which is usually a solitary lesion, and CNS tuberculosis. Diagnosis is by serology or biopsy showing tachyzoites, and treatment is sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine, with TMP-SMX prophylaxis below a CD4+ count of 100 cells/mm3.',
    '{"A":"Cytomegalovirus in advanced AIDS typically causes retinitis or colitis, and its CNS manifestations are periventricular encephalitis rather than scattered ring-enhancing abscesses.","B":"Cryptococcus neoformans causes subacute meningitis with a high cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure and yeast forms on India ink staining or cryptococcal antigen testing, rather than multiple ring-enhancing parenchymal lesions.","C":"CNS tuberculosis typically produces basilar meningitis, tuberculomas, or a single ring-enhancing mass in a patient from an endemic region, and it is not the classic multiple-lesion picture described.","D":"JC virus causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, which presents as nonenhancing, asymmetric white matter lesions without mass effect or ring enhancement."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma gondii',
    'A 3-day-old boy born to a 24-year-old primigravida is evaluated for microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, and jaundice. Fundoscopic examination shows bilateral chorioretinal scars, and head ultrasound shows ventricular dilation with scattered periventricular densities. The mother reported cleaning a cat litter box throughout pregnancy and recalls a flu-like illness in her second trimester.',
    'Which of the following sets of findings best describes the classic presentation of this congenital infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Romaña sign, megacolon, and apical cardiomyopathy"},{"id":"B","text":"Periorbital edema, myalgias, and eosinophilia"},{"id":"C","text":"Pigmented gallstones, cholangiocarcinoma, and biliary colic"},{"id":"D","text":"Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications"},{"id":"E","text":"Flask-shaped colonic ulcers, liver abscess, and right upper quadrant pain"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Congenital toxoplasmosis results from transplacental transmission, and risk is highest when primary maternal infection occurs during pregnancy, which is why pregnant patients are counseled to avoid cat litter boxes and to cook meat thoroughly. The classic triad is chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications. Diagnosis is supported by serology and by PCR of amniotic fluid, and treatment uses pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine.',
    '{"E":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a liver abscess and right upper quadrant pain describe invasive amebiasis from Entamoeba histolytica, which does not produce this congenital syndrome.","A":"Romaña sign, megacolon, and apical cardiomyopathy are features of Chagas disease from Trypanosoma cruzi, whose transmission is vector-borne rather than transplacental in the classic teaching.","B":"Periorbital edema, myalgias, and eosinophilia describe trichinosis from Trichinella spiralis in undercooked pork, not a congenital infection of the newborn.","C":"Pigmented gallstones, cholangiocarcinoma, and biliary colic point to Clonorchis sinensis acquired by eating undercooked fish, unrelated to intrauterine infection."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma gondii',
    'A 22-year-old college student presents with 2 weeks of fatigue, fever to 38.1 C, and tender cervical lymphadenopathy. She reports eating undercooked lamb at a family barbecue and cleaning her older sister''s litter box while cat-sitting. She has no pharyngitis, no rash, and no other localizing symptoms. A rapid heterophile antibody test is negative, and a peripheral smear shows atypical lymphocytosis without other abnormalities.',
    'Which of the following best explains this presentation?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Babesiosis from Babesia microti"},{"id":"B","text":"Scabies from Sarcoptes scabiei"},{"id":"C","text":"Infectious mononucleosis from Epstein-Barr virus"},{"id":"D","text":"Acute HIV seroconversion illness"},{"id":"E","text":"Toxoplasma gondii infection in an immunocompetent host"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'In immunocompetent hosts Toxoplasma gondii produces a mononucleosis-like illness with fever, lymphadenopathy, and fatigue, but the heterophile antibody test is negative, and that negative result is what separates it from Epstein-Barr virus infectious mononucleosis. First Aid emphasizes that tissue cysts in undercooked meat are the most common route of infection, with oocysts in cat feces as the other classic source. Most immunocompetent patients recover without antimicrobial therapy.',
    '{"A":"Babesiosis causes fever with hemolytic anemia, intraerythrocytic ring forms, and often a tick bite history, which is a different clinical picture and a different smear finding.","B":"Scabies produces pruritus worse at night with serpiginous burrows between the fingers, not a systemic febrile illness with cervical adenopathy.","C":"Epstein-Barr virus mononucleosis is the main differential, but it is characterized by a positive heterophile antibody test, which this patient did not have.","D":"Acute HIV infection can mimic mononucleosis, but it is diagnosed by HIV RNA or antigen-antibody testing and would not be favored by the specific dietary and cat exposures described."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Transposons, insertion sequences, and antibiotic resistance genes',
    'A 66-year-old man in intensive care is colonized with vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium. Weeks later, vancomycin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is isolated from a wound in the same unit. Molecular typing shows the vanA gene cluster is identical to that of the enterococcus and is carried on Tn1546.',
    'Which mechanism most likely explains the transfer of vanA resistance into the staphylococcal genome?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Specialized transduction by a lysogenic phage"},{"id":"B","text":"Generalized transduction by a lytic phage"},{"id":"C","text":"Transformation with naked chromosomal DNA"},{"id":"D","text":"F+ x F- conjugation of the F plasmid"},{"id":"E","text":"Transposition of a transposon carrying vanA"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Transposons are specialized DNA segments that can copy and excise themselves and insert into the same or an unrelated DNA molecule, including between plasmid and chromosome. Transposition is critical in creating plasmids with multiple drug resistance and in moving resistance across species lines, with Tn1546 carrying vanA from Enterococcus to S aureus as the textbook example. Unlike transduction, transposition needs no phage, and unlike simple conjugation it can move a block of resistance genes into an already existing plasmid. This is the mechanistic basis for multidrug-resistant hospital organisms.',
    '{"A":"Specialized transduction moves genes flanking a prophage integration site, and Tn1546 is a transposon, not a prophage component.","B":"Generalized transduction requires a phage packaging error and typically transfers chromosomal genes; the vanA cluster here is a mobile element, not a phage-packaged fragment.","C":"Transformation requires competence and uptake of naked DNA, which would not reliably deliver an intact multi-gene transposon into a plasmid or chromosome.","D":"F+ x F- conjugation transfers the F plasmid between gram-negatives; conjugative plasmid transfer can move DNA, but the element identified here is a transposon."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Basic Bacteriology', 'Transposons, insertion sequences, and antibiotic resistance genes',
    'A 52-year-old woman treated for a urinary tract infection returns with a more resistant isolate. The laboratory reports a single plasmid now carrying resistance to three unrelated antibiotic classes, although the original isolate was susceptible to two of them. Restriction analysis suggests new DNA segments of a few kilobases were inserted into the plasmid.',
    'Which process best explains how the plasmid acquired this clustered multiple drug resistance?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Transposition of a mobile DNA segment into an unrelated DNA molecule"},{"id":"B","text":"Generalized transduction of random chromosomal genes by a temperate phage"},{"id":"C","text":"Generalized transduction by a lytic phage carrying a packaging error"},{"id":"D","text":"Specialized transduction, following a site-specific excision event in a lysogenic prophage"},{"id":"E","text":"Conjugation through the sex pilus between an F positive donor and an F minus cell"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Transposons can copy and excise and then insert into an unrelated DNA molecule, a plasmid here, which is exactly how plasmids with multiple drug resistance are created. The insertion of new segments into the plasmid map and the simultaneous appearance of resistance to unrelated drug classes are the fingerprints. Transposition also permits movement across species lines, unlike chromosome mutations, which stay in one lineage. This single-event block acquisition is why multidrug-resistant plasmids can appear abruptly in clinical isolates.',
    '{"A":"Transformation imports short fragments of naked DNA and is DNase sensitive; an intact multi-class resistance cassette on a plasmid is not the typical product.","B":"Lysogenic conversion adds phage-encoded toxin genes (e.g., cholera, diphtheria, Shiga, botulinum, erythrogenic toxins), not antibiotic resistance cassettes.","D":"Decreased uptake via porin loss reduces entry of some drugs such as the tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones but does not insert new DNA or confer resistance to unrelated drug classes.","E":"Point mutations accumulate slowly and produce resistance to one drug family at a time; they do not create the multi-kilobase insertions or a coordinated resistance phenotype."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trematodes: Schistosoma and Clonorchis',
    'A 24-year-old woman from rural Brazil presents with 2 months of intermittent bloody stools, abdominal pain, and fatigue. She reports swimming most days in a freshwater river near her home. Examination shows mild hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL, a low mean corpuscular volume, and eosinophilia. Stool microscopy shows eggs with a lateral spine.',
    'Which of the following is the most likely route by which this patient was infected?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Larval penetration of skin from sandy soil"},{"id":"B","text":"Ingestion of cysts in untreated water"},{"id":"C","text":"Ingestion of larvae in raw freshwater fish"},{"id":"D","text":"A tick bite after exposure in wooded areas"},{"id":"E","text":"Cercariae from an intermediate snail host penetrating skin"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Schistosoma mansoni is acquired when cercariae released by a snail intermediate host penetrate the skin of a human in contact with contaminated fresh water, such as someone swimming or bathing in a river. The adult worms live in the mesenteric venules, and S mansoni and S japonicum produce intestinal schistosomiasis with diarrhea, abdominal pain, and iron deficiency anemia, as well as periportal fibrosis, liver and spleen enlargement, and portal hypertension. The lateral-spined egg identifies S mansoni, and treatment is praziquantel.',
    '{"A":"Larval penetration of skin from sandy soil is the route for hookworms, which cause cutaneous larva migrans and blood-loss anemia but not eggs with a lateral spine or portal fibrosis.","B":"Cysts in untreated water transmit Giardia and Entamoeba, which cause nonbloody fatty diarrhea or bloody dysentery with liver abscess rather than a trematode with a lateral-spined egg.","C":"Larvae in raw freshwater fish transmit Diphyllobothrium latum and Clonorchis sinensis, and the Clonorchis picture is biliary disease rather than intestinal and hepatic schistosomiasis.","D":"A tick bite after wooded exposure raises Babesia or Borrelia burgdorferi, which cause hemolytic febrile illness or erythema migrans rather than fluke disease."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trematodes: Schistosoma and Clonorchis',
    'A 58-year-old man who emigrated from Egypt 20 years ago presents with 3 months of painless gross hematuria. He has no fever or flank pain, and he recalls swimming in irrigation canals as a child. Urinalysis shows many red blood cells without casts or dysmorphic forms. Cystoscopy shows a sessile bladder lesion, and biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma.',
    'Which of the following correctly identifies the infecting organism, its egg morphology, and the resulting complication?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"S mansoni, whose eggs have a lateral spine and which causes squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder"},{"id":"B","text":"S haematobium, whose eggs have a lateral spine and which causes portal hypertension"},{"id":"C","text":"S haematobium, whose eggs have a terminal spine and which causes squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder"},{"id":"D","text":"S japonicum, whose eggs have a lateral spine and which causes painless hematuria"},{"id":"E","text":"Clonorchis sinensis, whose eggs have an operculum and which causes cholangiocarcinoma"}]'::jsonb,
    'C',
    'Chronic infection with Schistosoma haematobium causes squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, presenting with painless hematuria, and First Aid lists hematuria with squamous cell bladder cancer as the hint association; chronic infection can also produce pulmonary hypertension. The diagnostic egg has a terminal spine. In contrast, S mansoni eggs have a lateral spine and cause intestinal disease with periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension, as does S japonicum.',
    '{"A":"S mansoni eggs do have a lateral spine, but that species is linked to intestinal disease and portal hypertension rather than bladder squamous cell carcinoma.","B":"S haematobium is the right organism, but its eggs have a terminal spine rather than a lateral spine, and its complication is bladder disease rather than portal hypertension.","D":"S japonicum is associated with intestinal disease and portal hypertension, not with painless hematuria and bladder carcinoma.","E":"Clonorchis sinensis is acquired by eating undercooked fish and causes biliary tract inflammation with pigmented gallstones and cholangiocarcinoma, not bladder squamous cell carcinoma."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Treponema pallidum',
    'A 26-year-old man presents with a single, firm, painless ulcer on the shaft of his penis that appeared 3 weeks after unprotected intercourse. The ulcer has a clean base with raised indurated borders and there is mild, nontender inguinal lymphadenopathy. A VDRL test is reported as positive.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to confirm the diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Treat with penicillin G without further testing because a positive VDRL is diagnostic"},{"id":"B","text":"Perform a treponemal-specific test such as FTA-ABS, or examine exudate from the chancre by dark-field microscopy"},{"id":"C","text":"Repeat the VDRL in 4 weeks to demonstrate a rising titer"},{"id":"D","text":"Culture the ulcer exudate on chocolate agar"},{"id":"E","text":"Obtain a Gram stain of the ulcer base and treat if gram-negative cocci are seen"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Primary syphilis presents with a painless chancre, and the VDRL is positive in about 80% of such patients. VDRL is a nontreponemal test that is sensitive but not specific, so a positive result should be confirmed with a specific treponemal test such as FTA-ABS or TP-PA. Direct visualization of spirochetes in chancre fluid by dark-field or fluorescent antibody microscopy is also diagnostic.',
    '{"A":"A nontreponemal test such as VDRL can be falsely positive from pregnancy, viral infection, drugs, lupus, or leprosy, so confirmation before treatment is appropriate.","C":"Waiting for a rising titer delays diagnosis and treatment and is not the standard approach; a confirmatory specific test is available immediately.","D":"T pallidum cannot be cultured routinely on artificial media such as chocolate agar, so culture is not a diagnostic option.","E":"Gram stain does not visualize spirochetes well and cannot distinguish T pallidum; Haemophilus ducreyi would produce a painful chancre, unlike this lesion."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Treponema pallidum',
    'A 31-year-old woman presents with a diffuse maculopapular rash involving the palms and soles, along with low-grade fever, generalized lymphadenopathy, and patchy hair loss. Physical examination also shows several smooth, painless, wartlike white lesions around the vulva. She had an untreated genital ulcer 3 months earlier.',
    'Which of the following best describes the serologic findings expected in this stage of infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Negative nontreponemal test with a positive specific treponemal test only"},{"id":"B","text":"Both tests negative since the disease is now latent"},{"id":"C","text":"Positive nontreponemal test with a negative specific treponemal test"},{"id":"D","text":"Positive tests for anticardiolipin antibody but negative specific treponemal testing"},{"id":"E","text":"Positive nontreponemal test confirmed by a positive specific treponemal test"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'This is secondary syphilis: disseminated disease with constitutional symptoms, a maculopapular rash involving palms and soles, condylomata lata, lymphadenopathy, and patchy hair loss. At this stage serology is reactive, so a sensitive nontreponemal test (VDRL or RPR) is positive and is confirmed with a specific treponemal test such as FTA-ABS. Dark-field microscopy of the lesions can also confirm the diagnosis.',
    '{"A":"In secondary syphilis the nontreponemal test is characteristically reactive, often in high titer; negative nontreponemal with positive treponemal results suggests a treated or very late infection.","B":"Latent syphilis is defined by positive serology without symptoms; this patient is symptomatic with a disseminated rash.","C":"A negative specific treponemal test argues against syphilis and suggests the nontreponemal result is a false positive.","D":"Anticardiolipin antibodies can cause a false-positive VDRL, but this patient has classic secondary syphilis with palms and soles involvement, so specific testing will be positive."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Treponema pallidum',
    'A 58-year-old man presents with a several-year history of episodic sharp, shooting pains in his legs and progressive unsteadiness of gait, worse in the dark. Examination shows impaired vibration and proprioception in the lower extremities, a positive Romberg sign, and dilated pupils that constrict with accommodation but do not react to light. Serologic testing for syphilis is reactive.',
    'Which of the following best explains this patient''s pupillary abnormality?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Gummatous destruction of the ciliary ganglion by chronic granulomas"},{"id":"B","text":"Concurrent vitamin B12 deficiency causing autonomic neuropathy"},{"id":"C","text":"Iris synechiae from prior anterior uveitis fixing the pupil in a dilated state"},{"id":"D","text":"Neurosyphilis with an Argyll Robertson pupil that constricts with accommodation but is unreactive to light"},{"id":"E","text":"Aortic root dilation causing embolic damage to the iris sphincter"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Tertiary syphilis includes neurosyphilis, classically tabes dorsalis with broad-based ataxia, positive Romberg, Charcot joints, and the Argyll Robertson pupil that constricts with accommodation but is not reactive to light. The shooting leg pains reflect dorsal column involvement. Tertiary disease also includes gummas and aortitis from destruction of the vasa vasorum.',
    '{"A":"Gummas are chronic granulomas that occur in tertiary disease but are not the mechanism of the Argyll Robertson pupil.","B":"B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration with neuropathy and ataxia, but the pupillary dissociation with a reactive serology points to neurosyphilis.","C":"Posterior synechiae fix a constricted pupil, not a dilated one, and anterior uveitis is not the classic tertiary presentation shown here.","E":"Aortitis in tertiary syphilis results from vasa vasorum destruction of the aortic wall and causes aortic regurgitation or aneurysm, not embolic iris damage."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Treponema pallidum',
    'A 24-year-old woman is diagnosed with early syphilis and treated with a full course of penicillin G at 10 weeks of gestation. Three months after treatment, her VDRL titer has fallen fourfold and she has no symptoms. Her partner asks what follow-up testing should show over time.',
    'Which of the following best describes the expected long-term behavior of her nontreponemal versus treponemal serology?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Only direct tests such as dark-field microscopy can confirm cure after treatment"},{"id":"B","text":"The nontreponemal test may revert to negative with treatment, whereas the specific treponemal test often remains positive for life"},{"id":"C","text":"Both tests become negative within 6 months of successful treatment"},{"id":"D","text":"The specific treponemal test becomes negative first, followed later by the nontreponemal test"},{"id":"E","text":"Both tests remain positive indefinitely regardless of treatment success"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Nontreponemal tests (VDRL, RPR) and direct testing revert to negative after successful treatment, and titers fall, which is why they are used to monitor response. Antibodies detected by treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TP-PA) generally remain positive for life and are not used to judge cure. Treating early in pregnancy prevents congenital syphilis, since placental transmission typically occurs after the first trimester.',
    '{"A":"Dark-field microscopy is a direct test for active lesions; serologic titer trends are the standard means of following treatment response.","C":"Treponemal antibodies rarely disappear even with successful cure, so expecting both tests to become negative is incorrect.","D":"The order is reversed; the specific treponemal test is the one that persists.","E":"Nontreponemal titers decline and may revert to negative after treatment, which is how treatment response is assessed."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trichomonas vaginalis',
    'A 27-year-old woman presents with 1 week of copious, foul-smelling, greenish vaginal discharge, vulvar itching, and burning with urination. She reports two new sexual partners in the past 3 months. On speculum examination the cervix shows punctate hemorrhages, and wet mount of the discharge shows motile flagellated organisms without cysts.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment plan?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Oral fluconazole for the patient only"},{"id":"B","text":"Sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine for the patient only"},{"id":"C","text":"Permethrin cream applied at bedtime for the patient only"},{"id":"D","text":"Metronidazole for the patient and her partner"},{"id":"E","text":"Clindamycin vaginal cream for the patient only"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Trichomonas vaginalis is a sexually transmitted flagellated protozoan that cannot exist outside the human host because it cannot form cysts. It causes vaginitis with foul-smelling greenish discharge and itching plus punctate cervical hemorrhages, the strawberry cervix, and is diagnosed by motile trophozoites on wet mount. First Aid recommends metronidazole for the patient and her partner or partners as prophylaxis, along with evaluation for other sexually transmitted infections.',
    '{"A":"Fluconazole treats vulvovaginal candidiasis, which gives a thick white curd-like discharge with vulvar pruritus rather than a foul greenish discharge with motile organisms.","B":"Sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine is the regimen for Toxoplasma gondii, an intracellular parasite of brain and muscle that is not transmitted by sexual contact.","C":"Permethrin cream treats scabies from Sarcoptes scabiei, a mite burrowing in the stratum corneum between the fingers and toes, not a vaginal flagellate.","E":"Clindamycin cream targets bacterial vaginosis from Gardnerella vaginalis and other anaerobes, which is not a sexually transmitted flagellate and does not require partner treatment."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trypanosoma brucei and leishmaniasis',
    'A 44-year-old fisherman from a village on Lake Victoria in Uganda presents with 2 months of recurring fevers, enlarged cervical lymph nodes, daytime somnolence, and progressive confusion. He recalls several painful insect bites while working near cattle. A peripheral blood smear shows extracellular flagellated organisms with a kinetoplast and undulating membrane.',
    'Which of the following is the vector for this organism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Tsetse fly"},{"id":"B","text":"Triatomine insect"},{"id":"C","text":"Ixodes tick"},{"id":"D","text":"Female black fly"},{"id":"E","text":"Sandfly"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'African sleeping sickness is caused by Trypanosoma brucei and is transmitted by the tsetse fly, whose painful bite is a classic historical clue. The disease produces enlarged lymph nodes, recurring fever from antigenic variation, somnolence, and ultimately coma, and the trypomastigote is identified on the blood smear. First Aid pairs suramin for blood-borne disease with melarsoprol when CNS penetration is needed.',
    '{"B":"The triatomine (kissing) bug transmits Trypanosoma cruzi, which causes Chagas disease with dilated cardiomyopathy, megacolon, and megaesophagus in South America rather than African sleeping sickness.","C":"The Ixodes tick transmits Babesia, Borrelia burgdorferi, and Anaplasma species, pointing to hemolytic febrile illness or erythema migrans rather than a meningoencephalitic trypanosome.","D":"The female black fly transmits Onchocerca volvulus, producing black skin nodules and river blindness rather than circulating trypanosomes.","E":"The sandfly transmits Leishmania species, which cause kala-azar with hepatosplenomegaly and pancytopenia or cutaneous ulcers with amastigotes in macrophages, not a bloodstream trypomastigote."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trypanosoma brucei and leishmaniasis',
    'A 36-year-old man presents with 4 months of drenching night sweats, weight loss, and progressive abdominal fullness. He moved from rural Bihar, India, 1 year ago. Examination shows a temperature spiking to 40 C for a few hours each day, a spleen palpable to the umbilicus, and hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show pancytopenia with anemia, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia. Bone marrow aspirate shows macrophages filled with small intracellular bodies containing a kinetoplast.',
    'Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient''s diagnosis?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Suramin followed by melarsoprol"},{"id":"B","text":"Amphotericin B"},{"id":"C","text":"Praziquantel with surgical excision"},{"id":"D","text":"Sodium stibogluconate plus local wound care"},{"id":"E","text":"Amphotericin B combined with sodium stibogluconate"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is visceral leishmaniasis, or kala-azar, which causes spiking fevers, hepatosplenomegaly, and pancytopenia. The diagnostic finding is macrophages containing amastigotes, a tissue form that helps distinguish Leishmania from blood-borne trypanosomes. First Aid lists amphotericin B and sodium stibogluconate as the treatments, and amphotericin B is the appropriate choice for disseminated visceral disease.',
    '{"A":"Suramin and melarsoprol treat African trypanosomiasis, with suramin for blood-borne disease and melarsoprol for CNS penetration, so they target a different parasite.","C":"Praziquantel with excision applies to a cestode or trematode such as a complicated hydatid cyst, not to an intracellular protozoan of the reticuloendothelial system.","D":"Sodium stibogluconate is an antileishmanial agent, but combining it with local wound care describes management of a cutaneous ulcer rather than disseminated visceral disease with marrow involvement.","E":"Combining amphotericin B with an antimonial is not the First Aid approach, which lists monotherapy with either amphotericin B or sodium stibogluconate for leishmaniasis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trypanosoma cruzi',
    'A 29-year-old man who emigrated from rural Bolivia 8 years ago is evaluated after a routine electrocardiogram shows a right bundle branch block. He feels well but reports palpitations and chronic constipation with episodic difficulty swallowing solid food. Echocardiography shows right ventricular enlargement with a thinned cardiac apex. He recalls being bitten by insects around his mouth as a child while sleeping in a house with thatched walls.',
    'Which of the following best describes how this patient acquired his infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"A mosquito bite during a trip to West Africa"},{"id":"B","text":"A tick bite while hiking in Connecticut"},{"id":"C","text":"Larval penetration of bare feet on a sandy beach"},{"id":"D","text":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysts"},{"id":"E","text":"Fecal contamination of a bite site by a kissing bug"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Chagas disease is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi, which is found predominantly in South America and transmitted by the triatomine insect, commonly called the kissing bug. The bug bites and then defecates around the mouth or eyes, and trypomastigotes in the feces enter through the bite site or mucosal surfaces. Chronic infection produces dilated cardiomyopathy with apical atrophy, megacolon, and megaesophagus, which is why First Aid says that T cruzi causes big problems.',
    '{"A":"A mosquito bite in West Africa is the route for Plasmodium species and other mosquito-borne infections, not for the flagellate that produces apical atrophy and megacolon.","B":"A tick bite while hiking in Connecticut raises Babesia and Borrelia burgdorferi, which cause hemolytic febrile illness or Lyme disease rather than chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy.","C":"Larval penetration of bare feet on a sandy beach is the entry route for hookworms such as Ancylostoma and Necator, causing microcytic anemia and cutaneous larva migrans.","D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysts transmits Trichinella spiralis myositis or Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm, neither of which produces this cardiomyopathy picture."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Parasitology', 'Trypanosoma cruzi',
    'A 25-year-old man who emigrated from Brazil 3 weeks ago presents with 1 week of fever, malaise, and swelling of the right eyelid. He says the swelling began shortly after he noticed an insect bite near his eye while sleeping in a mud-walled house. Examination shows unilateral periorbital edema and a small, painless swelling of the preauricular lymph node. He has no neurologic deficits.',
    'Which of the following is the most characteristic finding in the acute stage of this patient''s infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Pancytopenia with amastigote-filled macrophages on bone marrow aspirate"},{"id":"B","text":"Unilateral periorbital swelling during the acute stage"},{"id":"C","text":"Trophozoites containing engulfed RBCs on stool microscopy"},{"id":"D","text":"Prolonged watery diarrhea with acid-fast oocysts on stool"},{"id":"E","text":"Multiple ring-enhancing lesions on brain MRI"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Acute Chagas disease from Trypanosoma cruzi is classically marked by unilateral periorbital swelling called Romaña sign, which occurs when trypomastigote-containing feces are introduced near the eye after a kissing bug bite. The blood smear would show trypomastigotes. Most patients then enter a long asymptomatic indeterminate phase before presenting decades later with dilated cardiomyopathy, megacolon, or megaesophagus, which is why the acute sign is a distinctive tested clue.',
    '{"A":"Pancytopenia with amastigote-filled macrophages describes visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar), which is sandfly-borne and does not cause periorbital swelling after a bug bite.","C":"Trophozoites with engulfed RBCs on stool microscopy indicate Entamoeba histolytica amebiasis, which involves the colon and liver rather than the conjunctiva or periorbital tissue.","D":"Prolonged watery diarrhea with acid-fast oocysts indicates Cryptosporidium, an intestinal coccidian of immunocompromised hosts with no association to facial swelling.","E":"Multiple ring-enhancing lesions indicate CNS toxoplasmosis from reactivation of latent tissue cysts, a subacute presentation in advanced HIV rather than an acute vector-borne sign."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Varicella-zoster virus',
    'A 68-year-old woman reports 3 days of burning pain along the right side of her chest before a vesicular rash appeared in a dermatomal distribution. The vesicles have now crusted, but 3 months later she still describes severe shooting pain in the same area that disrupts her sleep.',
    'Which of the following is the most common complication in this patient?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Reye syndrome following aspirin use"},{"id":"B","text":"Post-herpetic neuralgia"},{"id":"C","text":"Temporal lobe encephalitis"},{"id":"D","text":"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus from CN V1 involvement"},{"id":"E","text":"Owl''s eye inclusions in mononuclear cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Shingles results from reactivation of varicella-zoster virus, which remains latent in dorsal root or trigeminal ganglia after primary chickenpox. The most common complication of shingles is post-herpetic neuralgia, persistent pain in the affected dermatome that can last months. VZV is an enveloped linear dsDNA herpesvirus spread by respiratory secretions and contact with vesicle fluid.',
    '{"A":"Reye syndrome is associated with aspirin use during influenza or varicella in children, not with the chronic pain syndrome of shingles in older adults.","C":"Temporal lobe encephalitis is the classic neurologic presentation of HSV-1, not the usual complication of dermatomal zoster.","D":"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus occurs when reactivation involves the CN V1 branch of the trigeminal ganglion and presents with ocular findings, not with thoracic pain alone.","E":"Owl''s eye intranuclear inclusions are the cytologic signature of cytomegalovirus."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Varicella-zoster virus',
    'A 6-year-old unvaccinated boy develops a pruritic rash that began on the scalp and face and spread to the trunk and extremities, progressing through macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts that are present simultaneously at different stages. He had a low-grade fever and malaise for 1 day before the rash.',
    'Which of the following best describes the causative agent and its latency site?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Cytomegalovirus, latent in mononuclear cells"},{"id":"B","text":"Human herpesvirus 6, latent in CD4+ T cells and monocytes"},{"id":"C","text":"HSV-2, latent in sacral ganglia"},{"id":"D","text":"Varicella-zoster virus, latent in dorsal root or trigeminal ganglia"},{"id":"E","text":"Epstein-Barr virus, latent in B cells"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Chickenpox is the primary infection with varicella-zoster virus (HHV-3), an enveloped linear dsDNA herpesvirus transmitted by respiratory secretions and contact with vesicle fluid. The rash appears in crops so that lesions at several stages coexist. After resolution, VZV remains latent in dorsal root or trigeminal ganglia and can reactivate years later as shingles.',
    '{"A":"CMV causes mononucleosis-like illness and disease in immunocompromised hosts; its latency site is mononuclear cells, and it does not cause chickenpox.","B":"HHV-6 causes roseola infantum with high fever followed by a macular rash, not a vesicular rash in crops.","C":"HSV-2 causes herpes genitalis and neonatal herpes and latencies in sacral ganglia; it does not produce the generalized crops of chickenpox.","E":"EBV is spread by saliva and causes mononucleosis with pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly, not a vesicular exanthem."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Vibrio cholerae',
    'A 24-year-old man in a developing country with no access to clean water develops the sudden onset of profuse, painless, watery diarrhea that looks like cloudy rice water, followed by vomiting. Over 12 hours he produces more than 10 liters of stool and becomes profoundly dehydrated with sunken eyes, poor skin turgor, and hypovolemic shock. He is afebrile and has no blood in the stool.',
    'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of this diarrhea?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Direct damage of enterocytes by toxins A and B"},{"id":"B","text":"Permanent activation of Gs with increased intracellular cAMP"},{"id":"C","text":"Invasion of colonic mucosa with ulceration and bloody stools"},{"id":"D","text":"Cleavage of SNARE proteins in enterocytes"},{"id":"E","text":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 in enterocytes"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'Cholera toxin permanently activates Gs, raising intracellular cAMP and driving profuse rice-water secretory diarrhea. Vibrio cholerae is a comma-shaped, flagellated, oxidase-positive organism that grows in alkaline media and is sensitive to stomach acid, requiring a large inoculum unless gastric acidity is reduced. Prompt treatment with oral rehydration solution is the mainstay.',
    '{"A":"Damage of enterocytes by toxins A and B is the mechanism of Clostridioides difficile, which produces colitis rather than painless watery diarrhea without inflammation.","C":"Mucosal invasion with ulceration and bloody stools describes Shigella and enteroinvasive E coli, whereas cholera is purely toxin mediated with no mucosal invasion.","D":"Cleavage of SNARE proteins is the mechanism of clostridial neurotoxins and does not occur in enterocytes, which are unaffected cytoplasmically in cholera.","E":"ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 inhibits protein synthesis and belongs to diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A, not to cholera toxin."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Vibrio cholerae',
    'A 62-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis and hemochromatosis presents 2 days after wading in the Gulf of Mexico with a rapidly spreading, painful, bullous cellulitis of the lower leg, fever, and hypotension. Surgical exploration shows necrosis extending through the fascia, and cultures grow a gram-negative bacillus. He requires debridement and intensive care.',
    'Which of the following best explains his high risk for this severe infection?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Inhalation of aerosolized organisms from air conditioning systems"},{"id":"B","text":"Elevated serum iron favoring a marine gram-negative organism"},{"id":"C","text":"Acid-labile organisms surviving because of reduced gastric acidity"},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotic-associated loss of colonic colonization resistance"},{"id":"E","text":"Reduced IgA after ingestion of contaminated shellfish"}]'::jsonb,
    'B',
    'This is Vibrio vulnificus infection, a gram-negative bacillus found in marine environments that causes severe wound infection or septicemia after exposure to contaminated seawater. High-risk patients are those with high serum iron, such as patients with cirrhosis or hemochromatosis, and the cellulitis can progress to necrotizing fasciitis, requiring surgical debridement.',
    '{"A":"Aerosol transmission from environmental water such as air conditioning systems describes Legionella pneumophila pneumonia, not a wound infection after seawater exposure.","C":"Reduced gastric acidity raises cholera risk by permitting survival of acid-labile Vibrio cholerae, but that mechanism explains gastrointestinal infection rather than this necrotizing wound.","D":"Loss of colonization resistance after antibiotics predisposes to Clostridioides difficile colitis, not to marine wound infection.","E":"Ingestion of contaminated shellfish can transmit cholera and other vibrios, but this patient''s wound was from seawater, and high iron rather than low IgA explains the severe course."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Vibrio cholerae',
    'An outbreak of profuse watery diarrhea affects dozens of residents of a coastal village where drinking water is drawn from a contaminated river. The public health team collects stool specimens to confirm the cause. In the laboratory, the organism grows best in an alkaline medium and is oxidase positive with a comma shape on Gram stain.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible for this outbreak?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Entamoeba histolytica"},{"id":"B","text":"Campylobacter jejuni"},{"id":"C","text":"Shigella sonnei"},{"id":"D","text":"Vibrio cholerae"},{"id":"E","text":"Salmonella typhi"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Vibrio cholerae is a gram-negative, flagellated, comma-shaped, oxidase-positive organism that grows in alkaline media and is endemic to developing countries. It is transmitted by ingestion of contaminated water or uncooked food such as raw shellfish, and it produces profuse rice-water diarrhea through a toxin that raises cAMP.',
    '{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite that causes invasive amebic colitis with bloody stools and liver abscesses, not an oxidase-positive bacterial rod.","B":"Campylobacter jejuni is also curved with polar flagella and oxidase positive, but it characteristically grows at 42°C and causes bloody diarrhea rather than rice-water stool.","C":"Shigella sonnei is nonmotile, does not grow selectively in alkaline media, and causes bacillary dysentery with bloody mucoid stools and tenesmus.","E":"Salmonella typhi produces H2S, spreads hematogenously, and causes typhoid fever with a truncal rash rather than a cholera outbreak of rice-water diarrhea."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Viridans group streptococci and dental caries',
    'A 29-year-old man with bicuspid aortic valve and poor dentition undergoes an uneventful dental extraction. Four weeks later he develops low-grade fever, fatigue, and a new diastolic murmur. Blood cultures grow α-hemolytic cocci that are optochin resistant and bile insoluble, and echocardiography shows a large vegetation on the aortic valve.',
    'Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible, and what is its characteristic virulence mechanism?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptococcus mutans producing exfoliative toxin"},{"id":"B","text":"Enterococcus faecalis producing listeriolysin"},{"id":"C","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus producing Shiga toxin"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptococcus sanguinis producing dextrans that bind fibrin-platelet aggregates"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae producing IgA protease"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Among the viridans group streptococci, S sanguinis makes dextrans that bind to fibrin-platelet aggregates on damaged heart valves, causing infective endocarditis. The mnemonic is that sanguinis means blood, and there is lots of blood in the heart. Viridans strep are α-hemolytic, optochin resistant, and bile insoluble, and are normal oropharyngeal microbiota that enter the bloodstream with dental manipulation.',
    '{"A":"Exfoliative toxin is not a streptococcal product at all; it is a Staphylococcus aureus toxin that causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome.","B":"Listeriolysin is a pore-forming toxin of Listeria monocytogenes that allows escape from the phagosome; enterococci do not produce it and are typically nonhemolytic gut organisms.","C":"Shiga toxin is produced by Shigella dysenteriae and enterohemorrhagic E coli; S gallolyticus is a gut organism linked to colon cancer and endocarditis but not to Shiga toxin production.","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is also α-hemolytic, but it is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, and its classic diseases are meningitis, otitis media, pneumonia, and sinusitis rather than dental-procedure endocarditis."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Clinical Bacteriology', 'Viridans group streptococci and dental caries',
    'A 9-year-old boy has multiple deep, dark cavities in his primary molars. He drinks sugary juice frequently and rarely brushes. His dentist explains that a specific group of oral bacteria ferments dietary sugar into acid that demineralizes enamel. A swab of the carious lesion grows α-hemolytic cocci that are optochin resistant.',
    'Which of the following organisms is the principal cause of dental caries in this setting?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"id":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"id":"C","text":"Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae"},{"id":"D","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"id":"E","text":"Streptococcus mutans"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Streptococcus mutans, along with S mitis, is a viridans group streptococcus that causes dental caries. These organisms are normal oropharyngeal microbiota, are α-hemolytic, and are optochin resistant. They ferment dietary sugars into acid, which demineralizes enamel and produces the cavities seen here.',
    '{"A":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a β-hemolytic group A organism whose dental relevance is nil; it causes pharyngitis, impetigo, and their immune sequelae.","B":"Staphylococcus epidermidis is a catalase-positive, coagulase-negative skin organism associated with device biofilms and blood culture contamination.","C":"Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae is a zoonotic organism that causes erysipeloid in people who handle fish and meat, not a caries-forming oral organism.","D":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is α-hemolytic too but is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, and it causes MOPS disease rather than caries."}'::jsonb,
    'easy'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Virus structure, replication, and classification',
    'A virology laboratory receives two purified virion preparations from a colleague: an influenza A virus and a poliovirus. The technician treating both samples with ether and a nonionic detergent finds that one preparation loses infectivity completely while the other is unaffected.',
    'Which of the following best explains the differential susceptibility of these two viruses?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Poliovirus is susceptible because its ssRNA genome is easily degraded by lipophilic solvents"},{"id":"B","text":"Both viruses should be resistant because both have icosahedral capsids"},{"id":"C","text":"Influenza virus is susceptible because it lacks a capsid and relies only on its envelope for protection"},{"id":"D","text":"Poliovirus is susceptible because it acquires its envelope from the nuclear membrane during egress"},{"id":"E","text":"Influenza virus is susceptible because its lipid envelope is acquired from the host plasma membrane as it buds from the cell"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Enveloped viruses such as influenza acquire a lipid bilayer from the host plasma membrane when they bud out of the cell, and this lipid envelope is what detergents, ether, and changes in temperature or humidity destroy. Naked viruses like poliovirus have only a protein capsid, so they survive such treatment. This is the practical consequence of the enveloped versus naked classification: enveloped viruses are easier to inactivate and spread less readily by fomites.',
    '{"A":"Poliovirus is the resistant one here: it is a naked picornavirus with no lipid to attack. Its ssRNA is protected inside a protein capsid, not dissolved by solvent.","B":"Capsid geometry does not protect an envelope. Both viruses are icosahedral, yet only the one with a lipid bilayer is destroyed by detergent.","C":"Influenza virus does have a capsid; it is an enveloped virus with a helical nucleocapsid plus an outer lipid bilayer studded with hemagglutinin and neuraminidase.","D":"Poliovirus is nonenveloped and has no envelope at all. Herpesviruses are the family that unusually acquires envelope from the nuclear membrane, not picornaviruses."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Virus structure, replication, and classification',
    'A 52-year-old man with long-standing hepatitis B infection is superinfected with hepatitis D virus. Hepatitis D cannot produce its own envelope protein and can only infect hepatocytes when hepatitis B surface antigen is being produced.',
    'This relationship between HDV and HBV is the classic example of which viral genetic interaction?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Complementation"},{"id":"B","text":"Phenotypic mixing"},{"id":"C","text":"Antigenic drift"},{"id":"D","text":"Recombination"},{"id":"E","text":"Reassortment"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Complementation occurs when one of two viruses infecting a cell has a mutation that makes a protein nonfunctional; the other, nonmutated virus supplies a functional copy of that protein that serves both viruses. HDV is a defective virus lacking an envelope gene, and replicating HBV supplies HBsAg as its envelope protein. This is why HDV cannot exist without HBV.',
    '{"B":"Phenotypic mixing is the coating of virus A''s genome with virus B''s surface proteins in a pseudovirion; the genome itself is unchanged and the complementation is not genetic.","C":"Antigenic drift refers to accumulating point mutations in hemagglutinin or neuraminidase of influenza virus that cause seasonal epidemics; it is unrelated to HDV dependence on HBV.","D":"Recombination is exchange of genes between two chromosomes by crossing over in regions of homologous base sequence, producing a hybrid genome rather than one virus supplying a missing protein.","E":"Reassortment requires segmented genomes that exchange whole segments, as with influenza virus; HDV and HBV genomes are not segmented and no segments are swapped."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Virus structure, replication, and classification',
    'A research fellow purifies naked nucleic acid from several viruses and transfects each preparation into permissive cultured cells. Preparations from a poxvirus and a negative-sense ssRNA virus both fail to produce infectious progeny, whereas nucleic acid from most double-stranded DNA viruses does.',
    'Which of the following best explains why naked nucleic acid from negative-sense ssRNA viruses is not infectious?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Their genome is infectious but only after it integrates into the host chromosome"},{"id":"B","text":"Negative-sense genomes are immediately translated into protein by host ribosomes"},{"id":"C","text":"They are double-stranded RNA viruses, and dsRNA cannot enter the cytoplasm"},{"id":"D","text":"Their genome is circular and cannot be transcribed by host enzymes"},{"id":"E","text":"They lack the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase needed to transcribe the genome, which is carried inside the virion instead"}]'::jsonb,
    'E',
    'Naked nucleic acids of most dsDNA viruses (except poxviruses and HBV) and of positive-strand ssRNA viruses are infectious, but those of negative-strand ssRNA and dsRNA viruses are not, because they lack the polymerases required to replicate. Negative-strand virions therefore package their own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to transcribe minus strand into plus strand. Remember the mnemonic: always bring polymerase or fail replication.',
    '{"A":"Integration into the host chromosome is a property of retroviruses using integrase, not of negative-strand RNA viruses, which never integrate.","B":"Host ribosomes translate positive-sense RNA directly because it is equivalent to mRNA; a negative-sense genome must first be transcribed into a positive-sense message.","C":"These viruses are single-stranded negative-sense RNA, not dsRNA; dsRNA is the property of reoviruses such as rotavirus.","D":"Circularity is not the issue; bunyaviruses and arenaviruses are circular and segmented, but the failure here applies to all negative-sense ssRNA viruses regardless of shape."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Virus structure, replication, and classification',
    'In a mouse model, a single cell is simultaneously coinfected with virus A, whose genome is type A, and virus B. Progeny virion 1 contains the genome of virus A but is coated entirely with the surface proteins of virus B, which alters its tropism. When progeny virion 1 infects a new cell, the resulting progeny virion 2 has a type A coat again.',
    'Which viral genetic phenomenon does this experiment illustrate?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"Phenotypic mixing, producing a pseudovirion whose tropism is set by the acquired coat"},{"id":"B","text":"Lysogenic conversion of the host cell by a temperate phage"},{"id":"C","text":"Complementation between a mutated and a wild-type virus"},{"id":"D","text":"Reassortment of genome segments between two segmented viruses"},{"id":"E","text":"Recombination through crossing over at homologous regions"}]'::jsonb,
    'A',
    'Phenotypic mixing happens when a cell is coinfected with two viruses and the genome of virus A gets partially or completely coated with the surface proteins of virus B, forming a pseudovirion. Because the type B protein coat determines tropism, the hybrid infects what virus B would infect, yet its progeny return to the type A coat encoded by its own genome. No genetic material is exchanged, which distinguishes it from recombination and reassortment.',
    '{"B":"Lysogenic conversion is phage DNA integrated into a bacterial chromosome that alters bacterial phenotype, such as toxin production; this experiment involves neither bacteria nor phages.","C":"Complementation requires one virus with a nonfunctional protein to be rescued by the other''s functional protein; here both viruses are intact and the phenotype is a mixed coat, not rescue of a defect.","D":"Reassortment requires segmented genomes and produces a permanently new hybrid genome, not a transiently mis-coated particle that reverts in the next generation.","E":"Recombination permanently exchanges homologous stretches of genes, so progeny 2 would carry altered genetic material rather than the unchanged type A genome."}'::jsonb,
    'hard'
  ),
  (
    'Microbiology', 'Virology', 'Virus structure, replication, and classification',
    'A graduate student is asked to predict which of two enveloped viruses will remain infectious longest on a plastic surface at room temperature. One is an enveloped DNA virus with an icosahedral capsid; the other is a naked virus with an icosahedral capsid.',
    'Which property most directly determines the difference in environmental stability between these virions?',
    '[{"id":"A","text":"The icosahedral symmetry of the capsid"},{"id":"B","text":"Whether the genome is linear or circular"},{"id":"C","text":"The diameter of the virion"},{"id":"D","text":"The presence of a lipid envelope, which is easily disrupted by drying and by changes in temperature and humidity"},{"id":"E","text":"Whether the genome is DNA or RNA"}]'::jsonb,
    'D',
    'Environmental stability of a virion is largely a function of its envelope. Enveloped viruses carry a fragile lipid bilayer that detergents, drying, heat, and humidity changes destroy, so they survive poorly outside the host and transmit mainly by respiratory droplets or direct contact. Naked viruses such as parvovirus, adenovirus, and poliovirus withstand these stresses and spread readily by fomites.',
    '{"A":"Both virions are icosahedral in this scenario, so capsid symmetry cannot explain a difference between them.","B":"Genome topology such as circular versus linear affects replication and packaging, not sensitivity to drying, detergent, or temperature.","C":"Virion size affects aerosol behavior slightly but is not the determinant of envelope-related chemical fragility.","E":"Genome chemistry matters for replication strategy and for whether naked nucleic acid is infectious, but not for the lipid-dependent stability difference described here."}'::jsonb,
    'medium'
  )
)
insert into public.questions
  (subject, major_topic, subtopic, vignette, question, options,
   correct_answer, explanation_correct, explanation_incorrect, difficulty)
select i.subject, i.major_topic, i.subtopic, i.vignette, i.question, i.options,
       i.correct_answer, i.explanation_correct, i.explanation_incorrect, i.difficulty
from incoming i
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.questions q
  where q.vignette = i.vignette and q.question = i.question
);

